window.CERT_DATA = window.CERT_DATA || {}; window.CERT_DATA["gpcde"] = {"domains":[{"n":1,"title":"Designing Highly Scalable, Secure, and Reliable Cloud-Native Applications","short":"Design & Architecture","weight":"","desc":"Choosing compute and storage, microservices and API design, security and IAM for apps, scalability, caching and reliability patterns.","lessons":[{"id":"d1l1","num":1,"title":"Choosing a compute platform","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run</strong> is the default answer for a stateless HTTP/gRPC service with unpredictable or bursty traffic. It scales up fast, <strong>scales to zero</strong>, and you pay only while requests are served <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q60\">Exam 1 · Q60</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run is built on Knative.</strong> Migrating a container from on-prem Knative, keeping the same deployment strategy, on a fully managed service → Cloud Run <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run handles <strong>long-lived, persistent gRPC connections</strong> with surges around specific dates (an IVR backend with 1–30 minute calls) at the lowest cost and effort <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q44\">Exam 3 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run supports <strong>gRPC</strong> natively. A gRPC API that publishes to an async message service → Cloud Run + <strong>Pub/Sub</strong>, not GKE/Compute Engine + self-managed Kafka <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>Modernizing a Python 3 web API that needs autoscaling, frequent deployments, low cost and low ops → Cloud Run, not App Engine flexible, a single VM, or a GKE rewrite <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q54\">Exam 4 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>A containerized Python service with daytime traffic and <strong>no traffic at night</strong> → Cloud Run, logging errors to <strong>standard error</strong> so <strong>Error Reporting</strong> picks them up automatically <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>App Engine flexible</strong> keeps a minimum number of instances running, so it costs money while idle <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>GKE</strong> (Standard or Autopilot) and <strong>Compute Engine MIGs</strong> scale, but they add cluster/VM management and cost more for spiky traffic than Cloud Run <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q60\">Exam 1 · Q60</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Comparison</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Platform</th>\n<th>Scales to zero</th>\n<th>Ops overhead</th>\n<th>Picked when</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud Run</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Lowest</td>\n<td>Stateless containers, spiky traffic, gRPC, Knative migration</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud Functions</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>One function per endpoint, put behind an HTTPS LB</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>App Engine flexible</td>\n<td>No (min instances)</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Rarely the best answer in this bank</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q54\">Exam 4 · Q54</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GKE Autopilot / Standard</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Medium–high</td>\n<td>You need Kubernetes features</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q60\">Exam 1 · Q60</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compute Engine / MIG</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Highest</td>\n<td>Legacy VM workloads</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q60\">Exam 1 · Q60</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q54\">Exam 4 · Q54</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Unpredictable traffic\" + \"minimize cost\" + \"stateless\" → <strong>Cloud Run + Firestore</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q60\">Exam 1 · Q60</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Knative\" anywhere in the stem → <strong>Cloud Run</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>An API with several URL paths, each mapped to a different function → <strong>one Cloud Function per path</strong>, each a separate backend service behind an <strong>HTTPS load balancer</strong> with path routing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>Hybrid (cloud + on-prem) microservices with an emphasis on managed services and scalability → <strong>GKE clusters in each environment via Anthos + Cloud Run for Anthos</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>. See the questionable-answers note on product naming.</li>\n<li>Errors must show up in Error Reporting with minimal setup → write them to <strong>stderr</strong>, not to a Cloud Storage bucket <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Deploy on Compute Engine and enable autoscaling\" does not fix a monolith's architecture <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>GKE Autopilot sounds \"managed\", but Cloud Run is still cheaper and simpler for intermittent stateless traffic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q60\">Exam 1 · Q60</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>Self-managed Kafka on GKE or VMs loses to Pub/Sub whenever the stem says \"minimize infrastructure management\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson compares Cloud Run, Cloud Functions, App Engine, GKE and Compute Engine for cloud-native workloads. Exam questions reward the platform that scales to zero and minimizes operations, which is usually Cloud Run, unless the stem names a specific constraint.","objectives":["Pick Cloud Run for stateless, spiky, gRPC or Knative-based workloads and justify it over GKE and Compute Engine.","Explain why App Engine flexible and managed instance groups cost more for intermittent traffic.","Design a multi-path API from separate Cloud Functions behind an HTTPS load balancer.","Route application errors to Error Reporting with minimal setup."],"takeaways":["Unpredictable traffic + minimize cost + stateless → Cloud Run with Firestore.","Knative in the stem → Cloud Run.","gRPC API that fans out asynchronously → Cloud Run publishing to Pub/Sub, not self-managed Kafka.","Log errors to stderr and Error Reporting picks them up automatically.","App Engine flexible always keeps minimum instances, so it is never free while idle."],"questions":["e1q11","e1q16","e1q29","e1q60","e2q23","e3q17","e3q44","e4q4","e4q54","e6q5","e6q16"]},{"id":"d1l2","num":2,"title":"Choosing a database","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud SQL</strong>: managed MySQL/PostgreSQL. Handles backups, replication and updates. Right for an existing relational schema with heavy reads/writes when the team can't run a database full time <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spanner</strong>: globally distributed, strongly consistent relational database. Pick it for <strong>global consistency + high availability</strong> (for example, point trading in a multiplayer game migrating from MySQL) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q59\">Exam 4 · Q59</a>, and for a SQL schema that must scale with global growth <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q29\">Exam 2 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Firestore</strong>: managed document database with a <strong>flexible schema</strong>. Right when a rigid MySQL schema is hard to evolve for user-specific state <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q40\">Exam 2 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Firestore in Native mode</strong> gives mobile/web clients <strong>offline persistence</strong>, <strong>real-time listeners</strong>, ACID transactions, semi-structured data, multi-zone replication and direct client access with an optional middle tier <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>Firestore Native also meets \"immediate access after submission + sort by multiple fields + diverse record formats\" for purchase history <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bigtable</strong>: wide-column NoSQL for high-throughput workloads. No offline client libraries and no fit for relational schemas <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>BigQuery</strong>: analytics warehouse. Not for transactional, low-latency app data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q59\">Exam 4 · Q59</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Comparison</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need in the stem</th>\n<th>Pick</th>\n<th>Not</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Existing MySQL schema, minimal DBA effort</td>\n<td>Cloud SQL</td>\n<td>Spanner (overkill), Marketplace MySQL (self-managed)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Global strong consistency, 99.999%</td>\n<td>Spanner</td>\n<td>Cloud SQL (regional), Bigtable</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q59\">Exam 4 · Q59</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Schema evolves per user, flexible fields</td>\n<td>Firestore / Datastore</td>\n<td>Cloud SQL, Spanner</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q40\">Exam 2 · Q40</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mobile offline sync + real-time updates</td>\n<td>Firestore Native</td>\n<td>Cloud SQL, Bigtable, SQLite + Cloud Storage sync</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ACID + semi-structured + offline client libs</td>\n<td>Firestore</td>\n<td>Bigtable, Cloud SQL, BigQuery</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analytics over PBs</td>\n<td>BigQuery</td>\n<td>Firestore</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Offline\", \"sync across devices\", \"real-time updates\" → <strong>Firestore (Native mode)</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Hierarchical data for a mobile app\" + least privilege → <strong>Firestore Native + <code>roles/datastore.user</code></strong>. <code>datastore.viewer</code> is read-only; <code>cloudsql.editor</code> is too broad <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Global\", \"consistency\", \"multi-region\" + relational → <strong>Spanner</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q59\">Exam 4 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Team can handle minor admin only\" + MySQL → <strong>Cloud SQL</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Diverse record formats\" → a document database, not Cloud SQL <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Firestore in <strong>Datastore mode</strong> is not the answer when offline sync or real-time client features are required <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Spanner is \"overkill\" for a standard MySQL workload with no global requirement <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>A self-managed SQLite + Cloud Storage sync job means writing your own sync logic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>A migration path of \"Cloud SQL, then import into Firestore Native\" is keyed in one vague item; treat it as weak <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a>. See questionable answers.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson maps requirements to Cloud SQL, Spanner, Firestore, Bigtable and BigQuery. Questions hinge on keywords such as existing MySQL schema, global consistency, offline sync and flexible schema.","objectives":["Choose between Cloud SQL and Spanner based on scale, geography and administrative effort.","Identify when Firestore Native mode is required for offline persistence and real-time listeners.","Rule out Bigtable and BigQuery for transactional mobile and web app data.","Pair a database choice with the least-privilege IAM role for it."],"takeaways":["Existing MySQL + small ops team → Cloud SQL; Spanner is overkill without a global need.","Global strong consistency and high availability → Spanner.","Offline, sync across devices, real-time updates → Firestore Native mode.","Schema that keeps changing per user → Firestore's document model.","Mobile backend writing to Firestore → roles/datastore.user, not datastore.viewer."],"questions":["e1q17","e2q14","e2q19","e2q29","e2q36","e2q40","e3q15","e4q31","e4q34","e4q42","e4q59"]},{"id":"d1l3","num":3,"title":"Schema and key design for scale","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spanner hotspots</strong>: monotonically increasing primary keys (such as <strong>version 1 UUIDs</strong>, which are timestamp-based) concentrate inserts on one split and make scaling non-linear <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Spanner transaction tables should use <strong>random UUIDs</strong> for the key, not incremental numbers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q29\">Exam 2 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>To search customers by phone number in Spanner: a <strong><code>Phones</code> child table interleaved in <code>Customers</code></strong>, plus an <strong>index on the phone number</strong> column <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q30\">Exam 2 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bigtable row keys</strong>: concatenating several human-readable attributes (e.g. <code>&lt;region&gt;_&lt;customerID&gt;_&lt;orderID&gt;</code>) spreads load and keeps keys readable <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Firestore</strong>: an unbounded number of child items (comments, posts) belongs in a <strong>subcollection</strong> under the parent document, not in an array field <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q45\">Exam 4 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>Firestore documents have a <strong>1 MB size limit</strong>, so nesting every post inside a profile document doesn't scale <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q45\">Exam 4 · Q45</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Hotspot table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Database</th>\n<th>Bad key</th>\n<th>Good key</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Spanner</td>\n<td>UUIDv1, auto-increment</td>\n<td>Random UUID (v4)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q29\">Exam 2 · Q29</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bigtable</td>\n<td>Sequential integers, epoch timestamps</td>\n<td>Concatenated readable attributes</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bigtable</td>\n<td>MD5 hash of content</td>\n<td>Evenly spread but not human-readable</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Spanner load test scales non-linearly → look for a <strong>monotonically increasing key</strong> first <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Human-readable\" + \"even distribution\" in Bigtable → <strong>concatenated attributes</strong>, not a hash <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Firestore one-to-many with unbounded children → <strong>root collection for the parent + subcollection for children</strong>: articles → comments <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>, users → posts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q45\">Exam 4 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>Spanner one-to-many that you need to search by the child value → <strong>interleaved child table + secondary index on the child column</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q30\">Exam 2 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>LIKE vs STARTS_WITH, 64-bit vs 32-bit ints, STRING for numerics: none of these explain non-linear Spanner scaling <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Storing comment IDs in an array on the article still needs extra reads and grows unbounded <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Cloud SQL with UUIDs\" fixes uniqueness but not global scale <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q29\">Exam 2 · Q29</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers key and schema design that avoids hotspots in Spanner and Bigtable, and data modeling for unbounded relationships in Firestore. Many questions are about spotting a monotonically increasing key.","objectives":["Diagnose non-linear Spanner scaling caused by monotonically increasing primary keys.","Design Bigtable row keys that are both evenly distributed and human-readable.","Model one-to-many relationships with Spanner interleaved tables and Firestore subcollections.","Explain the Firestore 1 MB document limit and its effect on schema choices."],"takeaways":["UUIDv1 and auto-increment keys hotspot Spanner; use random UUIDs.","Bigtable readable + even keys → concatenate several attributes; hashes aren't readable.","Search Spanner customers by phone → interleaved Phones table plus an index on the phone column.","Unbounded comments or posts → a Firestore subcollection, never an array field."],"questions":["e1q35","e2q29","e2q30","e2q33","e4q19","e4q45"]},{"id":"d1l4","num":4,"title":"High availability and resilient architecture","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>GKE regional clusters</strong> replicate the control plane and nodes across zones in a region. They survive a zone failure. <strong>Multi-zonal</strong> clusters spread nodes but keep a <strong>single-zone control plane</strong>, so a zone outage can still take down the GKE API <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q60\">Exam 2 · Q60</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spanner 99.999% + low global read latency</strong> → a multi-region instance config spanning continents (<strong><code>nam-asia-eur1</code></strong>) with <strong>at least 3 nodes</strong>, not separate regional instances or Dataflow replication <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bigtable app profile with multi-cluster routing</strong> gives automatic failover across clusters. Single-cluster routing with manual failover is the problem to fix <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q11\">Exam 6 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Containers on GKE</strong>: one component per container (Apache, PHP, MySQL separate) with <strong>readiness and liveness probes</strong>, so Kubernetes can restart unhealthy containers. Avoid one container running everything under a process manager or bash script <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spanner</strong> is the database for global consistency and high availability <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q59\">Exam 4 · Q59</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Zone failure took down the GKE API\" → <strong>regional cluster</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q60\">Exam 2 · Q60</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Bigtable, more efficient and highly available connectivity\" → <strong>multi-cluster routing</strong>, not a cache or a bigger connection pool <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q11\">Exam 6 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Worldwide users, five nines\" → <strong>multi-continent Spanner config</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Best practice for availability when containerizing a LAMP stack\" → <strong>separate containers + probes</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Multi-zone ≠ regional for GKE: the control plane is the single point of failure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q60\">Exam 2 · Q60</a>.</li>\n<li>A Memcached layer in front of Bigtable improves read latency, not failover <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q11\">Exam 6 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Two independent Spanner instances in separate regions do not give you the single, consistent, five-nines database a multi-region config gives <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Coordinating container startup with a script is fragile; use Kubernetes' built-in health mechanisms <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers the availability patterns the exam tests: GKE regional clusters, Spanner multi-region configurations, Bigtable multi-cluster routing, and one component per container with health probes.","objectives":["Distinguish GKE zonal, multi-zonal and regional clusters by how they handle a zone failure.","Select a Spanner configuration for five-nines availability and low global read latency.","Improve Bigtable availability with app profile routing.","Containerize a multi-component stack following Google's availability best practices."],"takeaways":["Zone outage took down the GKE API → regional cluster; multi-zonal still has a single-zone control plane.","Worldwide five nines → multi-continent Spanner config such as nam-asia-eur1.","Bigtable manual failover → switch the app profile to multi-cluster routing.","One process per container plus readiness and liveness probes."],"questions":["e2q36","e2q60","e3q1","e4q59","e6q11"]},{"id":"d1l5","num":5,"title":"Storage classes, lifecycle, retention and data governance","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Object Lifecycle Management</strong> moves objects to colder classes declaratively (e.g. Standard → Coldline after 14 days, Standard → Archive after 3 years). It beats a scheduled job or a Cloud Function + Cloud Scheduler doing the same thing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Retention policy / Bucket Lock</strong> guarantees objects can't be deleted or modified before the retention period ends. This is how you meet \"keep for 7 years\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Logs reviewed for two weeks then only annually → <strong>Coldline</strong> after 14 days. Nearline costs more than Coldline for this infrequently accessed data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Object Versioning</strong> guards against accidental overwrite/delete but does not enforce retention and adds cost. <strong>IAM Conditions</strong> control access, not retention <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Large read-only dataset shared by a MIG</strong>, fast startup, filesystem access, low cost → <strong>one persistent disk attached read-only to many VMs</strong>. Cloud Storage FUSE adds latency; copying at startup slows boot; per-VM disks from a snapshot cost more <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q52\">Exam 2 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Images served fast and cheaply</strong> → <strong>Cloud CDN with a Cloud Storage backend</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>BigQuery compliance</strong>: EU data residency → a <strong>dataset created in an EU region</strong>; right to delete → <strong>DML <code>DELETE</code>/<code>UPDATE</code></strong> statements <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>BigQuery row-level separation by department</strong> → views filtering by department, granted per group, without copying data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a>. See questionable answers for where the views should live.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Comparison: compliance vs cost features</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Solves</th>\n<th>Doesn't solve</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Retention policy / Bucket Lock</td>\n<td>\"Must keep N years\", can't delete</td>\n<td>Cost</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lifecycle rule (SetStorageClass)</td>\n<td>Cost of aging data</td>\n<td>Retention guarantee</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Object Versioning</td>\n<td>Accidental overwrite</td>\n<td>Retention, cost</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IAM Conditions</td>\n<td>Who can read</td>\n<td>Retention</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>JWT for users</td>\n<td>Nothing storage-related</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Accessible for at least 7 years\" + \"rarely accessed after 3 years\" → <strong>retention policy 7 years + lifecycle to Archive at 3 years</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Review 2 weeks, then auditor yearly\" → <strong>lifecycle to Coldline at 14 days + Bucket Lock retention</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Choose two\" with both a lifecycle rule and a script doing the same transition → prefer the <strong>native lifecycle rule</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>Shared read-only data for many VMs → <strong>read-only multi-attach PD</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q52\">Exam 2 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li>Residency in BigQuery → <strong>regional dataset</strong>; deletion on request → <strong>DML</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A Cloud Function + Cloud Scheduler that moves old objects to another bucket is custom code that native lifecycle already provides <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>An EU Cloud Storage bucket or federated queries don't satisfy BigQuery residency or deletion by themselves; a Dataflow re-upload is unnecessary complexity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Copying data into per-department datasets with pipelines duplicates data and needs syncing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>App Engine or a VM with Persistent Disk serving images loses to CDN + Cloud Storage <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers Cloud Storage classes, lifecycle rules, retention policies and Bucket Lock, plus shared read-only disks, CDN for images, and BigQuery residency and access control. Questions usually ask you to combine one compliance feature with one cost feature.","objectives":["Combine a retention policy with a lifecycle rule to meet retention and cost requirements together.","Choose the right colder storage class from the stated access pattern.","Share large read-only datasets across a managed instance group efficiently.","Meet BigQuery data residency and deletion requirements, and restrict access by department."],"takeaways":["Keep N years → retention policy or Bucket Lock; reduce cost → lifecycle rule to Coldline or Archive.","Native lifecycle rules beat Cloud Functions, schedulers and scripts that move objects.","Object Versioning and IAM Conditions don't enforce retention.","Many VMs need the same read-only data → one persistent disk attached read-only to all of them.","EU residency in BigQuery → an EU-region dataset; deletion requests → DML statements."],"questions":["e1q2","e1q8","e1q54","e2q19","e2q31","e2q52","e3q20","e3q33"]},{"id":"d1l6","num":6,"title":"Microservices migration and API design","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Monolith → microservices</strong> should be incremental: replace functionality <strong>gradually over several phases</strong> (Strangler Fig). Big-bang refactors and parallel rewrites are high risk <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pick the first service to migrate</strong> by low risk and few dependencies: the <strong>product catalog</strong> (frontend + product DB), not payments, order fulfillment or the shopping cart <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Priorities when designing composable microservices: an <strong>API contract</strong> between caller and implementation, and a <strong>versioning scheme</strong> for future incompatible changes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Expose versioned REST microservices on GKE through an <strong>Ingress</strong> that routes by URL (e.g. <code>/api/v1/</code>, <code>/api/v2/</code>), so consumers and third parties aren't disrupted by new releases <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>gRPC</strong> for mobile APIs: HTTP/2, HTTPS, compact Protocol Buffers payloads (less bandwidth than JSON or SOAP XML), and generated client code for Android/iOS <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Header-based routing</strong> across service implementations on VMs/instance groups in several regions → <strong>Traffic Director</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Async decoupling</strong>: publish to a <strong>Pub/Sub topic</strong> so multiple services can consume <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SPA calling a slow third-party API</strong>: make the request <strong>asynchronous</strong> so it doesn't block rendering, and <strong>hide the widget</strong> on timeout or error <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Minimize risk during migration\" → move the <strong>least coupled, least critical</strong> feature first <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Minimize business impact\" → <strong>phased replacement</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Choose two design aspects\" for microservices → <strong>contract + versioning</strong>, not \"same language\", \"always async\" or \"enough instances\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>\"HTTPS + minimal bandwidth + mobile\" → <strong>gRPC</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Don't break API consumers on new releases\" on GKE → <strong>Ingress with URL-based versioned routing</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Route by HTTP header\" across regions, loosely coupled → <strong>Traffic Director</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Third-party data is less important; don't let it block rendering\" → <strong>async + hide the widget</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>MQTT is pub/sub for IoT, not a request/response mobile API; SOAP is verbose XML <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>Retrying with backoff \"until success\" in a UI widget adds load and keeps the user waiting <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Putting the API version in the POST body, or using multiple clusters with DNS per version, is worse than URL-routed Ingress <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>Service Directory does discovery, not content-based routing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>One Cloud Function handling all paths, or directly exposed functions without a load balancer, is not the keyed design <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers how to break up a monolith safely and how to design the APIs between the resulting services. It includes phased migration, contracts and versioning, gRPC for mobile, Ingress-based version routing, header-based routing and asynchronous UI calls.","objectives":["Plan a phased monolith-to-microservices migration and choose a low-risk first service.","Identify the design aspects that matter most for composable microservices.","Choose gRPC, REST, MQTT or SOAP based on bandwidth and client requirements.","Route traffic to versioned or header-selected backends without breaking consumers."],"takeaways":["Migrate gradually (Strangler Fig); start with a loosely coupled feature like the product catalog.","Microservice priorities: an API contract and a versioning scheme.","HTTPS + low bandwidth + mobile → gRPC with Protocol Buffers.","Protect API consumers on GKE → Ingress routing by versioned URL path.","Slow third-party data in a SPA → async request and hide the widget on error."],"questions":["e1q10","e1q16","e1q21","e2q12","e2q22","e2q23","e2q25","e2q34","e3q54","e6q5"]},{"id":"d1l7","num":7,"title":"Service identity, IAM and least privilege","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Every workload gets a <strong>dedicated user-managed service account</strong> with only the permissions it needs. Don't use the Compute Engine or App Engine <strong>default service accounts</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Function calling other services → a new service account with a <strong>custom role</strong>, and grant the deployer <strong>permission to act as</strong> that SA, not permission to obtain its access token <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run reading/writing one bucket → user-managed SA with a <strong>custom role</strong> (e.g. only <code>storage.objects.get</code>/<code>create</code>), not Storage Admin or Project Editor <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run reading images from a <strong>private</strong> bucket → enable <strong>public access prevention</strong>, run the service as a user-managed SA, grant <strong>Storage Object Viewer</strong>. Signed URLs are for temporary access, not routine service access <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-project</strong>: function in Project A writing to a bucket in Project B → create the SA <strong>in Project A</strong>, grant it <strong><code>roles/storage.objectCreator</code> on the bucket in Project B</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud SQL</strong>: an app connecting from Compute Engine or through the <strong>Cloud SQL Auth Proxy on GKE</strong> needs the <strong>Cloud SQL Client</strong> role, not Cloud SQL Editor, Project Editor or Owner <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Firestore from a mobile backend</strong> → <code>roles/datastore.user</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Developer access per team</strong> → put users in <strong>groups</strong>, grant roles to groups, and give them the <strong>Project ID</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Workforce Identity Federation</strong> lets employees from an external IdP sign in to the Google Cloud console, with <strong>attribute mapping</strong> for name/photo. <strong>Workload</strong> Identity Federation is for workloads, not people <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Role picker</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Reject</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>App → Cloud SQL (VM or Auth Proxy on GKE)</td>\n<td><code>roles/cloudsql.client</code></td>\n<td>Editor, Owner, <code>cloudsql.editor</code></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Function writes into another project's bucket</td>\n<td><code>roles/storage.objectCreator</code> on that bucket</td>\n<td>Default App Engine SA</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Read images from private bucket</td>\n<td>Storage Object Viewer</td>\n<td>Default Compute SA, signed URLs</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Read + write a bucket</td>\n<td>Custom role</td>\n<td>Storage Admin, Project Editor</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Firestore read/write</td>\n<td><code>roles/datastore.user</code></td>\n<td><code>datastore.viewer</code> (read-only)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Least privilege\" + broad predefined role in the options → pick the narrowest role or a <strong>custom role</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Deployer\" in the stem → grant <strong>act-as</strong> on the runtime SA <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"External IdP\" + \"console access for employees\" → <strong>Workforce</strong> Identity Federation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Many developers across projects → <strong>groups</strong>, never per-user bindings <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>A Windows VM using the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy: the keyed answer is a custom role with <code>cloudsql.instances.connect</code> plus the instance connection name in the proxy command, and <code>--unix-socket</code> is wrong on Windows <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q6\">Exam 6 · Q6</a>. See questionable answers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Basic roles (Owner, Editor) are always wrong in least-privilege questions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><code>roles/cloudsql.viewer</code> doesn't let the proxy connect <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q6\">Exam 6 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>One service account per employee, impersonated by each user, is not identity federation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>A service account created in the <em>bucket's</em> project and used from another project's function is the distractor <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers workload identity and human access: dedicated service accounts, custom and narrow predefined roles, cross-project grants, Cloud SQL Client, group-based access and Workforce Identity Federation.","objectives":["Assign a dedicated user-managed service account with the narrowest role to Cloud Run and Cloud Functions workloads.","Grant cross-project access by binding a role on the target resource.","Select the correct role for applications connecting to Cloud SQL.","Choose between Workforce and Workload Identity Federation."],"takeaways":["Default compute and App Engine service accounts and basic roles are always the distractors.","The deployer gets act-as on the runtime service account, not its access token.","Function in Project A writing to a bucket in Project B → SA in A with objectCreator on B's bucket.","Apps that connect to Cloud SQL get the Cloud SQL Client role.","Employees from an external IdP into the console → Workforce Identity Federation."],"questions":["e1q17","e1q25","e2q38","e3q2","e3q10","e3q22","e4q12","e4q36","e6q4","e6q6"]},{"id":"d1l8","num":8,"title":"Network security, exposure and connectivity","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Public, unauthenticated, but encrypted</strong> access to a dashboard on Compute Engine → <strong>external HTTP(S) load balancer + Google-managed SSL certificate</strong> (auto-renewing). IAP would force authentication <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Employees-only internal app</strong> reachable from anywhere with Google Workspace accounts → <strong>HTTP(S) load balancer + Identity-Aware Proxy</strong>, allowing the company domain. No VPN, no IP allowlists, no obscure URLs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q55\">Exam 2 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SSH for maintenance</strong> without public IPs → <strong>IAP TCP forwarding for SSH</strong>, web servers on <strong>private IPs behind an HTTP(S) load balancer</strong>. Better than a bastion host or public IPs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q53\">Exam 3 · Q53</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Encrypt pod-to-pod traffic in GKE</strong> with minimal app changes → a <strong>service mesh (Istio) with sidecar injection and mutual TLS</strong>. Network Policies restrict traffic but don't encrypt it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Lowest TTFB</strong> for thousands of devices calling a single-region REST backend on Premium Tier → <strong>MIG behind an external Application Load Balancer</strong>, not DNS round-robin or a passthrough Network Load Balancer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>App in one project, AlloyDB in another, keep projects isolated</strong> → keyed answer is a <strong>Shared VPC</strong> with both as service projects <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Comparison: who can reach the app</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n<th>Front end</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Anyone, no login, encrypted</td>\n<td>HTTPS LB + Google-managed cert</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Only company Workspace users, anywhere</td>\n<td>HTTPS LB + IAP</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q55\">Exam 2 · Q55</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Admin SSH, no public IPs</td>\n<td>IAP for TCP/SSH</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q53\">Exam 3 · Q53</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Service-to-service encryption in GKE</td>\n<td>Istio mTLS</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Without authentication\" → <strong>not IAP</strong>; use TLS on the load balancer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Company employees\", \"Google Workspace\", \"from anywhere\" → <strong>IAP</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q55\">Exam 2 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Encrypt traffic, minimize app changes, Google-supported\" → <strong>mesh + mTLS</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Minimize latency / TTFB\" for HTTP clients → <strong>global external Application LB</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A service account key can't encrypt web traffic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Exporting reports hourly to a public bucket breaks \"real-time\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Let's Encrypt certificates baked into every app = lots of app changes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Weighted or plain round-robin Cloud DNS gives no proximity optimization <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>A service account JSON key passed as <code>--credentials-file</code> doesn't solve network connectivity and adds key-management risk <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers how to expose applications safely and connect them across networks: TLS on load balancers, Identity-Aware Proxy for web and SSH, mTLS through a service mesh, external Application Load Balancers for latency, and cross-project database connectivity.","objectives":["Choose between a managed-certificate load balancer and IAP from the authentication requirement.","Provide administrative SSH access without public IPs or bastion hosts.","Encrypt in-cluster GKE traffic with minimal application changes.","Choose a load balancer that minimizes time to first byte for HTTP clients."],"takeaways":["Public and unauthenticated but encrypted → HTTPS load balancer with a Google-managed certificate.","Workspace employees from anywhere → HTTPS load balancer + IAP.","SSH to private VMs → IAP TCP forwarding, not a bastion host.","Encrypt pod-to-pod traffic → service mesh sidecars with mutual TLS; Network Policies don't encrypt.","Lowest TTFB for HTTP devices → MIG behind an external Application Load Balancer."],"questions":["e1q36","e2q41","e2q55","e3q53","e4q15","e6q8","e6q15"]},{"id":"d1l9","num":9,"title":"Caching, sessions and backend performance","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sessions</strong> → <strong>Memorystore</strong> (Redis or Memcached), which supports expiry for idle-timeout logouts. <strong>Shopping carts</strong> that must survive logout → <strong>Firestore</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>Session data in local memory with cookie-based affinity on the LB is not the recommended pattern <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Overloaded Cloud SQL behind an API</strong> → expose the API through <strong>Apigee</strong>, cache hot data in <strong>Memorystore for Redis</strong>, and retry failures with <strong>exponential backoff</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud CDN</strong> caches static content such as images in front of Cloud Storage. It is not the tool for dynamic, database-backed API responses <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Don't let a slow non-critical dependency block the UI: async call + graceful hiding <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Session\" + \"expire after N minutes\" → <strong>Memorystore</strong>; \"persist cart across logins\" → <strong>Firestore</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Database overwhelmed by repeat reads\" → <strong>in-memory cache (Redis)</strong> in front of it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Static images, fast, cheap\" → <strong>CDN + Cloud Storage</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Pub/Sub is not a session store; Cloud Storage files keyed by session ID are not a cart database; BigQuery is not for session data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>Queuing synchronous API calls through Pub/Sub doesn't reduce request latency <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Just adding memory to Cloud SQL plus a home-grown rate limiter is the distractor <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers where sessions and carts belong and how to take load off an overwhelmed database. The usual answer pairs Memorystore with Firestore for state, and Apigee, Redis caching and exponential backoff for API load.","objectives":["Store session data and persistent cart data in the appropriate managed services.","Relieve an overloaded Cloud SQL backend with caching and API management.","Tell when Cloud CDN fits (static content) and when it doesn't (dynamic API responses).","Apply retry with exponential backoff appropriately."],"takeaways":["Sessions with idle expiry → Memorystore; carts that survive logout → Firestore.","Overwhelmed Cloud SQL behind an API → Apigee + Memorystore for Redis + exponential backoff.","Cloud CDN + Cloud Storage for images, not for database-backed API responses.","Pub/Sub, BigQuery and Cloud Storage files are not session stores."],"questions":["e1q5","e1q21","e3q33","e3q59","e6q1"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li>Spiky or unpredictable stateless traffic + low cost → <strong>Cloud Run + Firestore</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q60\">Exam 1 · Q60</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Knative\" → <strong>Cloud Run</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>gRPC API + async fan-out → <strong>Cloud Run + Pub/Sub</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>Errors into Error Reporting → log to <strong>stderr</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>Existing MySQL, little DBA capacity → <strong>Cloud SQL</strong>. Global consistency + five nines → <strong>Spanner</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q59\">Exam 4 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>Offline + real-time mobile sync → <strong>Firestore Native</strong> with <code>roles/datastore.user</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Spanner keys: <strong>random UUIDs</strong>, never UUIDv1 or sequences <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q29\">Exam 2 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>Spanner search on child values → <strong>interleaved child table + index</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q30\">Exam 2 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>Bigtable readable + even keys → <strong>concatenated attributes</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Firestore unbounded children → <strong>subcollection</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q45\">Exam 4 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>Zone-proof GKE → <strong>regional cluster</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q60\">Exam 2 · Q60</a>. Bigtable HA → <strong>multi-cluster routing</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q11\">Exam 6 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Keep N years → <strong>retention policy / Bucket Lock</strong>. Cut cost → <strong>lifecycle rule</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery residency → <strong>regional dataset</strong>; right to erasure → <strong>DML</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Shared read-only data for a MIG → <strong>read-only multi-attach PD</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q52\">Exam 2 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li>Monolith migration → <strong>phased (Strangler Fig)</strong>, start with the <strong>product catalog</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Microservice design → <strong>API contract + versioning</strong>; expose versions via <strong>Ingress URL routing</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>Mobile API, low bandwidth → <strong>gRPC</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Header-based routing across regions → <strong>Traffic Director</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Dedicated <strong>user-managed SA</strong>, narrowest role; deployer gets <strong>act-as</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Cross-project write → SA in the <strong>function's</strong> project + <code>objectCreator</code> on the <strong>other</strong> bucket <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud SQL from apps → <strong>Cloud SQL Client</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Public, no login, encrypted → <strong>LB + Google-managed cert</strong>. Employees only → <strong>LB + IAP</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q55\">Exam 2 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li>SSH without public IPs → <strong>IAP TCP forwarding</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q53\">Exam 3 · Q53</a>. Pod traffic encryption → <strong>mesh mTLS</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Sessions → <strong>Memorystore</strong>; carts → <strong>Firestore</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>Overloaded Cloud SQL API → <strong>Apigee + Redis cache + exponential backoff</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d1l1","title":"Choosing a compute platform","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Unpredictable traffic\" + \"minimize cost\" + \"stateless\" → <strong>Cloud Run + Firestore</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q60\">Exam 1 · Q60</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Knative\" anywhere in the stem → <strong>Cloud Run</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>An API with several URL paths, each mapped to a different function → <strong>one Cloud Function per path</strong>, each a separate backend service behind an <strong>HTTPS load balancer</strong> with path routing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>Hybrid (cloud + on-prem) microservices with an emphasis on managed services and scalability → <strong>GKE clusters in each environment via Anthos + Cloud Run for Anthos</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>. See the questionable-answers note on product naming.</li>\n<li>Errors must show up in Error Reporting with minimal setup → write them to <strong>stderr</strong>, not to a Cloud Storage bucket <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l2","title":"Choosing a database","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Offline\", \"sync across devices\", \"real-time updates\" → <strong>Firestore (Native mode)</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Hierarchical data for a mobile app\" + least privilege → <strong>Firestore Native + <code>roles/datastore.user</code></strong>. <code>datastore.viewer</code> is read-only; <code>cloudsql.editor</code> is too broad <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Global\", \"consistency\", \"multi-region\" + relational → <strong>Spanner</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q59\">Exam 4 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Team can handle minor admin only\" + MySQL → <strong>Cloud SQL</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Diverse record formats\" → a document database, not Cloud SQL <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l3","title":"Schema and key design for scale","html":"<ul>\n<li>Spanner load test scales non-linearly → look for a <strong>monotonically increasing key</strong> first <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Human-readable\" + \"even distribution\" in Bigtable → <strong>concatenated attributes</strong>, not a hash <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Firestore one-to-many with unbounded children → <strong>root collection for the parent + subcollection for children</strong>: articles → comments <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>, users → posts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q45\">Exam 4 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>Spanner one-to-many that you need to search by the child value → <strong>interleaved child table + secondary index on the child column</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q30\">Exam 2 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l4","title":"High availability and resilient architecture","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Zone failure took down the GKE API\" → <strong>regional cluster</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q60\">Exam 2 · Q60</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Bigtable, more efficient and highly available connectivity\" → <strong>multi-cluster routing</strong>, not a cache or a bigger connection pool <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q11\">Exam 6 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Worldwide users, five nines\" → <strong>multi-continent Spanner config</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Best practice for availability when containerizing a LAMP stack\" → <strong>separate containers + probes</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l5","title":"Storage classes, lifecycle, retention and data governance","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Accessible for at least 7 years\" + \"rarely accessed after 3 years\" → <strong>retention policy 7 years + lifecycle to Archive at 3 years</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Review 2 weeks, then auditor yearly\" → <strong>lifecycle to Coldline at 14 days + Bucket Lock retention</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Choose two\" with both a lifecycle rule and a script doing the same transition → prefer the <strong>native lifecycle rule</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>Shared read-only data for many VMs → <strong>read-only multi-attach PD</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q52\">Exam 2 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li>Residency in BigQuery → <strong>regional dataset</strong>; deletion on request → <strong>DML</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l6","title":"Microservices migration and API design","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Minimize risk during migration\" → move the <strong>least coupled, least critical</strong> feature first <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Minimize business impact\" → <strong>phased replacement</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Choose two design aspects\" for microservices → <strong>contract + versioning</strong>, not \"same language\", \"always async\" or \"enough instances\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>\"HTTPS + minimal bandwidth + mobile\" → <strong>gRPC</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Don't break API consumers on new releases\" on GKE → <strong>Ingress with URL-based versioned routing</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Route by HTTP header\" across regions, loosely coupled → <strong>Traffic Director</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Third-party data is less important; don't let it block rendering\" → <strong>async + hide the widget</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l7","title":"Service identity, IAM and least privilege","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Least privilege\" + broad predefined role in the options → pick the narrowest role or a <strong>custom role</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Deployer\" in the stem → grant <strong>act-as</strong> on the runtime SA <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"External IdP\" + \"console access for employees\" → <strong>Workforce</strong> Identity Federation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Many developers across projects → <strong>groups</strong>, never per-user bindings <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>A Windows VM using the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy: the keyed answer is a custom role with <code>cloudsql.instances.connect</code> plus the instance connection name in the proxy command, and <code>--unix-socket</code> is wrong on Windows <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q6\">Exam 6 · Q6</a>. See questionable answers.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l8","title":"Network security, exposure and connectivity","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Without authentication\" → <strong>not IAP</strong>; use TLS on the load balancer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Company employees\", \"Google Workspace\", \"from anywhere\" → <strong>IAP</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q55\">Exam 2 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Encrypt traffic, minimize app changes, Google-supported\" → <strong>mesh + mTLS</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Minimize latency / TTFB\" for HTTP clients → <strong>global external Application LB</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l9","title":"Caching, sessions and backend performance","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Session\" + \"expire after N minutes\" → <strong>Memorystore</strong>; \"persist cart across logins\" → <strong>Firestore</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Database overwhelmed by repeat reads\" → <strong>in-memory cache (Redis)</strong> in front of it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Static images, fast, cheap\" → <strong>CDN + Cloud Storage</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d1l1","title":"Choosing a compute platform","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Deploy on Compute Engine and enable autoscaling\" does not fix a monolith's architecture <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>GKE Autopilot sounds \"managed\", but Cloud Run is still cheaper and simpler for intermittent stateless traffic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q60\">Exam 1 · Q60</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>Self-managed Kafka on GKE or VMs loses to Pub/Sub whenever the stem says \"minimize infrastructure management\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l2","title":"Choosing a database","html":"<ul>\n<li>Firestore in <strong>Datastore mode</strong> is not the answer when offline sync or real-time client features are required <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Spanner is \"overkill\" for a standard MySQL workload with no global requirement <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>A self-managed SQLite + Cloud Storage sync job means writing your own sync logic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>A migration path of \"Cloud SQL, then import into Firestore Native\" is keyed in one vague item; treat it as weak <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a>. See questionable answers.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l3","title":"Schema and key design for scale","html":"<ul>\n<li>LIKE vs STARTS_WITH, 64-bit vs 32-bit ints, STRING for numerics: none of these explain non-linear Spanner scaling <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Storing comment IDs in an array on the article still needs extra reads and grows unbounded <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Cloud SQL with UUIDs\" fixes uniqueness but not global scale <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q29\">Exam 2 · Q29</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l4","title":"High availability and resilient architecture","html":"<ul>\n<li>Multi-zone ≠ regional for GKE: the control plane is the single point of failure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q60\">Exam 2 · Q60</a>.</li>\n<li>A Memcached layer in front of Bigtable improves read latency, not failover <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q11\">Exam 6 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Two independent Spanner instances in separate regions do not give you the single, consistent, five-nines database a multi-region config gives <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Coordinating container startup with a script is fragile; use Kubernetes' built-in health mechanisms <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l5","title":"Storage classes, lifecycle, retention and data governance","html":"<ul>\n<li>A Cloud Function + Cloud Scheduler that moves old objects to another bucket is custom code that native lifecycle already provides <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>An EU Cloud Storage bucket or federated queries don't satisfy BigQuery residency or deletion by themselves; a Dataflow re-upload is unnecessary complexity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Copying data into per-department datasets with pipelines duplicates data and needs syncing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>App Engine or a VM with Persistent Disk serving images loses to CDN + Cloud Storage <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l6","title":"Microservices migration and API design","html":"<ul>\n<li>MQTT is pub/sub for IoT, not a request/response mobile API; SOAP is verbose XML <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>Retrying with backoff \"until success\" in a UI widget adds load and keeps the user waiting <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Putting the API version in the POST body, or using multiple clusters with DNS per version, is worse than URL-routed Ingress <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>Service Directory does discovery, not content-based routing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>One Cloud Function handling all paths, or directly exposed functions without a load balancer, is not the keyed design <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l7","title":"Service identity, IAM and least privilege","html":"<ul>\n<li>Basic roles (Owner, Editor) are always wrong in least-privilege questions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><code>roles/cloudsql.viewer</code> doesn't let the proxy connect <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q6\">Exam 6 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>One service account per employee, impersonated by each user, is not identity federation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>A service account created in the <em>bucket's</em> project and used from another project's function is the distractor <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l8","title":"Network security, exposure and connectivity","html":"<ul>\n<li>A service account key can't encrypt web traffic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Exporting reports hourly to a public bucket breaks \"real-time\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Let's Encrypt certificates baked into every app = lots of app changes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Weighted or plain round-robin Cloud DNS gives no proximity optimization <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>A service account JSON key passed as <code>--credentials-file</code> doesn't solve network connectivity and adds key-management risk <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l9","title":"Caching, sessions and backend performance","html":"<ul>\n<li>Pub/Sub is not a session store; Cloud Storage files keyed by session ID are not a cart database; BigQuery is not for session data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>Queuing synchronous API calls through Pub/Sub doesn't reduce request latency <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Just adding memory to Cloud SQL plus a home-grown rate limiter is the distractor <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Issue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys <strong>II + IV</strong> (scheduled job <em>and</em> lifecycle rule, which do the same thing) and rejects Bucket Lock as \"expensive\". Bucket Lock has no extra charge, and the stem requires 7-year retention. The same scenario in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a> keys <strong>Bucket Lock + Coldline lifecycle</strong>. <strong>Prefer I + IV.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys \"views inside the Master dataset\". Google's <strong>authorized view</strong> pattern puts the views in a <strong>separate dataset</strong> that users can access, then authorizes those views on the source dataset (option I). Granting dataset-level access inside \"Master\" risks exposing the base tables.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q6\">Exam 6 · Q6</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys a custom role with only <code>cloudsql.instances.connect</code>. Google's documentation recommends <strong><code>roles/cloudsql.client</code></strong> for the Auth Proxy (as in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a> and <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a>), and the proxy also needs <code>cloudsql.instances.get</code>. The real eliminator for option B is that <code>--unix-socket</code> doesn't apply on Windows.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a></strong></td>\n<td>The answer (Cloud Run) is right, but the explanation says App Engine <strong>standard</strong> doesn't scale to zero. It does; only flexible keeps minimum instances.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a></strong></td>\n<td>The key is fine, but the explanation calls <code>nam3</code> a \"regional\" config. <code>nam3</code> is a <strong>multi-region</strong> config limited to North America. \"3 nodes so one can fail\" also misdescribes Spanner: replication comes from the instance configuration, not the node count.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a></strong></td>\n<td>Traffic Director is now part of <strong>Cloud Service Mesh</strong>. The claim that internal HTTP(S) load balancing can't route on headers is wrong; internal Application LBs support header-based routing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a></strong></td>\n<td>The \"Istio on GKE\" add-on the references point to is deprecated. Today the Google-supported route is <strong>Cloud Service Mesh</strong> (managed Istio). The mTLS-via-sidecar idea still holds.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a></strong></td>\n<td>\"Anthos\" is now <strong>GKE Enterprise</strong>, and \"Cloud Run for Anthos\" became <strong>Knative serving</strong>, which is no longer offered as a managed add-on. Learn the key, but expect newer wording.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a></strong></td>\n<td>The stem has no scenario (no current database, no workload) and the key (\"migrate to Cloud SQL, then import into Firestore Native\") isn't justified. Don't build rules from it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q53\">Exam 3 · Q53</a></strong></td>\n<td>The key is fine, but the explanation says one HTTP(S) LB \"handles both web traffic and SSH\". IAP TCP forwarding for SSH is separate from the load balancer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a></strong></td>\n<td>The key (external Application LB) is fine, but the reasoning is off. The backend is in one region, so the gain comes from terminating connections at Google's edge on Premium Tier, not from \"routing to the closest backend\".</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a></strong></td>\n<td>Shared VPC is keyed, but it puts both projects on one network, which weakens the \"keep projects isolated\" requirement. Private Service Connect is the newer cross-project option for AlloyDB. Answer the key if PSC isn't offered.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a></strong></td>\n<td>Firestore Native is right (real-time). The claim that Datastore mode is \"less performant\" is not the real difference: Datastore mode lacks the real-time and offline client features.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a></strong></td>\n<td>\"REST uses HTTP/1.1\" is an overgeneralization. The bandwidth argument for gRPC rests on Protobuf, not on REST being limited to HTTP/1.1.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},"glossary":[{"term":"AlloyDB","definition":"Google Cloud's managed PostgreSQL-compatible database service.","exam_context":"Appears in a cross-project connectivity question where the key is a Shared VPC rather than service account keys or extra firewall rules.","lesson":"d1l8","domain":1},{"term":"Apigee","definition":"Google Cloud's API management platform, providing traffic control, rate limiting, caching, monitoring and analytics for APIs.","exam_context":"Paired with Memorystore and exponential backoff to protect an overwhelmed Cloud SQL backend.","lesson":"d1l9","domain":1},{"term":"Authorized view","definition":"A BigQuery view that is granted access to a source dataset so users can query the view's filtered rows without access to the underlying tables.","exam_context":"Used to limit employees to their own department's rows in a shared Master dataset.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Bigtable app profile","definition":"Settings that control how an application's requests are routed to the clusters of a Bigtable instance, using single-cluster or multi-cluster routing.","exam_context":"Switching from single-cluster routing with manual failover to multi-cluster routing is the high-availability fix.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"Bigtable row key","definition":"The single indexed key of a Bigtable row; its design determines how load spreads across nodes.","exam_context":"Concatenating readable attributes gives an even spread with readable keys; sequential IDs and timestamps cause hotspots.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"Bucket Lock","definition":"A Cloud Storage feature that locks a bucket's retention policy so objects can't be deleted or replaced before the retention period ends.","exam_context":"Combined with a lifecycle rule to meet multi-year retention and cost goals.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud CDN","definition":"Google's edge caching service, which serves cached content from locations close to users.","exam_context":"With Cloud Storage as the backend it is the cheap, fast way to serve images, but not dynamic API responses.","lesson":"d1l9","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud Run","definition":"A fully managed serverless platform, built on Knative, for running stateless containers that scale automatically, including to zero.","exam_context":"The default answer for spiky traffic, Knative migrations, gRPC APIs and low-ops modernization.","lesson":"d1l1","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud SQL Auth Proxy","definition":"A connector that provides authorized, encrypted connections from applications to Cloud SQL instances using IAM.","exam_context":"The service account running it needs the Cloud SQL Client role (or the connect permission), never Editor or Owner.","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud SQL Client role","definition":"The predefined role roles/cloudsql.client, which grants the permissions needed to connect to Cloud SQL instances.","exam_context":"The least-privilege answer for VMs and GKE workloads that talk to Cloud SQL.","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1},{"term":"Coldline Storage","definition":"A low-cost Cloud Storage class for data accessed rarely, cheaper to store than Nearline.","exam_context":"Logs reviewed for two weeks and then only yearly move to Coldline via a lifecycle rule after 14 days.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Exponential backoff","definition":"A retry strategy where the wait between retries grows exponentially to avoid overwhelming a struggling service.","exam_context":"Part of the fix for an overloaded backend, but unbounded retries in a UI widget are a trap.","lesson":"d1l9","domain":1},{"term":"Firestore Native mode","definition":"Firestore's document database mode, with real-time listeners, offline persistence in client libraries, and ACID transactions.","exam_context":"Chosen whenever a mobile or web app needs offline sync, real-time updates or a flexible schema.","lesson":"d1l2","domain":1},{"term":"Firestore subcollection","definition":"A collection nested under a document, used to hold an unbounded set of related child documents.","exam_context":"Comments under articles and posts under user profiles belong in subcollections, not arrays.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"Google-managed SSL certificate","definition":"A TLS certificate that Google provisions and renews automatically for a load balancer.","exam_context":"Encrypts a public dashboard that must stay reachable without authentication.","lesson":"d1l8","domain":1},{"term":"gRPC","definition":"A high-performance RPC framework that uses HTTP/2 and compact Protocol Buffers serialization, with generated client code for many languages.","exam_context":"The answer when an API needs HTTPS, minimal bandwidth and easy mobile integration.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1},{"term":"Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP)","definition":"A service that checks user identity and context before allowing access to web apps or TCP resources such as SSH.","exam_context":"Used for employee-only apps and SSH without public IPs, and rejected when access must be unauthenticated.","lesson":"d1l8","domain":1},{"term":"Ingress","definition":"A Kubernetes resource that routes external HTTP(S) traffic to Services based on hosts and URL paths; on GKE it provisions a Google Cloud load balancer.","exam_context":"Routes versioned API URLs to separate backends so new releases don't break consumers.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1},{"term":"Interleaved table","definition":"A Spanner child table whose rows are physically stored with their parent row, sharing the parent's primary key prefix.","exam_context":"A Phones table interleaved in Customers, plus an index on phone number, enables searching customers by phone.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"Knative","definition":"An open-source Kubernetes-based platform for serverless workloads that Cloud Run is built on.","exam_context":"Migrating from on-prem Knative without changing the deployment strategy points to Cloud Run.","lesson":"d1l1","domain":1},{"term":"Liveness and readiness probes","definition":"Kubernetes health checks: liveness restarts a stuck container, and readiness controls whether it receives traffic.","exam_context":"Required alongside one-component-per-container when containerizing a LAMP stack for availability.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"Memorystore","definition":"Google Cloud's managed in-memory data store service for Redis and Memcached.","exam_context":"Holds session data with expiry, and caches hot reads in front of Cloud SQL.","lesson":"d1l9","domain":1},{"term":"Multi-cluster routing","definition":"A Bigtable app profile policy that sends requests to the nearest available cluster and fails over automatically.","exam_context":"Replaces single-cluster routing with manual failover to improve availability.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"Mutual TLS (mTLS)","definition":"TLS in which both client and server authenticate with certificates, encrypting service-to-service traffic.","exam_context":"Enabled through a service mesh's sidecar proxies to encrypt GKE traffic without changing application code.","lesson":"d1l8","domain":1},{"term":"Object Lifecycle Management","definition":"Cloud Storage rules that automatically change an object's storage class or delete it based on conditions such as age.","exam_context":"Preferred over scheduled jobs or Cloud Functions for moving aging data to Coldline or Archive.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Pub/Sub","definition":"Google Cloud's managed asynchronous messaging service for decoupling publishers from multiple subscribers.","exam_context":"Beats self-managed Kafka for async fan-out, but is wrong as a session store or to speed up synchronous APIs.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1},{"term":"Read-only persistent disk","definition":"A Compute Engine persistent disk attached in read-only mode to multiple VMs at once.","exam_context":"The fast, low-cost way to give a managed instance group a large shared read-only dataset.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Regional GKE cluster","definition":"A GKE cluster whose control plane and nodes are replicated across multiple zones in a region.","exam_context":"The fix after a zone failure caused a GKE API outage; multi-zonal clusters still have a single-zone control plane.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"Retention policy","definition":"A Cloud Storage bucket setting that prevents deleting or modifying objects until they reach a minimum age.","exam_context":"Meets requirements such as keeping data accessible for at least 7 years.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Service account impersonation (act as)","definition":"Permission for a principal to deploy or run resources as a service account without holding its keys or tokens.","exam_context":"The deployer of a Cloud Function gets act-as on the function's service account, not access-token rights.","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1},{"term":"Shared VPC","definition":"A setup where a host project shares its VPC network with attached service projects.","exam_context":"The keyed way to connect an application and AlloyDB that live in separate projects.","lesson":"d1l8","domain":1},{"term":"Spanner","definition":"Google Cloud's globally distributed, strongly consistent, horizontally scalable relational database.","exam_context":"Chosen for global consistency and five nines; its keys must avoid monotonically increasing values.","lesson":"d1l2","domain":1},{"term":"Spanner multi-region configuration","definition":"A predefined Spanner instance configuration, such as nam-asia-eur1, that replicates data across several regions.","exam_context":"A multi-continent configuration plus enough nodes gives worldwide low-latency reads and 99.999% availability.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"Strangler Fig pattern","definition":"A migration approach that gradually replaces a monolith's features with new services while both run side by side.","exam_context":"The answer to monolith-to-microservices migration with minimal business impact.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1},{"term":"Traffic Director","definition":"Google Cloud's managed control plane for service mesh traffic, supporting advanced routing such as HTTP header matching across regions.","exam_context":"Chosen to call different backend implementations based on an HTTP header value.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1},{"term":"User-managed service account","definition":"A service account you create for a specific workload, as opposed to default service accounts created automatically.","exam_context":"Every least-privilege question expects a dedicated user-managed service account with a narrow or custom role.","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1},{"term":"UUID primary key","definition":"A universally unique identifier used as a key; random (v4) UUIDs spread writes, while time-based v1 UUIDs increase monotonically.","exam_context":"Random UUID keys avoid Spanner hotspots; UUIDv1 explains non-linear scaling.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"Workforce Identity Federation","definition":"An IAM feature that lets users from an external identity provider access Google Cloud, including the console, without Google accounts.","exam_context":"The answer for employee console access through an external IdP with attribute mapping; Workload Identity Federation is the workload distractor.","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1}]},{"n":2,"title":"Building and Testing Applications","short":"Build & Test","weight":"","desc":"Local development and emulators, Cloud Code, container builds, testing strategies, and instrumenting code for logs, traces and errors.","lessons":[{"id":"d2l1","num":1,"title":"Developer environments and source control","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud Shell</strong> runs in a browser and comes with a built-in code editor, Git and integrated access to your project's resources. Source code never lands on the machine you are sitting at <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Code</strong> is the IDE extension for VS Code and IntelliJ. It adds Kubernetes-aware completion and snippets, debugging against the cluster, deploy workflows and cluster resource management <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Workstations</strong> are managed development environments that can reach resources inside a VPC. They are built from base images that you can extend with custom images <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>kubeconfig</strong> file holds clusters, users and contexts. <code>kubectl config</code> switches between contexts, so one file can cover several GKE clusters <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Trunk-based development</strong> means small, frequent commits to the main branch. This keeps merge conflicts small when releases are frequent <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Which developer environment?</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement in the stem</th>\n<th>Pick</th>\n<th>Why not the others</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Emergency fix from a public or non-corporate computer, no local code allowed</td>\n<td><strong>Cloud Shell + its editor</strong>, then open a pull request <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a></td>\n<td>A public PC with Git or a VM stores code locally. A mounted Cloud Storage bucket is not a Git workflow.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rich IDE support for GKE in VS Code or IntelliJ</td>\n<td><strong>Cloud Code</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a></td>\n<td>The Cloud Shell editor is for quick edits. Notebooks are for data and ML work.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consistent security controls, access inside VPC-SC or PSC networks, custom tools allowed</td>\n<td><strong>Cloud Workstations with preconfigured base images + custom images rebuilt weekly</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a></td>\n<td>Letting developers build their own images breaks consistency. Cloud Code + VPN or IAP is more complex and less isolated.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>No local source code allowed: use <strong>Cloud Shell</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>IDE named (VS Code, IntelliJ) and GKE or Cloud Run work: use <strong>Cloud Code</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Security baseline plus custom tooling: use <strong>centrally managed images that get rebuilt on a schedule</strong>, not images developers create freely <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>Multiple clusters: the bank's key is <strong>one shared config file defining clusters, users and contexts + <code>kubectl config</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>. This key is debatable; see the questionable-answers section.</li>\n<li>Rapid releases with few merge conflicts: use <strong>trunk-based development</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Coding fundamentals that show up</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Aggregating a value per key (for example, summing transaction amounts per account number) calls for a <strong>hash table</strong>. It gives near-constant-time lookups. A linked list is O(n), and a 2-D array or a delimited string is a poor fit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Open three terminals, one per cluster\" is never the best practice <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Storage bucket mounted as a drive \"linked to Git\" is a distractor <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>Feature-branch options exist alongside trunk-based ones. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a> prefers trunk-based, but <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q47\">Exam 4 · Q47</a> keys feature branches plus CAB approval. Expect conflicting keys (see the questionable-answers section).</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Covers where developers write code and how they collaborate: Cloud Shell, Cloud Code, Cloud Workstations, multi-cluster kubectl access and branching strategy. Exam items describe a constraint, such as no local code, a specific IDE or VPC security controls, and ask which environment fits.","objectives":["Choose between Cloud Shell, Cloud Code and Cloud Workstations from a scenario's constraints","Configure developer access to multiple GKE clusters with kubeconfig contexts","Explain why trunk-based development reduces merge conflicts in rapid release cycles","Pick an efficient data structure for per-key aggregation tasks"],"takeaways":["No source code allowed on the local machine: Cloud Shell and its editor.","VS Code or IntelliJ plus GKE: Cloud Code.","Secure VPC dev environments with custom tools: Cloud Workstations with centrally managed images rebuilt weekly.","Rapid releases with minimal merge conflicts: trunk-based development.","Summing values per account number: a hash table."],"questions":["e1q45","e2q3","e3q18","e3q37","e4q28","e4q38","e4q47"]},{"id":"d2l2","num":2,"title":"Local development and emulators","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>Pub/Sub emulator</strong> is installed and started with gcloud, using a project ID. You point the app at it by exporting <strong><code>PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q21\">Exam 4 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Emulators avoid cloud costs and give an isolated, predictable environment. Google provides emulators for services such as <strong>Bigtable</strong> and <strong>Pub/Sub</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q8\">Exam 4 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q54\">Exam 2 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>Functions Framework</strong> runs a Cloud Function on your local machine so you can call it with curl <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Minikube</strong> gives you a lightweight single-node Kubernetes cluster. <strong>Skaffold</strong> builds, pushes and deploys to it, and rebuilds or redeploys automatically when code changes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q60\">Exam 3 · Q60</a>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>Spanner emulator</strong> is good for functional tests but <strong>does not reproduce real performance characteristics</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q52\">Exam 4 · Q52</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Fast and cheap testing of an MVP built on Cloud Run, Bigtable and Pub/Sub: <strong>emulators locally, then deploy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q8\">Exam 4 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Unit tests that assert Pub/Sub message ordering cheaply and reliably: use the <strong>Pub/Sub emulator</strong>, not mocks, not one topic per tester, and not subscription filters <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q54\">Exam 2 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>Integration test of a subscriber that handles credit card data: run the <strong>emulator + a publishing service that sends a predefined set of test messages</strong>. Don't use random content, and never replay production messages because they contain sensitive data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q58\">Exam 2 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li>Diagnosing production errors in a Cloud Run service that moves Pub/Sub messages from topic A to topic B with mock messages: run the <strong>Pub/Sub and Cloud Run emulators locally</strong>, set the log level to DEBUG or INFO, and publish mock messages to topic A. Don't point production at local topics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q26\">Exam 2 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>Developing a Cloud Function triggered by Cloud Storage: use the <strong>Functions Framework locally</strong>. Don't rewrite it as an HTTP function, and don't edit a copy in production <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>Local container build and run that matches the production GKE deployment, with low resource use: use <strong>Minikube + Skaffold</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q60\">Exam 3 · Q60</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code>gcloud config set api_endpoint_overrides/pubsub https://pubsubemulator...</code> is a fake endpoint. The emulator is local and is reached through <code>PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q21\">Exam 4 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Code + calling <code>pubsub.googleapis.com</code> still hits the real service <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q21\">Exam 4 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Docker Compose doesn't emulate Kubernetes. Terraform + kubeadm provisions infrastructure. kaniko + Tekton is in-cluster CI that is too heavy for a laptop <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q60\">Exam 3 · Q60</a>.</li>\n<li>An emulator is the wrong answer for <strong>performance</strong> tests <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q52\">Exam 4 · Q52</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Shows how to test against Google Cloud services locally with emulators, the Functions Framework and Minikube with Skaffold. It also covers the limits of emulators, most importantly that they say nothing about performance.","objectives":["Run the Pub/Sub emulator and point an application at it with PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST","Design safe local integration tests with predefined, non-production test messages","Test event-driven Cloud Functions locally with the Functions Framework","Set up a local Kubernetes loop with Minikube and Skaffold that mirrors production"],"takeaways":["Pub/Sub emulator is reached via the PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST environment variable, not an endpoint override.","Pub/Sub ordering unit tests: use the emulator rather than mocks or per-tester topics.","Never replay production messages, especially sensitive data, into tests.","Minikube + Skaffold for automated local build and deploy that mirrors GKE.","Emulators are for functional tests, not performance benchmarks."],"questions":["e2q26","e2q54","e2q58","e3q38","e3q60","e4q8","e4q21","e4q52"]},{"id":"d2l3","num":3,"title":"Building container images","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud Native Buildpacks</strong> detect the language and framework and build an image without a Dockerfile. <code>pack build</code> can push straight to Artifact Registry <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q59\">Exam 1 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>gcloud run deploy --source</code></strong> runs Cloud Build behind the scenes with Buildpacks and stores the image in a registry <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q59\">Exam 1 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Jib</strong> builds Docker or OCI images for Java from Maven or Gradle, with no Dockerfile and no Docker daemon <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q51\">Exam 3 · Q51</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Custom builder:</strong> if Cloud Build lacks a tool, package the tool in your own builder image and reference it in a build step <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Build speed</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Faster build?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Higher-CPU machine type for Cloud Build</td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q50\">Exam 3 · Q50</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--cache-from</code> a previous image in the build config</td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong>, layers are reused <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q50\">Exam 3 · Q50</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Switch <code>python:3.7-alpine</code> to <code>ubuntu:latest</code> + apt-installed Python</td>\n<td>No, the image becomes much larger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Self-hosted registry on a Compute Engine VM</td>\n<td>No, and it adds maintenance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Put source on Cloud Storage and fetch it with gsutil</td>\n<td>No, it doesn't reduce build time</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Cloud Run, no Dockerfile, central registry required: use <strong><code>pack build</code></strong> or <strong><code>gcloud run deploy --source</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q59\">Exam 1 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>Containerize a standalone Java app instead of lifting and shifting the VM: use <strong>Jib → Artifact Registry → GKE</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q51\">Exam 3 · Q51</a>.</li>\n<li>Missing build tool: use a <strong>custom cloud builder image</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Upload source code to Artifact Registry\" doesn't build anything. \"A Cloud Build job to push\" assumes an image already exists. <code>--platform=kubernetes</code> has nothing to do with building <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q59\">Exam 1 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>Downloading the binary during the build hurts security and reproducibility. Committing it to the repo bloats the repo. A feature request gives you no control <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>Migrate for Anthos and Migrate for Compute Engine are VM migration tools. A raw disk import is lift-and-shift <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q51\">Exam 3 · Q51</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Covers the ways to produce container images: Buildpacks, gcloud run deploy --source, Jib for Java and custom Cloud Build builders. It also covers which levers actually speed up an image build.","objectives":["Build container images without a Dockerfile using Buildpacks or --source deploys","Containerize a Java application with Jib and push it to Artifact Registry","Speed up Cloud Build image builds with machine type and layer caching","Provide missing build tools through a custom cloud builder image"],"takeaways":["No Dockerfile on Cloud Run: pack build or gcloud run deploy --source.","Java to container without a Dockerfile or daemon: Jib.","Faster builds: higher-CPU Cloud Build machine type plus --cache-from.","Missing tool in Cloud Build: create a custom builder image.","Switching from alpine to ubuntu:latest makes images bigger, not builds faster."],"questions":["e1q7","e1q59","e2q20","e3q50","e3q51"]},{"id":"d2l4","num":4,"title":"Cloud Build CI pipelines","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Cloud Build is a <strong>managed</strong> CI service with <strong>triggers</strong> on source commits. It needs far less operational work than Jenkins or a CI server on a VM <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Every step shares the <strong><code>/workspace</code></strong> directory, which holds the checked-out source <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Each step in the build config shows up <strong>separately in the build history</strong>, so you can see exactly which stage failed <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q8\">Exam 3 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>The Cloud Build <strong>service account</strong> can be given permissions in other projects <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q3\">Exam 1 · Q3</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Build and test on every commit with the least overhead: use a <strong>Cloud Build trigger per commit</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Unit tests that gate a Cloud Function deploy: run <strong>tests in Cloud Build and deploy automatically from the same build when they pass</strong>, using a dedicated service account. Don't add manual approval, test after deploy, or deploy by hand <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q3\">Exam 1 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Unit and integration tests whose failures must be easy to identify: use <strong>distinct build steps</strong>. Don't put RUN commands in the Dockerfile, a single combined step, or a separate pipeline <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q8\">Exam 3 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>A custom step that adds copyright headers on every commit: a <strong>container that edits files in <code>/workspace</code> and commits the changes back</strong> to the repo <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Ops wants one central, secure image registry: use a <strong>registry in a dedicated ops project + IAM for each team's Cloud Build service account</strong>. Never store keys or passwords in the repo <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>A test resource must be cleaned up even if the test fails: the <strong>test step writes results to a file and exits 0, the cleanup step runs, and a final step checks the file and fails the build</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Per-commit performance tests against Spanner that can run concurrently: <strong>create a new Spanner instance per build, load data, run the tests, delete the instance</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q52\">Exam 4 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li>Catch bugs before production with automated feedback: the key is <strong>Cloud Build triggered by Git tags</strong>, not Spinnaker. Spinnaker is for CD <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q51\">Exam 2 · Q51</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Environment variables don't carry over between Cloud Build steps, so \"set <code>result</code> in an env var and check it later\" fails <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Deleting the function before the test, or adding <code>waitFor</code>, doesn't fix cleanup after a failure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Copying files to or from Cloud Storage for a source-editing step adds overhead and sync problems <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>A new project per build is slow and hits quota and org-permission problems. One shared Spanner instance causes race conditions. The emulator doesn't measure performance <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q52\">Exam 4 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li>Pub/Sub → App Engine \"build service\" chains are extra moving parts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Explains how to structure Cloud Build CI pipelines: commit triggers, separate test steps, gated automatic deploys, shared /workspace and cleanup of test resources after failures. It also covers centralized registries and ephemeral test infrastructure for concurrent builds.","objectives":["Design a Cloud Build config with distinct test steps and automatic deploy on success","Guarantee cleanup of test resources even when a test step fails","Grant Cloud Build service accounts access to a central registry in another project","Choose isolated, ephemeral infrastructure for concurrent per-commit tests"],"takeaways":["Build and test on every commit with minimal ops: a Cloud Build trigger.","Separate build steps make the failing test stage visible in build history.","Cleanup after a failure: write results to a file, exit 0, delete resources, then check the file in a final step.","Central registry: dedicated ops project plus IAM for each team's Cloud Build service account, with no keys in the repo.","Concurrent Spanner performance tests: create and delete a Spanner instance per build."],"questions":["e1q1","e1q3","e1q40","e2q51","e3q8","e3q48","e4q6","e4q52"]},{"id":"d2l5","num":5,"title":"Vulnerability and security scanning","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Artifact Registry + the Container Scanning API (Container Analysis)</strong> scans images automatically when they are pushed for OS-package CVEs. It is managed and doesn't slow developers down <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Older items name <strong>Container Registry vulnerability scanning</strong> for the same need <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Web Security Scanner</strong> (older name: Cloud Security Scanner) scans running web apps on App Engine, GKE or Compute Engine for XSS and outdated or vulnerable libraries <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q58\">Exam 1 · Q58</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Which security tool?</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>Is it a scanner?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Container Scanning / Artifact Analysis</td>\n<td>CVEs in container image packages</td>\n<td>Yes, for images</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Web Security Scanner</td>\n<td>XSS, injection and vulnerable libraries in a deployed web app</td>\n<td>Yes, for apps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud Armor</td>\n<td>WAF and DDoS protection, IP allow/deny</td>\n<td>No <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q58\">Exam 1 · Q58</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Binary Authorization</td>\n<td>Enforces attestations at deploy time</td>\n<td>No, it consumes scan results <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Error Reporting / Debugger</td>\n<td>Error aggregation and debugging</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Scan images inside the pipeline without disrupting other teams: <strong>enable Container Scanning in Artifact Registry</strong>. Don't use Docker Hub, an external Aqua instance, or Binary Authorization alone <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Know about OS vulnerabilities before each daily deploy: <strong>enable Container Analysis, store images in Artifact Registry, review the results before deploying</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Check a web app for XSS or known-vulnerable libraries: use <strong>Web Security Scanner</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q58\">Exam 1 · Q58</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A Cloud Run service or Cloud Function that scans <em>code</em> on check-in misses vulnerabilities in the image's dependencies <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Banning non-commercial base images is policy, not scanning. Cloud Monitoring on Cloud Build output is not CVE scanning <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Calling the scanner through gcloud or REST for each image is less integrated than automatic scanning on push <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Covers how to find vulnerabilities in container images and running web applications with managed Google Cloud services. Separates real scanners from look-alikes such as Cloud Armor and Binary Authorization.","objectives":["Enable automatic CVE scanning of images in Artifact Registry","Distinguish image scanning from web application scanning","Explain why Cloud Armor and Binary Authorization are not vulnerability scanners"],"takeaways":["Image CVEs with managed services: Artifact Registry plus the Container Scanning API.","XSS or vulnerable libraries in a deployed app: Web Security Scanner.","Cloud Armor is a WAF; Binary Authorization enforces attestations; neither scans.","Scanning source code on check-in misses vulnerabilities in image dependencies."],"questions":["e1q6","e1q20","e1q49","e1q58","e2q6","e2q11"]},{"id":"d2l6","num":6,"title":"Load and performance testing","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Google's reference pattern is <strong>distributed load testing on GKE</strong>: Locust or JMeter in pods, scaled out by adding pods <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>During a load test, <strong>Cloud Trace</strong> shows where time goes across services and databases <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q10\">Exam 6 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>For JMeter results analysis, the bank keys <strong>log sink → BigQuery → Looker Studio</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q48\">Exam 4 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Load test that must autoscale to thousands of reads and writes per second and find bottlenecks: <strong>GKE + Locust or JMeter, analyzed in Cloud Trace</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>REST APIs on Cloud Run, Google best practice: a <strong>distributed load-testing framework on a private GKE cluster, scaled with more pods</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Bottlenecks across several microservices on Cloud Run under load: <strong>enable Cloud Trace and review request timings</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q10\">Exam 6 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>JMeter orchestration with a Compute Engine instance as the only load generator: <strong>sink to BigQuery, visualize in Looker Studio</strong>. Cloud Storage and Looker are distractors <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q48\">Exam 4 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Performance tests against Spanner: use a <strong>real, ephemeral instance per build</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q52\">Exam 4 · Q52</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>cURL on managed or unmanaged instance groups scales poorly. Load generators launched one after another in Cloud Shell are resource-limited <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Tasks with Cloud Scheduler is not a load-testing tool. VPC Flow Logs are too coarse. Apache Bench on a LAMP VM is limited <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Building custom timing into responses or logs, or Prometheus latency metrics, is weaker than tracing for end-to-end bottlenecks <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q10\">Exam 6 · Q10</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Covers load and performance testing practice on Google Cloud: distributed Locust or JMeter generators on GKE, Cloud Trace to find bottlenecks, and where to store and visualize results.","objectives":["Design a scalable distributed load test on GKE","Use Cloud Trace to locate bottlenecks under load","Route load test results to BigQuery and Looker Studio for analysis"],"takeaways":["Google pattern: Locust or JMeter in GKE pods, scaled by adding pods.","Bottlenecks across microservices under load: Cloud Trace.","cURL on instance groups or containers in Cloud Shell are distractors.","JMeter results: log sink to BigQuery, then Looker Studio."],"questions":["e1q9","e2q2","e4q48","e4q52","e6q10"]},{"id":"d2l7","num":7,"title":"Logging, Error Reporting and live debugging","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>On <strong>Cloud Run, anything written to stdout or stderr goes to Cloud Logging automatically</strong>. Use your language's normal logger. No client library or log files are needed <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Error Reporting</strong> reads from Cloud Logging. For Cloud Run, make sure errors are written to <strong>stderr</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q55\">Exam 4 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li>Raising the <strong>log level to DEBUG or INFO</strong> during local reproduction gives the most diagnostic detail <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q26\">Exam 2 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Logpoints</strong> (the legacy Stackdriver or Cloud Debugger feature) inject log statements into running code without redeploying. <strong>Snapshots</strong> capture state and variables at a single moment <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>App writes logs to local files on Cloud Run: <strong>switch to stdout and stderr</strong>. Don't use the logging library, cron jobs that copy to Cloud Storage, or public URLs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Java errors missing from Error Reporting on Cloud Run: <strong>check that they go to stderr</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q55\">Exam 4 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li>Ongoing code-level diagnosis with no redeploy: the bank's key is <strong>Debugger logpoints</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>. The product has been retired; see the questionable-answers section.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The Cloud Logging client library works, but it is \"more code to maintain\" than stdout <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Monitoring client libraries and storage buckets have nothing to do with Error Reporting <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q55\">Exam 4 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li>Trace and Monitoring show latency and health, not why the code misbehaves <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Covers how applications get logs and errors into Cloud Logging and Error Reporting, especially on Cloud Run where stdout and stderr are captured automatically. It also covers the legacy Debugger logpoints and snapshots that older questions still mention.","objectives":["Send Cloud Run application logs to Cloud Logging with minimal code","Make application errors appear in Error Reporting","Distinguish logpoints from snapshots in legacy Debugger questions"],"takeaways":["Cloud Run: log to stdout and stderr with the language logger; no files, no library needed.","Errors missing from Error Reporting on Cloud Run: make sure they go to stderr.","Raise the log level to DEBUG or INFO when reproducing issues locally.","Logpoints add logs without redeploying, but Cloud Debugger is retired."],"questions":["e2q26","e4q3","e4q9","e4q55"]},{"id":"d2l8","num":8,"title":"Distributed tracing with Cloud Trace","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Instrument microservices with <strong>OpenTelemetry</strong>, export spans to <strong>Cloud Trace</strong>, and use Trace analysis reports to find slow HTTP requests <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Trace samples requests. Sending the <strong><code>X-Cloud-Trace-Context</code> header with the right parameters</strong> forces a specific request to be traced <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>For backends spread across clouds, a <strong>Zipkin collector</strong> gathers spans and <strong>Cloud Trace</strong> (the old Stackdriver Trace) produces latency reports <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Trace measures <strong>request latency</strong>. It doesn't show CPU or memory per function <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q16\">Exam 3 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Slow URLs in a GKE microservices app: use <strong>OpenTelemetry → Cloud Trace → analysis report</strong>. Don't use workload metrics dashboards, STDOUT log filters, or tcpdump with Wireshark <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>A specific request must always be traced: <strong>add the <code>X-Cloud-Trace-Context</code> header</strong>. Don't wait, run a script, or add custom attributes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Network latency across clouds: use <strong>Zipkin collector + Trace</strong>, not Fluentd, Debugger or Profiler <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Custom attributes add context but <strong>don't guarantee</strong> that a request is sampled <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Fluentd is for logs, not traces <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Covers distributed tracing with OpenTelemetry and Cloud Trace: instrumenting microservices, forcing a request to be sampled, and collecting traces across clouds.","objectives":["Instrument microservices with OpenTelemetry and export spans to Cloud Trace","Force tracing of a specific request with the X-Cloud-Trace-Context header","Choose tracing over metrics, logs or packet capture for request latency analysis"],"takeaways":["Slow URLs across microservices: OpenTelemetry to Cloud Trace with analysis reports.","Guarantee a request is traced: send X-Cloud-Trace-Context with the right parameters.","Cross-cloud latency: Zipkin collector plus Trace.","Trace measures request latency, not per-function CPU or memory."],"questions":["e2q17","e2q28","e2q48","e3q11","e3q16"]},{"id":"d2l9","num":9,"title":"Profiling with Cloud Profiler","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud Profiler</strong> continuously samples CPU and memory (heap) in production with low overhead. It shows <strong>flame graphs</strong> at function or method level <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a>.</li>\n<li>Enabling it means adding the Profiler package or agent and starting it. The code changes are minimal <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q33\">Exam 1 · Q33</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Profiler vs Trace vs Debugger vs Logging</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question in the stem</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Which <strong>functions</strong> use the most CPU or memory?\"</td>\n<td><strong>Profiler</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Slower in production than locally; find the time-consuming functions\"</td>\n<td><strong>Profiler</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q33\">Exam 1 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"<strong>Performance and resource utilization</strong> of a Java microservice\"</td>\n<td><strong>Profiler</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q16\">Exam 3 · Q16</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Which <strong>request or service hop</strong> is slow?\"</td>\n<td><strong>Trace</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q10\">Exam 6 · Q10</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Inspect variables or state at a point in the code\"</td>\n<td>Debugger snapshot (legacy) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The word \"function\", \"method\", \"CPU\", \"memory\" or \"flame graph\" in the stem points to <strong>Profiler</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a>.</li>\n<li>Performance gap between local and Compute Engine: <strong>Profiler first</strong>, before Debugger, extra logging or a support ticket <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q33\">Exam 1 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>SSH + <code>top</code> shows processes, not functions. Monitoring dashboards are too high-level. A Fluent Bit daemonset plus log analysis is indirect <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Print statements or log-timestamp scripts are manual and imprecise <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>OpenTelemetry tracing or OpenCensus metrics measure latency, not resource use per method <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q16\">Exam 3 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Covers Cloud Profiler, the continuous low-overhead profiler that shows CPU and memory use per function as flame graphs. The lesson contrasts it with Trace, Debugger and logging so you pick the right tool from the wording of a question.","objectives":["Enable Cloud Profiler in an application with minimal code changes","Read flame graphs to find CPU-heavy and memory-heavy functions","Choose between Profiler, Trace and logging from the wording of a scenario"],"takeaways":["Which function uses the most CPU or memory: Cloud Profiler flame graph.","Slower after deployment than locally: start with Profiler.","SSH + top, dashboards and log timestamp scripts are distractors.","Request-level latency points to Trace; function-level resource use points to Profiler."],"questions":["e1q28","e1q33","e2q17","e3q11","e3q16","e4q9","e4q46","e4q53","e6q10"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li>No local code on a public PC: <strong>Cloud Shell + editor + PR</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>VS Code or IntelliJ with GKE: <strong>Cloud Code</strong>. Secure VPC dev environments with custom tools: <strong>Cloud Workstations + centrally managed images rebuilt weekly</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>Trunk-based development means fewer merge conflicts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Pub/Sub local testing: gcloud emulator + <strong><code>PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q21\">Exam 4 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Pub/Sub ordering unit tests: <strong>emulator</strong>, not mocks <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q54\">Exam 2 · Q54</a>. Sensitive data: <strong>predefined test messages</strong>, never production replay <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q58\">Exam 2 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li>Cheap and fast MVP testing: <strong>emulators locally, then deploy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q8\">Exam 4 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Function with a Storage trigger: <strong>Functions Framework locally</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>Local K8s that mirrors prod with auto-rebuild: <strong>Minikube + Skaffold</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q60\">Exam 3 · Q60</a>.</li>\n<li>No Dockerfile: <strong><code>pack build</code></strong> or <strong><code>gcloud run deploy --source</code></strong>. Java: <strong>Jib</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q59\">Exam 1 · Q59</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q51\">Exam 3 · Q51</a>.</li>\n<li>Faster Cloud Build: <strong>higher-CPU machine type + <code>--cache-from</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q50\">Exam 3 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Missing tool in Cloud Build: <strong>custom builder image</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>CI on every commit with minimal ops: <strong>Cloud Build trigger</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Tests then auto-deploy in the same Cloud Build run <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q3\">Exam 1 · Q3</a>. <strong>Separate steps</strong> per test type <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q8\">Exam 3 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Steps share <strong><code>/workspace</code></strong>. Commit generated changes back to the repo <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Cleanup even on failure: write results to a <strong>file</strong>, exit 0, clean up, then a final step checks the file <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Concurrent Spanner perf tests: an <strong>ephemeral Spanner instance per build</strong>. The emulator ≠ performance <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q52\">Exam 4 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li>Central registry: <strong>ops project + IAM grant to the Cloud Build SA</strong>, no keys in the repo <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>Image CVEs: <strong>Artifact Registry + Container Scanning API</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>XSS or vulnerable libraries in a running app: <strong>Web Security Scanner</strong>. Cloud Armor is a WAF, not a scanner <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q58\">Exam 1 · Q58</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>Load test: <strong>Locust or JMeter on GKE, scaled with pods</strong>, analyzed in <strong>Cloud Trace</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q10\">Exam 6 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run logs: <strong>stdout/stderr</strong>. Errors in Error Reporting: <strong>stderr</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q55\">Exam 4 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li>Force a trace: <strong><code>X-Cloud-Trace-Context</code> header</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a>. Slow URLs across microservices: <strong>OpenTelemetry → Trace</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Which function burns CPU or memory: <strong>Cloud Profiler flame graph</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Slower in the cloud than locally: <strong>Profiler</strong> first <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q33\">Exam 1 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d2l1","title":"Developer environments and source control","html":"<ul>\n<li>No local source code allowed: use <strong>Cloud Shell</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>IDE named (VS Code, IntelliJ) and GKE or Cloud Run work: use <strong>Cloud Code</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Security baseline plus custom tooling: use <strong>centrally managed images that get rebuilt on a schedule</strong>, not images developers create freely <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>Multiple clusters: the bank's key is <strong>one shared config file defining clusters, users and contexts + <code>kubectl config</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>. This key is debatable; see the questionable-answers section.</li>\n<li>Rapid releases with few merge conflicts: use <strong>trunk-based development</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l2","title":"Local development and emulators","html":"<ul>\n<li>Fast and cheap testing of an MVP built on Cloud Run, Bigtable and Pub/Sub: <strong>emulators locally, then deploy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q8\">Exam 4 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Unit tests that assert Pub/Sub message ordering cheaply and reliably: use the <strong>Pub/Sub emulator</strong>, not mocks, not one topic per tester, and not subscription filters <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q54\">Exam 2 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>Integration test of a subscriber that handles credit card data: run the <strong>emulator + a publishing service that sends a predefined set of test messages</strong>. Don't use random content, and never replay production messages because they contain sensitive data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q58\">Exam 2 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li>Diagnosing production errors in a Cloud Run service that moves Pub/Sub messages from topic A to topic B with mock messages: run the <strong>Pub/Sub and Cloud Run emulators locally</strong>, set the log level to DEBUG or INFO, and publish mock messages to topic A. Don't point production at local topics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q26\">Exam 2 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>Developing a Cloud Function triggered by Cloud Storage: use the <strong>Functions Framework locally</strong>. Don't rewrite it as an HTTP function, and don't edit a copy in production <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>Local container build and run that matches the production GKE deployment, with low resource use: use <strong>Minikube + Skaffold</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q60\">Exam 3 · Q60</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l3","title":"Building container images","html":"<ul>\n<li>Cloud Run, no Dockerfile, central registry required: use <strong><code>pack build</code></strong> or <strong><code>gcloud run deploy --source</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q59\">Exam 1 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>Containerize a standalone Java app instead of lifting and shifting the VM: use <strong>Jib → Artifact Registry → GKE</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q51\">Exam 3 · Q51</a>.</li>\n<li>Missing build tool: use a <strong>custom cloud builder image</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l4","title":"Cloud Build CI pipelines","html":"<ul>\n<li>Build and test on every commit with the least overhead: use a <strong>Cloud Build trigger per commit</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Unit tests that gate a Cloud Function deploy: run <strong>tests in Cloud Build and deploy automatically from the same build when they pass</strong>, using a dedicated service account. Don't add manual approval, test after deploy, or deploy by hand <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q3\">Exam 1 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Unit and integration tests whose failures must be easy to identify: use <strong>distinct build steps</strong>. Don't put RUN commands in the Dockerfile, a single combined step, or a separate pipeline <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q8\">Exam 3 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>A custom step that adds copyright headers on every commit: a <strong>container that edits files in <code>/workspace</code> and commits the changes back</strong> to the repo <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Ops wants one central, secure image registry: use a <strong>registry in a dedicated ops project + IAM for each team's Cloud Build service account</strong>. Never store keys or passwords in the repo <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>A test resource must be cleaned up even if the test fails: the <strong>test step writes results to a file and exits 0, the cleanup step runs, and a final step checks the file and fails the build</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Per-commit performance tests against Spanner that can run concurrently: <strong>create a new Spanner instance per build, load data, run the tests, delete the instance</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q52\">Exam 4 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li>Catch bugs before production with automated feedback: the key is <strong>Cloud Build triggered by Git tags</strong>, not Spinnaker. Spinnaker is for CD <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q51\">Exam 2 · Q51</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l5","title":"Vulnerability and security scanning","html":"<ul>\n<li>Scan images inside the pipeline without disrupting other teams: <strong>enable Container Scanning in Artifact Registry</strong>. Don't use Docker Hub, an external Aqua instance, or Binary Authorization alone <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Know about OS vulnerabilities before each daily deploy: <strong>enable Container Analysis, store images in Artifact Registry, review the results before deploying</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Check a web app for XSS or known-vulnerable libraries: use <strong>Web Security Scanner</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q58\">Exam 1 · Q58</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l6","title":"Load and performance testing","html":"<ul>\n<li>Load test that must autoscale to thousands of reads and writes per second and find bottlenecks: <strong>GKE + Locust or JMeter, analyzed in Cloud Trace</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>REST APIs on Cloud Run, Google best practice: a <strong>distributed load-testing framework on a private GKE cluster, scaled with more pods</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Bottlenecks across several microservices on Cloud Run under load: <strong>enable Cloud Trace and review request timings</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q10\">Exam 6 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>JMeter orchestration with a Compute Engine instance as the only load generator: <strong>sink to BigQuery, visualize in Looker Studio</strong>. Cloud Storage and Looker are distractors <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q48\">Exam 4 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Performance tests against Spanner: use a <strong>real, ephemeral instance per build</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q52\">Exam 4 · Q52</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l7","title":"Logging, Error Reporting and live debugging","html":"<ul>\n<li>App writes logs to local files on Cloud Run: <strong>switch to stdout and stderr</strong>. Don't use the logging library, cron jobs that copy to Cloud Storage, or public URLs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Java errors missing from Error Reporting on Cloud Run: <strong>check that they go to stderr</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q55\">Exam 4 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li>Ongoing code-level diagnosis with no redeploy: the bank's key is <strong>Debugger logpoints</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>. The product has been retired; see the questionable-answers section.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l8","title":"Distributed tracing with Cloud Trace","html":"<ul>\n<li>Slow URLs in a GKE microservices app: use <strong>OpenTelemetry → Cloud Trace → analysis report</strong>. Don't use workload metrics dashboards, STDOUT log filters, or tcpdump with Wireshark <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>A specific request must always be traced: <strong>add the <code>X-Cloud-Trace-Context</code> header</strong>. Don't wait, run a script, or add custom attributes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Network latency across clouds: use <strong>Zipkin collector + Trace</strong>, not Fluentd, Debugger or Profiler <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l9","title":"Profiling with Cloud Profiler","html":"<ul>\n<li>The word \"function\", \"method\", \"CPU\", \"memory\" or \"flame graph\" in the stem points to <strong>Profiler</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a>.</li>\n<li>Performance gap between local and Compute Engine: <strong>Profiler first</strong>, before Debugger, extra logging or a support ticket <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q33\">Exam 1 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d2l1","title":"Developer environments and source control","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Open three terminals, one per cluster\" is never the best practice <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Storage bucket mounted as a drive \"linked to Git\" is a distractor <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>Feature-branch options exist alongside trunk-based ones. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a> prefers trunk-based, but <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q47\">Exam 4 · Q47</a> keys feature branches plus CAB approval. Expect conflicting keys (see the questionable-answers section).</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l2","title":"Local development and emulators","html":"<ul>\n<li><code>gcloud config set api_endpoint_overrides/pubsub https://pubsubemulator...</code> is a fake endpoint. The emulator is local and is reached through <code>PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q21\">Exam 4 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Code + calling <code>pubsub.googleapis.com</code> still hits the real service <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q21\">Exam 4 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Docker Compose doesn't emulate Kubernetes. Terraform + kubeadm provisions infrastructure. kaniko + Tekton is in-cluster CI that is too heavy for a laptop <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q60\">Exam 3 · Q60</a>.</li>\n<li>An emulator is the wrong answer for <strong>performance</strong> tests <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q52\">Exam 4 · Q52</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l3","title":"Building container images","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Upload source code to Artifact Registry\" doesn't build anything. \"A Cloud Build job to push\" assumes an image already exists. <code>--platform=kubernetes</code> has nothing to do with building <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q59\">Exam 1 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>Downloading the binary during the build hurts security and reproducibility. Committing it to the repo bloats the repo. A feature request gives you no control <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>Migrate for Anthos and Migrate for Compute Engine are VM migration tools. A raw disk import is lift-and-shift <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q51\">Exam 3 · Q51</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l4","title":"Cloud Build CI pipelines","html":"<ul>\n<li>Environment variables don't carry over between Cloud Build steps, so \"set <code>result</code> in an env var and check it later\" fails <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Deleting the function before the test, or adding <code>waitFor</code>, doesn't fix cleanup after a failure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Copying files to or from Cloud Storage for a source-editing step adds overhead and sync problems <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>A new project per build is slow and hits quota and org-permission problems. One shared Spanner instance causes race conditions. The emulator doesn't measure performance <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q52\">Exam 4 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li>Pub/Sub → App Engine \"build service\" chains are extra moving parts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l5","title":"Vulnerability and security scanning","html":"<ul>\n<li>A Cloud Run service or Cloud Function that scans <em>code</em> on check-in misses vulnerabilities in the image's dependencies <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Banning non-commercial base images is policy, not scanning. Cloud Monitoring on Cloud Build output is not CVE scanning <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Calling the scanner through gcloud or REST for each image is less integrated than automatic scanning on push <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l6","title":"Load and performance testing","html":"<ul>\n<li>cURL on managed or unmanaged instance groups scales poorly. Load generators launched one after another in Cloud Shell are resource-limited <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Tasks with Cloud Scheduler is not a load-testing tool. VPC Flow Logs are too coarse. Apache Bench on a LAMP VM is limited <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Building custom timing into responses or logs, or Prometheus latency metrics, is weaker than tracing for end-to-end bottlenecks <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q10\">Exam 6 · Q10</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l7","title":"Logging, Error Reporting and live debugging","html":"<ul>\n<li>The Cloud Logging client library works, but it is \"more code to maintain\" than stdout <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Monitoring client libraries and storage buckets have nothing to do with Error Reporting <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q55\">Exam 4 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li>Trace and Monitoring show latency and health, not why the code misbehaves <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l8","title":"Distributed tracing with Cloud Trace","html":"<ul>\n<li>Custom attributes add context but <strong>don't guarantee</strong> that a request is sampled <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Fluentd is for logs, not traces <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l9","title":"Profiling with Cloud Profiler","html":"<ul>\n<li>SSH + <code>top</code> shows processes, not functions. Monitoring dashboards are too high-level. A Fluent Bit daemonset plus log analysis is indirect <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Print statements or log-timestamp scripts are manual and imprecise <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>OpenTelemetry tracing or OpenCensus metrics measure latency, not resource use per method <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q16\">Exam 3 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Issue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q47\">Exam 4 · Q47</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys feature branches + <strong>change advisory board</strong> approval as \"Google-recommended\". Google's DORA research recommends <strong>trunk-based development</strong> with small daily merges and finds heavyweight CAB approval slows delivery without improving stability. Option III (daily merge to main) fits DORA better. The key also contradicts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys <strong>Stackdriver Debugger logpoints</strong>. Cloud Debugger was shut down in 2023, and its successor Snapshot Debugger is archived and unsupported. On today's exam, prefer structured logging or Trace/Profiler options. Treat Debugger answers (also <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a> option I) as legacy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a></strong></td>\n<td>Key on <strong>Container Registry</strong>. Container Registry is deprecated and replaced by <strong>Artifact Registry</strong>, which also covers scanning <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a> and central repositories. If both appear, choose Artifact Registry.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys \"hand out a kubeconfig file\". GKE docs recommend each developer run <code>gcloud container clusters get-credentials</code> (which writes contexts to their own kubeconfig) and switch with <code>kubectl config use-context</code>. Sharing a credentials file is weaker practice. The claim that gcloud is an \"extra dependency\" is also wrong, because GKE authentication uses the gcloud auth plugin.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q51\">Exam 2 · Q51</a></strong></td>\n<td>\"Swiftly pinpoint bugs\" with fast developer feedback usually means building and testing on <strong>every commit or push</strong>. Git tags usually mark releases. The real lesson is Cloud Build (CI) over Spinnaker (CD).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q48\">Exam 4 · Q48</a></strong> vs <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a></strong></td>\n<td>Exam 4 calls JMeter on a single Compute Engine VM \"recommended practice\", but the other two items key distributed load generation on GKE as the Google pattern. The explanation's claim that Looker Studio is preferred over Looker \"for BigQuery integration\" is also unsupported.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a></strong></td>\n<td>The answer is fine, but the explanation's reasoning is muddled. The actual reason IV fails is that env vars don't persist across steps. Cloud Build also has <code>allowFailure</code> and <code>allowExitCodes</code> on steps today, which would be a cleaner design if offered.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q55\">Exam 4 · Q55</a></strong></td>\n<td>stderr routing is necessary, but Error Reporting also needs the <strong>stack trace or error format</strong> to group an error. Rejecting \"log exceptions properly instead of System.out.println\" (D) as irrelevant overstates the case.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q16\">Exam 3 · Q16</a></strong></td>\n<td>Old names: Stackdriver Trace is now <strong>Cloud Trace</strong>, and OpenCensus has been merged into <strong>OpenTelemetry</strong>, which is the current instrumentation path. Zipkin ingestion is legacy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q51\">Exam 3 · Q51</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a></strong></td>\n<td>Renamed products: Cloud Security Scanner is now <strong>Web Security Scanner</strong>, Migrate for Anthos is now <strong>Migrate to Containers</strong>, and Container Analysis is now <strong>Artifact Analysis</strong>. Also, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>'s explanation says option III lacks review \"before each deployment\" when it clearly includes that review. The difference between options is trivial.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a></strong></td>\n<td>They justify <strong>Cloud Source Repositories</strong> as the IAM-auditable choice. CSR is closed to new customers. Current guidance is GitHub, GitLab or Bitbucket connected to Cloud Build (or Secure Source Manager).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},"glossary":[{"term":"Artifact Registry","definition":"Google Cloud's managed repository for container images and language packages, the successor to Container Registry.","exam_context":"The correct place to store images, both for central governance and for automatic vulnerability scanning.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"Binary Authorization","definition":"A deploy-time policy control that only admits container images carrying the required attestations.","exam_context":"Appears as a distractor when the question asks for a vulnerability scanner, because it consumes scan results rather than producing them.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"Buildpacks","definition":"Cloud Native Buildpacks inspect source code, detect the language and framework, and build a container image without a Dockerfile.","exam_context":"The answer for building Cloud Run images with no Dockerfile, via pack build or gcloud run deploy --source.","lesson":"d2l3","domain":2},{"term":"--cache-from","definition":"A Docker build option that reuses layers from a previously built image as cache.","exam_context":"One of the two keyed ways to speed up Cloud Build image builds, alongside a higher-CPU machine type.","lesson":"d2l3","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Build","definition":"Google Cloud's serverless CI service that runs build steps as containers and can be started by source repository triggers.","exam_context":"The default managed answer for building and testing on every commit, preferred over Jenkins or self-managed CI.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Build trigger","definition":"A rule that starts a Cloud Build run automatically on repository events such as commits or tags.","exam_context":"Chosen when a scenario asks to build and test on each commit with minimal operational overhead.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Code","definition":"IDE extensions for VS Code and IntelliJ that add Kubernetes and Cloud Run authoring, debugging and deployment features.","exam_context":"The answer when the stem names VS Code or IntelliJ and GKE developer productivity.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Debugger","definition":"A retired Google Cloud service, formerly Stackdriver Debugger, that captured snapshots and injected logpoints into running code.","exam_context":"Older questions key logpoints for diagnosing code without redeploying; treat these answers as legacy.","lesson":"d2l7","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Profiler","definition":"A continuous, low-overhead statistical profiler that reports CPU and memory use per function as flame graphs.","exam_context":"The answer whenever a question asks which functions or methods consume the most CPU or memory.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Shell","definition":"A browser-based shell with a built-in code editor, Git and gcloud preconfigured for your project.","exam_context":"The answer for emergency development from a computer where source code must not be stored locally.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Trace","definition":"Google Cloud's distributed tracing service that records request latency as spans across services.","exam_context":"Used to find slow requests and bottlenecks across microservices, including during load tests.","lesson":"d2l8","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Workstations","definition":"Managed, VPC-connected development environments built from base or custom container images.","exam_context":"Chosen when a scenario needs consistent security controls, VPC access and custom tools, with images rebuilt regularly.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"Container Registry","definition":"Google's older container image registry, now deprecated in favor of Artifact Registry.","exam_context":"Appears in older keys for scanning and central registries; prefer Artifact Registry when both are offered.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"Container Scanning API","definition":"The Artifact Analysis feature that automatically scans images pushed to Artifact Registry for known OS-package CVEs.","exam_context":"The managed way to add vulnerability scanning to a pipeline without disrupting developers.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"Custom cloud builder","definition":"A container image you create that packages extra tools, used as a step in a Cloud Build config.","exam_context":"The answer when the build needs a tool that Cloud Build does not provide.","lesson":"d2l3","domain":2},{"term":"Error Reporting","definition":"A service that groups and counts application errors found in Cloud Logging.","exam_context":"On Cloud Run, errors must be written to stderr to show up here.","lesson":"d2l7","domain":2},{"term":"Flame graph","definition":"A visualization of sampled stack traces in which wider frames mean more resource consumption.","exam_context":"The Profiler view used to pinpoint CPU-heavy or memory-heavy functions.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Functions Framework","definition":"An open-source library that runs Cloud Functions code locally as a server you can call.","exam_context":"The recommended way to develop and test Cloud Functions locally, including event-triggered ones.","lesson":"d2l2","domain":2},{"term":"Hash table","definition":"A key-value data structure with near-constant-time lookups and inserts.","exam_context":"The best structure for summing transaction amounts per account while parsing logs.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"Jib","definition":"A Google tool that builds Docker or OCI images for Java apps from Maven or Gradle without a Dockerfile or Docker daemon.","exam_context":"The answer for containerizing a standalone Java application instead of migrating the VM.","lesson":"d2l3","domain":2},{"term":"JMeter","definition":"An open-source load-testing tool that generates configurable request traffic.","exam_context":"Appears in distributed load tests on GKE, or on Compute Engine with results sunk to BigQuery.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"kubeconfig context","definition":"A named combination of cluster, user and namespace in a kubeconfig file, selected with kubectl config.","exam_context":"Used to let developers switch between multiple GKE clusters.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"Locust","definition":"An open-source, Python-based distributed load-testing framework.","exam_context":"Run in GKE pods as Google's reference distributed load-testing pattern.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"Logpoint","definition":"A Debugger feature that injects a log statement into running code without redeploying.","exam_context":"Keyed in an older question for continuous code diagnosis on App Engine; the product is now retired.","lesson":"d2l7","domain":2},{"term":"Minikube","definition":"A tool that runs a lightweight single-node Kubernetes cluster on a local machine.","exam_context":"Paired with Skaffold for low-resource local testing that mirrors GKE.","lesson":"d2l2","domain":2},{"term":"OpenTelemetry","definition":"The open-source standard for generating and exporting traces, metrics and logs, which absorbed OpenCensus.","exam_context":"The instrumentation layer that sends microservice traces to Cloud Trace.","lesson":"d2l8","domain":2},{"term":"Pub/Sub emulator","definition":"A local emulation of the Pub/Sub service, installed and started with gcloud.","exam_context":"The answer for cheap, isolated Pub/Sub unit and integration tests, reached through PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST.","lesson":"d2l2","domain":2},{"term":"PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST","definition":"An environment variable that makes Pub/Sub client libraries connect to the local emulator.","exam_context":"The correct configuration step; endpoint override commands are fake distractors.","lesson":"d2l2","domain":2},{"term":"Skaffold","definition":"A command-line tool that automates the build, push and deploy loop for Kubernetes and rebuilds on code changes.","exam_context":"Chosen with Minikube for automated local testing that mirrors the production deployment.","lesson":"d2l2","domain":2},{"term":"Spanner emulator","definition":"A local emulator for Cloud Spanner suitable for functional testing.","exam_context":"A distractor for performance tests, because it doesn't reproduce real performance; use an ephemeral instance instead.","lesson":"d2l2","domain":2},{"term":"Trunk-based development","definition":"A branching model where developers integrate small changes into the main branch frequently.","exam_context":"The answer for rapid release cycles with minimal merge conflicts.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"Web Security Scanner","definition":"A Security Command Center service, formerly Cloud Security Scanner, that crawls deployed web apps for vulnerabilities such as XSS.","exam_context":"The answer for checking App Engine or GKE web apps for XSS or vulnerable libraries.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"/workspace","definition":"The directory shared by all steps of a Cloud Build run, holding the checked-out source.","exam_context":"Custom steps that inspect or modify source files work on /workspace, not on a Cloud Storage bucket.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"X-Cloud-Trace-Context","definition":"An HTTP header that carries trace ID, span ID and sampling options for Cloud Trace.","exam_context":"Sending it with the right options forces a specific request to be traced despite sampling.","lesson":"d2l8","domain":2},{"term":"Zipkin","definition":"An open-source distributed tracing system whose collector can gather spans from heterogeneous backends.","exam_context":"Paired with Trace for analyzing API network latency across multiple cloud providers.","lesson":"d2l8","domain":2}]},{"n":3,"title":"Configuring and Deploying Applications","short":"Deployment","weight":"","desc":"Deploying to Cloud Run, GKE, App Engine and Cloud Functions, rollouts and traffic splitting, configuration and secrets, operating apps in production.","lessons":[{"id":"d3l1","num":1,"title":"Choosing where to deploy","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Container image, no OS dependencies, least ops:</strong> deploy it to <strong>Cloud Run</strong>. GKE Autopilot still means Kubernetes management, GKE Standard means more, and Compute Engine means managing VMs, scaling, health checks and OS updates <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q12\">Exam 6 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Microservice in C++ with custom libraries, minimal infrastructure management:</strong> <strong>Cloud Build</strong> builds the container, <strong>Cloud Run</strong> runs it. Cloud Functions suits short, event-driven tasks rather than a continuously running microservice with custom native libraries. A Container-Optimized OS VM or standard GKE adds infrastructure work <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q51\">Exam 4 · Q51</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Static site (HTML, CSS, JS, images) with minimal effort:</strong> upload it to a <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> bucket configured for website hosting. App Engine, a VM with Apache, or a GKE container is overkill <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q34\">Exam 3 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Whole website with variants to compare:</strong> App Engine (versions + traffic splitting) or Cloud Run (revisions + traffic splitting). Cloud Functions is the wrong shape for hosting a whole website <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Custom domain on App Engine</strong> (<code>www.example.com</code>): <strong>verify domain ownership</strong>, then create a <strong>CNAME to <code>ghs.googlehosted.com</code></strong>. Don't use an A record to a \"single global App Engine IP\", and don't use <code>dispatch.yaml</code>, which routes requests <em>within</em> the app between services <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Platform selector</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario wording</th>\n<th>Pick</th>\n<th>Reject</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Container, \"reduce infrastructure management\", no OS dependencies</td>\n<td><strong>Cloud Run</strong></td>\n<td>GKE Autopilot/Standard, Compute Engine</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q12\">Exam 6 · Q12</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom native libraries, minimal management</td>\n<td><strong>Cloud Build → Cloud Run</strong></td>\n<td>Cloud Functions, COS VM, GKE</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q51\">Exam 4 · Q51</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Static content only</td>\n<td><strong>Cloud Storage</strong> website</td>\n<td>App Engine, VM, GKE</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q34\">Exam 3 · Q34</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Needs stable identity + its own disk per replica</td>\n<td><strong>GKE StatefulSet</strong> (see §2)</td>\n<td>Deployment</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Header-based routing (user-agent, OS)</td>\n<td><strong>GKE + Anthos Service Mesh</strong> (see §6)</td>\n<td>Cloud Run tags, App Engine IP split</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>When the stem says \"minimal management\" and the workload is a stateless container, choose <strong>Cloud Run</strong> before any GKE option <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q12\">Exam 6 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q51\">Exam 4 · Q51</a>.</li>\n<li>When there is no server-side logic at all, choose <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q34\">Exam 3 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>For an App Engine custom domain, the answer is always <strong>verify + CNAME <code>ghs.googlehosted.com</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"GKE Autopilot\" sounds managed, but it is still Kubernetes. Cloud Run wins on operational simplicity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q12\">Exam 6 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li><code>dispatch.yaml</code> is not domain mapping <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Split the site into three services/functions\" to test three designs adds complexity. Use <strong>one service with versions/revisions and traffic splitting</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers choosing the right Google Cloud platform for a deployment: Cloud Run, GKE, Compute Engine, App Engine, Cloud Functions or Cloud Storage. Exam questions reward the option with the least operational overhead that still meets the workload's needs, and test App Engine custom-domain setup.","objectives":["Choose Cloud Run over GKE Autopilot, GKE Standard or Compute Engine for stateless containers that need minimal management.","Recognize when a static site belongs in Cloud Storage rather than on a compute service.","Configure an App Engine custom domain with domain verification and a CNAME to ghs.googlehosted.com.","Reject Cloud Functions for hosting whole websites or long-running microservices."],"takeaways":["Stateless container with no OS dependencies and 'least ops' means Cloud Run.","Static HTML, CSS, JS and images mean a Cloud Storage website.","Custom native libraries plus minimal management means Cloud Build building a container for Cloud Run.","App Engine custom domain: verify ownership, then CNAME to ghs.googlehosted.com; dispatch.yaml is for routing between services."],"questions":["e1q34","e2q1","e3q3","e3q12","e3q34","e4q5","e4q51","e6q12"]},{"id":"d3l2","num":2,"title":"GKE workload objects and in-cluster service discovery","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Deployment</strong> manages replicas of stateless Pods. Use one Deployment per microservice so each can have its own replica count <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>StatefulSet</strong> gives each Pod a <strong>stable network identity</strong> (persistent hostname/DNS), <strong>its own PersistentVolumeClaim</strong>, and ordered scaling. Pick it when replicas each need \"a stable network identity and its own persistent disk\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>DaemonSet</strong> runs one Pod <strong>on every node</strong>. Node-level agents (third-party logging/monitoring) go here. Don't embed agents in app images, SSH into nodes, or use a Deployment, which doesn't guarantee one per node <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Service</strong> gives a stable virtual IP plus a cluster DNS name (<code>name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code>) and load-balances across Pods. Clients inside the cluster <strong>use the Service name in the URL</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>ClusterIP</strong> Service = reachable only inside the cluster. <strong>NodePort</strong> exposes on node ports outside the cluster <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ingress</strong> is for <strong>external</strong> HTTP(S) traffic into the cluster. On GKE an Ingress provisions an HTTP(S) load balancer. To serve a second domain on the <strong>same external IP</strong>, <strong>add a host rule to the existing Ingress</strong>. A new Ingress or a new <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service provisions a new IP <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Object comparison</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Object</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>N interchangeable stateless replicas</td>\n<td>Deployment</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stable hostname + own disk per replica</td>\n<td>StatefulSet</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One agent Pod per node</td>\n<td>DaemonSet</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stable internal address for a set of Pods</td>\n<td>Service (ClusterIP) + Service DNS name</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Several external domains on one LB IP</td>\n<td>One Ingress with multiple host rules</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Specific number of replicas per microservice + uniform addressing\" → <strong>Deployment + Service + Service DNS name</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Only other services in the cluster may call it\" → <strong>ClusterIP</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Install X on every node\" → <strong>DaemonSet</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Bare <strong>Pods</strong> instead of Deployments: no replica management or self-healing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ingress for service-to-service traffic</strong> inside the cluster is wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"GKE Endpoint\" / Cloud DNS A records for in-cluster discovery add pointless layers. Kubernetes DNS already does it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>ReplicaSet and ReplicationController don't give stable identity or per-Pod storage. ReplicationController is legacy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Converting a stateless Deployment to a StatefulSet just to roll out safely adds complexity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q49\">Exam 3 · Q49</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers the Kubernetes objects used to deploy and expose applications on GKE: Deployments, StatefulSets, DaemonSets, Services and Ingress. Exam questions test matching the workload's needs to the right object and using Service DNS names for in-cluster discovery.","objectives":["Pick between Deployment, StatefulSet and DaemonSet from the scenario's requirements.","Expose a microservice internally with a ClusterIP Service and address it by its DNS name.","Serve an additional domain on the same external IP by adding a host rule to an existing Ingress.","Reject Ingress, bare Pods or Cloud DNS records for service-to-service traffic inside a cluster."],"takeaways":["Stable network identity plus its own persistent disk per replica means StatefulSet.","An agent on every node means DaemonSet.","Per-microservice replica counts plus uniform addressing means Deployment + Service + Service DNS name.","Internal-only access means ClusterIP; NodePort exposes the service outside the cluster.","A new Ingress or LoadBalancer Service gets a new IP; edit the existing Ingress to keep the same one."],"questions":["e1q23","e1q43","e2q1","e2q10","e2q18","e2q47","e3q14","e3q49"]},{"id":"d3l3","num":3,"title":"Pod lifecycle: probes, hooks and graceful shutdown","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Kubernetes sends <strong>SIGTERM</strong> before killing a container. To close DB connections cleanly during a Deployment update, <strong>handle SIGTERM in the code</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q59\">Exam 2 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li><code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> only gives <em>more time</em>. A <strong>PodDisruptionBudget</strong> only limits voluntary disruptions. A <strong>preStop</strong> hook runs extra logic. None of them fixes code that ignores SIGTERM <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q59\">Exam 2 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>The bank's key for \"check DB connectivity and run a graceful shutdown script on failure\" is a <strong>livenessProbe that fails without DB connectivity + a preStop handler</strong> for the shutdown script. An initContainer only checks once before start, PostStart runs once after start, and separate Jobs are complex and slow (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q19\">Exam 1 · Q19</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li>A legacy app whose config lives on NFS: mount it via a <strong>PersistentVolumeClaim</strong> and start the service with an <strong>ENTRYPOINT script</strong> that verifies the config before serving. Baking config into the image forces rebuilds; a node startup script couples config to infrastructure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Preemptible VMs</strong> (Compute Engine) get a shutdown script. Make the script send the application a <strong>Linux signal</strong> so it disconnects from the database. Polling a file every 5 minutes is too slow; broadcasting to logged-in users or publishing to Pub/Sub doesn't stop the process cleanly <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"App crashes/aborts DB connections during rollout\" → <strong>SIGTERM handler</strong> first <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q59\">Exam 2 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Continuous health check + shutdown script\" → <strong>probe + preStop</strong>, not init/PostStart <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q19\">Exam 1 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Graceful shutdown on preemption\" → <strong>shutdown script → signal to process</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Raising <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> looks helpful but doesn't make the app react <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q59\">Exam 2 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li><code>COPY</code> config into the image <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a> and \"one image per environment\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a> are both config-in-image anti-patterns.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers how containers and VMs start and stop cleanly: SIGTERM handling, probes, lifecycle hooks and shutdown scripts. Exam questions test which mechanism actually fixes an abrupt shutdown or gates traffic until dependencies are ready.","objectives":["Explain why handling SIGTERM in code is the fix for connections dropped during a Deployment update.","Distinguish probes, preStop, PostStart and initContainers by when they run.","Design a graceful shutdown for preemptible Compute Engine VMs using a shutdown script that signals the process.","Gate a legacy app on NFS-hosted configuration with a PVC and an ENTRYPOINT check."],"takeaways":["Abrupt DB disconnects during rollout: handle SIGTERM; a longer grace period alone does nothing.","PodDisruptionBudgets and preStop hooks don't replace SIGTERM handling in the app.","Continuous check plus shutdown script: probe + preStop, not initContainer or PostStart.","Preemptible VM: shutdown script sends a Linux signal to the app; file polling is too slow."],"questions":["e1q19","e2q4","e2q59","e3q26","e3q40"]},{"id":"d3l4","num":4,"title":"Configuration and storage for deployed apps","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Compute Engine instance metadata</strong> is the built-in way to pass per-instance or per-environment configuration. The app <strong>queries the metadata server</strong> at runtime <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Golden image across test/staging/prod:</strong> set a metadata key such as <code>DATABASE</code> at instance creation and <strong>read it from the metadata server</strong>. Metadata does <strong>not</strong> appear as an environment variable automatically, so the \"read the DATABASE env var\" option is wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Same custom image, several MIGs, different config each:</strong> put the values in <strong>each instance template's metadata</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Service endpoint/version discovery for new VMs:</strong> endpoint details as <strong>metadata fetched at runtime</strong>. Labels organize resources; they aren't runtime config. A fixed hostname/port is inflexible <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Shared read/write config file for many instances, no code rewrite:</strong> <strong>Filestore</strong> mounted as an <strong>NFS PersistentVolume</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Expanded a persistent disk but Pods don't see the space:</strong> edit <strong><code>spec.resources.requests.storage</code> on the PVC</strong> and apply it (the StorageClass must allow volume expansion). Don't edit the PV's capacity, redeploy, or run <code>fdisk</code> in the Pod <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q58\">Exam 4 · Q58</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Storage selector</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n<th>Choose</th>\n<th>Reject</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Many Pods read + write one file, NFS, no code change</td>\n<td><strong>Filestore NFS PV</strong></td>\n<td>Shared PD (complex ReadWriteMany setup, single point of failure), ConfigMap (not built for concurrent modification), GCS FUSE (performance overhead)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Each replica has its own disk</td>\n<td>StatefulSet + PVC per Pod</td>\n<td>Deployment</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Legacy config on existing NFS share</td>\n<td>PVC + ENTRYPOINT check</td>\n<td>gsutil in container, COPY in image</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grow a volume</td>\n<td>Edit <strong>PVC</strong> request</td>\n<td>Edit PV, fdisk</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q58\">Exam 4 · Q58</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>One image, different environments on Compute Engine → <strong>metadata + metadata server</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Shared writable file system on GKE → <strong>Filestore</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Instance <strong>tags/labels</strong> for config values <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>Startup script with hardcoded values, Bigtable or a PD for small config <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Editing the <strong>PV</strong> instead of the <strong>PVC</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q58\">Exam 4 · Q58</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers passing environment-specific configuration to deployed apps and providing storage they can share. It focuses on Compute Engine metadata for golden images and on GKE volumes: PVCs, Filestore and volume expansion.","objectives":["Use instance or instance-template metadata, read from the metadata server, to configure a single golden image per environment.","Choose Filestore as an NFS PersistentVolume for a shared read/write file system.","Expand a GKE volume by editing the PersistentVolumeClaim request.","Reject tags, labels, startup scripts and baked-in config as configuration mechanisms."],"takeaways":["Same image, different environments on Compute Engine: metadata key read from the metadata server, not an env var.","Different config per MIG: put values in each instance template's metadata.","Many Pods need read/write access to one file without code changes: Filestore NFS PV.","Volume grown but Pods don't see it: edit spec.resources.requests.storage on the PVC, not the PV."],"questions":["e2q1","e2q4","e2q7","e3q26","e3q36","e4q25","e4q58"]},{"id":"d3l5","num":5,"title":"Secrets on GKE","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Recommended pattern:</strong> store the secret in <strong>Secret Manager</strong>, grant a <strong>Google service account (GSA)</strong> access, run the Pod as a <strong>Kubernetes service account (KSA)</strong>, and bind KSA → GSA with <strong>Workload Identity</strong>. No keys to export, and the secret isn't readable through the Kubernetes API server <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>Kubernetes Secret</strong> injected as an env var is base64-encoded and is exposed through the Kubernetes API <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Application-layer Secrets Encryption</strong> (Cloud KMS key) encrypts Secrets at rest in etcd, but they are still served by the API server <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>When Secret Manager isn't among the options and the task is \"store DB credentials for an on-prem DB with rotation\", the bank keys <strong>Kubernetes Secret + Cloud KMS plugin</strong> for encryption (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>If Secret Manager + Workload Identity is an option, pick it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Reject any option that <strong>exports a service account key as JSON</strong> (into a ConfigMap or volume) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Store it in Cloud KMS\": KMS manages <em>keys</em>, not secrets <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Sidecar proxy, service mesh or an encrypted PVC don't solve credential storage and rotation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers keeping secrets out of reach of the Kubernetes API on GKE. The recommended answer is Secret Manager accessed through Workload Identity, with Kubernetes Secrets encrypted by Cloud KMS as a fallback when Secret Manager isn't offered.","objectives":["Describe the Secret Manager + Google service account + Kubernetes service account + Workload Identity pattern.","Explain why Kubernetes Secrets in env vars and Application-layer Secrets Encryption don't stop exposure through the API server.","Reject options that export service account keys as JSON files."],"takeaways":["Secret Manager + Workload Identity is the default answer for secrets on GKE.","Kubernetes Secrets are base64-encoded and readable through the API server.","Cloud KMS stores keys, not secrets.","If Secret Manager isn't an option, a Kubernetes Secret encrypted with the Cloud KMS plugin is the bank's key."],"questions":["e1q41","e3q25","e3q46"]},{"id":"d3l6","num":6,"title":"Rollout strategies and traffic splitting","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Canary:</strong> route a <strong>small share of real traffic</strong> to the new version, watch metrics, increase gradually. It minimizes how many users a production-only bug can hit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>. In a <strong>CD pipeline with Cloud Monitoring</strong> it's the automated answer when load testing wasn't possible <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q26\">Exam 1 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Blue/green:</strong> two full environments, switch all traffic; the selling point is <strong>fast, complete rollback</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rolling update:</strong> replaces instances sequentially. Zero downtime, but no fine-grained traffic control and an issue can still reach a large share of users <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q57\">Exam 2 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Recreate:</strong> downtime <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q57\">Exam 2 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Traffic mirroring (shadowing):</strong> copy live traffic to the new version without affecting users, so you can <strong>test against the full production load before launch</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q27\">Exam 2 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>A/B test for a business metric</strong> (sales, conversion): split a predetermined percentage between variants and look for a <strong>statistically significant difference</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>. Three designs → one service with <strong>traffic splitting</strong> across versions/revisions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run gradual release:</strong> deploy the new revision with <strong>\"Serve this revision immediately\" off</strong> (no traffic), give it a small percentage, monitor, then increase <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>GKE + Anthos Service Mesh (Istio):</strong> two Deployments labeled with version names + an <strong>Istio routing rule</strong> sending a small percentage to the new one gives zero-downtime A/B/canary <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q57\">Exam 2 · Q57</a>. Mesh routing can match <strong>HTTP headers such as <code>user-agent</code></strong>, so \"route by operating system\" points to ASM <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Strategy selector</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement in the stem</th>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Issues only appear with real users; limit the number affected</td>\n<td><strong>Canary</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automated, no prior load test, avoid performance glitches</td>\n<td><strong>CD pipeline + canary + Cloud Monitoring</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q26\">Exam 1 · Q26</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Swift full rollback</td>\n<td><strong>Blue/green</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Full production load before launch, no user impact</td>\n<td><strong>Traffic mirroring</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q27\">Exam 2 · Q27</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Does variant X increase sales?</td>\n<td><strong>A/B split + statistical analysis</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Segment by OS/user-agent with quick rollback</td>\n<td><strong>GKE + ASM header routing</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud Run, gradual, least config</td>\n<td><strong>No-traffic revision + percentage split</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Minimal configuration\" on Cloud Run → use <strong>built-in revision traffic splitting</strong>, not a load balancer or code-level routing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Small portion of users first, then everyone\" → <strong>traffic splitting / canary</strong>, not \"smoke test then 100%\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>For the key <strong>\"safest rollout of a new GKE revision\"</strong>, the bank's answer is <strong>rolling update + PodDisruptionBudget</strong>, not a StatefulSet conversion or an HPA scale-down policy (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q49\">Exam 3 · Q49</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Blue/green doesn't give gradual exposure; canary doesn't give the fastest complete rollback <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Feature flag to half of all users</strong> looks like A/B, but the bank rejects it for not giving the clean, predetermined traffic split of the LB option <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run tags/revisions and App Engine IP-based splits can't target an OS <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Separate namespaces don't give traffic control by percentage <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q57\">Exam 2 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li>Weekly load tests or local load tests don't reduce the blast radius of a new feature <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers release strategies (canary, blue/green, rolling, recreate, traffic mirroring and A/B testing) and how to implement traffic splitting on Cloud Run, App Engine and GKE with Anthos Service Mesh. Exam questions hinge on matching the stem's goal to the strategy.","objectives":["Match canary, blue/green, rolling, mirroring and A/B testing to the goal stated in a scenario.","Roll out a Cloud Run revision gradually with no initial traffic and percentage splits.","Route traffic by HTTP header such as user-agent using Anthos Service Mesh on GKE.","Design an A/B experiment that measures a business metric with a predetermined split."],"takeaways":["Limit how many users a production-only bug can reach: canary.","Swift complete rollback: blue/green.","Test against full production load before launch without user impact: traffic mirroring.","Cloud Run gradual release: deploy with 'Serve this revision immediately' off, then shift percentages.","Segment users by OS or user-agent: GKE + Anthos Service Mesh routing rules."],"questions":["e1q26","e1q34","e1q37","e1q46","e2q27","e2q32","e2q57","e3q3","e3q49","e4q5","e4q30","e6q2"]},{"id":"d3l7","num":7,"title":"Autoscaling deployed workloads","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HPA</strong> changes <strong>Pod count</strong>; <strong>VPA</strong> changes <strong>Pod size</strong> (requests/limits); <strong>Cluster Autoscaler</strong> changes <strong>node count</strong>. For \"pods and nodes follow CPU\" → <strong>Cluster Autoscaler + HPA on CPU</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Stateless service, autoscaling needed urgently with minimal changes</strong> → <strong>HPA on CPU</strong>. A custom-metric HPA or any VPA takes longer to set up <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Internal service that must scale rapidly</strong> → <strong>HPA + ClusterIP Service</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pub/Sub consumer with bursty publishing</strong> → <strong>HPA on an external metric</strong> such as <code>pubsub.googleapis.com/subscription/num_undelivered_messages</code>. CPU may not track backlog <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compute Engine MIG latency spikes while scaling on CPU</strong> → <strong>lower the target CPU utilization</strong> (scale out earlier) and <strong>shorten the cooldown/initialization period</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q14\">Exam 1 · Q14</a>. Never remove health checks; there is no \"AUTOSCALE\" label.</li>\n<li><strong>Right-size Cloud Spanner for cost without hurting reliability:</strong> watch <strong>CPU utilization in Cloud Monitoring</strong> and reduce processing units gradually to the minimum needed. Error logs, Trace RPS and Snapshot Debugger don't measure capacity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q24\">Exam 3 · Q24</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Autoscaler comparison</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Signal / need</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>More Pods under CPU load, fastest to implement</td>\n<td>HPA (CPU)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Queue backlog drives work</td>\n<td>HPA (external metric)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nodes as well as Pods</td>\n<td>Cluster Autoscaler + HPA</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bigger/smaller Pods</td>\n<td>VPA (not for bursts)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VM fleet</td>\n<td>MIG autoscaler: lower target, shorter cooldown</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q14\">Exam 1 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Tight deadline beats elegance: <strong>CPU-based HPA</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Messages waiting → <strong>external metric</strong>, not resource utilization <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Enabling <strong>MIG autoscaling on GKE node pools directly</strong> instead of Cluster Autoscaler <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>VPA Recommendation mode</strong> doesn't scale anything <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Raising the CPU target delays scale-out and makes latency worse <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q14\">Exam 1 · Q14</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers autoscaling for GKE workloads and Compute Engine MIGs, plus right-sizing managed capacity. Exam questions test HPA vs VPA vs Cluster Autoscaler, choosing the scaling metric, and tuning a MIG autoscaler for latency.","objectives":["Distinguish HPA, VPA and Cluster Autoscaler by what they scale.","Choose CPU-based HPA for fast implementation and an external metric for queue backlogs.","Tune a MIG autoscaler (target utilization, cooldown) to reduce latency during scale-out.","Right-size Cloud Spanner processing units using CPU utilization in Cloud Monitoring."],"takeaways":["Urgent autoscaling for a stateless GKE service: HPA on CPU.","Pub/Sub backlog drives load: HPA on an external metric such as num_undelivered_messages.","Pods and nodes must both scale: Cluster Autoscaler + HPA.","MIG latency while scaling: lower the CPU target and shorten the cooldown.","Spanner cost cuts: watch CPU utilization and reduce processing units gradually."],"questions":["e1q12","e1q14","e1q27","e1q39","e3q14","e3q24","e4q29"]},{"id":"d3l8","num":8,"title":"CI/CD deployment pipelines","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud Build trigger on push</strong> to a branch runs the existing <code>cloudbuild.yaml</code> (build + <code>gcloud run deploy</code>). Pub/Sub, webhook triggers and cron jobs are extra plumbing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q58\">Exam 3 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Simplest fully managed continuous deployment to Cloud Run</strong> per commit (bank key): <strong>Cloud Build trigger</strong> that builds and deploys. Workstation cron jobs and self-managed Jenkins are rejected (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q21\">Exam 2 · Q21</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li><strong>Release only specific commits on the main branch:</strong> trigger on a <strong>Git tag pattern</strong> (e.g. <code>v*</code>) with a tag convention <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Build deploys Cloud Functions and fails right after code validation:</strong> the <strong>Cloud Build service account lacks the Cloud Functions Developer role</strong>. Instance limits, trigger parameters and retries with backoff don't fix permissions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Deploy Dev → Test auto-promotion</strong> (bank key): a <strong>Cloud Function triggered by SUCCEEDED messages on the <code>clouddeploy-operations</code> topic</strong> calls the Cloud Deploy API to promote. The <code>cloud-builds</code> topic carries build events, not deploy events (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li><strong>Isolated, automated test environment for Cloud Run system tests:</strong> <strong>Cloud Build runs Terraform</strong> to create a <strong>new project</strong> and deploy the service there. A new revision in the production project lacks isolation; gcloud scripts are less declarative <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q49\">Exam 4 · Q49</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Deploy step fails, code is fine → <strong>check the build service account's IAM role</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Release commits\" → <strong>tags</strong>; \"every push to dev\" → <strong>branch trigger</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q58\">Exam 3 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Separate from production, fully automated\" → <strong>new project via IaC</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q49\">Exam 4 · Q49</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A second repository just for release builds fragments history <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Build monitoring Pub/Sub to drive promotion, or the gke-deploy builder, is not the bank's Cloud Deploy answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers automating deployments with Cloud Build triggers and Cloud Deploy. Exam questions test choosing the right trigger type, diagnosing permission failures in the deploy step, promoting releases between environments, and creating isolated test environments.","objectives":["Choose between branch, tag, webhook and Pub/Sub Cloud Build triggers.","Diagnose a Cloud Build deploy step that fails because the build service account lacks a role.","Automate Cloud Deploy promotion from Dev to Test based on deploy events.","Create an isolated test environment by running Terraform from Cloud Build."],"takeaways":["Release only specific commits: trigger on a Git tag pattern.","Deploy on every push to a branch: a Cloud Build branch trigger running the existing build file.","Functions deploy fails right after validation: grant the Cloud Build service account Cloud Functions Developer.","Cloud Deploy events come from clouddeploy-operations, not cloud-builds.","Isolated system tests: Cloud Build runs Terraform to create a new project."],"questions":["e1q42","e2q8","e2q21","e2q44","e3q58","e4q49"]},{"id":"d3l9","num":9,"title":"Supply-chain security: Binary Authorization","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Only images built by the pipeline may run on GKE</strong> → <strong>Cloud Build + Artifact Registry + Binary Authorization</strong>. Cloud Deploy and Cloud Armor (a WAF) don't enforce image policy; Cloud Storage isn't the image repository <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Only images that passed regression tests:</strong> create an <strong>attestor and a policy</strong>, and add a <strong>Cloud Build step that creates an attestation</strong> for images that pass. Voucher adds needless components; a vulnerability-scan attestation proves a different thing; Pod Security Standards don't verify provenance <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q14\">Exam 6 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Only images that passed UAT reach production:</strong> after UAT, <strong>sign the attestation with a key in Cloud KMS</strong> and add a <strong>cluster-specific rule in the production project's</strong> Binary Authorization policy. Keys as Kubernetes Secrets and rules in the UAT project are wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Block images with known critical vulnerabilities:</strong> enable the <strong>Container Scanning API</strong>, review results <strong>programmatically</strong> in the pipeline, create an <strong>attestation</strong> for clean images, and enforce it with <strong>Binary Authorization</strong>. Web Security Scanner crawls web apps, not images; manual console review doesn't scale <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The attestation must match the gate in the stem: tests passed → test attestation; UAT passed → UAT attestation; no critical CVEs → scan attestation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q14\">Exam 6 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>Enforcement lives in the <strong>target (production) project's policy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Signing keys live in <strong>Cloud KMS</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Cloud Armor in a supply-chain question <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Web Security Scanner for container CVEs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers enforcing that only trusted, tested container images reach GKE using Binary Authorization. Exam questions test the service combination, where attestations are created, where signing keys live, and which project's policy enforces the rule.","objectives":["Name Cloud Build, Artifact Registry and Binary Authorization as the trusted-image pipeline.","Create attestations in a Cloud Build step after the relevant gate (tests, UAT, vulnerability scan).","Place signing keys in Cloud KMS and enforcement rules in the production project's policy.","Reject Cloud Armor, Web Security Scanner and Pod Security Standards for image provenance."],"takeaways":["Only pipeline-built images on GKE: Cloud Build + Artifact Registry + Binary Authorization.","Only images that passed tests: attestor + policy + attestation step in Cloud Build.","Only UAT-passed images in prod: KMS-signed attestation + cluster-specific rule in the production project.","Block critical CVEs: scan programmatically, attest clean images, enforce with Binary Authorization."],"questions":["e2q15","e3q30","e4q18","e6q14"]},{"id":"d3l10","num":10,"title":"Networking and connectivity for deployed apps","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run → Cloud SQL over private IP</strong> (bank key): configure a <strong>Serverless VPC Access connector</strong> on the Cloud Run service <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q5\">Exam 3 · Q5</a>. The full managed pattern: Cloud SQL and Cloud Run <strong>in the same project</strong>, Cloud SQL <strong>private IP + private services access</strong>, Cloud Run uses the connector <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run → Memorystore gives 502 Bad Gateway:</strong> the <strong>connector is in a different region</strong> from the Cloud Run service; all components must be in the same region. Memorystore doesn't need a public IP <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compute Engine VMs → Cloud SQL without IP allowlisting:</strong> enable <strong>private IP</strong> on the Cloud SQL instance <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q19\">Exam 3 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Clients in the same VPC find a VM-hosted API:</strong> Compute Engine <strong>internal DNS</strong>, <code>https://[INSTANCE_NAME].[ZONE].c.[PROJECT_ID].internal/</code>. External IPs, load balancers and A records are overkill <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Hybrid microservices crash on latency over Cloud VPN</strong> with fluctuating bandwidth: <strong>add Cloud VPN tunnels</strong> for more bandwidth and redundancy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q39\">Exam 2 · Q39</a>. Caching PII in Memorystore conflicts with the compliance reason it stays on-prem.</li>\n<li><strong>Istio: requests to certain URLs fail, others between the same Pods succeed</strong> → an <strong>AuthorizationPolicy</strong> blocking those <strong>paths</strong>. NetworkPolicy works at L3/L4 (not URLs), a wrong port or an mTLS mismatch would break all requests <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Layer comparison</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Control</th>\n<th>Works on</th>\n<th>Can fail \"only some URLs\"?</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Kubernetes NetworkPolicy</td>\n<td>IP/port (L3/L4)</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Istio mTLS (PeerAuthentication)</td>\n<td>Connection encryption</td>\n<td>No, affects all traffic</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Istio <strong>AuthorizationPolicy</strong></td>\n<td>HTTP method/path (L7)</td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Serverless → private VPC resource: <strong>connector, same region</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q5\">Exam 3 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Path-specific failures in a mesh → <strong>AuthorizationPolicy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>\"No allowlisting\" for Cloud SQL → <strong>private IP</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q19\">Exam 3 · Q19</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Cross-project VPN between Cloud Run and Cloud SQL, or self-managed PostgreSQL on a VM <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Firewall rules as the cause of a Cloud Run → Memorystore failure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Lowering the VPN MTU or adding a service mesh for a bandwidth problem <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q39\">Exam 2 · Q39</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers connecting deployed apps to private resources and to each other: serverless VPC access, Cloud SQL private IP, Compute Engine internal DNS, hybrid VPN capacity, and Istio policy debugging. Exam questions test the managed path that keeps traffic private.","objectives":["Connect Cloud Run privately to Cloud SQL and Memorystore with a Serverless VPC Access connector in the same region.","Connect Compute Engine to Cloud SQL without IP allowlisting using private IP.","Address a VM-hosted API from inside the VPC with Compute Engine internal DNS.","Diagnose URL-specific failures in an Istio mesh as an AuthorizationPolicy issue."],"takeaways":["Cloud Run to private Cloud SQL: private IP + private services access + Serverless VPC Access connector, same project.","502 from Cloud Run to Memorystore: the connector is in a different region.","No allowlisting for Cloud SQL: enable private IP.","Only some URLs fail between the same Pods: Istio AuthorizationPolicy (L7), not NetworkPolicy (L3/L4)."],"questions":["e1q4","e1q32","e2q39","e3q4","e3q5","e3q19","e3q39","e4q22"]},{"id":"d3l11","num":11,"title":"Compute Engine deployment operations and troubleshooting","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Image built (Packer) but won't boot:</strong> read the <strong>serial port output</strong> (also visible in Cloud Logging). Don't rebuild blindly or export and mount the disk first <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q52\">Exam 1 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>One instance failing and SSH unresponsive:</strong> <strong>enable and inspect serial port output</strong> before restarting or replacing it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q56\">Exam 1 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Broke a low-level Linux config file, VM won't boot normally:</strong> fix it through the <strong>interactive serial console</strong> connection. SSH, scp and RDP need a booted OS and network <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q50\">Exam 2 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Instance fails to launch (choose two):</strong> check whether the <strong>boot disk is full</strong> and whether the <strong>file system is corrupted</strong>. Firewall, alternative SSH user and dropped traffic assume the OS booted <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Copy a local directory to a VM:</strong> <code>gcloud compute scp --recurse … instance:~/path --zone …</code>. <code>gsutil cp</code> targets Cloud Storage; there's no <code>gcloud compute mv</code> for this <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q35\">Exam 2 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Slow Cloud Storage reads, data for support:</strong> <code>gsutil perfdiag -o output.json gs://bucket</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>GKE upgrade/security bulletin awareness</strong> for release-channel clusters: <strong>cluster notifications to Pub/Sub</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>VM not booting or SSH dead → <strong>serial port</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q52\">Exam 1 · Q52</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q56\">Exam 1 · Q56</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q50\">Exam 2 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Diagnose before you destroy: restart/recreate loses the evidence <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q56\">Exam 1 · Q56</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code>gsutil test</code> and <code>gcloud services test</code> don't exist as performance diagnostics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>RSS feeds, BigQuery release-notes queries or polling the GKE API for upgrade notices <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers day-two operations for Compute Engine deployments: diagnosing boot and SSH failures, copying files to VMs, collecting Cloud Storage performance diagnostics, and getting GKE upgrade notifications. Diagnose before destroying is the recurring theme.","objectives":["Use serial port output and the interactive serial console to diagnose and fix VMs that won't boot or accept SSH.","Identify boot disk and file system problems as causes of launch failures.","Use gcloud compute scp and gsutil perfdiag for the right jobs.","Receive GKE upgrade and security bulletins through cluster notifications to Pub/Sub."],"takeaways":["VM won't boot or SSH hangs: serial port output first, not restart or replace.","Broken low-level config file: fix it through the serial console.","Instance won't launch: check for a full boot disk and a corrupted file system.","Copy a directory to a VM: gcloud compute scp --recurse.","GKE release-channel events: cluster notifications to a Pub/Sub topic."],"questions":["e1q52","e1q56","e2q13","e2q35","e2q43","e2q50","e4q37"]},{"id":"d3l12","num":12,"title":"Production monitoring, logging and alerting","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Text log files on a VM → Cloud Logging, no code change:</strong> install the logging agent (the bank's \"Google-provided fluentd\"/Stackdriver Logging Agent) and configure it to collect those files <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q53\">Exam 2 · Q53</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>. The first step after installing it is to <strong>define the app log file as a custom source</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory usage of a VM</strong> is not a default hypervisor metric; you need the <strong>monitoring agent</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q52\">Exam 3 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Alert on error rate &gt; 15%</strong> (Cloud Run 500s), on <strong>service degradation</strong>, or on <strong>GKE memory outside 20–80%</strong>: a <strong>Cloud Monitoring alerting policy</strong>. Scheduled Cloud Functions, Composer, console checks and <code>kubectl top</code> scripts are all rejected <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q6\">Exam 3 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q57\">Exam 4 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Users can't reach the app behind the LB:</strong> <strong>uptime checks</strong> with alerting. LB/MIG health checks protect routing and autohealing; they don't notify you <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q10\">Exam 4 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Process stopped and you can't change the app:</strong> <strong>metric-absence</strong> condition on the process's metrics for 5 minutes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q23\">Exam 4 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Count requests to <code>/api/alpha/*</code> from logs:</strong> a <strong>logs-based counter metric</strong> with a wildcard path filter. <code>/api/alpha/</code> alone misses sub-paths; exporting to GCS or Pub/Sub and counting lines is manual <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q40\">Exam 4 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Correlate Prometheus metrics with logs and alert in real time, cheaply:</strong> bring Prometheus metrics into <strong>Cloud Monitoring</strong> and create <strong>log-based metrics</strong> there to correlate and alert. BigQuery/Bigtable exports add cost and latency <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q55\">Exam 3 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>BigQuery query execution time:</strong> Cloud Monitoring metrics, not Trace or slot usage <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q53\">Exam 1 · Q53</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Legacy on-prem monitoring is slow for cloud apps:</strong> use <strong>Cloud Logging + Cloud Monitoring</strong> for capture and alerting and <strong>forward alerts to the existing platform</strong>, rather than a big-bang replacement <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q35\">Exam 3 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Log retention of 1 and 3 years with easy search:</strong> keep the logs in <strong>Cloud Logging with custom retention</strong>, rather than chained buckets <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Signal selector</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question asks</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Is it reachable from outside?\"</td>\n<td>Uptime check</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q10\">Exam 4 · Q10</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Has X stopped reporting?\"</td>\n<td>Metric-absence condition</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q23\">Exam 4 · Q23</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Threshold on error rate / memory\"</td>\n<td>Metric-threshold alerting policy</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q6\">Exam 3 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q57\">Exam 4 · Q57</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Count log lines matching a pattern\"</td>\n<td>Logs-based counter metric</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q40\">Exam 4 · Q40</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"VM memory\"</td>\n<td>Monitoring agent</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q52\">Exam 3 · Q52</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Log files from disk\"</td>\n<td>Logging agent + custom source</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Minimal development time\" for alerts → <strong>native Cloud Monitoring</strong>, never a custom function <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q6\">Exam 3 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>\"No code change\" for logs → <strong>agent</strong>, not a client library <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Error Reporting is for exception analysis, not resource alerting <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>collectd is for metrics; syslog alone doesn't ship to Cloud Logging <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q53\">Exam 2 · Q53</a>.</li>\n<li>Giving the VM \"full access to all Cloud APIs\" is never the first step <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Write logs to /var/log and they'll be shipped automatically\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers observing applications in production with Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring: agents, alerting policies, uptime checks, metric-absence conditions and logs-based metrics. Exam questions favor native features over custom functions or exports.","objectives":["Ship VM log files and memory metrics with the logging and monitoring agents without code changes.","Pick the right alert type: metric threshold, uptime check or metric absence.","Count log entries matching a path pattern with a logs-based counter metric.","Correlate Prometheus metrics and logs in Cloud Monitoring cost-effectively."],"takeaways":["Any 'alert when X' with minimal development: Cloud Monitoring alerting policy, never a scheduled function.","App unreachable for users: uptime check; process stopped without app changes: metric absence.","VM memory needs the monitoring agent; log files need the logging agent with a custom source.","Count /api/alpha/* requests: logs-based counter metric with a wildcard."],"questions":["e1q53","e2q5","e2q45","e2q53","e3q6","e3q23","e3q35","e3q42","e3q52","e3q55","e4q10","e4q23","e4q40","e4q57"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li>Stateless container + least ops → <strong>Cloud Run</strong>; static site → <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q12\">Exam 6 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q34\">Exam 3 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>App Engine custom domain → <strong>verify + CNAME <code>ghs.googlehosted.com</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Deployment = replicas; <strong>StatefulSet</strong> = stable identity + own disk; <strong>DaemonSet</strong> = one per node <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>In-cluster calls → <strong>Service DNS name</strong>; internal only → <strong>ClusterIP</strong>; new domain on same IP → <strong>host rule on existing Ingress</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Graceful shutdown → <strong>handle SIGTERM</strong>; preemptible VM → <strong>shutdown script sends a signal</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q59\">Exam 2 · Q59</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>Golden image + per-env config → <strong>instance metadata read from the metadata server</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Shared writable file for many Pods → <strong>Filestore NFS PV</strong>; grow a volume → <strong>edit the PVC</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q58\">Exam 4 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li>Secrets → <strong>Secret Manager + Workload Identity</strong>, never exported SA keys <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Limit users hit by a bad release → <strong>canary</strong>; swift full rollback → <strong>blue/green</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>Full production load before launch → <strong>traffic mirroring</strong>; sales impact → <strong>A/B split + statistics</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q27\">Exam 2 · Q27</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run gradual → <strong>no-traffic revision, then percentage split</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>Route by OS/user-agent → <strong>GKE + Anthos Service Mesh</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q57\">Exam 2 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li>Urgent autoscaling → <strong>HPA on CPU</strong>; Pub/Sub backlog → <strong>HPA external metric</strong>; nodes too → <strong>+ Cluster Autoscaler</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>MIG latency during scale-out → <strong>lower CPU target + shorter cooldown</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q14\">Exam 1 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>Release specific commits → <strong>Git tag trigger</strong>; deploy fails after validation → <strong>Cloud Build SA role</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Isolated test env → <strong>Cloud Build runs Terraform to create a new project</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q49\">Exam 4 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>Only trusted images → <strong>Cloud Build + Artifact Registry + Binary Authorization</strong>; attestation step after tests; KMS key; rule in <strong>prod</strong> policy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q14\">Exam 6 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run → private Cloud SQL/Memorystore → <strong>Serverless VPC Access connector in the same region</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Istio, only some URLs fail → <strong>AuthorizationPolicy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>VM won't boot / SSH dead → <strong>serial port</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q56\">Exam 1 · Q56</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q50\">Exam 2 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Alerts → <strong>Cloud Monitoring alerting policy</strong>; reachability → <strong>uptime check</strong>; process gone → <strong>metric absence</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q6\">Exam 3 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q10\">Exam 4 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q23\">Exam 4 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>Count matching log entries → <strong>logs-based counter metric</strong> with wildcard path <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q40\">Exam 4 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>Logs from files without code change → <strong>logging agent</strong>; VM memory → <strong>monitoring agent</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q52\">Exam 3 · Q52</a>.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d3l1","title":"Choosing where to deploy","html":"<ul>\n<li>When the stem says \"minimal management\" and the workload is a stateless container, choose <strong>Cloud Run</strong> before any GKE option <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q12\">Exam 6 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q51\">Exam 4 · Q51</a>.</li>\n<li>When there is no server-side logic at all, choose <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q34\">Exam 3 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>For an App Engine custom domain, the answer is always <strong>verify + CNAME <code>ghs.googlehosted.com</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l2","title":"GKE workload objects and in-cluster service discovery","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Specific number of replicas per microservice + uniform addressing\" → <strong>Deployment + Service + Service DNS name</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Only other services in the cluster may call it\" → <strong>ClusterIP</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Install X on every node\" → <strong>DaemonSet</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l3","title":"Pod lifecycle: probes, hooks and graceful shutdown","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"App crashes/aborts DB connections during rollout\" → <strong>SIGTERM handler</strong> first <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q59\">Exam 2 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Continuous health check + shutdown script\" → <strong>probe + preStop</strong>, not init/PostStart <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q19\">Exam 1 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Graceful shutdown on preemption\" → <strong>shutdown script → signal to process</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l4","title":"Configuration and storage for deployed apps","html":"<ul>\n<li>One image, different environments on Compute Engine → <strong>metadata + metadata server</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Shared writable file system on GKE → <strong>Filestore</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l5","title":"Secrets on GKE","html":"<ul>\n<li>If Secret Manager + Workload Identity is an option, pick it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Reject any option that <strong>exports a service account key as JSON</strong> (into a ConfigMap or volume) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l6","title":"Rollout strategies and traffic splitting","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Minimal configuration\" on Cloud Run → use <strong>built-in revision traffic splitting</strong>, not a load balancer or code-level routing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Small portion of users first, then everyone\" → <strong>traffic splitting / canary</strong>, not \"smoke test then 100%\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>For the key <strong>\"safest rollout of a new GKE revision\"</strong>, the bank's answer is <strong>rolling update + PodDisruptionBudget</strong>, not a StatefulSet conversion or an HPA scale-down policy (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q49\">Exam 3 · Q49</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l7","title":"Autoscaling deployed workloads","html":"<ul>\n<li>Tight deadline beats elegance: <strong>CPU-based HPA</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Messages waiting → <strong>external metric</strong>, not resource utilization <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l8","title":"CI/CD deployment pipelines","html":"<ul>\n<li>Deploy step fails, code is fine → <strong>check the build service account's IAM role</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Release commits\" → <strong>tags</strong>; \"every push to dev\" → <strong>branch trigger</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q58\">Exam 3 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Separate from production, fully automated\" → <strong>new project via IaC</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q49\">Exam 4 · Q49</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l9","title":"Supply-chain security: Binary Authorization","html":"<ul>\n<li>The attestation must match the gate in the stem: tests passed → test attestation; UAT passed → UAT attestation; no critical CVEs → scan attestation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q14\">Exam 6 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>Enforcement lives in the <strong>target (production) project's policy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Signing keys live in <strong>Cloud KMS</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l10","title":"Networking and connectivity for deployed apps","html":"<ul>\n<li>Serverless → private VPC resource: <strong>connector, same region</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q5\">Exam 3 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Path-specific failures in a mesh → <strong>AuthorizationPolicy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>\"No allowlisting\" for Cloud SQL → <strong>private IP</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q19\">Exam 3 · Q19</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l11","title":"Compute Engine deployment operations and troubleshooting","html":"<ul>\n<li>VM not booting or SSH dead → <strong>serial port</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q52\">Exam 1 · Q52</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q56\">Exam 1 · Q56</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q50\">Exam 2 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Diagnose before you destroy: restart/recreate loses the evidence <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q56\">Exam 1 · Q56</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l12","title":"Production monitoring, logging and alerting","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Minimal development time\" for alerts → <strong>native Cloud Monitoring</strong>, never a custom function <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q6\">Exam 3 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>\"No code change\" for logs → <strong>agent</strong>, not a client library <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d3l1","title":"Choosing where to deploy","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"GKE Autopilot\" sounds managed, but it is still Kubernetes. Cloud Run wins on operational simplicity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q12\">Exam 6 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li><code>dispatch.yaml</code> is not domain mapping <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Split the site into three services/functions\" to test three designs adds complexity. Use <strong>one service with versions/revisions and traffic splitting</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l2","title":"GKE workload objects and in-cluster service discovery","html":"<ul>\n<li>Bare <strong>Pods</strong> instead of Deployments: no replica management or self-healing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ingress for service-to-service traffic</strong> inside the cluster is wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"GKE Endpoint\" / Cloud DNS A records for in-cluster discovery add pointless layers. Kubernetes DNS already does it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>ReplicaSet and ReplicationController don't give stable identity or per-Pod storage. ReplicationController is legacy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Converting a stateless Deployment to a StatefulSet just to roll out safely adds complexity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q49\">Exam 3 · Q49</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l3","title":"Pod lifecycle: probes, hooks and graceful shutdown","html":"<ul>\n<li>Raising <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> looks helpful but doesn't make the app react <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q59\">Exam 2 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li><code>COPY</code> config into the image <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a> and \"one image per environment\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a> are both config-in-image anti-patterns.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l4","title":"Configuration and storage for deployed apps","html":"<ul>\n<li>Instance <strong>tags/labels</strong> for config values <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>Startup script with hardcoded values, Bigtable or a PD for small config <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Editing the <strong>PV</strong> instead of the <strong>PVC</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q58\">Exam 4 · Q58</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l5","title":"Secrets on GKE","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Store it in Cloud KMS\": KMS manages <em>keys</em>, not secrets <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Sidecar proxy, service mesh or an encrypted PVC don't solve credential storage and rotation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l6","title":"Rollout strategies and traffic splitting","html":"<ul>\n<li>Blue/green doesn't give gradual exposure; canary doesn't give the fastest complete rollback <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Feature flag to half of all users</strong> looks like A/B, but the bank rejects it for not giving the clean, predetermined traffic split of the LB option <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run tags/revisions and App Engine IP-based splits can't target an OS <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Separate namespaces don't give traffic control by percentage <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q57\">Exam 2 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li>Weekly load tests or local load tests don't reduce the blast radius of a new feature <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l7","title":"Autoscaling deployed workloads","html":"<ul>\n<li>Enabling <strong>MIG autoscaling on GKE node pools directly</strong> instead of Cluster Autoscaler <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>VPA Recommendation mode</strong> doesn't scale anything <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Raising the CPU target delays scale-out and makes latency worse <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q14\">Exam 1 · Q14</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l8","title":"CI/CD deployment pipelines","html":"<ul>\n<li>A second repository just for release builds fragments history <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Build monitoring Pub/Sub to drive promotion, or the gke-deploy builder, is not the bank's Cloud Deploy answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l9","title":"Supply-chain security: Binary Authorization","html":"<ul>\n<li>Cloud Armor in a supply-chain question <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Web Security Scanner for container CVEs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l10","title":"Networking and connectivity for deployed apps","html":"<ul>\n<li>Cross-project VPN between Cloud Run and Cloud SQL, or self-managed PostgreSQL on a VM <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Firewall rules as the cause of a Cloud Run → Memorystore failure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Lowering the VPN MTU or adding a service mesh for a bandwidth problem <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q39\">Exam 2 · Q39</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l11","title":"Compute Engine deployment operations and troubleshooting","html":"<ul>\n<li><code>gsutil test</code> and <code>gcloud services test</code> don't exist as performance diagnostics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>RSS feeds, BigQuery release-notes queries or polling the GKE API for upgrade notices <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l12","title":"Production monitoring, logging and alerting","html":"<ul>\n<li>Error Reporting is for exception analysis, not resource alerting <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>collectd is for metrics; syslog alone doesn't ship to Cloud Logging <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q53\">Exam 2 · Q53</a>.</li>\n<li>Giving the VM \"full access to all Cloud APIs\" is never the first step <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Write logs to /var/log and they'll be shipped automatically\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Issue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q49\">Exam 3 · Q49</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys \"rolling update with a PodDisruptionBudget at 80%\". A PDB limits <strong>voluntary evictions</strong> (node drains, upgrades); it does not govern a Deployment rollout, which is controlled by <code>maxSurge</code>/<code>maxUnavailable</code>. The explanation's claim that the PDB keeps 80% of Pods up during the update is wrong. Pick it among these options, but don't learn it as how rollouts are protected.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a></strong> vs <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys Kubernetes Secret + KMS plugin for credentials that need <strong>rotation</strong>. The other two items (and Google's guidance) prefer <strong>Secret Manager + Workload Identity</strong>, which also handles versioning/rotation. Q46 only wins because Secret Manager isn't offered.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q19\">Exam 1 · Q19</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys a <strong>livenessProbe that fails when the DB is unreachable</strong>. Liveness probes that check external dependencies are a known anti-pattern: a DB outage makes every Pod restart in a loop. A readiness probe is the usual tool for dependency checks. The explanation's line that \"a PreStop hook doesn't automatically trigger if a liveness probe fails\" is also inaccurate: kubelet runs preStop before killing a container for a failed liveness probe.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a></strong></td>\n<td>Cloud Function on <code>clouddeploy-operations</code> to auto-promote. Cloud Deploy now has <strong>built-in automation (promote-release rules)</strong>, which is the simpler current answer if offered.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q14\">Exam 1 · Q14</a></strong></td>\n<td>Answers are fine, but the explanation says a shorter cooldown lets new VMs \"handle traffic sooner\". The cooldown/initialization period controls when the <strong>autoscaler trusts the new VM's metrics</strong>; it doesn't change when the VM serves traffic.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q27\">Exam 2 · Q27</a></strong></td>\n<td>Calls traffic <strong>mirroring</strong> \"A/B testing\". Mirroring is shadow testing (users never see the new version); A/B testing exposes users to variants <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>. Learn the cue \"full production load before launch → mirroring\".</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q21\">Exam 2 · Q21</a></strong></td>\n<td>Dismisses Cloud Run's <strong>continuous deployment from a repository with buildpacks</strong>, which is a built-in, fully managed option (it creates a Cloud Build trigger for you) and arguably simpler. Expect either; the bank keys the explicit Cloud Build trigger.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q5\">Exam 3 · Q5</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a></strong></td>\n<td>Serverless VPC Access connectors are still valid, but Cloud Run now also supports <strong>Direct VPC egress</strong> without a connector. Q5's claim that the Cloud SQL connector/Cloud Run Cloud SQL connection \"won't solve\" private IP overstates it; they can use private IP once a VPC path exists.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stackdriver / legacy agents</strong>: <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q53\">Exam 2 · Q53</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q52\">Exam 3 · Q52</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q53\">Exam 1 · Q53</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q10\">Exam 4 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q23\">Exam 4 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q40\">Exam 4 · Q40</a></td>\n<td>\"Stackdriver\" is now Cloud Logging/Monitoring, and the separate fluentd logging agent and collectd monitoring agent are replaced by the <strong>Ops Agent</strong>. The concepts hold; the product names are outdated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a></strong></td>\n<td>Uses <strong>Container Registry</strong>, which is deprecated in favor of <strong>Artifact Registry</strong>; vulnerability scanning now sits under <strong>Artifact Analysis</strong>. The attestation + Binary Authorization logic still holds.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q21\">Exam 2 · Q21</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q58\">Exam 3 · Q58</a></strong></td>\n<td>Built around <strong>Cloud Source Repositories</strong>, which is closed to new customers. The trigger logic (tag vs branch vs push) carries over to GitHub/GitLab-connected Cloud Build triggers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q58\">Exam 4 · Q58</a></strong></td>\n<td>Resizing the PD first and then editing the PVC is backwards from the normal flow (edit the PVC and let the CSI driver grow the disk), and it only works when the StorageClass has <code>allowVolumeExpansion: true</code>. With a manually created PV, as in the stem, expansion may not apply at all.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a></strong></td>\n<td>\"Webmaster Central\" is now <strong>Search Console</strong>. The CNAME to <code>ghs.googlehosted.com</code> remains the answer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a></strong></td>\n<td>The explanation says gsutil in the container means \"embedding credentials\"; with Workload Identity it doesn't. Also, the Kubernetes-native way to hold traffic until config is present is a <strong>readiness probe</strong>. Learn the key, not the reasoning.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a></strong></td>\n<td>A Cloud Logging bucket has <strong>one</strong> retention value; you'd set 3 years (which also covers the 1-year need). The explanation implies setting two retention periods.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},"glossary":[{"term":"A/B testing","definition":"Exposing users to two or more variants with a predetermined traffic split and comparing a business metric for a statistically significant difference.","exam_context":"Shows up as 'will the new color scheme increase sales' or 'compare three designs', answered with traffic splitting.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Alerting policy","definition":"A Cloud Monitoring configuration of conditions and notification channels that fires when a metric crosses a threshold.","exam_context":"Beats scheduled Cloud Functions, Composer jobs or scripts for error-rate and memory alerts.","lesson":"d3l12","domain":3},{"term":"Anthos Service Mesh","definition":"Google's managed Istio-based service mesh for GKE that controls traffic routing, security and observability between services.","exam_context":"The answer when traffic must be split by HTTP header such as user-agent, or for zero-downtime canary/A/B on GKE.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Application-layer Secrets Encryption","definition":"A GKE feature that encrypts Kubernetes Secrets in etcd with a Cloud KMS key.","exam_context":"A distractor when the goal is keeping secrets away from the Kubernetes API server; Secret Manager + Workload Identity wins.","lesson":"d3l5","domain":3},{"term":"Artifact Registry","definition":"Google Cloud's managed repository for container images and packages.","exam_context":"Part of the trusted-image trio with Cloud Build and Binary Authorization; Cloud Storage is the wrong store.","lesson":"d3l9","domain":3},{"term":"Attestation","definition":"A signed statement that a container image passed a specific check, such as tests, UAT or a vulnerability scan.","exam_context":"Created as a Cloud Build step after the relevant gate and signed with a Cloud KMS key.","lesson":"d3l9","domain":3},{"term":"AuthorizationPolicy","definition":"An Istio resource that allows or denies requests based on attributes such as HTTP method and path.","exam_context":"The cause when only certain URLs fail between two Pods that otherwise communicate.","lesson":"d3l10","domain":3},{"term":"Binary Authorization","definition":"A deploy-time control that only admits container images carrying the attestations required by a policy.","exam_context":"The enforcement piece whenever only pipeline-built, tested or scanned images may run on GKE; rules belong in the production project.","lesson":"d3l9","domain":3},{"term":"Blue/green deployment","definition":"Running two full environments and switching all traffic from the old (blue) to the new (green) one.","exam_context":"Chosen for swift, complete rollback; rejected when gradual exposure is required.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Canary deployment","definition":"Sending a small share of real traffic to a new version and increasing it as metrics stay healthy.","exam_context":"The answer for limiting how many users a production-only bug can reach, often inside a CD pipeline with Cloud Monitoring.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Cloud Build trigger","definition":"A rule that starts a Cloud Build run on events such as a push to a branch, a Git tag, a webhook or a Pub/Sub message.","exam_context":"Branch triggers deploy every push; tag-pattern triggers release specific commits.","lesson":"d3l8","domain":3},{"term":"Cloud Deploy","definition":"A managed continuous delivery service that promotes releases through a sequence of targets such as Dev, Test and Prod.","exam_context":"Its events go to the clouddeploy-operations Pub/Sub topic, which the bank uses to drive automatic promotion.","lesson":"d3l8","domain":3},{"term":"Cloud Run traffic splitting","definition":"Cloud Run's built-in ability to divide traffic among revisions by percentage.","exam_context":"Deploy a revision with no traffic, then shift percentages, for a gradual rollout with minimal configuration.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Cloud SQL private IP","definition":"Configuring a Cloud SQL instance with an internal address in a VPC through private services access.","exam_context":"Removes the need for IP allowlisting and keeps Cloud Run or VM traffic private.","lesson":"d3l10","domain":3},{"term":"Cluster Autoscaler","definition":"A GKE feature that adds or removes nodes based on pending Pods and node utilization.","exam_context":"Paired with HPA when both Pods and nodes must follow load; editing the node MIG autoscaler directly is a distractor.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"ClusterIP Service","definition":"The default Kubernetes Service type, reachable only from inside the cluster.","exam_context":"The answer when a GKE service must be accessible only to other services in the cluster.","lesson":"d3l2","domain":3},{"term":"Compute Engine internal DNS","definition":"Automatic DNS names for VMs in a VPC, such as INSTANCE_NAME.ZONE.c.PROJECT_ID.internal.","exam_context":"How clients in the same VPC find a VM-hosted API without external IPs or load balancers.","lesson":"d3l10","domain":3},{"term":"Container Scanning API","definition":"The API that scans container images for known vulnerabilities, now part of Artifact Analysis.","exam_context":"Review results programmatically in the pipeline and attest clean images for Binary Authorization.","lesson":"d3l9","domain":3},{"term":"DaemonSet","definition":"A Kubernetes workload that runs one copy of a Pod on every node.","exam_context":"The way to install a third-party logging or monitoring agent on each GKE node.","lesson":"d3l2","domain":3},{"term":"Deployment","definition":"A Kubernetes workload that manages a replicated set of stateless Pods and their rolling updates.","exam_context":"Use one per microservice to control replica counts; bare Pods are the distractor.","lesson":"d3l2","domain":3},{"term":"Filestore","definition":"Google Cloud's managed NFS file service.","exam_context":"Mounted as an NFS PersistentVolume when many instances need read/write access to a shared file without code changes.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"GKE cluster notifications","definition":"GKE events such as available upgrades and security bulletins, published to a Pub/Sub topic.","exam_context":"The recommended way to stay informed about release-channel clusters, instead of RSS or API polling.","lesson":"d3l11","domain":3},{"term":"gsutil perfdiag","definition":"A gsutil command that runs Cloud Storage performance diagnostics and can write results to a file.","exam_context":"The command to collect data for support when Cloud Storage reads are slow.","lesson":"d3l11","domain":3},{"term":"Horizontal Pod Autoscaler","definition":"A Kubernetes controller that changes the number of Pod replicas based on CPU, custom or external metrics.","exam_context":"CPU-based HPA is the fast answer; an external metric is used for Pub/Sub backlog.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"Ingress","definition":"A Kubernetes resource that routes external HTTP(S) traffic to Services; on GKE it provisions an HTTP(S) load balancer.","exam_context":"Add a host rule to the existing Ingress to serve a new domain on the same IP; never use it for in-cluster calls.","lesson":"d3l2","domain":3},{"term":"Instance metadata","definition":"Key-value data attached to a Compute Engine instance or instance template, readable from the metadata server.","exam_context":"The built-in way to pass per-environment config to a golden image or per-MIG values to one custom image.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Interactive serial console","definition":"Access to a Compute Engine VM's serial port that works even when the OS or network isn't up.","exam_context":"Used to read boot logs and fix a VM that won't boot or accept SSH.","lesson":"d3l11","domain":3},{"term":"Liveness probe","definition":"A container check whose failure makes the kubelet restart the container.","exam_context":"Keyed with a preStop hook for a DB-connectivity check, though checking external dependencies this way is questionable.","lesson":"d3l3","domain":3},{"term":"Logs-based metric","definition":"A Cloud Monitoring metric derived from log entries that match a filter, such as a counter.","exam_context":"Used to count requests to a path pattern like /api/alpha/* and to correlate logs with metrics.","lesson":"d3l12","domain":3},{"term":"Metric absence condition","definition":"A Cloud Monitoring alert condition that fires when a metric stops reporting for a set duration.","exam_context":"The answer for 'alert if a process hasn't run for 5 minutes' when the app can't be changed.","lesson":"d3l12","domain":3},{"term":"Ops Agent","definition":"Google's current unified agent for collecting logs and metrics from Compute Engine VMs, replacing the separate logging (fluentd) and monitoring (collectd) agents.","exam_context":"Questions still say 'Stackdriver Logging/Monitoring Agent'; treat that as the agent you install for log files and VM memory.","lesson":"d3l12","domain":3},{"term":"PersistentVolumeClaim","definition":"A Pod's request for storage that binds to a PersistentVolume.","exam_context":"Edit its spec.resources.requests.storage to expand a volume; also used to mount NFS config for a legacy app.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"PodDisruptionBudget","definition":"A Kubernetes object that limits how many Pods of an app can be down during voluntary disruptions like node drains.","exam_context":"Keyed with a rolling update for safe rollouts, although it doesn't actually govern Deployment rollouts.","lesson":"d3l3","domain":3},{"term":"preStop hook","definition":"A container lifecycle handler that runs just before the container is terminated.","exam_context":"Holds a graceful shutdown script, but doesn't replace handling SIGTERM in the application.","lesson":"d3l3","domain":3},{"term":"Secret Manager","definition":"Google Cloud's managed service for storing, versioning and accessing secrets.","exam_context":"With Workload Identity, the recommended way to give GKE Pods secrets without exposing them through the Kubernetes API.","lesson":"d3l5","domain":3},{"term":"Serverless VPC Access connector","definition":"A managed resource that lets Cloud Run, Cloud Functions and App Engine reach private IPs in a VPC.","exam_context":"Required for Cloud Run to reach Cloud SQL private IP or Memorystore, and it must be in the same region.","lesson":"d3l10","domain":3},{"term":"SIGTERM","definition":"The termination signal Kubernetes sends to a container before killing it.","exam_context":"Handling it in code is the fix for DB connections dropped during a Deployment update.","lesson":"d3l3","domain":3},{"term":"StatefulSet","definition":"A Kubernetes workload giving each Pod a stable network identity, its own persistent volume and ordered scaling.","exam_context":"The answer when each replica needs a stable identity and its own persistent disk.","lesson":"d3l2","domain":3},{"term":"Traffic mirroring","definition":"Copying live production traffic to a new version without returning its responses to users.","exam_context":"The answer for testing against the full production load before launch.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Uptime check","definition":"A Cloud Monitoring probe that periodically requests a public endpoint and can trigger alerts.","exam_context":"The way to be alerted when users can't reach an app behind a load balancer; LB health checks don't notify.","lesson":"d3l12","domain":3},{"term":"Vertical Pod Autoscaler","definition":"A Kubernetes controller that adjusts Pods' CPU and memory requests and limits.","exam_context":"A distractor for bursty or rapid scaling needs, where HPA is the answer.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"Workload Identity","definition":"A GKE feature that lets a Kubernetes service account act as a Google service account without keys.","exam_context":"Paired with Secret Manager so Pods fetch secrets without exported JSON keys.","lesson":"d3l5","domain":3}]},{"n":4,"title":"Integrating Applications with Google Cloud Services","short":"Service Integration","weight":"","desc":"Pub/Sub, Eventarc, Cloud Tasks and Workflows, databases and Cloud Storage from code, API management and service-to-service authentication.","lessons":[{"id":"d4l1","num":1,"title":"Service identity, Application Default Credentials and the metadata server","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Code runs as a service account, not as you.</strong> When a function's write to another project's bucket fails with 403, the fix is to grant a narrow role (<code>roles/storage.objectCreator</code>) <strong>on the bucket</strong> to the identity the function runs as. Granting the role to your user account, or giving yourself <code>iam.serviceAccountUser</code>, doesn't change what the function can do. Enabling the API in project B doesn't fix a permissions error (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>, see ⚠️ for which service account that really is).</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-project access = home-project service account + role on the remote resource.</strong> A Compute Engine app in project A that publishes to a topic in project B runs with a <strong>service account from project A</strong>, which is granted publisher <strong>directly on the topic</strong> in project B <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Application Default Credentials (ADC)</strong> pick up the service account attached to the VM, so no key file has to be distributed. Google manages rotation of those credentials <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Metadata server:</strong> <code>http://metadata.google.internal/</code> with the header <code>Metadata-Flavor: Google</code>. It is reachable only from inside the instance and returns live values, such as <code>computeMetadata/v1/project/project-id</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q46\">Exam 2 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>No client library for your language?</strong> Get a short-lived <strong>access token from the metadata server</strong> and send it in the <code>Authorization</code> header of your REST calls <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>BigQuery from a script or CLI:</strong> querying needs <strong>BigQuery Data Viewer</strong> (read tables) <strong>plus BigQuery Job User</strong> (create query jobs). Data Editor adds write access you don't need <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Credential options compared</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Verdict</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Attached service account + ADC</td>\n<td>✅ Best</td>\n<td>No keys to distribute, Google rotates credentials</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metadata-server token in <code>Authorization</code> header</td>\n<td>✅ For raw REST</td>\n<td>Short-lived, refreshed by the platform</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>JSON key in source repo / CI package</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td>Exposed if the repo leaks</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>P12 file copied to host</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td>Manual transfer, leak risk</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>API key in the code or in <code>GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS</code></td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td>An API key isn't a service-account identity</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>gcloud auth application-default print-access-token</code> at startup, saved to a file</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td>The token expires, and you would have to manage refresh yourself</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signed URLs \"for authentication\"</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td>They grant access to objects, not general API auth</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>403 from code calling an API: find the <strong>runtime identity</strong>, then grant the <strong>least-privileged role on the target resource</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Securely distribute credentials to VMs\": the answer is <strong>don't</strong>. Use the attached service account with ADC <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>Need project ID or region at runtime on Cloud Run: query the <strong>metadata server</strong>, not console-copied environment variables or the Cloud Asset Inventory API <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q46\">Exam 2 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery permission error when running queries: <strong>Data Viewer + Job User</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Options that use a service account <strong>from the other project</strong> (project B's account used on project A's VMs) create a reverse dependency <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Use ADC with the private key of a service account\" sounds modern, but it still means handling a key file <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>Creating a view or copying the table to a new dataset doesn't fix a permission error <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Application code on Google Cloud calls APIs as a service account, not as a person. This lesson covers Application Default Credentials, the metadata server and cross-project IAM grants, which together remove the need to ever distribute key files.","objectives":["Diagnose a 403 from application code by identifying the runtime identity and the missing resource-level role","Configure cross-project access by granting a home-project service account a role on the remote resource","Obtain credentials and runtime metadata from the metadata server instead of keys or hard-coded config","Choose the minimal BigQuery roles needed to run queries from a script"],"takeaways":["On a 403, grant the narrowest role on the target resource to the identity the code runs as.","Use the attached service account with ADC. Key files, P12 files and API keys are always distractors.","No client library? Fetch a token from the metadata server and send it in the Authorization header.","The metadata server lives at metadata.google.internal and requires the Metadata-Flavor: Google header.","Running BigQuery queries needs Data Viewer plus Job User."],"questions":["e1q13","e1q47","e2q46","e2q49","e3q41","e4q32"]},{"id":"d4l2","num":2,"title":"Workload Identity on GKE","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Workload Identity</strong> binds a <strong>Kubernetes service account (KSA)</strong> to a <strong>Google service account (GSA)</strong>. Pods get short-lived tokens, so no keys are stored in the cluster. It is Google's recommended way for GKE workloads to call Google Cloud APIs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q51\">Exam 1 · Q51</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>Setup steps: create the GSA with the needed role (BigQuery, Spanner and so on) → create a KSA → <strong>enable Workload Identity on the cluster</strong> → bind KSA to GSA with <strong><code>roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser</code></strong> → set the KSA in the Deployment spec <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>It is a <strong>one-time setup</strong>, and credentials are rotated for you <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>It needs minimal code change. Client libraries pick up the identity automatically, for example when accessing Spanner from microservices <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>It works <strong>across projects</strong>: grant the GSA roles in whichever project holds the resource <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q51\">Exam 1 · Q51</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Option comparison</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Problem</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Node's service account</td>\n<td>Every Pod on the node shares it, which breaks least privilege</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q51\">Exam 1 · Q51</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GSA key in a Kubernetes Secret, exposed as env var or file</td>\n<td>A long-lived key you must store and rotate</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GSA key in Secret Manager, fetched by an init container or the client lib</td>\n<td>Still a key, just stored somewhere else</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Key encrypted with Cloud KMS</td>\n<td>Adds complexity, and you still handle a decrypted credential</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HashiCorp Vault dynamic account</td>\n<td>Extra infrastructure to run</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GKE RBAC</td>\n<td>Controls access <strong>inside</strong> Kubernetes, not to Google APIs</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q51\">Exam 1 · Q51</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VPC-native routing</td>\n<td>That's connectivity, not credentials</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Key embedded in source code</td>\n<td>Never</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Any \"GKE + Google API + best practice\" question: pick <strong>Workload Identity</strong>. For \"choose two\", pick <strong>enable on cluster + bind KSA↔GSA</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Words like \"auto-rotating\", \"one-time setup\" and \"no keys\" all point to Workload Identity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Anything that mentions a <strong>service account key</strong>, however well protected, is a distractor <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Workload Identity is Google's recommended way for GKE Pods to call Google Cloud APIs. It binds a Kubernetes service account to a Google service account, so no keys are stored or rotated by hand.","objectives":["Describe how Workload Identity maps a Kubernetes service account to a Google service account","List the setup steps: enable on the cluster, bind with roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser, reference the KSA in the Deployment","Explain why node service accounts, Kubernetes Secrets and Secret Manager-held keys are weaker options"],"takeaways":["GKE + Google API + best practice means Workload Identity, every time.","For a choose-two question, pick enabling Workload Identity on the cluster and binding the KSA to the GSA.","Any option that stores a service account key, however securely, is a distractor.","GKE RBAC governs Kubernetes resources, not access to Google Cloud APIs."],"questions":["e1q44","e1q51","e1q57","e2q37","e3q27","e3q31"]},{"id":"d4l3","num":3,"title":"OAuth 2.0 for accessing users' data","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>When an app acts <strong>on behalf of a user</strong> on that user's own data, such as their Google Drive, it needs an <strong>OAuth 2.0 client ID</strong>. Not an API key (that identifies only the project), not a service account (server-to-server, with no user), and not SAML (SSO) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q56\">Exam 2 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Request a narrow scope</strong>: <code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file</code> gets a per-user access token limited to files the app works with <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Authorization-code flow:</strong> redirect to the Google consent screen → user signs in (SSO) and consents → the app receives an <strong>authorization code</strong> → the app <strong>exchanges the code for an access token</strong> → it calls the API with that token <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>The app <strong>never sees or stores the user's Google password</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Users' Drive\", \"on behalf of users\", \"without domain restrictions\" → <strong>OAuth client ID + per-user token + minimal scope</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Domain-wide delegation</strong> (with an OAuth client or a service account) gives access to the whole Workspace domain. That is excessive and doesn't fit users outside your domain <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li>The runtime doesn't change the answer. The same OAuth answer applies on Compute Engine or any other Google Cloud runtime <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>An option that stops at \"receive an authorization code, store it in cookies and use it to authenticate\" is missing the <strong>code-for-token exchange</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Service account + <code>drive.file</code> scope + signed JWT\" has the right scope on the wrong identity, since a service account isn't the user <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li>Collecting usernames and passwords, hashed or forwarded, is always wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"When an application works with a user's own data, such as files in Google Drive, it must act on that user's behalf through OAuth 2.0. This lesson covers the authorization-code flow and why narrow scopes beat service accounts and domain-wide delegation.","objectives":["Pick an OAuth 2.0 client ID over API keys, service accounts or SAML for user-data access","Walk through the authorization-code flow, including the code-for-token exchange","Choose a minimal scope such as drive.file and reject domain-wide delegation"],"takeaways":["Users' Drive means an OAuth client ID, the drive.file scope and an access token per user.","The flow is consent, then authorization code, then token exchange, then the API call.","Domain-wide delegation is excessive and doesn't fit users outside your domain.","Never collect or store users' Google passwords."],"questions":["e2q56","e3q7","e4q20","e4q56"]},{"id":"d4l4","num":4,"title":"Securing invocations between services","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Only authorized services may call a function:</strong> create a service account with the <strong>Cloud Functions Invoker</strong> role and make calls as that account. The <strong>Viewer</strong> role can only read configuration and can't invoke. <strong>IAP</strong> is for users reaching web apps, not service-to-service calls. A separate OAuth client ID adds complexity for no benefit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>CORS:</strong> if a browser page on <code>https://www.example.com</code> posts to a function on <code>https://fn.example.com</code>, the function returns <strong><code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://www.example.com</code></strong>, the exact calling origin. <code>*</code> lets any site in, <code>https://*.example.com</code> is broader than needed, and the function's own origin blocks the real callers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Scheduling calls to an internal-ingress Cloud Run service:</strong> the keyed fix for the 403 is <strong>Cloud Scheduler + Pub/Sub</strong> delivering to Cloud Run. Switching ingress to \"Internal and Cloud Load Balancing\" exposes the service, a cron VM adds management overhead, and <code>roles/run.developer</code> is overly broad (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q14\">Exam 4 · Q14</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Invoke\" means <strong>Invoker</strong> role (<code>roles/cloudfunctions.invoker</code> style roles), granted to the <strong>calling</strong> service account <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>Restricting browser callers means CORS with the <strong>exact origin of the page</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Viewer vs Invoker: if a role name doesn't say \"Invoker\", it can't invoke <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>The function's own domain in <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> blocks the real callers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Calls between services must be authorized explicitly. This lesson covers the Invoker role for functions, CORS headers for browser callers on another domain, and scheduling calls to internal-ingress Cloud Run.","objectives":["Restrict function invocation to authorized callers with an Invoker-role service account","Set Access-Control-Allow-Origin correctly for a cross-domain browser caller","Choose managed scheduling over VM cron or loosened ingress for internal Cloud Run services"],"takeaways":["To invoke, the caller needs the Invoker role. Viewer can't invoke.","IAP protects user access to web apps, not service-to-service calls.","CORS allow-origin should be the exact calling page origin, never * or the function's own domain.","Don't loosen Cloud Run ingress to fix a scheduling 403."],"questions":["e1q30","e3q29","e4q14"]},{"id":"d4l5","num":5,"title":"API management: Cloud Endpoints and Apigee","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Authenticate callers + enforce quotas + track metrics</strong> in front of a backend: use an <strong>API management layer</strong>. In the bank this is <strong>Cloud Endpoints</strong>, which sits between Cloud Load Balancing and the app <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q55\">Exam 1 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Backward-incompatible API change on Cloud Functions:</strong> leave the original function unchanged, deploy a <strong>second function</strong> with the new API, and expose a <strong>versioned API through Cloud Endpoints</strong>. One function branching on a version field risks breaking old callers. A plain load balancer has no API-management features, and Cloud Functions doesn't route by version automatically <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Subscription tiers with daily request limits:</strong> put the GKE service behind an <strong>Apigee proxy</strong>, identify client apps with <strong>API keys</strong>, and apply a <strong>Quota policy</strong> per tier <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q3\">Exam 6 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Apigee environment isolation by hostname:</strong> attach each environment to its <strong>own environment group</strong> (orders-test → test group, orders-prod → prod group), then add each <strong>hostname to the environment group</strong>. Hostnames belong to environment groups, not to environments <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Apigee policies and API tools</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Not</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>N requests <strong>per day</strong> per client</td>\n<td><strong>Quota</strong> policy</td>\n<td>SpikeArrest (it smooths bursts and isn't a daily quota)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q3\">Exam 6 · Q3</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identify client apps</td>\n<td><strong>API keys</strong></td>\n<td>One cluster or project per tier</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q3\">Exam 6 · Q3</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auth + quotas + metrics in front of an app</td>\n<td><strong>Cloud Endpoints</strong> / API management</td>\n<td>IAP (user identity), GKE Ingress (routing only), Cloud Run (a runtime)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q55\">Exam 1 · Q55</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serve v1 and v2 side by side</td>\n<td>Two backends + <strong>versioned API in Endpoints</strong></td>\n<td>LB split, one function with a version switch</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Duplicating infrastructure per tier (two GKE clusters, two projects with their own load balancers) is never the recommended way to implement quotas <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q3\">Exam 6 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Putting both Apigee environments in one environment group lets both hostnames reach both environments <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"An API management layer sits in front of backends to authenticate callers, enforce quotas, collect metrics and version APIs. The bank uses Cloud Endpoints for versioning and gateway features, and Apigee for per-tier quotas and hostname routing by environment group.","objectives":["Select an API management layer when a question asks for authentication, quotas and metrics","Expose backward-incompatible API versions side by side through a gateway","Implement daily per-client limits with the Apigee Quota policy and API keys","Isolate Apigee environments by hostname using environment groups"],"takeaways":["Auth, quota and metrics for API callers point to API management (Cloud Endpoints or Apigee).","Daily request limits per tier use the Apigee Quota policy. SpikeArrest only smooths bursts.","Hostnames attach to Apigee environment groups, with one group per environment for isolation.","For a breaking change, keep v1, deploy v2 separately and version through the gateway."],"questions":["e1q18","e1q55","e4q27","e6q3"]},{"id":"d4l6","num":6,"title":"Pub/Sub messaging patterns","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fan-out:</strong> one topic per event type, with <strong>one subscription per consumer</strong>. Each subscription gets its own copy of every message. New downstream steps add a subscription without changing the publisher <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q42\">Exam 2 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Every message must go to two sinks</strong> (Cloud Storage and BigQuery) with low operational overhead: <strong>one topic, 2 subscriptions, 2 Cloud Run services</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Competing consumers / load sharing:</strong> a pool of audit-service VMs that should split the work uses <strong>one topic + one pull subscription</strong> that all instances share. A subscription per instance would duplicate every message <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Durability against downstream failure:</strong> if a function that sends emails throws 500s, have the sender <strong>publish to Pub/Sub</strong> and let the function consume the topic. Messages persist until processed. A longer timeout, aggressive one-second retries or a Memorystore hand-off don't give you durability <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ordering:</strong> to keep updates per stock symbol in order in an in-memory DB, use a <strong>pull subscription with message ordering enabled and the stock symbol as the ordering key</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Serverless consumer that costs nothing when idle:</strong> a <strong>Cloud Function with a Pub/Sub trigger</strong>. You don't need to also create a pull subscription in code. Compute Engine and GKE cost money even when no messages arrive <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Several processes analyzing logs in near real time:</strong> a <strong>Cloud Logging sink → Pub/Sub topic</strong>, with each process subscribing. Polling the Logging API doesn't scale, and a Cloud Storage sink isn't near real time <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q24\">Exam 4 · Q24</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Subscription design selector</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Goal</th>\n<th>Topology</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Each team or step gets every event</td>\n<td>1 topic, <strong>N subscriptions</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q42\">Exam 2 · Q42</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Same message to Cloud Storage <strong>and</strong> BigQuery</td>\n<td>1 topic, <strong>2 subs</strong>, 2 services</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>N worker instances share the load</td>\n<td>1 topic, <strong>1 pull sub</strong> shared by all</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Per-key ordering</td>\n<td>Pull sub, <strong>ordering key</strong> = entity ID</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Copies or duplicates: a <strong>subscription</strong> is the unit of fan-out. A <strong>topic per publisher instance</strong> makes subscribers listen to everything <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Two subscribers attached to <strong>one</strong> subscription split the messages between them. They don't each get every message <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Per-entity order matters (prices, balances): use an <strong>ordering key</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Avoid losing emails, orders or events\" → put <strong>Pub/Sub</strong> between producer and consumer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Bank explanations disagree on push vs pull for high volume. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a> says push is less suited to large volumes, and <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a> claims the opposite and keys \"push + exactly-once\" (see ⚠️).</li>\n<li>One Cloud Run service writing to both sinks via push couples both writes into one handler <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>A separate topic for each processing step <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q42\">Exam 2 · Q42</a> makes the publisher know about every step.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Pub/Sub decouples producers from consumers. Topic and subscription design decides whether every consumer gets a copy of each message or the consumers share the work. This lesson covers fan-out, competing consumers, ordering keys, durability and log sinks.","objectives":["Design topic and subscription layouts for fan-out versus load sharing","Use ordering keys to keep per-entity updates in order","Introduce Pub/Sub to prevent loss when a downstream consumer fails","Route Cloud Logging entries to several real-time consumers through a Pub/Sub sink"],"takeaways":["One subscription per consumer that needs every message. One shared pull subscription for workers that split the load.","Per-key ordering means a pull subscription with message ordering and an ordering key such as the stock symbol.","To stop losing emails or events, publish to Pub/Sub and let the consumer read from it.","Pay only when messages arrive by using a function with a Pub/Sub trigger.","Multiple real-time log consumers read from a Logging sink to Pub/Sub."],"questions":["e2q9","e2q42","e4q7","e4q13","e4q24","e4q26","e4q44","e4q50"]},{"id":"d4l7","num":7,"title":"Event-driven triggers and orchestration","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Object uploaded → message on a topic, minimal effort:</strong> configure <strong>Cloud Storage Pub/Sub notifications</strong> on the bucket. That needs no code, unlike a function, App Engine app or GKE app that publishes for you <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q47\">Exam 3 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Flexible, team-maintainable triggers that route to Cloud Run:</strong> an <strong>Eventarc trigger</strong> on Cloud Storage events with <strong>Cloud Run as the target</strong>. Pub/Sub → Cloud Build misuses a CI tool as a runtime, and Firebase functions are aimed at Firebase-based apps (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>, see ⚠️ about filename filtering).</li>\n<li><strong>React to resource creation without changing how resources are provisioned:</strong> a <strong>Cloud Audit Logs-based trigger</strong> fires a <strong>Cloud Function</strong> each time a VM is created, and the function adds missing labels. Periodic Cloud Scheduler scripts leave gaps between runs, and <code>gcloud terraform vet</code> only covers Terraform-provisioned resources <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q9\">Exam 6 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Orchestrating Cloud Functions where each output drives the next step, with low latency:</strong> use <strong>Workflows with conditional jumps</strong> (<code>switch</code>/<code>next</code>). <strong>Cloud Composer</strong> (Airflow) is heavier and adds latency. Callbacks are for waiting on external events, not for branching <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Scheduling:</strong> use <strong>Cloud Scheduler</strong> rather than cron on VMs (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q14\">Exam 4 · Q14</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li><strong>Ticket seat reservation:</strong> the bank keys <strong>Pub/Sub + Eventarc → GKE</strong> over Cloud Tasks + Workflows, a Cloud Storage drop or a load balancer + MIG (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q13\">Exam 6 · Q13</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Orchestration vs choreography</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Service</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordered serverless steps with branching on results, low latency</td>\n<td><strong>Workflows</strong> (conditional jumps)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy, long-running ETL DAGs</td>\n<td>Cloud Composer (Airflow)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Broadcast an event to independent consumers</td>\n<td><strong>Pub/Sub</strong> topic + subscriptions</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q42\">Exam 2 · Q42</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Route Google Cloud events to Cloud Run with filters</td>\n<td><strong>Eventarc</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bucket change → message on a topic, no code</td>\n<td><strong>Cloud Storage Pub/Sub notifications</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q47\">Exam 3 · Q47</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Act when someone creates a resource</td>\n<td><strong>Audit-log trigger → function</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q9\">Exam 6 · Q9</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time-based invocation</td>\n<td><strong>Cloud Scheduler</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q14\">Exam 4 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Minimal implementation effort\" + Cloud Storage → Pub/Sub: use the <strong>built-in notification</strong>, not code <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q47\">Exam 3 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Each function's output guides the workflow\": use <strong>Workflows</strong>, not Composer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Automatically correct …\", \"without altering the current process\" → an <strong>event trigger on audit logs</strong>, not a poller <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q9\">Exam 6 · Q9</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Cloud Build is CI/CD, not an application runtime <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>Workflows <strong>callbacks</strong> are a distractor when the branching logic only depends on the step results <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Google Cloud offers several ways to react to events and coordinate steps: Cloud Storage notifications, Eventarc, audit-log triggers, Workflows and Cloud Scheduler. This lesson maps each requirement to the lightest-weight managed option.","objectives":["Publish bucket changes to Pub/Sub with built-in notifications rather than custom code","Route events to Cloud Run with Eventarc triggers","Use audit-log triggers to react to resource creation without changing how resources are provisioned","Orchestrate functions with Workflows conditional jumps instead of Cloud Composer"],"takeaways":["Minimal-effort bucket change to topic means Cloud Storage Pub/Sub notifications.","Flexible event routing to Cloud Run means Eventarc. Cloud Build is not a runtime.","Auto-fix newly created resources with an audit-log trigger and a function, not a periodic poller.","Low-latency branching between function steps means Workflows, not Composer."],"questions":["e2q42","e3q47","e4q1","e4q14","e4q16","e6q9","e6q13"]},{"id":"d4l8","num":8,"title":"Cloud Storage from application code","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signed URLs</strong> give <strong>time-limited access to a single object</strong> to anyone holding the URL, <strong>without a Google account</strong>. Your app authenticates users first and then hands out the URL <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Downloads for a limited period → signed URL. Email attachments, temporary buckets and expiring cookies are all distractors <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Large uploads (2 MB–1 GB) with minimal infrastructure → clients <strong>upload directly to Cloud Storage via signed URLs</strong>, not through your web server, file store or database <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>Share with outsiders with a <strong>customizable expiry</strong> → a signed URL per document. ACLs don't expire, <code>allAuthenticatedUsers</code> Viewer is far too broad, and <strong>signed policy documents</strong> are aimed at uploads <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Request-rate ramp-up:</strong> a cold bucket hit with thousands of requests per second returns 429/5xx. <strong>Ramp up gradually</strong> by throttling clients. Switching to the XML API or relaying error codes doesn't help <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>429/503 handling:</strong> <strong>retry with truncated exponential backoff</strong>, meaning increasing intervals up to a maximum number of attempts. Fixed or decreasing intervals and batch retries are wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Read only some metadata properties:</strong> use the JSON API <strong><code>fields</code> request parameter</strong> on <code>objects.get</code>. <code>patch</code> updates metadata, <code>compose</code> concatenates objects, and <code>copy</code> duplicates them <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Empty \"folder\":</strong> create a zero-byte object whose name ends in a <strong>trailing slash</strong> (<code>user123/</code>). There is no special content type for folders <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q56\">Exam 3 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Copy local files to a bucket from the command line:</strong> <code>gsutil cp LOCAL gs://BUCKET/</code> (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>, see ⚠️ for the current CLI).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Access options for private objects</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Verdict</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Signed URL issued after your app authenticates the user</td>\n<td>✅ Anonymous users, time-limited, per object</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>allUsers</code> / <code>allAuthenticatedUsers</code> + Object Viewer</td>\n<td>❌ Public to the internet</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IAP</td>\n<td>❌ Needs Google identities, gates apps not objects</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud Armor IP allowlist</td>\n<td>❌ IPs are shared or change</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Per-user buckets + per-bucket service accounts handed to clients</td>\n<td>❌ Complex, leaks credentials</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Limited time\", \"external users\", \"no Google account\", \"large upload without servers\" → <strong>signed URL</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Spike on a dormant bucket → <strong>ramp up</strong>. Throttling during peaks → <strong>exponential backoff</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>429 at peak: <strong>backoff</strong> is the immediate fix. A quota increase is slow, caching misses on a cold cache, and a multi-regional class doesn't change request quotas <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Spreading uploads over many buckets isn't the keyed fix for a ramp-up problem <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a> keys \"multipart upload API\" over signed URLs for 500 MB uploads to App Engine, which contradicts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a> (see ⚠️).</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Applications use Cloud Storage for uploads, downloads and sharing. Signed URLs give time-limited access without Google accounts, and request-rate ramp-up and exponential backoff keep calls reliable under load. The lesson also covers metadata retrieval and folder conventions.","objectives":["Use signed URLs for time-limited downloads, direct client uploads and external sharing","Handle 429 and 5xx responses with truncated exponential backoff and gradual ramp-up","Retrieve only selected object metadata with the fields parameter","Create empty folder placeholders with trailing-slash object names"],"takeaways":["Limited time, outside users or large direct uploads all point to signed URLs.","429 or 503 means retry with truncated exponential backoff at increasing intervals.","A cold bucket hit by a sudden spike needs a gradual ramp-up of request rate.","allUsers, IAP or IP allowlists are wrong for private objects served to anonymous users.","Partial metadata uses the fields parameter. An empty folder is a zero-byte object ending in /."],"questions":["e1q15","e1q22","e1q24","e1q48","e2q24","e3q21","e3q32","e3q43","e3q45","e3q56","e6q7"]},{"id":"d4l9","num":9,"title":"Operational databases from code: Spanner, Firestore, SQL","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spanner, latency over consistency:</strong> use <strong>stale reads with single-read methods</strong>. Strong reads and read-only transactions may coordinate with the leader. Read-write transactions are for reads and writes together <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spanner performance from Go (or any language):</strong> use the <strong>Cloud Client Libraries</strong>. They handle connections, retries and errors idiomatically, unlike the generic Google API Client Libraries or a custom gRPC/HTTP client <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spanner credentials from GKE:</strong> use Workload Identity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>. See section 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Firestore (Datastore mode) memory blow-ups on large lists:</strong> paginate with <strong>query cursors</strong>. Integer offsets still scan the skipped entities, adding latency and cost. A bare limit doesn't page through the rest, and more memory only hides the problem <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q57\">Exam 3 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Firestore 429 RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED before a traffic surge:</strong> <strong>optimize queries to cut reads and writes, and retry with exponential backoff</strong>. A quota increase alone or fixed backoff isn't enough <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Your API's write to Datastore failed with a 5xx:</strong> return <strong>HTTP 500</strong> to your caller so the failure is visible. 204 looks like success, and 406 is about content negotiation (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li><strong>SQL: combine two tables with matching columns and remove duplicates:</strong> use <strong><code>UNION</code></strong>. <code>UNION ALL</code> keeps duplicates, and <code>JOIN</code> combines columns, not rows <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q13\">Exam 3 · Q13</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Spanner read types</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Read</th>\n<th>Freshness</th>\n<th>Latency</th>\n<th>Use when</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stale read, single-read method</strong></td>\n<td>Slightly old</td>\n<td>Lowest</td>\n<td>Latency &gt; consistency</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strong read</td>\n<td>Latest</td>\n<td>Higher</td>\n<td>Must see latest data</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Read-only transaction</td>\n<td>Consistent snapshot</td>\n<td>Higher</td>\n<td>Several consistent reads</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Read-write transaction</td>\n<td>Locks</td>\n<td>Highest</td>\n<td>Read-then-write</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Large result sets cause memory or latency problems → use <strong>cursors</strong>, not offsets <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q57\">Exam 3 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li>429 from a database API → <strong>reduce the load + exponential backoff</strong>. \"Fixed backoff\" is always a distractor <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Recommended way to call service X from code\" → <strong>Cloud Client Libraries</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Raising Cloud Run memory treats the symptom <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q57\">Exam 3 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li><code>UNION ALL</code> vs <code>UNION</code>: \"eliminate duplicates\" means <code>UNION</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q13\">Exam 3 · Q13</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Application code must use operational databases efficiently. This lesson covers Spanner read types and client libraries, Firestore pagination and handling 429 errors, how to report storage failures to callers, and basic SQL set operations.","objectives":["Choose stale single reads in Spanner when latency matters more than consistency","Paginate Firestore queries with cursors instead of offsets","Mitigate Firestore RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED errors through query optimization and exponential backoff","Return accurate HTTP status codes when a backend write fails"],"takeaways":["Spanner latency over consistency means stale reads with single-read methods.","Use Cloud Client Libraries rather than generic API clients or custom gRPC.","Large Firestore result sets need cursors. Offsets still scan the skipped entities.","Firestore 429 calls for fewer reads and writes plus exponential backoff, never fixed backoff.","UNION removes duplicates. UNION ALL keeps them."],"questions":["e1q44","e1q50","e2q16","e3q13","e3q57","e4q2","e4q41"]},{"id":"d4l10","num":10,"title":"BigQuery and streaming analytics pipelines","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flat JSON from an HTTP function that many users need to analyze right away:</strong> <strong>stream it into BigQuery</strong>. Datastore is for entity lookups, Cloud SQL isn't built for that analytics load, and one file per request in Cloud Storage makes querying hard <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q38\">Exam 1 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Near real-time SQL analytics at growing scale, managed services only:</strong> <strong>Pub/Sub → Dataflow → BigQuery</strong>. Cloud Storage or Firestore as the sink doesn't give SQL analytics, and self-managed Kafka adds operational load <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>High-volume voting in 30-minute windows, counted and stored for reporting:</strong> <strong>Pub/Sub → Dataflow</strong> (counts plus raw votes) <strong>→ BigQuery</strong>, visualized in Data Studio <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>BigQuery Storage Write API without duplicates:</strong> create a write stream of <strong>committed type</strong> (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a>, see ⚠️ for the explanation's mistakes).</li>\n<li><strong>A user who doesn't care about job duration and mustn't impede critical work:</strong> run their queries as <strong>batch</strong> jobs, which start when resources are free. You don't need a separate project or manual timing, and <code>jobUser</code> alone doesn't set a priority (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li><strong>Query permissions:</strong> Data Viewer + Job User <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Analytics sink selector</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n<th>Pipeline</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Per-request JSON, immediate ad-hoc analytics</td>\n<td>Function → <strong>BigQuery streaming</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q38\">Exam 1 · Q38</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Event stream, near real-time SQL, rapid growth</td>\n<td><strong>Pub/Sub → Dataflow → BigQuery</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High-volume windowed counts + history</td>\n<td><strong>Pub/Sub → Dataflow → BigQuery</strong> + dashboard</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Real-time log analysis by several consumers</td>\n<td><strong>Logging sink → Pub/Sub</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q24\">Exam 4 · Q24</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Analysts\", \"SQL\", \"aggregate\" → land the data in <strong>BigQuery</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q38\">Exam 1 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"High volume stream + transform or count\" → <strong>Dataflow</strong> between Pub/Sub and BigQuery, rather than Cloud Functions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Don't impede other workloads, duration irrelevant\" → <strong>batch priority</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Memorystore as the store for high-volume event capture, or a CSV export to Sheets for reporting, are both distractors <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Building your own dedup layer (Kafka with UUIDs, Pub/Sub + Functions) when the Write API stream type handles it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Analytics workloads land data in BigQuery, usually through Pub/Sub and Dataflow for streams. This lesson covers streaming inserts, Storage Write API stream types, batch query priority and the IAM needed to query.","objectives":["Choose BigQuery as the sink when users need SQL analytics on fresh data","Build a Pub/Sub to Dataflow to BigQuery pipeline for high-volume streams","Avoid duplicates with a committed-type Storage Write API stream","Use batch query priority so low-priority work doesn't impede critical jobs"],"takeaways":["Analysts, SQL and aggregation mean landing the data in BigQuery.","High-volume streams with transformations or counts use Pub/Sub, Dataflow and BigQuery.","Use a committed-type write stream to prevent duplicates with the Storage Write API.","Queries where duration doesn't matter should run at batch priority."],"questions":["e1q38","e3q41","e4q11","e4q17","e4q24","e4q39","e4q43"]},{"id":"d4l11","num":11,"title":"Client tooling, private connectivity and service discovery","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Listing Bigtable, Memorystore and Cloud SQL resources with user-defined filters:</strong> run <code>gcloud bigtable instances list</code>, <code>gcloud redis instances list</code> and <code>gcloud sql databases list</code> <strong>with <code>--filter</code></strong>, so filtering happens at the source. Data-plane APIs (HBase, Redis, MySQL) are for data, not inventory <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Calling Google APIs from code:</strong> prefer <strong>Cloud Client Libraries</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>A VM with no external IP calling the Cloud Storage API:</strong> enable <strong>Private Google Access</strong> on the subnet. Carrier Peering connects on-prem to Google, VPC Peering connects VPCs, and Shared VPC shares networks across projects. None of those give access to Google APIs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Dynamic discovery between Cloud Run services and functions:</strong> use a <strong>Service Directory</strong> namespace. Services register at deploy time and are looked up at runtime. That beats hand-maintained environment variables, naming conventions, querying the Admin APIs, or Consul on a single VM <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Resilient connection to a legacy REST service running in two regional GKE clusters, with probe-based health checks on a separate port:</strong> the key is <strong>Traffic Director with a sidecar proxy</strong>, plus a firewall rule that allows the health checks (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q35\">Exam 4 · Q35</a>, see ⚠️ on where health checks really come from).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"No public IP\" + \"Google API\" → <strong>Private Google Access</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Service registry\", \"register at deploy, query at runtime\" → <strong>Service Directory</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Filter results\" with gcloud → the <strong><code>--filter</code></strong> flag, not filtering in the app <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Peering products (Carrier, VPC) sound like connectivity fixes, but they don't reach Google APIs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Self-managed Consul on one VM is both overhead and a single point of failure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li><code>gcloud dataproc cp</code> doesn't exist, and <code>hadoop fs</code> needs the connector <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Integration also depends on tooling and network paths: gcloud filtering, Cloud Client Libraries, Private Google Access for VMs without external IPs, Service Directory for runtime discovery, and Traffic Director for resilient multi-cluster connections.","objectives":["Filter resource listings at the source with the gcloud --filter flag","Enable Private Google Access so VMs without external IPs can reach Google APIs","Use Service Directory to register and discover services at runtime","Recognize Traffic Director with sidecar proxies for connections to services spread across GKE clusters"],"takeaways":["No external IP plus a Google API means Private Google Access, not any kind of peering.","Register at deploy time and look up at runtime with Service Directory.","Filter gcloud output with --filter instead of filtering in your own code.","Copy local files to a bucket with the Cloud Storage CLI, not gcloud dataproc or hadoop fs."],"questions":["e1q31","e1q48","e2q16","e3q9","e3q28","e4q33","e4q35"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li>Code runs as a <strong>service account</strong>: on a 403, grant a <strong>narrow role on the target resource</strong> to the runtime identity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>On VMs and Cloud Run, use <strong>ADC / metadata server tokens</strong>, never key files, P12s or API keys <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Metadata server: <code>metadata.google.internal</code> + <code>Metadata-Flavor: Google</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q46\">Exam 2 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>GKE → Google APIs = <strong>Workload Identity</strong>: enable on the cluster + bind KSA↔GSA with <code>workloadIdentityUser</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>Users' Drive = <strong>OAuth client ID + <code>drive.file</code> scope + per-user token</strong>. No domain-wide delegation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li>OAuth flow: consent → <strong>auth code → exchange for access token</strong> → call API <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Service-to-service: <strong>Invoker</strong> role on the caller's service account. IAP is for users <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>CORS: <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> = the <strong>exact page origin</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>Auth + quotas + metrics → <strong>API management</strong> (Endpoints). Daily tier limits → <strong>Apigee Quota policy + API keys</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q55\">Exam 1 · Q55</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q3\">Exam 6 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Apigee: <strong>hostnames go on environment groups</strong>, one group per environment <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>Breaking API change: keep v1, deploy v2, <strong>version through the gateway</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Pub/Sub fan-out = <strong>one subscription per consumer</strong>. Load sharing = <strong>one shared pull subscription</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q42\">Exam 2 · Q42</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Per-key order → <strong>ordering key</strong> on a pull subscription <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>Don't lose work when a consumer fails → <strong>put Pub/Sub in between</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Pay only when messages arrive → <strong>function with a Pub/Sub trigger</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Bucket change → topic, no code: <strong>Cloud Storage Pub/Sub notifications</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q47\">Exam 3 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>Flexible event routing to Cloud Run → <strong>Eventarc</strong>. React to resource creation → <strong>audit-log trigger</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q9\">Exam 6 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Branching orchestration of functions → <strong>Workflows conditional jumps</strong>, not Composer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Temporary or external object access, big direct uploads → <strong>signed URLs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>429/503 → <strong>truncated exponential backoff</strong>. Cold bucket spike → <strong>ramp up gradually</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Partial metadata → <code>fields</code> parameter. Empty folder → object name ending in <code>/</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q56\">Exam 3 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li>Spanner latency first → <strong>stale single reads</strong>. Use Cloud Client Libraries <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>Firestore big lists → <strong>cursors</strong>. Firestore 429 → <strong>optimize + exponential backoff</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q57\">Exam 3 · Q57</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Analytics: <strong>Pub/Sub → Dataflow → BigQuery</strong>. Dedup with the Write API → <strong>committed stream</strong>. Low-priority queries → <strong>batch</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>No external IP → <strong>Private Google Access</strong>. Runtime discovery → <strong>Service Directory</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d4l1","title":"Service identity, Application Default Credentials and the metadata server","html":"<ul>\n<li>403 from code calling an API: find the <strong>runtime identity</strong>, then grant the <strong>least-privileged role on the target resource</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Securely distribute credentials to VMs\": the answer is <strong>don't</strong>. Use the attached service account with ADC <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>Need project ID or region at runtime on Cloud Run: query the <strong>metadata server</strong>, not console-copied environment variables or the Cloud Asset Inventory API <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q46\">Exam 2 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery permission error when running queries: <strong>Data Viewer + Job User</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l2","title":"Workload Identity on GKE","html":"<ul>\n<li>Any \"GKE + Google API + best practice\" question: pick <strong>Workload Identity</strong>. For \"choose two\", pick <strong>enable on cluster + bind KSA↔GSA</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Words like \"auto-rotating\", \"one-time setup\" and \"no keys\" all point to Workload Identity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l3","title":"OAuth 2.0 for accessing users' data","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Users' Drive\", \"on behalf of users\", \"without domain restrictions\" → <strong>OAuth client ID + per-user token + minimal scope</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Domain-wide delegation</strong> (with an OAuth client or a service account) gives access to the whole Workspace domain. That is excessive and doesn't fit users outside your domain <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li>The runtime doesn't change the answer. The same OAuth answer applies on Compute Engine or any other Google Cloud runtime <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l4","title":"Securing invocations between services","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Invoke\" means <strong>Invoker</strong> role (<code>roles/cloudfunctions.invoker</code> style roles), granted to the <strong>calling</strong> service account <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>Restricting browser callers means CORS with the <strong>exact origin of the page</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l6","title":"Pub/Sub messaging patterns","html":"<ul>\n<li>Copies or duplicates: a <strong>subscription</strong> is the unit of fan-out. A <strong>topic per publisher instance</strong> makes subscribers listen to everything <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Two subscribers attached to <strong>one</strong> subscription split the messages between them. They don't each get every message <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Per-entity order matters (prices, balances): use an <strong>ordering key</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Avoid losing emails, orders or events\" → put <strong>Pub/Sub</strong> between producer and consumer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l7","title":"Event-driven triggers and orchestration","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Minimal implementation effort\" + Cloud Storage → Pub/Sub: use the <strong>built-in notification</strong>, not code <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q47\">Exam 3 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Each function's output guides the workflow\": use <strong>Workflows</strong>, not Composer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Automatically correct …\", \"without altering the current process\" → an <strong>event trigger on audit logs</strong>, not a poller <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q9\">Exam 6 · Q9</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l8","title":"Cloud Storage from application code","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Limited time\", \"external users\", \"no Google account\", \"large upload without servers\" → <strong>signed URL</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Spike on a dormant bucket → <strong>ramp up</strong>. Throttling during peaks → <strong>exponential backoff</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>429 at peak: <strong>backoff</strong> is the immediate fix. A quota increase is slow, caching misses on a cold cache, and a multi-regional class doesn't change request quotas <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l9","title":"Operational databases from code: Spanner, Firestore, SQL","html":"<ul>\n<li>Large result sets cause memory or latency problems → use <strong>cursors</strong>, not offsets <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q57\">Exam 3 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li>429 from a database API → <strong>reduce the load + exponential backoff</strong>. \"Fixed backoff\" is always a distractor <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Recommended way to call service X from code\" → <strong>Cloud Client Libraries</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l10","title":"BigQuery and streaming analytics pipelines","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Analysts\", \"SQL\", \"aggregate\" → land the data in <strong>BigQuery</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q38\">Exam 1 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"High volume stream + transform or count\" → <strong>Dataflow</strong> between Pub/Sub and BigQuery, rather than Cloud Functions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Don't impede other workloads, duration irrelevant\" → <strong>batch priority</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l11","title":"Client tooling, private connectivity and service discovery","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"No public IP\" + \"Google API\" → <strong>Private Google Access</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Service registry\", \"register at deploy, query at runtime\" → <strong>Service Directory</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Filter results\" with gcloud → the <strong><code>--filter</code></strong> flag, not filtering in the app <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d4l1","title":"Service identity, Application Default Credentials and the metadata server","html":"<ul>\n<li>Options that use a service account <strong>from the other project</strong> (project B's account used on project A's VMs) create a reverse dependency <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Use ADC with the private key of a service account\" sounds modern, but it still means handling a key file <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>Creating a view or copying the table to a new dataset doesn't fix a permission error <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l2","title":"Workload Identity on GKE","html":"<ul>\n<li>Anything that mentions a <strong>service account key</strong>, however well protected, is a distractor <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l3","title":"OAuth 2.0 for accessing users' data","html":"<ul>\n<li>An option that stops at \"receive an authorization code, store it in cookies and use it to authenticate\" is missing the <strong>code-for-token exchange</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Service account + <code>drive.file</code> scope + signed JWT\" has the right scope on the wrong identity, since a service account isn't the user <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li>Collecting usernames and passwords, hashed or forwarded, is always wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l4","title":"Securing invocations between services","html":"<ul>\n<li>Viewer vs Invoker: if a role name doesn't say \"Invoker\", it can't invoke <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>The function's own domain in <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> blocks the real callers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l5","title":"API management: Cloud Endpoints and Apigee","html":"<ul>\n<li>Duplicating infrastructure per tier (two GKE clusters, two projects with their own load balancers) is never the recommended way to implement quotas <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q3\">Exam 6 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Putting both Apigee environments in one environment group lets both hostnames reach both environments <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l6","title":"Pub/Sub messaging patterns","html":"<ul>\n<li>Bank explanations disagree on push vs pull for high volume. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a> says push is less suited to large volumes, and <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a> claims the opposite and keys \"push + exactly-once\" (see ⚠️).</li>\n<li>One Cloud Run service writing to both sinks via push couples both writes into one handler <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>A separate topic for each processing step <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q42\">Exam 2 · Q42</a> makes the publisher know about every step.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l7","title":"Event-driven triggers and orchestration","html":"<ul>\n<li>Cloud Build is CI/CD, not an application runtime <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>Workflows <strong>callbacks</strong> are a distractor when the branching logic only depends on the step results <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l8","title":"Cloud Storage from application code","html":"<ul>\n<li>Spreading uploads over many buckets isn't the keyed fix for a ramp-up problem <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a> keys \"multipart upload API\" over signed URLs for 500 MB uploads to App Engine, which contradicts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a> (see ⚠️).</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l9","title":"Operational databases from code: Spanner, Firestore, SQL","html":"<ul>\n<li>Raising Cloud Run memory treats the symptom <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q57\">Exam 3 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li><code>UNION ALL</code> vs <code>UNION</code>: \"eliminate duplicates\" means <code>UNION</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q13\">Exam 3 · Q13</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l10","title":"BigQuery and streaming analytics pipelines","html":"<ul>\n<li>Memorystore as the store for high-volume event capture, or a CSV export to Sheets for reporting, are both distractors <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Building your own dedup layer (Kafka with UUIDs, Pub/Sub + Functions) when the Write API stream type handles it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l11","title":"Client tooling, private connectivity and service discovery","html":"<ul>\n<li>Peering products (Carrier, VPC) sound like connectivity fixes, but they don't reach Google APIs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Self-managed Consul on one VM is both overhead and a single point of failure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li><code>gcloud dataproc cp</code> doesn't exist, and <code>hadoop fs</code> needs the connector <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Issue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys <strong>push + exactly-once delivery</strong> and says push suits high volume better. Pub/Sub exactly-once delivery is a <strong>pull</strong>-subscription feature, and <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a> says push is <em>less</em> suited to large volumes. The same scenario in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a> keys <strong>pull + ordering key</strong>. Expect the pull answer to be the defensible one.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a></strong></td>\n<td><code>service-PROJECTA@gcf-admin-robot.iam.gserviceaccount.com</code> is the Cloud Functions <strong>service agent</strong> (used by Google to manage the function), not the <strong>runtime</strong> service account your code executes as. The principle (grant <code>objectCreator</code> on the bucket to the function's identity) is right, but in practice the grant goes to the function's runtime service account.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys \"multipart upload API\" for 500 MB uploads to App Engine and rejects signed URLs as \"unnecessary complexity\". App Engine request sizes are limited far below 500 MB, and Google's pattern (taught in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>) is direct-to-Cloud Storage uploads via <strong>signed URLs</strong>. Learn the key, but expect signed URLs elsewhere.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e6q13\">Exam 6 · Q13</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys asynchronous Pub/Sub + Eventarc for \"<strong>immediate confirmation</strong> of a specific seat\". Publishing only confirms that the message was received, not that the seat was reserved. The rejection of the load-balancer option (\"relies on polling the load balancer\") is technically wrong.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a></strong></td>\n<td>The answer (committed type) is right, but the explanation says the default stream is \"buffered\". The default stream gives at-least-once semantics, and buffered is a separate type. Committed-type streams avoid duplicates through <strong>stream offsets</strong>, not some automatic dedup.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q14\">Exam 4 · Q14</a></strong></td>\n<td>A 403 calling Cloud Run usually means the caller lacks <strong><code>roles/run.invoker</code></strong>. The explanation says Cloud Run \"subscribes\" to Pub/Sub, but delivery is a push subscription to the service. Treat the key as \"use managed Scheduler, not VM cron or loosened ingress\".</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a></strong></td>\n<td>The explanation says Eventarc Cloud Storage triggers filter by object name and that Cloud Functions can't. Direct Cloud Storage events filter by bucket, and Cloud Functions (2nd gen) triggers are themselves Eventarc triggers. Learn \"Eventarc → Cloud Run for flexible triggers\" but not the filtering claims.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q35\">Exam 4 · Q35</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys health checks \"initiated from the sidecar proxy\". For Traffic Director (now Cloud Service Mesh), backend health checks come from <strong>Google Cloud health-check probers</strong>, and firewall rules normally allow those ranges. Know the key, but it's shaky.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a></strong></td>\n<td>The answer (batch queries) is fine, but the explanation says batch jobs are \"less expensive\". Batch priority changes scheduling, not on-demand price, and the cited link is for Google Cloud <em>Batch</em>, a different product.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys C (return 500), but its own explanation recommends \"C <strong>and</strong> D\" (retry with backoff, then report). In practice, retrying transient 5xx with bounded backoff and then surfacing a 5xx is best. Pick C if you must pick one.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a></strong></td>\n<td>Option V describes itself as \"not as endorsed\" yet is keyed correct. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a> writes the role as <code>roles/iam.workloadIdentity</code>, but the real role is <strong><code>roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser</code></strong>. The feature is now called <em>Workload Identity Federation for GKE</em>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a></strong></td>\n<td><code>gsutil</code> is legacy. Google now recommends <strong><code>gcloud storage cp</code></strong>. The explanation also wrongly says <code>gcloud</code> has a general <code>cp</code> command.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e2q46\">Exam 2 · Q46</a></strong></td>\n<td>The explanation says to read the region from <code>instance/zone</code>. Cloud Run's metadata server exposes <code>instance/region</code> for that.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e1q55\">Exam 1 · Q55</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gpcde/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a></strong></td>\n<td>Older product framing. Cloud Endpoints still exists, but API Gateway and Apigee are the current API-management options. Data Studio is now <strong>Looker Studio</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},"glossary":[{"term":"Access-Control-Allow-Origin","definition":"CORS response header that tells the browser which origin may read the response to a cross-origin request.","exam_context":"Set it to the exact page origin (https://www.example.com), not *, a wildcard subdomain or the function's own domain.","lesson":"d4l4","domain":4},{"term":"Apigee environment group","definition":"A set of Apigee environments that share a list of hostnames. Requests are routed by hostname to the environments attached to the group.","exam_context":"To make each environment reachable only through its own URL, put each environment in its own group and add the hostname to that group.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Apigee Quota policy","definition":"An Apigee policy that caps how many requests a client, usually identified by API key, can make in a time interval such as a day.","exam_context":"It's the answer for per-tier daily limits. SpikeArrest is the distractor because it only smooths bursts.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Application Default Credentials (ADC)","definition":"The strategy client libraries use to find credentials automatically, such as the service account attached to the runtime.","exam_context":"The recommended way for VM-hosted apps to authenticate, instead of distributing JSON or P12 keys.","lesson":"d4l1","domain":4},{"term":"BigQuery batch query","definition":"A query run at batch priority. It is queued and starts when resources are available, rather than immediately.","exam_context":"For a user who doesn't care about duration but mustn't impede important workloads.","lesson":"d4l10","domain":4},{"term":"BigQuery Job User","definition":"IAM role that lets a principal create jobs, including query jobs, in a project.","exam_context":"Combined with Data Viewer, it fixes permission errors when a service account runs queries through the CLI.","lesson":"d4l1","domain":4},{"term":"BigQuery Storage Write API","definition":"A high-throughput API for streaming rows into BigQuery through write streams of different types.","exam_context":"Create a committed-type stream to avoid duplicate rows without building your own dedup layer.","lesson":"d4l10","domain":4},{"term":"Cloud Client Libraries","definition":"Google's idiomatic, per-language libraries for Google Cloud services. They handle connections, retries and authentication.","exam_context":"The recommended choice over generic Google API Client Libraries or custom gRPC/HTTP clients, for example with Spanner from Go.","lesson":"d4l9","domain":4},{"term":"Cloud Endpoints","definition":"An API management service that adds authentication, quotas, monitoring and versioning in front of backends.","exam_context":"The answer when an architecture needs auth, quotas and metrics for API callers, or a versioned API over two Cloud Functions.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Cloud Functions Invoker","definition":"IAM role that grants permission to call (invoke) a Cloud Function.","exam_context":"Grant it to the calling service account so only authorized services can invoke a sensitive function. Viewer can't invoke.","lesson":"d4l4","domain":4},{"term":"Cloud Scheduler","definition":"Managed cron service that triggers HTTP targets or Pub/Sub topics on a schedule.","exam_context":"Preferred over cron on VMs for scheduled invocations of Cloud Run.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Cloud Storage Pub/Sub notifications","definition":"A bucket setting that publishes a message to a Pub/Sub topic when objects change.","exam_context":"The minimal-effort answer for publishing a message whenever a report is uploaded, with no code to write.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Cursor (Firestore/Datastore)","definition":"An opaque position marker returned by a query, used to start the next page where the last one ended.","exam_context":"Use cursors rather than offsets or more memory to stop memory blow-ups when listing many entities.","lesson":"d4l9","domain":4},{"term":"Dataflow","definition":"Managed Apache Beam service for batch and streaming data processing.","exam_context":"The middle of Pub/Sub → Dataflow → BigQuery pipelines for near real-time SQL analytics and windowed counts.","lesson":"d4l10","domain":4},{"term":"Domain-wide delegation","definition":"Workspace feature that lets an app or service account impersonate any user in a domain.","exam_context":"A distractor for accessing users' Drive files, because it's excessive and doesn't cover users outside the domain.","lesson":"d4l3","domain":4},{"term":"drive.file scope","definition":"The OAuth scope https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file, which limits an app to files it creates or opens.","exam_context":"Pair it with an OAuth client ID to get a per-user access token for saving files to users' Drives.","lesson":"d4l3","domain":4},{"term":"Eventarc","definition":"Service that routes events from Google Cloud sources, audit logs and Pub/Sub to targets such as Cloud Run, using triggers.","exam_context":"Keyed for flexible, team-maintainable Cloud Storage triggers targeting Cloud Run.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Exponential backoff","definition":"A retry strategy that waits progressively longer between attempts, usually truncated at a maximum delay or attempt count.","exam_context":"The answer for 429 and 503 errors from Cloud Storage or Firestore. Fixed or decreasing intervals are wrong.","lesson":"d4l8","domain":4},{"term":"fields request parameter","definition":"A JSON API parameter that returns only the requested properties of a resource.","exam_context":"Retrieve only specific object metadata quickly, rather than using patch, compose or copy.","lesson":"d4l8","domain":4},{"term":"Metadata server","definition":"An endpoint inside each instance (metadata.google.internal, header Metadata-Flavor: Google) that serves project and instance metadata and access tokens.","exam_context":"Read the project ID and region on Cloud Run, or get a token for raw REST calls when no client library exists.","lesson":"d4l1","domain":4},{"term":"OAuth 2.0 authorization-code flow","definition":"Flow in which the user consents on Google's screen, the app receives an authorization code, and the app exchanges the code for an access token.","exam_context":"The correct option includes the code-for-token exchange. Options that use the code directly or collect passwords are wrong.","lesson":"d4l3","domain":4},{"term":"OAuth client ID","definition":"Credentials that identify an application in OAuth 2.0 flows, used to obtain user-authorized access tokens.","exam_context":"Required when a web app accesses users' Google Drive. API keys, service accounts and SAML are distractors.","lesson":"d4l3","domain":4},{"term":"Ordering key","definition":"A Pub/Sub message attribute. On a subscription with ordering enabled, messages that share the key are delivered in publish order.","exam_context":"Use the stock symbol as the ordering key so price updates per symbol apply in order.","lesson":"d4l6","domain":4},{"term":"Private Google Access","definition":"A subnet setting that lets VMs with only internal IPs reach Google APIs and services.","exam_context":"The answer for a VM without an external IP calling Cloud Storage, rather than any peering option.","lesson":"d4l11","domain":4},{"term":"Pull subscription","definition":"A Pub/Sub subscription where subscribers request messages. Several subscribers on one subscription share the messages.","exam_context":"One shared pull subscription lets a pool of workers split the load without duplicates.","lesson":"d4l6","domain":4},{"term":"Request rate ramp-up","definition":"Cloud Storage guidance to increase request rates on a bucket gradually so the service can scale its capacity.","exam_context":"The fix for 429/5xx errors when a dormant bucket suddenly gets thousands of requests per second.","lesson":"d4l8","domain":4},{"term":"Service Directory","definition":"Managed service registry where services register endpoints and clients look them up at runtime.","exam_context":"Replaces hand-maintained environment variables for inter-service discovery between Cloud Run and Cloud Functions.","lesson":"d4l11","domain":4},{"term":"Signed URL","definition":"A URL that carries a cryptographic signature and expiry, granting time-limited access to a specific Cloud Storage object to anyone holding it.","exam_context":"The answer for temporary downloads, direct large uploads, anonymous users and sharing with outsiders with a custom expiry.","lesson":"d4l8","domain":4},{"term":"Stale read (Spanner)","definition":"A Spanner read at a past timestamp that can be served without leader coordination, which lowers latency.","exam_context":"Pick stale reads with single-read methods when latency matters more than consistency.","lesson":"d4l9","domain":4},{"term":"Subscription fan-out","definition":"Attaching several subscriptions to one topic so that each subscription receives its own copy of every message.","exam_context":"One topic with a subscription per downstream team or sink, as in timesheet steps or Cloud Storage + BigQuery writers.","lesson":"d4l6","domain":4},{"term":"Traffic Director","definition":"Google's managed control plane for service mesh traffic. It configures sidecar or proxyless clients and is now part of Cloud Service Mesh.","exam_context":"Keyed with a sidecar proxy for a resilient connection to a legacy service spread across two regional GKE clusters.","lesson":"d4l11","domain":4},{"term":"UNION","definition":"A SQL set operator that appends the rows of two queries and removes duplicate rows.","exam_context":"Pick UNION over UNION ALL (keeps duplicates) or JOIN (combines columns) to merge two matching tables without duplicates.","lesson":"d4l9","domain":4},{"term":"Workflows","definition":"Serverless orchestration service that runs ordered steps calling services and APIs, with conditions and branching.","exam_context":"Use conditional jumps in Workflows, not Cloud Composer, for low-latency orchestration of Cloud Functions.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Workload Identity","definition":"GKE feature that lets a Kubernetes service account act as a Google service account, bound with roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser.","exam_context":"The recommended answer whenever GKE workloads need Google Cloud API access, instead of keys, Secrets or node service accounts.","lesson":"d4l2","domain":4}]}],"questions":{"e1q1":{"id":"e1q1","exam":1,"n":1,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>What approach should you take to implement a custom step in Cloud Build that ensures a copyright comment is present at the beginning of all source files, triggered by each source commit, and adds one if it's missing?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Develop a new Docker container to inspect files in /workspace, validating and appending a copyright comment to each source file. There's no requirement to commit the modified files back to the source repository.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Develop a new Docker container to inspect files in a Cloud Storage bucket, validating and appending a copyright comment to each source file. Modified files are explicitly committed back to the source repository.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Develop a new Docker container to analyze files in /workspace, verifying and adding a copyright comment to each source file. Any modified files are directly committed back to the source repository.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Develop a new Docker container to inspect files in a Cloud Storage bucket, verifying and appending a copyright comment to each source file. Any changed files are written back to the Cloud Storage bucket.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Develop a new Docker container to analyze files in /workspace, verifying and adding a copyright comment to each source file. Any modified files are directly committed back to the source repository.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this stands out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Integration with Source Control:</strong> Directly committing changes back to the repository ensures the copyright comment exists in the canonical source code, simplifying collaboration and preventing future inconsistencies.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>/workspace Context:</strong> Cloud Build provides the <code>/workspace</code> directory, which contains the current state of your source repository. This aligns seamlessly with your Docker container's file analysis.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Trigger Efficiency:</strong> Triggering the build step on each source commit guarantees that the copyright comment is always present.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. No Commit:</strong> Without committing changes, your source code wouldn't reflect the updated copyright comments, leading to potential inaccuracies.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III &amp; IV. Cloud Storage Bucket:</strong> While you could temporarily use a Cloud Storage bucket, it introduces unnecessary overhead compared to the direct <code>/workspace</code> approach and potential syncing complications if multiple builds run concurrently.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>References:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/configuring-builds/pass-data-between-steps#passing_data_using_workspaces\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/configuring-builds/pass-data-between-steps#passing_data_using_workspaces</a></p>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e1q2":{"id":"e1q2","exam":1,"n":2,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Which storage solution would best fulfill the requirements of transferring log data to a Cloud Storage bucket daily, allowing authenticated users to review logs from the prior two weeks for critical events and enabling annual reviews by an external auditor while minimizing costs? Choose two options from the following:</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Establish a lifecycle management policy to transition the storage class to Coldline for objects older than 14 days.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Generate a JSON Web Token (JWT) for users necessitating access to the Coldline storage buckets.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>V. Implement a lifecycle management policy to transition the storage class to Nearline for objects older than 14 days.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Implement the Bucket Lock feature to enforce a retention policy on the data.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize a scheduled job to transition the storage class to Coldline for objects older than 14 days.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The two storage solutions that would best fulfill the requirements of transferring log data to a Cloud Storage bucket daily, allowing authenticated users to review logs from the prior two weeks for critical events and enabling annual reviews by an external auditor while minimizing costs are:</p>\n<p><strong>I. Implement the Bucket Lock feature to enforce a retention policy on the data.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Establish a lifecycle management policy to transition the storage class to Coldline for objects older than 14 days.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Option I suggests implementing the Bucket Lock feature to enforce a retention policy on the data. This ensures that the log data cannot be modified or deleted for a specified period, which aligns with the requirement for allowing authenticated users to review logs from the prior two weeks and enabling annual reviews by an external auditor.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option IV proposes establishing a lifecycle management policy to transition the storage class to Coldline for objects older than 14 days. This allows for cost-effective storage of older log data while ensuring accessibility for the required duration and minimizing costs by moving data to a lower-cost storage class after the two-week review period.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option II suggests transitioning the storage class to Coldline for objects older than 14 days, which aligns with the requirement for minimizing costs but does not address the requirement for authenticated users to review logs or enable annual reviews by an external auditor.</p>\n<p>Option III mentions generating a JSON Web Token (JWT) for users necessitating access to the Coldline storage buckets, which addresses access control but does not directly fulfill the requirements related to data storage and management.</p>\n<p>Option V proposes transitioning the storage class to Nearline for objects older than 14 days, which might reduce costs but does not ensure that the data remains accessible for the required duration or address the need for annual reviews by an external auditor.</p>\n<p>Therefore, Options I and IV are the most suitable choices for fulfilling the specified requirements.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/bucket-lock  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/lifecycle\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/bucket-lock</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/bucket-lock  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/lifecycle\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/lifecycle</a></p>","lessons":["d1l5"]},"e1q3":{"id":"e1q3","exam":1,"n":3,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>How should you set up the unit testing process for Cloud Function code stored in a Cloud Source Repositories repository, ensuring deployment only occurs after passing the tests?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Cloud Build to perform unit tests, employing the specific service account for building; upon test success, Cloud Build automatically deploys the Cloud Function.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Set up Cloud Build to handle Cloud Function deployment, with deployment approval triggered after passing tests.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Implement Cloud Build to execute unit tests; upon successful testing, developers can proceed with Cloud Function deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Employ Cloud Build for deploying the Cloud Function, utilizing the specific service account for the build process, and executing unit tests post-deployment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The correct option is</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV: Utilize Cloud Build to perform unit tests, employing the specific service account for building; upon test success, Cloud Build automatically deploys the Cloud Function.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Here's why:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Automation &amp; Efficiency:</strong> This option streamlines testing and deployment into a single integrated process, saving time and effort.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Enforced Testing:</strong> Guarantees code quality because deployment happens <em>only</em> after tests pass. This is critical for preventing broken code from reaching production.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Build:</strong> This Google Cloud service is ideal for building, testing, and deploying cloud-native applications, including Cloud Functions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Service Account:</strong> Using a specific service account with deployment permissions ensures controlled access and security.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Here's why the other options aren't as suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Set up Cloud Build to handle Cloud Function deployment, with deployment approval triggered after passing tests.</strong> While correct in ensuring that tests run, this option introduces a manual approval step. This can slow down deployment and might be unnecessary in many scenarios.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Employ Cloud Build for deploying the Cloud Function, utilizing the specific service account for the build process, and executing unit tests post-deployment.</strong> This approach is problematic because it deploys potentially untested code. Executing tests after deployment could reveal issues, necessitating rollbacks and adding complexity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Implement Cloud Build to execute unit tests; upon successful testing, developers can proceed with Cloud Function deployment.</strong> This option places the deployment burden on developers and is less efficient than automated deployment through Cloud Build. It risks inconsistent deployment procedures as different developers handle this step.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e1q4":{"id":"e1q4","exam":1,"n":4,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Upon encountering HTTP request failures between two Pods directed at specific URLs, while other requests between the pods to different URLs are successful in your microservices application on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) utilizing Istio, what is the probable cause of the connection issue?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. If the cluster is configured with mTLS in permissive mode but the Pod's sidecar proxy is transmitting unencrypted traffic in plain text, it could lead to connection failures for specific URLs, particularly if mTLS enforcement is mandated for those URLs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. It's plausible that the Pod initiating the HTTP requests is trying to connect to the target Pod via an incorrect TCP port, resulting in failed communication for specific URLs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. The connection issue might stem from a Kubernetes NetworkPolicy resource, which could be limiting HTTP traffic between the Pods, potentially obstructing communication to specific URLs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. The observed connection failures for certain URLs could be attributable to the AuthorizationPolicy of your cluster being configured to block HTTP requests for specific paths within your application.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>The probable cause of the connection issue between two Pods directed at specific URLs in your microservices application on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) utilizing Istio is:</p>\n<p><strong>III. The observed connection failures for certain URLs could be attributable to the AuthorizationPolicy of your cluster being configured to block HTTP requests for specific paths within your application.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Option I suggests that the connection issue might be caused by a Kubernetes NetworkPolicy resource limiting HTTP traffic between the Pods. While NetworkPolicy could potentially cause connectivity problems, it typically applies to all traffic, not specific URLs, making it less likely to be the cause of failures for certain URLs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option II implies that the failure might be due to incorrect TCP port configuration. However, if other requests between the pods to different URLs are successful, this is less likely to be the cause, as it would likely affect all requests.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option III points to the AuthorizationPolicy of the cluster being configured to block HTTP requests for specific paths within the application. This is a plausible cause, as an AuthorizationPolicy can indeed restrict access to certain URLs based on specific criteria.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option IV mentions the potential mismatch between mTLS configuration and the Pod's sidecar proxy transmitting unencrypted traffic. While this could lead to connection failures, it's less likely if other requests between the pods are successful, as mTLS enforcement would generally apply to all traffic equally.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, Option III is the most probable cause of the observed connection failures for specific URLs in this scenario.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/security/authorization/authz-http/  https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/  https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/security/authentication/mtls-migration/\">https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/security/authorization/authz-http/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/security/authorization/authz-http/  https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/  https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/security/authentication/mtls-migration/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies0</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/security/authorization/authz-http/  https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/  https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/security/authentication/mtls-migration/\">https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/security/authentication/mtls-migration/</a></p>","lessons":["d3l10"]},"e1q5":{"id":"e1q5","exam":1,"n":5,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>How should you securely manage session and shopping cart data for your ecommerce platform while adhering to Google's recommended standards?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Utilize advanced techniques such as storing session and cart data in local memory and enabling cookie-based session affinity within a global external HTTP(S) load balancer, ensuring optimal performance and security.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement a comprehensive approach by utilizing Memorystore for Redis to securely store session data and Firestore for the shopping cart information, ensuring data integrity and scalability for your ecommerce platform.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Opt for BigQuery as the storage solution for both session and shopping cart information, leveraging its scalability and analytical capabilities for robust data management.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Employ Cloud Storage to store the shopping cart details, associating each cart with a unique session identifier, thus facilitating efficient retrieval and management of user data.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The most suitable approach for securely managing session and shopping cart data for your ecommerce platform while adhering to Google's recommended standards is:</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Implement a comprehensive approach by utilizing Memorystore for Redis to securely store session data and Firestore for the shopping cart information, ensuring data integrity and scalability for your ecommerce platform.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Option IV suggests a comprehensive approach by leveraging Memorystore for Redis to securely store session data and Firestore for shopping cart information. This approach aligns with Google's recommended standards as it provides a secure, scalable, and managed solution for both session and shopping cart data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option I mentions storing session and cart data in local memory and enabling cookie-based session affinity within a global external HTTP(S) load balancer. While this might offer performance benefits, it may not provide the same level of security and scalability as managed services like Memorystore for Redis and Firestore.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option II proposes using Cloud Storage to store shopping cart details, which may not be the most suitable solution for managing session data due to its eventual consistency and lack of real-time querying capabilities.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option III suggests using BigQuery for both session and shopping cart information. While BigQuery is powerful for analytical purposes, it may not be the most optimal solution for real-time data retrieval and management of session and shopping cart data.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, Option IV provides a balanced and recommended approach for securely managing session and shopping cart data while ensuring scalability and data integrity for your ecommerce platform.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/memorystore\">https://cloud.google.com/memorystore</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/datastore/docs\">https://cloud.google.com/datastore/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d1l9"]},"e1q6":{"id":"e1q6","exam":1,"n":6,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You are preparing to deploy a microservices application on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) that will undergo daily updates. With a large number of distinct containers running on the Linux operating system (OS), you aim to stay informed about any known OS vulnerabilities in the new containers. Prioritizing Google's recommended best practices, what is the most suitable approach for achieving this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Enable Container Analysis, store new container images in Artifact Registry, and specifically review critical vulnerability results before each deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize the Container Analysis REST API to invoke Container Analysis for scanning new container images, then review the vulnerability results before each deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Enable Container Analysis and store new container images in Artifact Registry, subsequently reviewing the vulnerability results before each deployment.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize the gcloud CLI to invoke Container Analysis for scanning new container images and review the vulnerability results before each deployment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Enable Container Analysis and store new container images in Artifact Registry, subsequently reviewing the vulnerability results before each deployment.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Option II is the most suitable approach for achieving the goal of staying informed about any known OS vulnerabilities in the new containers while prioritizing Google's recommended best practices. Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Enable Container Analysis</strong> : By enabling Container Analysis, you ensure that each container image pushed to Artifact Registry is automatically scanned for vulnerabilities. This allows you to proactively identify any potential security issues.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Store new container images in Artifact Registry</strong> : Storing container images in Artifact Registry provides a centralized location for managing and storing your images securely. It integrates seamlessly with Container Analysis, allowing vulnerability scanning to be performed automatically on each image upload.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Review the vulnerability results before each deployment</strong> : Before deploying a new container image, it's crucial to review the vulnerability results generated by Container Analysis. This ensures that you are aware of any known vulnerabilities present in the image and can take appropriate actions, such as patching or updating dependencies, before deployment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Options I, III, and IV also involve utilizing Container Analysis for scanning container images, but they either lack the integration with Artifact Registry (option I) or do not emphasize the importance of reviewing vulnerability results before each deployment (options III and IV).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Therefore, option II aligns with Google's recommended best practices and provides a comprehensive approach to staying informed about OS vulnerabilities in container images before deployment on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/analysis\">https://cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/analysis </a></p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e1q7":{"id":"e1q7","exam":1,"n":7,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Considering the scenario of configuring a continuous integration pipeline using Cloud Build to automate container image builds, which optimization can be implemented based on the provided code segment? Choose two options.</strong></p>\n<p>FROM python:3.7-alpine </p>\n<p>COPY . /app WORKDIR /app </p>\n<p>RUN pip install -r requirements.txt </p>\n<p>CMD [\"gunicorn\", \"-w 4\", \"main:app\"]</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>E. Store the application's source code on Cloud Storage and configure the pipeline to download it using gsutil.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>A. Opt for a larger virtual machine (VM) size with increased CPU capacity for Cloud Build operations.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Establish a Container Registry on a Compute Engine VM within a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) to store final images.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Employ caching for container images in subsequent builds using the --cache-from argument in the build configuration file.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Adjust the base image in the build file to ubuntu:latest and install Python 3.7 using a package manager.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>Correct answer</p>\n<p><strong>A. Opt for a larger virtual machine (VM) size with increased CPU capacity for Cloud Build operations.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Employ caching for container images in subsequent builds using the --cache-from argument in the build configuration file.</strong></p>\n<p><em>A is correct because a high-CPU virtual machine type can increase the speed of your build.<br />\nB is not correct because a Container Registry on a VM will not speed up the build.<br />\nC is correct because the same container is used in subsequent steps for testing and to be pushed to the registry.<br />\nD is not correct because an ubuntu container image will be significantly larger than the python:3.7-alpine image.<br />\nE is not correct because storing the application source code on Cloud Storage does not decrease the time to build the application.</em></p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"%0d https:/cloud.google.com/cloud-build/docs/speeding-up-builds%0d\">https://cloud.google.com/cloud-build/docs/speeding-up-builds</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"%0d https:/cloud.google.com/build/docs/optimize-builds/increase-vcpu-for-builds%0d\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/optimize-builds/increase-vcpu-for-builds</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e1q8":{"id":"e1q8","exam":1,"n":8,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Which actions should you take to configure object storage while ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements and optimizing storage cost? (choose two)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Utilize IAM Conditions to restrict read access to objects based on their creation date, which is included in the object's prefix name.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Configure a retention policy on the bucket specifying a 7-year retention period.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>V. Develop a Cloud Function to evaluate the age of each object and transfer those older than 3 years to a second bucket configured with the Archive Storage class. Employ Cloud Scheduler to execute the Cloud Function daily.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Implement an object lifecycle policy that automatically transitions objects from Standard Storage to Archive Storage after they have existed for 3 years.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Enable Object Versioning to safeguard against accidental deletions for 7 years after object creation.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The actions that should be taken to configure object storage while ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements and optimizing storage cost are:</p>\n<p><strong>I. Configure a retention policy on the bucket specifying a 7-year retention period.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Implement an object lifecycle policy that automatically transitions objects from Standard Storage to Archive Storage after they have existed for 3 years.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Option I suggests configuring a retention policy on the bucket with a 7-year retention period. This ensures that objects within the bucket cannot be deleted or modified until the retention period expires, thereby helping to maintain compliance with regulatory requirements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option II proposes implementing an object lifecycle policy to automatically transition objects from Standard Storage to Archive Storage after they have existed for 3 years. This helps optimize storage cost by moving less frequently accessed data to a lower-cost storage class, while still retaining compliance with regulatory requirements by preserving the data.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option III mentions utilizing IAM Conditions to restrict read access to objects based on their creation date, which may help with access control but does not directly address regulatory compliance or storage cost optimization.</p>\n<p>Option IV suggests enabling Object Versioning to safeguard against accidental deletions, which is a useful feature but does not directly relate to regulatory compliance or storage cost optimization.</p>\n<p>Option V involves developing a Cloud Function and employing Cloud Scheduler to evaluate and transfer objects older than 3 years to Archive Storage. While this may help with storage cost optimization, it does not directly ensure compliance with regulatory requirements.</p>\n<p>Therefore, Options I and II are the most appropriate actions for configuring object storage while ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements and optimizing storage cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/bucket-lock\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/bucket-lock</a></p>","lessons":["d1l5"]},"e1q9":{"id":"e1q9","exam":1,"n":9,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You've recently launched a new service on Cloud Run, which authenticates via a custom service and records transactional data in a Cloud Spanner database. Your aim is to validate the application's capability to handle up to 5,000 read and 1,000 write transactions per second while pinpointing any bottlenecks. Additionally, your testing setup should be able to autoscale. What's the optimal approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Develop a testing framework to generate requests and deploy it on Cloud Run. Analyze VPC Flow Logs using Cloud Logging.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Set up a Google Kubernetes Engine cluster with Locust or JMeter images to dynamically generate load tests. Analyze outcomes with Cloud Trace.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy a Compute Engine instance with a LAMP stack image from the Marketplace. Utilize Apache Bench for load testing against the service. Analyze findings with Cloud Trace.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Use Cloud Tasks to generate a test load. Schedule 60,000 Cloud Task transactions per minute for 10 minutes using Cloud Scheduler. Review results via Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Set up a Google Kubernetes Engine cluster with Locust or JMeter images to dynamically generate load tests. Analyze outcomes with Cloud Trace.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Suitable Load Testing Tools:</strong> Locust and JMeter are industry-standard load testing tools designed specifically to simulate heavy user traffic and stress test systems. They offer flexibility for defining custom request patterns and simulating realistic user behavior.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability with Kubernetes:</strong> GKE provides scalable and self-healing infrastructure for running load testing tools. You can spin up multiple instances of JMeter or Locust pods as needed to achieve the desired load of 5,000 read/1,000 write requests per second.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>In-depth Tracing with Cloud Trace:</strong> Cloud Trace integrates seamlessly with Cloud Run and Cloud Spanner. It provides detailed visibility into request latencies, error rates, and spans, allowing you to pinpoint bottlenecks at different layers of your system.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Develop a testing framework to generate requests and deploy it on Cloud Run. Analyze VPC Flow Logs using Cloud Logging.</strong> Cloud Run can scale during the tests, but building your own framework would be time-consuming. VPC Flow Logs don't provide the granular telemetry needed to identify bottlenecks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Use Cloud Tasks to generate a test load. Schedule 60,000 Cloud Task transactions per minute for 10 minutes using Cloud Scheduler. Review results via Cloud Monitoring.</strong> While Cloud Tasks can generate load, they are not specialized load testing tools and lack the flexibility to customize request patterns. Cloud Monitoring on its own may not uncover performance bottlenecks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Deploy a Compute Engine instance with a LAMP stack image from the Marketplace. Utilize Apache Bench for load testing against the service. Analyze findings with Cloud Trace.</strong> Deploying and managing a Compute Engine instance for load testing adds overhead. Apache Bench is not as advanced as Locust or JMeter in terms of customizability.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>References:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/distributed-load-testing-using-gke\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/distributed-load-testing-using-gke</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e1q10":{"id":"e1q10","exam":1,"n":10,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You've been assigned to oversee the transfer of your company's on-premises application to Google Cloud. The application, serving as an ecommerce platform, will undergo a phased migration to microservices on Google Cloud. As the majority of your company's revenue comes from online sales, it's crucial to minimize risks during the transition. Your task is to prioritize features and select the initial functionality for migration. What's the optimal approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Migrate the Shopping cart, integrating it with the frontend, cart database, inventory system, and payment processing platform.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Move Order fulfillment, establishing connections with the order database, inventory system, and third-party shipping partner.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Shift Payment processing, integrating it with the frontend, order database, and third-party payment provider.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Transfer the Product catalog, ensuring seamless integration with the frontend and product database.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Transfer the Product catalog, ensuring seamless integration with the frontend and product database.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's my reasoning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Prioritizing the User Experience:</strong> The product catalog lies at the heart of the customer experience on an ecommerce website. Users need to easily browse and search for products to initiate the purchase process. A disruption-free transfer of the catalog minimizes visible changes to customers, maintaining a positive user experience.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Isolation:</strong> It's likely that the product catalog has relatively independent dependencies, compared to the more interconnected payment and fulfillment systems. This allows for a cleaner, less disruptive migration phase.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Risk Minimization:</strong> Starting with the product catalog allows the team to test the migration process, infrastructure, and integration within Google Cloud with a less critical component. It lowers the risk of major service outages during the initial stages.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let's analyze why the other options aren't optimal in this initial phase:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Shift Payment processing...:</strong> Payment processing is a highly sensitive and mission-critical function. Introducing complexity via migration in the early stages increases the risk of outages or security issues that could lead to lost revenue and customer trust.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Move Order fulfillment...:</strong> This option introduces dependencies on external shipping partners and internal inventory systems, adding potential points of failure. It is better postponed until the core product catalog feature is successfully migrated.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Migrate the Shopping cart...:</strong> The shopping cart, while frontend-facing, has tightly coupled dependencies on inventory, payment, and possibly order data. Migrating it too early creates a chain of complex changes that can increase risk.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#choosing_an_initial_migration_effort\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#choosing_an_initial_migration_effort</a></p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e1q11":{"id":"e1q11","exam":1,"n":11,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Which Google Cloud service is the optimal choice for deploying your container while ensuring seamless migration from an on-premises Knative environment, maintaining your application's deployment strategy, and utilizing a fully managed service?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Google Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Google App Engine Flexible Environment</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Google Kubernetes Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Google Cloud Run</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Google Cloud Run</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why it's the best fit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Knative Compatibility:</strong> Cloud Run is built on Knative, ensuring a smooth migration from your on-premises environment and preserving your existing deployment strategies.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Fully Managed:</strong> Cloud Run eliminates the need to manage Kubernetes clusters and underlying infrastructure, aligning with your goal of a fully managed solution.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Focus on Containers:</strong> Cloud Run's primary focus is running containerized applications, making it a natural fit for your container-based workflow.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why the other options aren't as ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Google Compute Engine:</strong> Compute Engine gives you full control but requires managing the underlying virtual machines, deviating from your goal of a fully managed service.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Google Kubernetes Engine:</strong> While GKE provides a powerful environment, it entails managing Kubernetes clusters, which may introduce more overhead than you're willing to take on.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Google App Engine Flexible Environment:</strong> App Engine Flexible supports containers but is oriented towards custom runtimes. It might not offer the same Knative compatibility as Cloud Run.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>References:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/serverless/knative-based-cloud-run-services-are-ga\">https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/serverless/knative-based-cloud-run-services-are-ga</a></p>","lessons":["d1l1"]},"e1q12":{"id":"e1q12","exam":1,"n":12,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your application deployed in Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) is responsible for reading and processing Pub/Sub messages. The message publication rate to the Pub/Sub topic varies significantly throughout the day and week, occasionally leading to large batches of messages being published simultaneously. To ensure timely message processing, what GKE feature should you employ to automatically adjust your workload?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Vertical Pod Autoscaler in Auto mode.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Horizontal Pod Autoscaler based on an external metric.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Vertical Pod Autoscaler in Recommendation mode.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Horizontal Pod Autoscaler based on resource utilization.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Horizontal Pod Autoscaler based on an external metric.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this choice is optimal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scaling Based on Demand:</strong> A Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) combined with an external metric like the Pub/Sub backlog will directly scale your pods in proportion to the number of unprocessed messages. This ensures timely processing even during bursts of activity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Specific to Pub/Sub:</strong> The HPA can leverage metrics from Cloud Monitoring specific to Pub/Sub, such as <code>pubsub.googleapis.com/subscription/num_undelivered_messages</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficiency:</strong> Horizontal scaling allows you to add/remove processing capacity as needed, optimizing resource usage and cost.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Vertical Pod Autoscaler in Auto mode.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vertical scaling changes the resources allocated to individual pods (CPU/memory). This may not be the most efficient way to handle bursts if your bottleneck is the number of pods rather than per-pod resources.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Vertical Pod Autoscaler in Recommendation mode.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recommendations might be useful for long-term adjustments, but don't address the need for rapid scaling in response to workload spikes.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Horizontal Pod Autoscaler based on resource utilization.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While useful for many scenarios, resource utilization (CPU/memory) might not directly correlate to message backlog. If Pods are processing messages quickly, they might show low CPU usage even with a backlog due to network latency/other factors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>References:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/autoscaling-metrics#pubsub\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/autoscaling-metrics#pubsub</a></p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e1q13":{"id":"e1q13","exam":1,"n":13,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>Your code, operating on Cloud Functions within project A, aims to write an object to a Cloud Storage bucket owned by project B. Unfortunately, the write operation fails with a \"403 Forbidden\" error. How can you address this issue effectively?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Allocate the roles/storage.objectCreator role to the service-PROJECTA@gcf-admin-robot.iam.gserviceaccount.com service account for the Cloud Storage bucket.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Ensure the Cloud Storage API is enabled within project B.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Provide your user account with the roles/iam.serviceAccountUser role for the service-PROJECTA@gcf-admin-robot.iam.gserviceaccount.com service account.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Grant your user account the roles/storage.objectCreator role for the Cloud Storage bucket.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Allocate the roles/storage.objectCreator role to the service-PROJECTA@gcf-admin-robot.iam.gserviceaccount.com service account for the Cloud Storage bucket.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the correct approach:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Service Account Permissions:</strong> Cloud Functions execute using a service account. It is this service account that needs the necessary permissions to write to the Cloud Storage bucket in project B.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> Granting the specific role <code>roles/storage.objectCreator</code> provides the minimal permission required, limiting the potential security impact.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cross-Project Access:</strong> This approach addresses the issue of cross-project communication, the core reason why the write operation fails.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Not Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Grant your user account the roles/storage.objectCreator role ...</strong> While this might work, it's overly broad and ties permissions to your personal user account rather than the function's identity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Provide your user account with the roles/iam.serviceAccountUser ...</strong> This role grants you the ability to impersonate service accounts, but doesn't directly give the function's service account the needed bucket permissions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Ensure the Cloud Storage API is enabled for project B</strong> This is a prerequisite for any interaction with Cloud Storage, but does not address the specific permissions issue.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/concepts/iam#troubleshooting_permission_errors\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/concepts/iam#troubleshooting_permission_errors</a></p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e1q14":{"id":"e1q14","exam":1,"n":14,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You've set up automatic scaling for your Compute Engine instance group based on overall CPU usage. However, you've noticed a significant uptick in response latency as the cluster scales up. To ensure a smoother user experience, you're considering adjustments to the instance group autoscaler configuration. What are the two recommended changes?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Shorten the cooldown period for newly added instances in the group.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Decrease the target CPU usage threshold for the instance group autoscaler.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Increase the target CPU usage threshold for the instance group autoscaler.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>V. Remove the health check for individual VMs in the instance group.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Apply the \"AUTOSCALE\" label to the instance group template.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Shorten the cooldown period for newly added instances in the group.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Faster Readiness:</strong> A shorter cooldown period lets new instances handle traffic sooner after being created, reducing the time where existing instances are overloaded.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cooldown Considerations:</strong> Choose a cooldown period that allows for your application's initialization time. Too short, and new instances will receive traffic before they're ready.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IV. Decrease the target CPU usage threshold for the instance group autoscaler.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Proactive Scaling:</strong> Lowering the threshold triggers scaling earlier. This helps avoid the period where existing instances hit high CPU usage (and therefore higher latency) before new ones are added.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Balance with Cost:</strong> Scaling out earlier might increase costs. Tune the threshold to find the sweet spot between responsiveness and efficient resource usage.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Apply the \"AUTOSCALE\" label ...</strong> Autoscaling works without an explicit label, this likely won't help your latency issue.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Increase the target CPU usage threshold ...</strong> This would worsen the problem. It would delay scaling until instances are even more heavily loaded, leading to worse latency.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>V. Remove the health check ...</strong> Health checks are critical! They prevent unhealthy instances from receiving traffic, which would be disastrous for user experience.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e1q15":{"id":"e1q15","exam":1,"n":15,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>To enable clients to download a file from your website for a limited period while adhering to Google's recommended best practices, how should you architect the application?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Generate and allocate a Cloud Storage-signed URL for the file, then provide the URL to the client for downloading.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Create a temporary Cloud Storage bucket with a specified time expiration, granting download permissions to the bucket, copying the file, and providing it to the client.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Generate HTTP cookies with a specified time expiration. If the time is valid, retrieve the file from the Cloud Storage bucket and make it accessible for the client to download.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Configure the application to deliver the file to the client as an email attachment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Generate and allocate a Cloud Storage signed URL for the file, then provide the URL to the client for downloading.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the ideal choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Secure and Scalable:</strong> Signed URLs leverage Cloud Storage's security mechanisms and scale well without impacting your application's workload.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Time-limited Access:</strong> You can set an expiration time directly on the signed URL, controlling the download window.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Simplicity:</strong> Generating signed URLs is relatively straightforward, reducing implementation complexity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Best Practice:</strong> Google recommends signed URLs for this use case.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Let's analyze the other options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Email Attachment:</strong> This approach is less scalable and secure, especially for larger files compared to a direct download link.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Temporary Bucket:</strong> Creating and managing temporary buckets adds unnecessary overhead and complexity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. HTTP Cookies:</strong> Cookies are designed to store client-side data and aren't ideal for managing file access and security.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/signed-urls\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/signed-urls</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e1q16":{"id":"e1q16","exam":1,"n":16,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>To transition a monolithic application into a microservices model efficiently while minimizing business impact, which strategy should you pursue?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Refactor the monolithic application by integrating appropriate microservices in a single concerted effort and deploy it.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Develop a new application with the necessary microservices independently of the monolith and switch it upon completion.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Deploy the application on Compute Engine and activate autoscaling.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Gradually replace the application's functionalities with suitable microservices over several phases.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Gradually replace the application's functionalities with suitable microservices over several phases.</strong></p>\n<p>This incremental approach offers several advantages for a monolith-to-microservices transition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reduced Risk:</strong> Iteratively replacing components minimizes the chances of widespread disruption and makes errors easier to isolate.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Controlled Pace:</strong> The gradual transition allows your team to learn and adapt to the microservices architecture without overwhelming pressure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Agility:</strong> You can prioritize the modules that provide the most immediate value or are easiest to refactor as microservices first.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Coexistence:</strong> During the transition, the monolith and new microservices can coexist, often using techniques like the Strangler Fig pattern to manage interactions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Have Drawbacks:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Deploy the application on Compute Engine and activate autoscaling.</strong> This focuses on scaling but doesn't address the inherent architectural challenges of a monolithic application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Refactor the monolithic application ... in a single ... effort and deploy it.</strong> Such a \"big bang\" refactor is high-risk. It can lead to prolonged downtime and increased likelihood of errors and complexities.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Develop a new application ... independently ... and switch it upon completion.</strong> While sometimes necessary for extremely outdated monoliths, it requires maintaining two codebases in parallel for a while and introduces a risky cutover event.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/microservices-architecture-refactoring-monoliths\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/microservices-architecture-refactoring-monoliths</a></p>","lessons":["d1l1","d1l6"]},"e1q17":{"id":"e1q17","exam":1,"n":17,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>For a mobile application aiming to store hierarchical data structures in a database, allowing offline syncing and scalability, while ensuring security, which combination of database and IAM role is most suitable?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Utilize Firestore in Native mode for its offline syncing capabilities, and assign the roles/datastore.user role to the service account for secure data handling.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Employ Bigtable as the database solution, and assign the roles/bigtable.viewer role to the service account for enriched data processing.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Opt for Cloud SQL as the database solution, and assign the roles/cloudsql.editor role to the service account for enriched data management.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Select Firestore in Datastore mode, and assign the roles/datastore.viewer role to the service account to facilitate secure and scalable data operations.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Utilize Firestore in Native mode for its offline syncing capabilities, and assign the roles/datastore.user role to the service account for secure data handling.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this choice stands out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Hierarchical Data:</strong> Firestore's document-oriented structure excels at handling hierarchical data, a common requirement for mobile applications.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Offline Syncing:</strong> Firestore's Native mode offers robust offline support, ensuring your app functions even with intermittent connectivity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> Firestore scales effortlessly, accommodating the unpredictable usage patterns of mobile apps.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> The <code>roles/datastore.user</code> role provides the necessary permissions for data manipulation while adhering to the principle of least privilege.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Cloud SQL:</strong> Cloud SQL is a relational database, making it less natural for hierarchical data and less straightforward for offline use. The <code>roles/cloudsql.editor</code> role grants excessive permissions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Bigtable:</strong> Bigtable is a powerful columnar database but is overkill for many mobile apps. It might bring unnecessary complexity. The <code>roles/bigtable.viewer</code> role focuses on read operations, not data modification.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Firestore (Datastore Mode):</strong> While suitable, Native mode offers enhanced offline capabilities compared to Datastore mode. The <code>roles/datastore.viewer</code> role is read-only.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/building-scalable-apps-with-cloud-firestore\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/building-scalable-apps-with-cloud-firestore</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2","d1l7"]},"e1q18":{"id":"e1q18","exam":1,"n":18,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You recently migrated a monolithic application to Google Cloud, breaking it down into microservices. One of these microservices is deployed using Cloud Functions. As you modernize the application, you introduce a backward-incompatible change to the service's API. You now need to support both existing callers using the original API and new callers utilizing the updated API. What's the best approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Keep the original Cloud Function unchanged and deploy a second Cloud Function containing only the modified API. Calls are automatically routed to the appropriate function.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Update the Cloud Function's code to support the new API and redeploy it. Requests for both API versions are handled based on a version identifier included in the call.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Retain the original Cloud Function unchanged and deploy a second Cloud Function with the new API. Utilize Cloud Endpoints to provide an API gateway exposing a versioned API.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Maintain the original Cloud Function unchanged and deploy a second Cloud Function with the new API. Employ a load balancer to distribute calls between the versions.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Retain the original Cloud Function unchanged and deploy a second Cloud Function with the new API. Utilize Cloud Endpoints to provide an API gateway exposing a versioned API.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach is superior:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Clear Versioning:</strong> Cloud Endpoints allows you to explicitly define different API versions, providing clarity to developers and avoiding confusion over ambiguous routing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>API Management:</strong> Cloud Endpoints offers additional features like authentication, rate limiting, and API analytics, which can be beneficial as your API evolves.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flexibility:</strong> You can control how traffic is routed between versions for gradual rollout, testing, and eventual deprecation of the old version.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Maintainability:</strong> Keeping the original function untouched simplifies rollback if necessary and allows you to isolate changes within the new version.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Load Balancer:</strong> While possible, manually configuring a load balancer for API versioning is less streamlined and lacks the features of a dedicated API management tool like Cloud Endpoints.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Automatic Routing:</strong> Cloud Functions don't natively support automatic routing based on API versioning.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Single Function Update:</strong> This approach risks downtime for consumers of the old API, making the migration disruptive. It also adds complexity to the codebase.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Refrences:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/versioning-an-api#backwards-incompatible  https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#versioning  https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/versioning-an-api#backwards-incompatible\">https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/versioning-an-api#backwards-incompatible</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/versioning-an-api#backwards-incompatible  https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#versioning  https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/versioning-an-api#backwards-incompatible\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#versioning</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/versioning-an-api#backwards-incompatible  https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#versioning  https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/versioning-an-api#backwards-incompatible\">https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/versioning-an-api#backwards-incompatible</a></p>","lessons":["d4l5"]},"e1q19":{"id":"e1q19","exam":1,"n":19,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>When configuring a Deployment on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), including a check to ensure containers can connect to the database, and initiating a graceful shutdown script if connection fails, what configuration should you employ?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Configure the Deployment with an initContainer to check service availability. Set up a Prestop lifecycle handler to execute the shutdown script if the Pod is failing.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Establish two jobs: one to assess container-database connectivity, and another to execute the shutdown script if the Pod is encountering connectivity issues.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Set up the Deployment with a PostStart lifecycle handler to verify service availability. Configure a PreStop lifecycle handler to execute the shutdown script if the container fails.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Construct the Deployment with a livenessProbe for the container, which will fail if database connectivity is not established. Configure a Prestop lifecycle handler to execute the shutdown script if the container fails.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>The most suitable answer in this case is</p>\n<p><strong>II. Construct the Deployment with a livenessProbe for the container, which will fail if database connectivity is not established. Configure a Prestop lifecycle handler to execute the shutdown script if the container fails.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>LivenessProbe:</strong> A livenessProbe is specifically designed to monitor the health of a container. If it cannot connect to the database, Kubernetes will recognize the container as unhealthy and automatically restart it, potentially re-establishing the connection.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>PreStop Lifecycle Handler:</strong> This hook is triggered before the container is terminated. It's the perfect place to execute your graceful shutdown script, ensuring any in-process tasks are completed cleanly.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here's why the other options aren't as ideal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Separate Jobs:</strong> This approach adds complexity and might introduce delays. Jobs might not run with the frequency needed to detect connectivity issues promptly.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. PostStart Handler:</strong> PostStart is executed once after container startup. It wouldn't provide continuous monitoring, and a PreStop hook doesn't automatically trigger if a liveness probe fails.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. InitContainer:</strong> An initContainer runs to completion before the main container starts. It might not accurately reflect ongoing connectivity issues after startup and delays the main application container.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>References:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/best-practices-for-running-cost-effective-kubernetes-applications-on-\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/best-practices-for-running-cost-effective-kubernetes-applications-on-</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3"]},"e1q20":{"id":"e1q20","exam":1,"n":20,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You want to assess the security vulnerabilities of an application running on App Engine. Which service should you use for this purpose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Cloud Armor</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Stackdriver Error Reporting</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Stackdriver Debugger</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Cloud Security Scanner</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Cloud Security Scanner</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why it's the right choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Vulnerability Scanning:</strong> Cloud Security Scanner is designed specifically to scan web applications for common vulnerabilities like cross-site scripting (XSS), SQL injection, and outdated libraries.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>App Engine Integration:</strong> It seamlessly integrates with App Engine, making it easy to scan your deployed application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Actionable Reports:</strong> Cloud Security Scanner provides detailed reports on discovered vulnerabilities, along with recommendations for remediation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Cloud Armor</strong> Cloud Armor is a web application firewall (WAF) primarily focused on mitigating DDoS attacks and enforcing IP-based allow/deny lists. While it enhances security, it doesn't directly scan for application-level vulnerabilities.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Stackdriver Debugger</strong> Debugger helps inspect an application's state in production for troubleshooting errors, but doesn't have a security focus.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Stackdriver Error Reporting</strong> Error Reporting aids in error analysis, but may indirectly reveal consequences of an exploit rather than identifying the vulnerability itself.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/python/application-security:  https://cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/concepts-web-security-scanner-overview\">https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/python/application-security:</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/python/application-security:  https://cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/concepts-web-security-scanner-overview\">https://cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/concepts-web-security-scanner-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e1q21":{"id":"e1q21","exam":1,"n":21,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>In the development of a single-page web application with a user interface that interacts with a third-party API using XMLHttpRequest, prioritizing rendering performance is crucial. While the API data displayed on the UI is less critical than other data, delays in API calls should not impact the rendering of other UI elements. Efficient handling of API errors or timeouts is necessary. What's the optimal approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Implement error handling to capture timeout or error exceptions from the API call and retry with exponential backoff until a successful response is received.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement error handling to capture timeout or error exceptions from the API call and display the error response in the UI widget.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Disable the asynchronous option for API requests and conceal the UI widget displaying API results upon encountering a timeout or error.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Enable the asynchronous option for API requests and conceal the UI widget displaying API results upon encountering a timeout or error.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Enable the asynchronous option for API requests and conceal the UI widget displaying API results upon encountering a timeout or error.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach offers the best trade-off between performance and user experience:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Asynchronous for Performance:</strong> Asynchronous API calls prevent blocking the main UI thread, ensuring the rest of your single-page application remains responsive and can render independently.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>User Experience:</strong> Concealing the UI widget specifically for the API data provides a smooth user experience. It indicates that the data is being fetched while not disrupting other page elements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Error Handling:</strong> While not the primary focus, basic error handling with concealment of the widget maintains a usable application state.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Disable the asynchronous option...</strong> Synchronous API calls will block the main thread, degrading overall user experience and potentially causing the entire page to become unresponsive.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Implement error handling to capture ...and retry...</strong> Retry logic can be valuable, but blindly retrying too often can put unnecessary load on the third-party API and still frustrate users if the issue persists.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. ...display the error response in the UI widget.</strong> While informative for debugging, directly displaying raw error messages to users is generally not the best user experience. It's better to provide a more user-friendly indicator that something went wrong.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l6","d1l9"]},"e1q22":{"id":"e1q22","exam":1,"n":22,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>While conducting load testing on your server application, you observe that an inactive Cloud Storage bucket is now handling 2000 write requests per second and 7500 read requests per second within the initial 30 seconds. Consequently, your application begins experiencing intermittent 5xx and 429 HTTP responses from the Cloud Storage JSON API due to increased demand. To mitigate the occurrence of failed responses from the Cloud Storage API, what action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Opt for the XML API instead of the JSON API for interacting with Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Transmit the HTTP response codes back to clients initiating uploads from your application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Distribute the uploads across multiple individual storage buckets.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Restrict the upload rate from your application clients to ensure a gradual increase in the dormant bucket's peak request rate.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Restrict the upload rate from your application clients to ensure a gradual increase in the dormant bucket's peak request rate.</strong></p>\n<p>Let's break down why it's the best choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Addresses the Root Cause:</strong> The sudden surge in requests is overwhelming Cloud Storage's capacity to handle them, leading to 5xx errors (server-side issues) and 429 errors (rate limiting). Slowing down uploads directly tackles the cause of the problem.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Rate Limiting/Throttling</strong> : Implementing rate limiting in your application logic allows you to control the flow of requests, preventing the Cloud Storage bucket from being flooded.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Prevents Recurrence:</strong> A gradual increase allows Cloud Storage to scale its request handling capabilities, reducing the risk of further outages in your application.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Distribute the uploads across multiple individual storage buckets.</strong> While this could help long-term with scalability, it doesn't immediately address the issue at hand with the current bucket.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Opt for the XML API ...</strong> Changing the API won't solve the fundamental problem of overwhelming the bucket with requests.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Transmit the HTTP response codes ...</strong> While passing these responses to clients is informative, it doesn't fix the core issue of too many requests hitting Cloud Storage.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/request-rate#ramp-up\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/request-rate#ramp-up</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e1q23":{"id":"e1q23","exam":1,"n":23,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>While developing a JPEG image-resizing API on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), the service's callers will reside within the same GKE cluster. You aim to facilitate clients in obtaining the service's IP address. What's the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Configure a GKE Endpoint. Clients should obtain the endpoint name from Cloud DNS.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Configure a GKE Endpoint. Clients should retrieve the endpoint name from the appropriate environment variable within the client container.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Establish a GKE Service. Clients should use the A record's name in Cloud DNS to locate the service's cluster IP address.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Set up a GKE Service. Clients should utilize the service name within the URL to access the service.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Set up a GKE Service. Clients should utilize the service name within the URL to access the service.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Using a GKE Service with its service name is the most efficient and reliable way to enable service discovery within a Kubernetes cluster:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Internal DNS:</strong> Kubernetes automatically assigns a DNS name to each Service, providing a simple abstraction for clients. The service name becomes the hostname (e.g., <code>my-image-resizer.default.svc.cluster.local</code>)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Load Balancing:</strong> Kubernetes Services handle load balancing across the pods that actually run your API.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Simplicity for Clients:</strong> Clients don't need to maintain specific IP addresses, which can change as pods are rescheduled or scaled.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Establish a GKE Service ... Cloud DNS to locate ... cluster IP address</strong> While technically possible, manually managing Cloud DNS and cluster IPs adds complexity and potential points of failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III &amp; IV. Configure a GKE Endpoint ... Clients should retrieve the endpoint name ...</strong> GKE Endpoints are primarily for exposing services outside the cluster. Since you're working within the cluster, they add unnecessary layers.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/dns-pod-service/  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/service-discovery\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/dns-pod-service/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/dns-pod-service/  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/service-discovery\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/service-discovery</a></p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e1q24":{"id":"e1q24","exam":1,"n":24,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>Your service annotates images fetched from Cloud Storage. During peak periods, requests to Cloud Storage result in HTTP 429 \"Too Many Requests\" errors. How should you address this issue?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Switch the storage class of the Cloud Storage bucket to Multi-regional.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Request a quota increase through the GCP Console.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Retry the request employing a truncated exponential backoff strategy.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Apply a cache-control header to the objects.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Retry the request employing a truncated exponential backoff strategy.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the most effective immediate solution and why the others have limitations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Addresses the Root Issue:</strong> HTTP 429 errors signal that you're exceeding your Cloud Storage request rate. Retrying with exponential backoff allows your service to gracefully handle temporary throttling, giving the quota time to reset.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Quick Implementation:</strong> Implementing this retry mechanism likely involves minor code changes in the component making requests to Cloud Storage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> Exponential backoff helps your service automatically adjust its behavior to handle temporary load spikes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Might Help, But Have Limitations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Apply a cache-control header...</strong> Caching can reduce future requests for the same images, but it doesn't directly address the 429 errors when the cache is empty.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Request a quota increase...</strong> This can be a valid solution, but it might take time for approval and involves additional costs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Switch ... to Multi-regional.</strong> Multi-regional storage can improve availability and redundancy but might not directly alleviate the current quota limitations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e1q25":{"id":"e1q25","exam":1,"n":25,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You have authored a Cloud Function that interacts with other Google Cloud services. To ensure security following the principle of least privilege, what is the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Create a fresh service account with Editor permissions for accessing the resources. Give the deployer authorization to act as the new service account.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Generate a new service account with a custom IAM role tailored for accessing the resources. Provide the deployer authorization to act as the new service account.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Set up a new service account with a custom IAM role crafted for accessing the resources. Grant the deployer authorization to obtain the access token.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Establish a new service account with Editor permissions for accessing the resources. Provide the deployer authorization to acquire the access token.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Generate a new service account with a custom IAM role tailored for accessing the resources. Provide the deployer authorization to act as the new service account.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this stands out as the best choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> A custom IAM role grants only the absolutely necessary permissions required by the Cloud Function, reducing the risk of unintended consequences or privilege escalation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Separation of Concerns:</strong> Granting the deployer the ability to 'act as' the service account maintains a separation between deployment permissions and the Cloud Function's runtime permissions. This improves auditability and makes the flow of permissions clearer.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security Best Practice:</strong> This is the approach explicitly recommended by Google for adhering to the principle of least privilege in Cloud Functions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I &amp; III. Editor Permissions:</strong> The Editor role is a basic role that encompasses a wide range of permissions. This significantly violates the principle of least privilege, making your system more vulnerable.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Custom Role but Deployer Gets Access Token:</strong> While a custom role is better, granting the deployer the ability to obtain the service account's access token still increases the attack surface unnecessarily.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#individual\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#individual</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#per-function_identity\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#per-function_identity</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity</a></p>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e1q26":{"id":"e1q26","exam":1,"n":26,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>The latest iteration of your containerized application has undergone rigorous testing and stands poised for deployment to production on Google Kubernetes Engine. Due to limitations, thorough load testing couldn't be conducted in pre-production environments. Ensuring a seamless deployment free from performance glitches is paramount, with automation being a prerequisite. What actions should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Execute the deployment using kubectl and configure the spec.updateStrategy.type to RollingUpdate. Utilize Cloud Monitoring to flag any performance issues and initiate a rollback using the kubectl command if necessary.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Employ Cloud Load Balancing to gradually increase traffic between versions, while relying on Cloud Monitoring to detect any performance anomalies.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Implement a continuous delivery pipeline featuring canary deployments, utilizing Cloud Monitoring to pinpoint performance issues and incrementally scaling up traffic based on observed metrics.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Opt for a continuous delivery pipeline with blue/green deployments, leveraging Cloud Monitoring to identify performance bottlenecks and proceeding with full deployment once metrics confirm readiness.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Implement a continuous delivery pipeline featuring canary deployments, utilizing Cloud Monitoring to pinpoint performance issues and incrementally scaling up traffic based on observed metrics.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Risk Mitigation:</strong> Canary deployments are ideal for this scenario, letting you test the new version with a small subset of real production traffic. This limits potential impact if issues arise.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Gradual Rollout:</strong> With incremental traffic increases, you have greater control while gathering live performance data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Monitoring-Driven:</strong> Cloud Monitoring plays a key role in observing key metrics (latency, error rates, etc.). This data will inform your decision on whether to continue the rollout or roll back.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Automation:</strong> A continuous delivery pipeline automates the entire process, ensuring consistency and speed.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Aren't as Suitable</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Cloud Load Balancing with Monitoring:</strong> While this can help distribute traffic, it lacks the fine-grained control and automated rollback mechanisms offered by a proper canary deployment strategy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Blue/Green Deployments:</strong> Blue/green is well-suited for rapid rollbacks, but it requires double the resources and can be overkill if the changes aren't drastic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Manual Rollout with Kubectl:</strong> This option lacks the safeguards and automation of a dedicated continuous delivery pipeline. It's error-prone and less scalable.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/deployment-strategies/canary#types_of_canary\">https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/deployment-strategies/canary#types_of_canary</a></p>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e1q27":{"id":"e1q27","exam":1,"n":27,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Given the urgent need to implement autoscaling for your stateless and distributed service operating in a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster without significant alterations, which approach should you pursue?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Opting for a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler would enable scaling the number of Pods based on CPU load, offering a straightforward solution that aligns with the stateless and distributed nature of the service, with minimal changes required.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Deploying a Vertical Pod Autoscaler to scale based on a custom metric could entail substantial changes and additional configurations, potentially complicating the deployment within the constrained two-week timeframe.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Deploying a Vertical Pod Autoscaler would dynamically adjust the resource requests and limits for individual Pods based on their actual usage, but it doesn't directly cater to autoscaling according to workload demands.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Implementing a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler to scale based on a custom metric might necessitate more intricate configurations and adjustments, potentially impeding the deployment within the two-week timeframe.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>Given the urgent need to implement autoscaling for your stateless and distributed service operating in a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster without significant alterations, the most appropriate approach to pursue is:</p>\n<p><strong>III. Opting for a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler would enable scaling the number of Pods based on CPU load, offering a straightforward solution that aligns with the stateless and distributed nature of the service, with minimal changes required.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Option III suggests deploying a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler, which is designed specifically for scaling the number of Pods based on CPU load. This aligns well with the stateless and distributed nature of the service, as it can automatically adjust the number of Pods to handle varying workload demands without significant changes to the deployment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option I discusses deploying a Vertical Pod Autoscaler, which adjusts resource requests and limits for individual Pods based on their usage but doesn't directly address autoscaling according to workload demands. This approach might not be suitable for rapidly changing workloads.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option II mentions deploying a Vertical Pod Autoscaler to scale based on a custom metric. While this could be possible, it may entail substantial changes and additional configurations, potentially complicating the deployment within the constrained timeframe.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option IV discusses implementing a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler to scale based on a custom metric, which might necessitate more intricate configurations and adjustments, potentially impeding the deployment within the two-week timeframe.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, Option III provides the most straightforward and suitable solution for implementing autoscaling for the stateless and distributed service in a GKE cluster within the urgent timeframe.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/vertical-pod-autoscaling  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/verticalpodautoscaler  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/custom-metrics-autoscaling\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/vertical-pod-autoscaling</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/vertical-pod-autoscaling  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/verticalpodautoscaler  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/custom-metrics-autoscaling\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/verticalpodautoscaler</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/vertical-pod-autoscaling  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/verticalpodautoscaler  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/custom-metrics-autoscaling\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/custom-metrics-autoscaling</a></p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e1q28":{"id":"e1q28","exam":1,"n":28,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You've recently launched a Go application on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). The operations team has observed high CPU usage even during periods of low production traffic. They've requested you to optimize the application's CPU resource consumption. To identify which Go functions are consuming the most CPU, what action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Access your GKE nodes via SSH. Execute the top command in the shell to extract the CPU utilization of your application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Develop a customized dashboard in Cloud Monitoring to assess the CPU performance metrics of your application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Adjust your Go application to gather profiling data. Evaluate the CPU metrics of your application using flame graphs in Profiler.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Implement a Fluent Bit daemonset on the GKE cluster for logging data in Cloud Logging. Analyze the logs to gain insights into your application code's performance.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The most suitable option is </p>\n<p><strong>IV. Adjust your Go application to gather profiling data. Evaluate the CPU metrics of your application using flame graphs in Profiler.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Here's why:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Profiling provides function-level granularity:</strong> Profiling tools in Go allow pinpointing the exact functions within your application that consume the most CPU resources. This level of detail is crucial for focused optimization efforts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Here's why the other options aren't as suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Logs</strong> Logs might indicate performance issues, but they won't directly reveal which functions are consuming the most CPU time. Analyzing logs for this purpose can be cumbersome and indirect.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Cloud Monitoring dashboards</strong> Dashboards provide a high-level overview of CPU usage but don't offer the function-level detail needed to pinpoint the root cause of high CPU usage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. SSH and top</strong> The <code>top</code> command provides real-time process-level CPU usage but lacks the fine-grained analysis that profiling provides for pinpointing the most CPU-intensive functions within your Go application's code.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs\">https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e1q29":{"id":"e1q29","exam":1,"n":29,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You are developing a marquee stateless web application to run on Google Cloud. The incoming user traffic rate is expected to be unpredictable, with some days experiencing no traffic and others encountering large spikes. You need the application to automatically scale up and down while minimizing associated costs. What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Develop the application in Python with Firestore as the database. Deploy it to Cloud Run.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Develop the application in Python with Firestore as the database. Deploy it to a Compute Engine managed instance group with autoscaling.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Develop the application in Python with CloudSQL as the database. Deploy it to the App Engine standard environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Develop the application in C# with Firestore as the database. Deploy it to the App Engine flexible environment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Develop the application in Python with Firestore as the database. Deploy it to Cloud Run.</strong></p>\n<p>Let's break down why this is the ideal choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Serverless Cost-Efficiency:</strong> Cloud Run is a serverless platform that scales down to zero instances when there's no traffic, ensuring you only pay for resources when the app is active.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Rapid Scaling:</strong> Cloud Run can scale up very quickly in response to sudden traffic spikes, handling your unpredictable traffic patterns.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Stateless Design Alignment:</strong> Your description of a stateless application is a perfect fit for the serverless model of Cloud Run.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Firestore Integration:</strong> Firestore, as a NoSQL database, complements serverless paradigms with its scalability and ease of development.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. App Engine Flexible Environment:</strong> While suitable for web applications, the flexible environment has a minimum number of instances, incurring costs even during periods of inactivity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. App Engine Standard Environment:</strong> The standard environment doesn't scale down to zero, and Cloud SQL, as a managed SQL database, is not as ideal a fit for a serverless application as Firestore.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Compute Engine Managed Instance Groups:</strong> While Compute Engine offers autoscaling, it involves overhead in managing VMs and is less cost-effective for unpredictable workloads than a serverless solution like Cloud Run.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l1"]},"e1q30":{"id":"e1q30","exam":1,"n":30,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>Your team is responsible for developing services on Google Cloud. You're tasked with constructing a data processing service using Cloud Functions. However, the data being processed is sensitive, necessitating robust security measures. You must ensure that invocations originate only from authorized services, adhering to Google's recommended security practices for functions. What's the optimal approach to achieve this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Create a service account with the Cloud Functions Invoker role, and utilize that service account for invoking the function.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Create a service account with the Cloud Functions Viewer role, and utilize that service account for invoking the function.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Enable Identity-Aware Proxy in your project and secure function access using its permissions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Generate an OAuth 2.0 client ID for your calling service within the same project as the function you intend to secure, and utilize those credentials for invoking the function.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Create a service account with the Cloud Functions Invoker role, and utilize that service account for invoking the function.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's the breakdown:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> The Cloud Functions Invoker role grants the service account the specific permission needed to invoke the function, nothing more. This minimizes the risk should the service account credentials be compromised.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Identity and Access Management (IAM):</strong> Google Cloud's IAM system provides granular control over permissions assigned to service accounts, allowing you to precisely manage which services can invoke the function.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let's analyze the other options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Enable Identity-Aware Proxy...</strong> IAP is excellent for protecting web applications accessed by users, but it's less suitable for direct service-to-service authorization within Google Cloud.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Create a service account with the Cloud Functions Viewer role...</strong> The Cloud Functions Viewer role only grants read-only access to functions and their configurations, not the ability to invoke them.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Generate an OAuth 2.0 client ID...</strong> While OAuth 2.0 is a valid authorization mechanism, it adds a layer of complexity that might be unnecessary for simple service-to-service calls within the same project.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/reference/iam/roles#cloud-functions-roles  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/authenticating\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/reference/iam/roles#cloud-functions-roles</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/reference/iam/roles#cloud-functions-roles  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/authenticating\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/authenticating</a></p>","lessons":["d4l4"]},"e1q31":{"id":"e1q31","exam":1,"n":31,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>Your operations team requires a script for listing Cloud Bigtable, Memorystore, and Cloud SQL databases within a project. The script should accommodate user-defined filter expressions to narrow down the presented results. How should you retrieve the data?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Employ the HBase API, Redis API, and MySQL connection to fetch database lists. Independently filter the results, then merge them for display.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Execute <code>**gcloud bigtable instances list**</code>, <code>**gcloud redis instances list**</code>, and <code>**gcloud sql databases list**</code> commands. Apply the <code>**--filter**</code> flag with each command, then showcase the results.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize the HBase API, Redis API, and MySQL connection to fetch database lists. Merge the results and apply the filter for displaying.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Execute <code>**gcloud bigtable instances list**</code>, <code>**gcloud redis instances list**</code>, and <code>**gcloud sql databases list**</code> commands. Implement a filter within the application, then showcase the results.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Execute gcloud bigtable instances list, gcloud redis instances list, and gcloud sql databases list commands. Apply the --filter flag with each command, then showcase the results.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficiency and Simplicity:</strong> The <code>gcloud</code> CLI is the most efficient way to interact with Google Cloud services. These commands directly provide the desired information without complex API interactions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Filtering at the Source:</strong> The <code>--filter</code> flag for each command allows filtering during data retrieval. This is the most efficient approach, reducing processing required within your script.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Consistency:</strong> <code>gcloud</code> offers a consistent interface for these tasks, minimizing script complexity.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Disadvantages of other options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I &amp; II. Unnecessary Complexity:</strong> Using HBase, Redis, and MySQL APIs introduces unnecessary complexity. These APIs are designed for data interaction, not listing databases. Moreover, merging and filtering would add overhead to your script.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Filtering Overhead:</strong> Retrieving all data then filtering within the application is less efficient. It increases data transfer and puts more processing burden on your script.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/topic/filter\">https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/topic/filter</a>s</p>","lessons":["d4l11"]},"e1q32":{"id":"e1q32","exam":1,"n":32,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You manage a microservices application on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) using Istio. You secure the communication channels between your microservices by implementing an Istio AuthorizationPolicy, a Kubernetes NetworkPolicy, and mTLS on your GKE cluster. You discover that HTTP requests between two Pods to specific URLs fail, while other requests to other URLs succeed. What is the likely cause of the connection issue?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. The Authorization Policy within your cluster might be blocking HTTP requests for specific paths within your application.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. It's possible that the Pod initiating the HTTP requests is trying to connect to the target Pod via an incorrect TCP port.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. The cluster might have mTLS configured in permissive mode, but the Pod's sidecar proxy could be transmitting unencrypted traffic in plain text, causing the failure.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. The Kubernetes NetworkPolicy resource is likely blocking HTTP traffic between the Pods for specific URLs.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. The Authorization Policy within your cluster might be blocking HTTP requests for specific paths within your application.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Istio AuthorizationPolicy: Istio AuthorizationPolicy allows you to define fine-grained access control policies for services within your mesh. If certain HTTP requests between Pods are failing for specific URLs, it's possible that an AuthorizationPolicy is blocking access to those URLs. This could be due to misconfiguration or intentional access restrictions.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Options I, II, and IV are less likely to be the cause of the connection issue:</p>\n<p>I. While Kubernetes NetworkPolicy could potentially block HTTP traffic between Pods for specific URLs, it's less likely to be the cause of the issue if other HTTP requests to different URLs are succeeding. NetworkPolicy usually works at the network level and is not concerned with specific URLs.</p>\n<p>II. If the Pod initiating the HTTP requests is trying to connect to the target Pod via an incorrect TCP port, it would likely result in a connection error or timeout rather than only affecting specific URLs.</p>\n<p>IV. If mTLS is configured in permissive mode, it allows both encrypted and unencrypted traffic. However, this scenario is less likely to cause the failure of specific HTTP requests to certain URLs unless there's a specific rule or configuration related to those URLs causing the issue.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/security/authorization/authz-http/  https://istio.io/latest/docs/ops/common-problems/network-issues/#sending-https-to-an-http-port  https://cloud.google.com/service-mesh/docs/troubleshooting/troubleshoot-security#authorization_policy_denial_logging\">https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/security/authorization/authz-http/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/security/authorization/authz-http/  https://istio.io/latest/docs/ops/common-problems/network-issues/#sending-https-to-an-http-port  https://cloud.google.com/service-mesh/docs/troubleshooting/troubleshoot-security#authorization_policy_denial_logging\">https://istio.io/latest/docs/ops/common-problems/network-issues/#sending-https-to-an-http-port</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/security/authorization/authz-http/  https://istio.io/latest/docs/ops/common-problems/network-issues/#sending-https-to-an-http-port  https://cloud.google.com/service-mesh/docs/troubleshooting/troubleshoot-security#authorization_policy_denial_logging\">https://cloud.google.com/service-mesh/docs/troubleshooting/troubleshoot-security#authorization_policy_denial_logging </a></p>","lessons":["d3l10"]},"e1q33":{"id":"e1q33","exam":1,"n":33,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Your application exhibits significantly slower performance after deployment to a Compute Engine instance, despite performing well during local testing. What is the recommended course of action to diagnose the issue?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Utilize Cloud Debugger snapshots to examine a specific moment in the application's execution.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Employ Cloud Profiler to identify the functions within the application consuming the most time.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Integrate logging commands within the application code and leverage Cloud Logging to pinpoint the source of latency issues.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Submit a ticket to Cloud Support, highlighting the contrast in performance between local testing and deployment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Employ Cloud Profiler to identify the functions within the application consuming the most time.</strong></p>\n<p>Why Profiling is the First Line of Defense:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Pinpointing Bottlenecks:</strong> Cloud Profiler excels at identifying performance-intensive areas within your code. This quickly focuses your investigation on the root cause, rather than just symptoms.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Low Overhead:</strong> It often works with minimal code changes. Profiling production environments gives the most realistic performance picture.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Targeted Troubleshooting:</strong> Knowing which functions are slow helps you determine <em>why</em> they're slow (e.g., network calls, suboptimal algorithms, resource constraints on the VM).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When Other Options Become Relevant:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Utilize Cloud Debugger snapshots ...</strong> Debugger is great if you suspect the issue is related to incorrect application state or logic at specific points, but less likely to be the first step for <em>general</em> slowness.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Integrate logging ... Cloud Logging ...</strong> Logging is always useful, but if the core issue is a slow function, logs might show you the consequence of that (long requests), not the cause within the code itself.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Submit a ticket to Cloud Support ...</strong> Support can be helpful if the issue seems related to the underlying infrastructure (VM, network, etc.), but it's a good idea to gather performance data from your application first.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e1q34":{"id":"e1q34","exam":1,"n":34,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your website resides on Compute Engine. The marketing team wishes to assess conversion rates across three different website designs. What method should you adopt?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy the website on Cloud Functions, dividing it into three distinct functions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Host the website on App Engine and implement traffic splitting.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy the website on App Engine, dividing it into three separate services.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize Cloud Functions to host the website, employing traffic splitting.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Host the website on App Engine and implement traffic splitting.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>App Engine is the most suitable choice for this scenario for several reasons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Traffic Splitting:</strong> App Engine has built-in support for traffic splitting, allowing you to distribute traffic among different versions of your website (each with a different design) based on percentages you define.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> App Engine handles scaling automatically, ensuring your website can manage traffic surges during the experiment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ease of Use:</strong> App Engine provides a managed environment simplifying deployment and configuration.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Suited:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Deploy the website on App Engine, dividing it into three separate services.</strong> While technically possible, this adds unnecessary complexity to your setup and could potentially introduce inconsistencies across the separate services.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Utilize Cloud Functions to host the website, employing traffic splitting.</strong> Cloud Functions are better suited for individual, task-based functions rather than hosting entire websites. They lack the built-in traffic routing of App Engine.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Deploy the website on Cloud Functions, dividing it into three distinct functions.</strong> Similar to option III, this approach fractures your website logic and introduces potential overhead in communication between the functions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/python/splitting-traffic\">https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/python/splitting-traffic</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l6"]},"e1q35":{"id":"e1q35","exam":1,"n":35,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Identify the reason for the non-linear scalability of Spanner performance during a load test for an ecommerce application that stores customer, order, and inventory data as relational tables within Cloud Spanner.</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Opting for the LIKE keyword over the STARTS_WITH keyword in parameterized SQL queries.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilizing the STRING data type for arbitrary-precision values.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Implementing 64-bit numeric types for 32-bit numbers.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Employing Version 1 UUIDs as primary keys, leading to monotonically increasing values.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Employing Version 1 UUIDs as primary keys, leading to monotonically increasing values.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spanner Distribution:</strong> Spanner splits data into tablets and distributes them across servers for scalability. Monotonically increasing primary keys can lead to hotspotting, where inserts concentrate on a narrow range of tablets, affecting the load distribution and scalability.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let's analyze the other options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Implementing 64-bit numeric types for 32-bit numbers.</strong> While potentially less space-efficient, this alone is unlikely to cause significant scalability issues.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Utilizing the STRING data type for arbitrary-precision values.</strong> This can impact storage and query efficiency, but its effect on scalability is less direct compared to hotspotting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Opting for the LIKE keyword over the STARTS_WITH keyword in parameterized SQL queries.</strong> This would primarily affect query performance and less so Spanner's underlying scalability.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/schema-and-data-model#choosing_a_primary_key  https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/schema-design#primary-key-prevent-hotspots\">https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/schema-and-data-model#choosing_a_primary_key</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/schema-and-data-model#choosing_a_primary_key  https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/schema-design#primary-key-prevent-hotspots\">https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/schema-design#primary-key-prevent-hotspots</a></p>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e1q36":{"id":"e1q36","exam":1,"n":36,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your team manages an application that aggregates news articles from various sources. The monitoring dashboard, which provides publicly accessible real-time reports, operates as a web application on a Compute Engine instance. External stakeholders and analysts require secure access to these reports without the need for authentication. How should you configure this secure channel?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Employ an HTTP(S) load balancer in front of the monitoring dashboard and configure it with a Google-managed SSL certificate for encrypting traffic.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize Cloud Scheduler to trigger Cloud Build hourly, creating an export from the reports, and store them in a public Cloud Storage bucket.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Implement an HTTP(S) load balancer in front of the monitoring dashboard and configure Identity-Aware Proxy to secure the communication channel.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Assign a public IP address to the instance and employ the service account key of the instance to encrypt traffic.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Employ an HTTP(S) load balancer in front of the monitoring dashboard and configure it with a Google-managed SSL certificate for encrypting traffic.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this stands out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Publicly Accessible and Secure:</strong> An HTTP(S) load balancer provides a public-facing endpoint while ensuring that all traffic to the monitoring dashboard is encrypted in transit with SSL/TLS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Managed Certificates:</strong> Google-managed SSL certificates simplify setup and maintenance. They rotate automatically, ensuring security without manual intervention.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ease of Use:</strong> This approach doesn't require complex setup or authentication mechanisms (like Identity-Aware Proxy), making it easy for external stakeholders to access the reports.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> If the demand for the reports grows, the load balancer can scale to handle the increased traffic.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Assign a public IP address to the instance and employ the service account key of the instance to encrypt traffic.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This is insecure! Exposing a Compute Engine directly to the internet can create vulnerabilities. Also, managing encryption with service account keys adds unnecessary complexity.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Utilize Cloud Scheduler to trigger Cloud Build hourly, creating an export from the reports, and store them in a public Cloud Storage bucket.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This creates static reports, not a real-time monitoring dashboard as required by the scenario.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Implement an HTTP(S) load balancer in front of the monitoring dashboard and configure Identity-Aware Proxy to secure the communication channel.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Identity-Aware Proxy requires authentication, which you want to avoid for this publicly accessible dashboard.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>References:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/ssl-certificates/google-managed-certs\">https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/ssl-certificates/google-managed-certs</a></p>","lessons":["d1l8"]},"e1q37":{"id":"e1q37","exam":1,"n":37,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your application is live in production, but certain issues only manifest once the new version starts receiving traffic from users. To mitigate both the impact and the number of users affected, which deployment strategy should you employ?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Rolling deployment</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Recreate deployment</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Blue/green deployment</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Canary deployment</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Canary deployment</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Traffic Control:</strong> Canary deployments allow you to route a small percentage of real user traffic to the new version, while the majority still gets routed to the stable version.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimized Impact:</strong> Any issues arising in the new version affect only a limited subset of users, reducing the overall impact on your application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Live Testing:</strong> You can carefully monitor the new version under real-world conditions, detecting errors or performance issues before they affect your entire userbase.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Gradual Rollout:</strong> If the new version performs well, you can gradually increase the percentage of traffic routed to it, eventually replacing the old version.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Blue/green deployment:</strong> While offering a safe rollback option, blue/green involves switching entirely between versions. This lacks the gradual exposure offered by canary deployments when issues are subtle.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Rolling deployment:</strong> This sequentially updates instances, which can still affect a significant portion of users if an issue arises.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Recreate deployment:</strong> This has downtime involved and lacks the controlled testing that a canary deployment allows.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/application-deployment-and-testing-strategies#canary_test_pattern  https://cloud.google.com/architecture/implementing-deployment-and-testing-strategies-on-gke#perform_a_bluegreen_deployment\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/application-deployment-and-testing-strategies#canary_test_pattern</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/application-deployment-and-testing-strategies#canary_test_pattern  https://cloud.google.com/architecture/implementing-deployment-and-testing-strategies-on-gke#perform_a_bluegreen_deployment\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/implementing-deployment-and-testing-strategies-on-gke#perform_a_bluegreen_deployment</a></p>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e1q38":{"id":"e1q38","exam":1,"n":38,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You have an HTTP Cloud Function that's triggered by POST requests. Each request contains a flat, unnested JSON structure with both numerical and textual data. Post-execution, the aggregated data must be promptly accessible for comprehensive analytics by multiple users concurrently. How should you store the submitted data persistently?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Save each POST request's JSON data as an individual file within Cloud Storage, with the file name containing the request identifier.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Process the JSON data from the POST request and store it in a regional Cloud SQL cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Process the JSON data from the POST request and stream it into BigQuery.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Directly persist each POST request's JSON data into Datastore.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Process the JSON data from the POST request and stream it into BigQuery.</strong> Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Analytics Focus:</strong> BigQuery is explicitly designed for large-scale data analysis and querying. It excels at handling structured data (even from flat JSON) and enables complex analysis with SQL-like queries.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Real-Time Streaming:</strong> BigQuery's streaming ingestion allows data to be made available for analytics almost immediately after it's received by your Cloud Function, minimizing latency.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability &amp; Concurrent Access:</strong> BigQuery handles massive datasets, and its serverless nature means it scales seamlessly to support many concurrent analysis requests.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let's analyze the other options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Directly persist each POST request's JSON data into Datastore:</strong> Datastore is a NoSQL database better suited for storing and retrieving individual data entities with relationships. While it can store JSON, it's less optimal for large-scale, ad-hoc analytics involving complex aggregations.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Process the JSON data ... store it in a regional Cloud SQL cluster:</strong> Cloud SQL (MySQL, PostgreSQL) are relational databases. While they can handle JSON, their strength lies in storing highly structured data with well-defined relationships, not in the type of flexible analytics needed here.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Save each POST request's JSON data as an individual file...</strong> Cloud Storage is object storage. This approach creates overhead with individual files and makes analysis complex, requiring you to merge data across files before querying.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l10"]},"e1q39":{"id":"e1q39","exam":1,"n":39,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your company's product team has introduced a new requirement, prompted by customer demand, to implement autoscaling for your stateless and distributed service operating within a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster. Given the tight timeline of two weeks before this feature needs to be live, what would be the most suitable approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Implement a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler, with scaling determined by a custom metric.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Implement a Vertical Pod Autoscaler, with scaling determined by a custom metric.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Implement a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler, with scaling determined by the CPU load.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Implement a Vertical Pod Autoscaler, with scaling determined by the CPU load.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Implement a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler, with scaling determined by CPU load.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Here's a breakdown of why this is the best choice, and why the other options are less ideal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Horizontal vs. Vertical Scaling:</strong> Horizontal scaling (adding/removing replicas of your pods) is inherently better suited for distributed, stateless services than vertical scaling (changing resource allocation of existing pods). This is because horizontal scaling allows you to distribute load and handle more requests in parallel, a key goal of autoscaling.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>CPU Load vs. Custom Metrics:</strong> While custom metrics can be valuable, using CPU load as the scaling metric has a significant advantage in this scenario - <strong>speed of implementation</strong>. You can set up a CPU-based Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) within minutes. Custom metrics usually require defining the metric, ensuring monitoring is in place, and potentially additional code changes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Aren't as Suitable</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Vertical Pod Autoscaler (CPU Load):</strong> While vertical scaling can handle increased load, it has limits. There comes a point where a single pod can't handle any more load, and horizontal scaling becomes necessary.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Vertical Pod Autoscaler (Custom Metric):</strong> VPA with custom metrics could be a good long-term solution, but the two-week deadline makes implementation difficult.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (Custom Metric):</strong> This combines the potential complexity of custom metrics with horizontal scaling. While powerful, it's likely too complex for the timeframe.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/horizontalpodautosc\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/horizontalpodautosc</a>aler</p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e1q40":{"id":"e1q40","exam":1,"n":40,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Your company's development teams wish to employ Cloud Build in their projects to build and push Docker images to Container Registry. However, the operations team mandates that all Docker images must be published to a centralized and securely managed Docker registry they oversee. What's the best approach to address this requirement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Create a dedicated project for the operations team, equipped with Container Registry. Grant appropriate permissions to the Cloud Build service account in each developer team's project to enable access to the operations team's registry.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Set up a separate project for the operations team, integrating Container Registry. Generate a Service Account for each development team and allocate necessary permissions to access the operations team's registry. Store the service account key file in the source code repository for authentication against the registry.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Establish Container Registry within each development team's project. Configure Cloud Build builds to push Docker images to the respective project's registry. Provide the operations team access to each team's registry.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy an open-source Docker Registry on a Compute Engine virtual machine instance within a distinct project for the operations team. Assign each development team a unique username and password, storing these credentials in the source code repository for authentication against the operations team's Docker registry.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Create a dedicated project for the operations team, equipped with Container Registry. Grant appropriate permissions to the Cloud Build service account in each developer team's project to enable access to the operations team's registry.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach excels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Centralization:</strong> A single registry under the operations team's control provides a unified location for managing and governing Docker images.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> IAM permissions ensure fine-grained access control to the centralized registry, aligning with operational security requirements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Service Accounts:</strong> Using the Cloud Build service account for authentication is streamlined and avoids the security risks of storing credentials in source code repositories.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Maintainability:</strong> Simplifies the setup and reduces complexity for development teams.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Establish Container Registry within each development team's project ...</strong> This creates management overhead for the operations team, who would have to maintain access to numerous registries.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. ... Generate a Service Account for each development team ...</strong> Storing the key file in the repository is a significant security risk and creates more management complexity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Deploy an open-source Docker Registry on a Compute Engine virtual machine instance ...</strong> This increases operational overhead compared to using a managed service like Container Registry and introduces additional security considerations in self-managing a registry.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e1q41":{"id":"e1q41","exam":1,"n":41,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>To ensure the security of a secret in your application deployed on a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster, preventing its exposure via calls to the Kubernetes API server, what is the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Place the secret in Secret Manager, then establish a Google service account authorized to access the secret from Secret Manager. Generate a Kubernetes service account for the container, and employ Workload Identity to authenticate as the Google service account, ensuring protected access to the secret without vulnerability to Kubernetes API calls.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy GKE Application-layer Secrets Encryption on the cluster using a Cloud Key Management Service (KMS) key, guaranteeing that secrets are encrypted at the application layer, thereby averting exposure through API calls to the Kubernetes server.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Safeguard the secret in Cloud KMS, then configure a Google service account with authorization to access the secret from Cloud KMS. Export the service account key in JSON format, and attach the JSON file to the container as a ConfigMap volume, enabling secure retrieval of the secret from Cloud KMS.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Create a Kubernetes Secret and transmit the secret as an environment variable to the container, which could potentially risk exposing it through logs or other channels, thus jeopardizing its security.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The recommended approach to ensure the security of a secret in your application deployed on a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster, preventing its exposure via calls to the Kubernetes API server, is:</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Place the secret in Secret Manager, then establish a Google service account authorized to access the secret from Secret Manager. Generate a Kubernetes service account for the container, and employ Workload Identity to authenticate as the Google service account, ensuring protected access to the secret without vulnerability to Kubernetes API calls.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Option IV suggests using Google Cloud's Secret Manager to store the secret, which is designed specifically for securely storing and managing secrets. By establishing a Google service account with appropriate permissions to access the secret from Secret Manager, and then using Workload Identity to authenticate as the Google service account, the application can securely retrieve the secret without directly exposing it to the Kubernetes API server.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option I suggests transmitting the secret as an environment variable to the container, which can risk exposure through logs or other channels, making it less secure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option II mentions deploying GKE Application-layer Secrets Encryption, which can help encrypt secrets at the application layer, but it doesn't specifically address preventing exposure via calls to the Kubernetes API server.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option III involves safeguarding the secret in Cloud KMS and configuring a service account to access it, but exporting the service account key as a JSON file and attaching it to the container as a ConfigMap volume might introduce additional security risks compared to using Secret Manager and Workload Identity.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, Option IV provides the most robust and secure approach for protecting secrets in a GKE environment.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity  https://cloud.google.com/kms/docs  https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/  https://cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity  https://cloud.google.com/kms/docs  https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/  https://cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/kms/docs</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity  https://cloud.google.com/kms/docs  https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/  https://cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/overview\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity  https://cloud.google.com/kms/docs  https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/  https://cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e1q42":{"id":"e1q42","exam":1,"n":42,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You've constructed multiple Cloud Functions in Java, accompanied by integration and service tests. Utilizing Cloud Build, you deploy these functions and initiate tests. However, your Cloud Build job flags deployment failures right after validating the code. What steps should you take to address this issue?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Review the maximum number of Cloud Function instances.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Confirm that the Cloud Build service account holds the Cloud Functions Developer role.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Attempt the tests again, employing the truncated exponential backoff polling strategy.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Ensure that your Cloud Build trigger is configured with accurate build parameters.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Confirm that the Cloud Build service account holds the Cloud Functions Developer role.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Importance of Service Account Permissions:</strong> Cloud Functions deployment involves resource creation and modification, permissions for which are held by service accounts. The Cloud Functions Developer role is specifically designed to provide the essential set of permissions for Cloud Functions management and deployment. If the Cloud Build service account lacks this role, it won't have adequate authorization, thus impeding the deployment process even if the code itself is valid.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Unsuitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Review the maximum number of Cloud Function instances:</strong> While important for scalability and cost management, this wouldn't prevent a deployment failure right after code validation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Ensure that your Cloud Build trigger is configured with accurate build parameters:</strong> Incorrect build parameters might lead to issues in the build process, but the question implies the failure occurs during deployment specifically, pointing towards a permissions problem.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Attempt the tests again, employing the truncated exponential backoff polling strategy.:</strong> This approach addresses transient errors but wouldn't resolve a fundamental lack of permissions in the service account.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/configure-access-for-cloud-build-service-account#granting_a_role_using_the_iam_page   https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/troubleshooting#build_trigger_fails_due_to_missing_cloudbuildbuildscreate_permission  https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/configure-access-for-cloud-build-service-account\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/configure-access-for-cloud-build-service-account#granting_a_role_using_the_iam_page https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/troubleshooting#build_trigger_fails_due_to_missing_cloudbuildbuildscreate_permission</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/configure-access-for-cloud-build-service-account#granting_a_role_using_the_iam_page   https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/troubleshooting#build_trigger_fails_due_to_missing_cloudbuildbuildscreate_permission  https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/configure-access-for-cloud-build-service-account\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/configure-access-for-cloud-build-service-account</a></p>","lessons":["d3l8"]},"e1q43":{"id":"e1q43","exam":1,"n":43,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>How should you implement a solution on Google Kubernetes Engine to configure each microservice with a specific number of replicas and ensure uniform addressing within the cluster?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>D. Deploying microservices as Pods without leveraging Deployments can hinder scalability and management. Expose the Pods within the cluster using an Ingress, and depend on the Ingress IP address for addressing, potentially leading to inconsistent access as the number of replicas scales.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Directly deploying each microservice as a Pod limits scalability and management capabilities. Expose the Pods within the cluster through a Service, and utilize the Service DNS name for addressing, ensuring uniform access from other microservices within the cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>A. Deploy each microservice as a Deployment, which facilitates easy scaling and replica management. Expose the Deployment within the cluster via a Service, and utilize the Service DNS name for addressing the microservices from other microservices, ensuring consistent access regardless of the number of replicas.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Use Deployments for each microservice to manage replicas efficiently. Expose the Deployments within the cluster using an Ingress, and use the Ingress IP address to address the microservices from other microservices within the cluster, maintaining a consistent addressing mechanism despite replica scaling.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct option is:</p>\n<p><strong>A. Deploy each microservice as a Deployment, which facilitates easy scaling and replica management. Expose the Deployment within the cluster via a Service, and utilize the Service DNS name for addressing the microservices from other microservices, ensuring consistent access regardless of the number of replicas.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Option A suggests using Deployments for each microservice, which allows easy scaling and management of replicas. Services should be used to expose the Deployments within the cluster. This ensures that the microservices can be addressed consistently using the Service DNS name regardless of the number of replicas. This approach provides uniform addressing within the cluster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option B suggests using Ingress to expose the Deployments within the cluster, but Ingress is typically used for routing external traffic to services within the cluster, not for internal communication between microservices.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option C recommends directly deploying each microservice as a Pod, which limits scalability and management capabilities compared to using Deployments. While it suggests exposing the Pods within the cluster through a Service, it doesn't mention utilizing Service DNS for addressing, which could lead to inconsistent access.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option D also suggests deploying microservices as Pods without leveraging Deployments, which hinders scalability and management. Additionally, using Ingress for addressing internal services is not a recommended practice and can lead to inconsistent access, especially as the number of replicas scales.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, Option A is the most suitable approach for configuring each microservice with a specific number of replicas and ensuring uniform addressing within the cluster.</p>\n<p>References:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/service\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/service</a></p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e1q44":{"id":"e1q44","exam":1,"n":44,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You're developing a microservice-based application intended for deployment on a Google Kubernetes Engine cluster. This application requires both reading from and writing to a Spanner database. Your objective is to ensure adherence to security best practices while minimizing code adjustments. How should you configure the application to access Spanner credentials?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Safeguard the application credentials using Cloud Key Management Service and retrieve them as needed when establishing a database connection.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Configure suitable routing rules and utilize a VPC-native cluster to establish a direct connection to the database.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Protect the application credentials as Kubernetes Secrets and expose them as environment variables.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Establish the required service accounts and employ Workload Identity to execute the pods.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Establish the required service accounts and employ Workload Identity to execute the pods.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the most secure and streamlined approach:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> Workload Identity eliminates the need to directly manage Spanner credentials within your pods or Kubernetes Secrets. Google handles the secure binding of service accounts and provides temporary tokens.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> You can associate each microservice with a specific service account that has only the necessary permissions on the Spanner database, adhering to the principle of least privilege.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimal Code Changes:</strong> Workload Identity integration often requires minimal adjustments to your application code, as the authentication flow is handled by the underlying Kubernetes environment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Best Practice:</strong> This is the recommended approach by Google for secure access to GCP services from GKE.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Kubernetes Secrets:</strong> While Secrets help improve over storing credentials directly in code, they still introduce a layer of management and potential exposure within the cluster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. VPC-native Cluster:</strong> Network configuration impacts connectivity but doesn't directly address how your application handles credentials.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Cloud KMS:</strong> Cloud KMS is great for encryption but adds complexity compared to Workload Identity for this scenario. Your application would still need a mechanism to retrieve and manage the decrypted credentials.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/introducing-workload-identity-better-authentication-for-your-gke-applications  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/connect-kubernetes-engine#secrets   https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity\">https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/introducing-workload-identity-better-authentication-for-your-gke-applications</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/introducing-workload-identity-better-authentication-for-your-gke-applications  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/connect-kubernetes-engine#secrets   https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity\">https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/connect-kubernetes-engine#secrets </a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/introducing-workload-identity-better-authentication-for-your-gke-applications  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/connect-kubernetes-engine#secrets   https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity</a></p>","lessons":["d4l2","d4l9"]},"e1q45":{"id":"e1q45","exam":1,"n":45,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>As the sole developer available over the weekend, your manager tasks you with making an emergency update to a website for your startup's web development team. Your development environment relies on Google Cloud services like Cloud Storage and Cloud Build, with version control managed through Git. Without your work laptop, you cannot store source code locally on a non-corporate computer. How should you configure your developer environment?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Store the necessary source code in a Cloud Storage bucket. Mount the bucket as a drive on a public computer and use a code editor to make the required updates. Enable versioning for the bucket and link it to the team's Git repository.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize a text editor and Git command line to submit source code updates as pull requests from a public computer.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Employ a text editor and Git command line to submit source code updates as pull requests from a virtual machine running on a public computer.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize Cloud Shell and its built-in code editor for development tasks, sending source code updates as pull requests.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Utilize Cloud Shell and its built-in code editor for development tasks, sending source code updates as pull requests.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the best choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Accessibility:</strong> Cloud Shell is readily available from any modern web browser, eliminating the need to install software locally.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Integration:</strong> Cloud Shell provides integrated access to your GCP project's resources, including Cloud Storage, Cloud Build, and your Git repository.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> Your code stays within the secure Google Cloud environment. You don't risk storing sensitive source code on a public computer.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Familiar Tools:</strong> Cloud Shell's code editor and command-line tools provide a familiar development experience.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options have issues:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Public Computer (Basic Text Editor + Git):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security Risk:</strong> Storing or cloning the project on a public computer compromises the security of your source code.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Limited Tools:</strong> Basic text editors may lack the features you need for effective development.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Public Computer (VM + Text Editor + Git):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Complexity:</strong> Requires setting up a VM, which adds overhead and time pressure to your task.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security Risk:</strong> Still introduces the security concerns of storing code on a public machine.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Cloud Storage Mount with Versioning:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Not ideal for Git:</strong> While versioning helps, managing a Git project this way is cumbersome and prone to errors.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Local Editing:</strong> Still requires a capable code editor on the public computer.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/shell/docs\">https://cloud.google.com/shell/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e1q46":{"id":"e1q46","exam":1,"n":46,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>After deploying your application on Google Kubernetes Engine, you now seek the capability to swiftly revert to the prior version if complications arise with the new release. Which deployment model is most suitable for your scenario?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Opt for a blue/green deployment strategy and test the new application after the deployment process is finalized.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Implement A/B testing and periodically test the application after integrating the new features.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Execute a rolling deployment and conduct testing of the new application post-deployment completion.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Employ a canary deployment approach and periodically test the new application after deploying the updated version.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Opt for a blue/green deployment strategy and test the new application after the deployment process is finalized.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Swift Rollback:</strong> Blue/green deployments offer a core advantage – the ability to quickly switch back to the previous version (blue) if the new version (green) exhibits issues. This aligns directly with your need for rapid reversions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Complete Pre-Release Testing:</strong> Before switching traffic to the new version, the blue/green strategy allows for comprehensive testing of the new application in a production-like environment. This minimizes the risk of surprises.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Clear Separation:</strong> This model maintains two separate environments (blue and green), reducing the risk of interference between old and new versions during testing.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Execute a rolling deployment and conduct testing of the new application post-deployment completion.</strong> While rolling updates are effective for gradual rollout, if issues are found after the deployment is complete, rollback can be more complex and disruptive.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Implement A/B testing and periodically test the application after integrating the new features.</strong> A/B testing focuses primarily on comparing versions for user experience or conversion purposes. It doesn't inherently provide the quick, full rollback mechanism of blue/green.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Employ a canary deployment approach and periodically test the new application after deploying the updated version.</strong> Canary deployments help with gradual rollout and testing but introduce an additional layer of complexity if you need a swift, complete rollback.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/application-deployment-and-testing-strategies#choosing_the_right_strategy\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/application-deployment-and-testing-strategies#choosing_the_right_strategy</a></p>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e1q47":{"id":"e1q47","exam":1,"n":47,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>As you develop a Compute Engine-hosted application in project A, you need to establish secure authentication to a Cloud Pub/Sub topic in project B. What should be your approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Equip the instances with a service account from project A, and authorize this service account as a publisher directly on the Cloud Pub/Sub topic.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Application Default Credentials with the private key of a service account from project A, and designate this service account as a publisher directly on the Cloud Pub/Sub topic in project B.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Set up the instances with a service account from project B, then grant this service account publisher permissions on the Cloud Pub/Sub topic in project A.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Configure Application Default Credentials to utilize the private key of a service account from project B, and assign this service account publisher rights in project A's Cloud Pub/Sub.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Equip the instances with a service account from project A, and authorize this service account as a publisher directly on the Cloud Pub/Sub topic.</strong></p>\n<p>Let's break down why this approach excels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> Granting the service account in project A (where your application runs), the <code>roles/pubsub.publisher</code> role on the topic in project B aligns with the principle of least privilege.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Identity Ownership:</strong> Project A, being the resource owner, naturally controls the identities used within its environment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Application Default Credentials (ADC):</strong> ADCs simplify authentication management within your application code. They leverage the service account attached to the Compute Engine instance.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Drawbacks of Other Options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Set up the instances with a service account from project B ...</strong><br />\nThis creates a reverse dependency, where project B would manage credentials used by resources in project A, complicating management.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III &amp; IV. ... Utilize Application Default Credentials with the private key of a service account ...</strong> Manually managing private keys introduces security risks and operational complexity compared to relying on ADCs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/access-control\">https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/access-control</a></p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e1q48":{"id":"e1q48","exam":1,"n":48,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You aim to move files from an on-premises virtual machine to Google Cloud Storage for utilization within a Cloud DataProc Hadoop cluster in a GCP environment. Which command is the appropriate choice for this task?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. <code>**gsutil cp [LOCAL_OBJECT] gs://[DESTINATION_BUCKET_NAME]/**</code></p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. <code>**gcloud dataproc cp [LOCAL_OBJECT] gs://[DESTINATION_BUCKET_NAME]/**</code></p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. <code>**gcloud cp [LOCAL_OBJECT] gs://[DESTINATION_BUCKET_NAME]/**</code></p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. <code>**hadoop fs cp [LOCAL_OBJECT] gs://[DESTINATION_BUCKET_NAME]/**</code></p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. gsutil cp [LOCAL_OBJECT] gs://[DESTINATION_BUCKET_NAME]/</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>gsutil</code> is the command-line tool specifically designed to interact with Google Cloud Storage. It's the most efficient and reliable way to transfer files between your local machine and Google Cloud Storage buckets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Not Suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. gcloud cp [LOCAL_OBJECT] gs://[DESTINATION_BUCKET_NAME]/</strong> While the <code>gcloud</code> tool has a <code>cp</code> command, it's intended for more general-purpose copying operations within the Google Cloud environment. For interacting specifically with Cloud Storage, <code>gsutil</code> is the preferred tool.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. hadoop fs cp [LOCAL_OBJECT] gs://[DESTINATION_BUCKET_NAME]/</strong> The <code>hadoop fs</code> command with a <code>gs://</code> destination can work, but it requires the Google Cloud Storage connector for Hadoop. This adds a layer of setup and might be less efficient than using <code>gsutil</code> directly.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. gcloud dataproc cp [LOCAL_OBJECT] gs://[DESTINATION_BUCKET_NAME]/</strong> The <code>gcloud dataproc</code> tool doesn't have a <code>cp</code> command. It's designed for managing Cloud Dataproc clusters, not general data transfer tasks.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/uploading-objects\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/uploading-objects</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8","d4l11"]},"e1q49":{"id":"e1q49","exam":1,"n":49,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>To efficiently scan container images for Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs) without impeding software development agility using managed services, which approach should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Activate the Container Analysis API to conduct vulnerability scans on images stored in Artifact Registry, enabling seamless integration of vulnerability scanning into the container image management workflow.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Utilize Cloud Monitoring to evaluate the outcomes of Cloud Build and identify if a vulnerable version has been utilized, which may provide insights into potential vulnerabilities but does not offer direct vulnerability scanning of container images.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Restrict the usage of non-commercially supported base images within your development environment, which is a proactive measure but does not address the need for automated vulnerability scanning.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Establish a Cloud Run service triggered by code check-ins to scan the code for CVEs, which may offer continuous monitoring but might not directly address vulnerabilities within container images.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The most efficient approach to scan container images for Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs) without impeding software development agility using managed services is:</p>\n<p><strong>A. Activate the Container Analysis API to conduct vulnerability scans on images stored in Artifact Registry, enabling seamless integration of vulnerability scanning into the container image management workflow.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Option A suggests activating the Container Analysis API to conduct vulnerability scans on images stored in Artifact Registry. This approach directly addresses the need for automated vulnerability scanning of container images within the container image management workflow. It seamlessly integrates vulnerability scanning without impeding software development agility.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option B involves establishing a Cloud Run service triggered by code check-ins to scan the code for CVEs. While continuous monitoring is valuable, this approach does not directly address vulnerabilities within container images, which are a crucial aspect of container security.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option C proposes restricting the usage of non-commercially supported base images within the development environment as a proactive measure. While this is important for security, it does not automate vulnerability scanning of container images, which is necessary for efficient and timely detection of vulnerabilities.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option D suggests utilizing Cloud Monitoring to evaluate the outcomes of Cloud Build and identify if a vulnerable version has been utilized. While this provides insights into potential vulnerabilities, it does not offer direct vulnerability scanning of container images as requested.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, Option A provides the most efficient approach by seamlessly integrating vulnerability scanning into the container image management workflow using managed services.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/container-registry/docs/get-image-vulnerabilities#viewing_vulnerabilities_and_other_occurrences  https://cloud.google.com/container-registry/docs/container-analysis\">https://cloud.google.com/container-registry/docs/get-image-vulnerabilities#viewing_vulnerabilities_and_other_occurrences</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/container-registry/docs/get-image-vulnerabilities#viewing_vulnerabilities_and_other_occurrences  https://cloud.google.com/container-registry/docs/container-analysis\">https://cloud.google.com/container-registry/docs/container-analysis</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e1q50":{"id":"e1q50","exam":1,"n":50,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>Your application collects user input and publishes it to the user's contacts. This input is stored in a Cloud Spanner table. Given that your application prioritizes latency over consistency, how should you handle reads from Cloud Spanner?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Conduct stale reads using single-read methods.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Carry out stale reads using read-write transactions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Execute strong reads using single-read methods.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Perform Read-Only transactions.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Conduct stale reads using single-read methods.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Since your application prioritizes fast reads and can tolerate slightly outdated data, stale reads offer the best balance:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Low Latency:</strong> Stale reads can often be served directly from a replica without the coordination required for strong reads, reducing overall latency.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Read Scalability:</strong> Stale reads help scale under high read loads by reducing contention on the Spanner leader.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Single-Read Methods:</strong> These methods are the most efficient way to perform reads, adding minimal overhead.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Perform Read-Only transactions.</strong> Read-only transactions still offer consistency guarantees that might involve contacting the Spanner leader, adding potential overhead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Execute strong reads using single-read methods.</strong> These ensure the latest data but introduce the potential for increased latency due to leader communication.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Carry out stale reads using read-write transactions.</strong> Read-write transactions are intended for scenarios where you need both reads and writes within the same transaction.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/reference/rest/v1/TransactionOptions  https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/reads\">https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/reference/rest/v1/TransactionOptions</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/reference/rest/v1/TransactionOptions  https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/reads\">https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/reads</a></p>","lessons":["d4l9"]},"e1q51":{"id":"e1q51","exam":1,"n":51,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>How can you enable your application, developed within Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), to access Google Cloud services across various projects?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize GKE role-based access control (RBAC) with a designated Google service account.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Configure a Google service account for the GKE nodes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Securely store the Google service account credentials within a Kubernetes Secret.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Implement Workload Identity with a designated Google service account for running the Pod.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Implement Workload Identity with a designated Google service account for running the Pod.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the most secure and streamlined approach:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Identity-based Access:</strong> Workload Identity eliminates the need to manage long-lived credentials within your Kubernetes cluster. It binds Kubernetes Service Accounts (KSAs) to Google Service Accounts (GSAs), granting fine-grained IAM permissions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> You can associate each Pod with a specific GSA, adhering to the principle of least privilege within GCP.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Best Practice:</strong> Google explicitly recommends Workload Identity for secure access to Google Cloud services from GKE.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. GKE Node Service Account:</strong> This grants access to the <em>entire node,</em> not just individual applications, violating the least privilege principle.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Kubernetes Secrets:</strong> While secrets are helpful, they still introduce the risk of credentials being exposed within the cluster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. GKE RBAC:</strong> GKE RBAC controls permissions <em>within</em> the Kubernetes cluster, not access to external Google Cloud services.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>References:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity#what_is\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity#what_is\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity#what_is</a></p>","lessons":["d4l2"]},"e1q52":{"id":"e1q52","exam":1,"n":52,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>In your role as a developer collaborating with the CI/CD team, you're tasked with troubleshooting a new feature recently introduced by your team. Utilizing HashiCorp Packer, the CI/CD team created a new Compute Engine image from your development branch. Despite a successful build, the image fails to boot up. What's the appropriate step to investigate the issue with the CI/CD team?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Initiate a new feature branch and request the build team to recompile the image.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Install Packer on your local environment, build the Compute Engine image locally, and then test it within your personal Google Cloud project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Analyze the Compute Engine OS logs via the serial port and verify access to the serial port by examining the Cloud Logging logs.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Halt the deployed virtual machine, export the disk, and subsequently mount the disk locally to access the boot logs.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Analyze the Compute Engine OS logs via the serial port and verify access to the serial port by examining the Cloud Logging logs.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the best approach:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Direct Problem Diagnosis:</strong> The serial port provides access to boot logs generated by the operating system installed on the image. These logs frequently reveal the root cause of boot failures.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Logging Integration:</strong> Cloud Logging captures serial port output, making it easy to access and analyze the logs even if the VM doesn't fully boot.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Troubleshooting Focus:</strong> This approach focuses on identifying the source of the issue, allowing for a targeted fix.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. New Feature Branch:</strong> This might be necessary later but is premature without identifying the root cause of the issue.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Mounting the Disk:</strong> While potentially useful, this is more complex than accessing serial port logs and might not reveal the issue if it's related to early boot processes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Replicating Locally:</strong> This could be helpful but may not fully reproduce the CI/CD team's environment and can be time-consuming.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/troubleshooting/troubleshooting-using-serial-console  https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/troubleshooting/vm-startup#identify_the_reason_why_the_boot_disk_isnt_booting\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/troubleshooting/troubleshooting-using-serial-console</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/troubleshooting/troubleshooting-using-serial-console  https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/troubleshooting/vm-startup#identify_the_reason_why_the_boot_disk_isnt_booting\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/troubleshooting/vm-startup#identify_the_reason_why_the_boot_disk_isnt_booting</a></p>","lessons":["d3l11"]},"e1q53":{"id":"e1q53","exam":1,"n":53,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You're managing an analytics application that executes numerous queries on BigQuery via the BigQuery API at regular intervals. How can you determine the execution time of these queries?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Stackdriver Monitoring to monitor query execution times.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize Stackdriver Monitoring to visualize slot usage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize Stackdriver Trace to monitor query execution time.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Employ Stackdriver Trace to track API execution time.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Utilize Stackdriver Monitoring to monitor query execution times.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Query-Centric Metrics:</strong> Stackdriver Monitoring (now called Cloud Monitoring) provides metrics specifically tailored to BigQuery, including query execution time. Using it allows you to track and visualize this information as time-series data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Alerts and Analysis:</strong> You can set alerts based on query execution time thresholds and analyze trends over time to identify performance bottlenecks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>BigQuery Integration:</strong> These metrics are collected directly from BigQuery by Stackdriver Monitoring.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Not Ideal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Utilize Stackdriver Monitoring to visualize slot usage.</strong> Slot usage shows your BigQuery query resource utilization, which can <em>indirectly</em> relate to performance, but isn't a direct measure of execution time.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II &amp; III. Utilize Stackdriver Trace...</strong> Stackdriver Trace is primarily used for distributed tracing and performance profiling within applications. While you might instrument your application code to include parts that interact with BigQuery, it's unnecessary overhead for simply measuring query execution time.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference</strong> :</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/monitoring\">https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/monitoring</a></p>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e1q54":{"id":"e1q54","exam":1,"n":54,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>In developing an application to handle sensitive unstructured data objects stored in a Cloud Storage bucket, you must ensure compliance with regulatory mandates dictating that all data objects remain accessible for a minimum of 7 years from their inception. However, objects older than 3 years are rarely accessed. Your objective is to configure object storage while optimizing storage costs. What actions should you take? (Select two.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Utilize IAM Conditions to grant access to objects based on a 7-year period following object creation.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Establish an object lifecycle policy on the bucket to transition objects from Standard Storage to Archive Storage after 3 years.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Enable Object Versioning to safeguard objects against accidental deletion for 7 years after their creation.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Implement a retention policy on the bucket with a duration of 7 years.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>V. Develop a Cloud Function to assess the age of each object in the bucket and relocate objects older than 3 years to a second bucket employing the Archive Storage class. Employ Cloud Scheduler to trigger the Cloud Function on a daily basis.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The two best actions to take in this scenario are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Implement a retention policy on the bucket with a duration of 7 years.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Establish an object lifecycle policy on the bucket to transition objects from Standard Storage to Archive Storage after 3 years.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here's the breakdown:</p>\n<p><strong>Why these options are essential:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Regulatory Compliance:</strong> A 7-year retention policy directly addresses the mandatory requirement to keep data accessible for a minimum of 7 years, ensuring your application remains compliant.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Cost Optimization:</strong> Object lifecycle management transitions infrequently used objects (older than 3 years) to the more cost-effective Archive Storage class, reducing your storage bill without sacrificing data access.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options aren't as suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Utilize IAM Conditions to grant access to objects based on a 7-year period following object creation.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>IAM Conditions control access but don't guarantee the data will be retained or prevent accidental deletion.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Enable Object Versioning to safeguard objects against accidental deletion for 7 years after their creation.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Object Versioning offers a powerful safeguard, but it might increase storage costs due to the retention of previous versions. Also, it doesn't directly address the need for cost optimization through tiered storage.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>V. Custom Cloud Function &amp; Scheduler</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While technically feasible, this introduces complexity and development overhead compared to native Object Lifecycle Management's built-in functionality.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/bucket-lock\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/bucket-lock</a></p>","lessons":["d1l5"]},"e1q55":{"id":"e1q55","exam":1,"n":55,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>Which tool should you employ to construct an architecture that authenticates, enforces quotas, and tracks metrics for API callers?</strong></p>\n<p>**</p>\n<p>**</p>\n<p><strong>Diagram showing a Google Cloud architecture. An arrow from a “computer” to “Cloud Load Balancing”. An arrow from “Cloud Load Balancing” to an empty box. An arrow from the empty box to “PHP application”.</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>C. Consider Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP) for securing your APIs by verifying user identities and controlling access based on their permissions, while also offering features for monitoring and reporting API usage metrics.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Leverage GKE Ingress for HTTP(S) Load Balancing to configure routing rules and manage traffic efficiently to your PHP application, while incorporating additional layers of security and metrics reporting as per your requirements.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Choose Cloud Endpoints to streamline the process of building, deploying, and managing APIs with robust security features, including authentication mechanisms, quota enforcement capabilities, and comprehensive metric tracking for API usage.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>A. Opt for Cloud Run to establish a scalable and fully managed environment for deploying containerized applications, ensuring efficient execution and seamless integration with authentication, quota enforcement, and metric reporting functionalities.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>B. Choose Cloud Endpoints to streamline the process of building, deploying, and managing APIs with robust security features, including authentication mechanisms, quota enforcement capabilities, and comprehensive metric tracking for API usage.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why Cloud Endpoints is the optimal choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Authentication:</strong> Cloud Endpoints offers built-in authentication mechanisms like OAuth 2.0, API keys, and IAM roles, allowing you to control access to your API.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Quota Enforcement:</strong> Cloud Endpoints enables you to define quotas and rate limits for API calls, preventing misuse and ensuring resource optimization.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Metric Tracking:</strong> Cloud Endpoints provides comprehensive metrics and logging capabilities that give you insights into API usage patterns and performance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Integration with Cloud Load Balancing:</strong> Cloud Endpoints seamlessly integrates with Cloud Load Balancing, which is already present in your architecture, for efficient traffic management.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A. Cloud Run:</strong> While Cloud Run is a great platform for deploying containerized applications, it lacks the built-in security features like authentication and quota enforcement that Cloud Endpoints offers. You'd need additional tools to achieve these functionalities.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C. Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP):</strong> IAP primarily focuses on user identity verification and access control at the perimeter level. While it can provide some usage metrics, it's not as comprehensive as Cloud Endpoints for API-specific metrics and quota enforcement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>D. GKE Ingress:</strong> GKE Ingress is a load balancing solution for containerized applications deployed on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). It doesn't have built-in functionalities for authentication, quota enforcement, or detailed API metrics tracking.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/quotas-configure  https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/monitoring-your-api\">https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/quotas-configure</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/quotas-configure  https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/monitoring-your-api\">https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/monitoring-your-api</a></p>","lessons":["d4l5"]},"e1q56":{"id":"e1q56","exam":1,"n":56,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your application deployed on Compute Engine is encountering persistent failures with a limited number of requests. Upon investigation, you've identified the problem to a particular Compute Engine instance. However, SSH access to the instance is unresponsive. What should be your next course of action?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Capture a disk snapshot and link it to a new instance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Restart the machine.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Activate and inspect the serial port output.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Terminate the instance and create a replacement.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The best course of action in this scenario would be to:</p>\n<p><strong>II. Activate and inspect the serial port output.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Unresponsive SSH:</strong> This indicates a potential issue at the operating system level or deeper. Inspecting the serial port output can provide valuable insights into the underlying problem, such as kernel panics, boot failures, or other critical errors that might not be visible through other means.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Limited Impact:</strong> The problem is isolated to a single instance, so actions like restarting the machine or terminating it might disrupt the service unnecessarily.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis First:</strong> Before taking any drastic action, it's crucial to understand the root cause of the failure. The serial port output can help diagnose the problem accurately, allowing you to take targeted corrective measures.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other options and why they might not be the best first step:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Restart the machine:</strong> This might temporarily resolve the issue if it's a minor glitch, but it won't help diagnose the underlying problem, and the issue could recur.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Terminate the instance and create a replacement:</strong> This is a more drastic step that should be considered only after exhausting other troubleshooting options. It might lead to data loss if not handled carefully.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Capture a disk snapshot and link it to a new instance:</strong> This is a good practice for preserving data, but it doesn't address the root cause of the failure. The new instance might encounter the same problem if the underlying issue is not resolved.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>In summary, activating and inspecting the serial port output is the most logical first step to diagnose the problem on the unresponsive Compute Engine instance.</strong></p>","lessons":["d3l11"]},"e1q57":{"id":"e1q57","exam":1,"n":57,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>To ensure proper authentication of services accessing various Google Cloud APIs within a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster, adhering to Google's recommended best practices, which approaches should you implement? (Choose two)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>V. Utilize gcloud to bind Google service accounts and Kubernetes service accounts using roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser, which is a feasible approach but not as endorsed as enabling Workload Identity directly on the cluster.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Storing Google service account keys in Secret Manager (as mentioned) could be a method, but it's not a recommended best practice by Google for authenticating services within a GKE cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Employ the service account linked to the GKE node, enabling services to authenticate with Google Cloud APIs using the node's identity.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Accessing Google service account keys from Secret Manager (as mentioned) could be a method, but it's not a recommended best practice by Google for authenticating services within a GKE cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Enable Workload Identity on the cluster through the gcloud command-line tool, establishing a secure mechanism for workloads operating in the cluster to access Google Cloud services with fine-grained IAM permissions.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>The recommended approaches for ensuring proper authentication of services accessing various Google Cloud APIs within a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster are:</p>\n<p><strong>II. Enable Workload Identity on the cluster through the gcloud command-line tool, establishing a secure mechanism for workloads operating in the cluster to access Google Cloud services with fine-grained IAM permissions.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>V. Utilize gcloud to bind Google service accounts and Kubernetes service accounts using roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser, which is a feasible approach but not as endorsed as enabling Workload Identity directly on the cluster.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Option II suggests enabling Workload Identity on the GKE cluster, which is a recommended best practice by Google. Workload Identity allows GKE workloads to securely access Google Cloud services with fine-grained IAM permissions, without the need to manage service account keys directly.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option V proposes binding Google service accounts and Kubernetes service accounts using roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser. While it's a feasible approach, it's not as endorsed as enabling Workload Identity directly on the cluster, but it still provides a secure mechanism for service authentication.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options I, III, and IV are not recommended best practices:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Option I suggests employing the service account linked to the GKE node for authentication. While this might work, it's not the recommended approach because it lacks granularity and control over IAM permissions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option III mentions accessing Google service account keys from Secret Manager, which is not a recommended best practice by Google for authenticating services within a GKE cluster. It introduces unnecessary complexity and potential security risks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option IV suggests storing Google service account keys in Secret Manager, which again is not a recommended best practice. Managing service account keys manually introduces security risks and operational overhead.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity#enable_on_existing_cluster\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity#enable_on_existing_cluster\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity#enable_on_existing_cluster</a></p>","lessons":["d4l2"]},"e1q58":{"id":"e1q58","exam":1,"n":58,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In order to assess whether the web application running on Google Kubernetes Engine is susceptible to cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks or if it utilizes libraries with known vulnerabilities, which service should you employ?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Google Cloud Armor</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Error Reporting</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Debugger</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Web Security Scanner</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The most suitable option in this case is:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>III. Web Security Scanner</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Designed for Vulnerability Detection:</strong> Web Security Scanner is specifically designed to find web application vulnerabilities, including XSS and those caused by outdated libraries with known flaws.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Active Assessment:</strong> It thoroughly examines your application, simulating attacks and analyzing the results to uncover potential attack vectors.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Comprehensive Reports:</strong> It provides detailed reports outlining vulnerabilities, making it easier to focus your remediation efforts.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Google Cloud Armor:</strong> Google Cloud Armor is a Web Application Firewall (WAF). While important for defense, it mainly protects against already-known attack patterns. It doesn't actively scan for new vulnerabilities like a dedicated security scanner.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Debugger:</strong> A debugger is a development tool used to troubleshoot code problems. It's not designed for proactive vulnerability detection.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Error Reporting:</strong> Error logs can sometimes reveal issues caused by XSS attempts, but this is more of an indirect and reactive approach. Monitoring errors is helpful, but doesn't replace a proactive vulnerability scanner.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/concepts-web-security-scanner-overview\">https://cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/concepts-web-security-scanner-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e1q59":{"id":"e1q59","exam":1,"n":59,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You have developed a new application and aim to deploy it on Cloud Run without utilizing a Dockerfile. Your organization mandates that all container images are stored in a centrally managed container repository. Which approach should you take to construct your container using Google Cloud services? (Select two.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Upload your source code to Artifact Registry.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize the pack build command through pack CLI.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Incorporate the --source flag within the gcloud run deploy CLI command.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>V. Add the --platform=kubernetes flag within the gcloud run deploy CLI command.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Initiate a Cloud Build job for image pushing.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The two correct answers are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Utilize the pack build command through the pack CLI.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Incorporate the --source flag within the gcloud run deploy CLI command.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here's why these choices work together, and why the others aren't suitable:</p>\n<p><strong>III. Using the pack build command:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Buildpacks:</strong> This command leverages Cloud Native Buildpacks, which automatically examine your source code and detect the appropriate language/framework. Buildpacks then handle the construction of the container image for you, eliminating the need for a Dockerfile.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Pushing to Repository:</strong> The <code>pack build</code> command can directly push the built container image to a supported registry, like Artifact Registry, satisfying your organization's central repository requirement.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IV. Using the gcloud run deploy --source flag:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Direct Source Deployment:</strong> This flag allows you to deploy your application code directly to Cloud Run. Cloud Build will be triggered under the hood, utilizing Buildpacks to create a container image from your source code.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Automated Repository Storage:</strong> Cloud Build automatically stores the resulting image in a compatible container repository (such as Artifact Registry).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options aren't correct:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Upload your source code to Artifact Registry.</strong> While Artifact Registry is the right place to store images, simply uploading source code does not create a container image.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Initiate a Cloud Build job for image pushing.</strong> You'd need a container image to begin with for Cloud Build to push; this option doesn't address image creation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>V. Add the --platform=kubernetes flag within the gcloud run deploy CLI command.</strong> This flag is used to manage Cloud Run deployments for Kubernetes environments and isn't relevant to building a container image without a Dockerfile.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>References:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/google-cloud-now-supports-buildpacks\">https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/google-cloud-now-supports-buildpacks</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e1q60":{"id":"e1q60","exam":1,"n":60,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>What is the optimal approach to develop and deploy a web application on Google Cloud, considering unpredictable traffic patterns and cost minimization?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>B. Develop the application using Firestore as the database and deploy it to a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) Standard cluster, providing flexibility and scalability but potentially leading to higher operational costs compared to serverless options.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Develop the application using Cloud SQL as the database and deploy it to a Google Kubernetes Engine Autopilot cluster, which automatically manages and scales the underlying infrastructure, optimizing costs while ensuring scalability.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Develop the application using Firestore as the database and deploy it to a Compute Engine managed instance group with autoscaling, which offers scalability but may involve more operational overhead and potentially higher costs compared to serverless options like Cloud Run.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>A. Develop the application using Firestore as the database and deploy it to Cloud Run, which offers serverless scaling to handle varying traffic loads efficiently while minimizing costs.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>The optimal approach to develop and deploy a web application on Google Cloud, considering unpredictable traffic patterns and cost minimization, is:</p>\n<p><strong>A. Develop the application using Firestore as the database and deploy it to Cloud Run, which offers serverless scaling to handle varying traffic loads efficiently while minimizing costs.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Option A suggests developing the application using Firestore as the database, which is a scalable and managed NoSQL database service provided by Google Cloud. Deploying the application to Cloud Run leverages serverless computing, which automatically scales the application up or down based on traffic demand, ensuring optimal resource utilization and minimizing costs during periods of low traffic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud Run offers a pay-per-use pricing model, where you only pay for the resources consumed during the execution of your application, making it cost-effective for applications with unpredictable traffic patterns.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option B involves deploying the application to a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) Standard cluster. While GKE provides flexibility and scalability, managing a Kubernetes cluster may lead to higher operational costs and complexity compared to serverless options like Cloud Run.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option C suggests deploying the application to a Google Kubernetes Engine Autopilot cluster, which automatically manages and scales the underlying infrastructure. While Autopilot optimizes costs and ensures scalability, it may not offer the same level of simplicity and cost-effectiveness as Cloud Run for applications with unpredictable traffic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option D involves deploying the application to a Compute Engine managed instance group with autoscaling. While this offers scalability, it may involve more operational overhead and potentially higher costs compared to serverless options like Cloud Run.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, Option A, deploying the application to Cloud Run with Firestore as the database, is the most optimal approach for handling unpredictable traffic patterns while minimizing costs.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/serverless-options  https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/the-appengine-environments  https://cloud.google.com/run\">https://cloud.google.com/serverless-options</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/serverless-options  https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/the-appengine-environments  https://cloud.google.com/run\">https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/the-appengine-environments</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/serverless-options  https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/the-appengine-environments  https://cloud.google.com/run\">https://cloud.google.com/run</a></p>","lessons":["d1l1"]},"e2q1":{"id":"e2q1","exam":2,"n":1,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You're strategizing the deployment of your application on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). Your application demands horizontal scalability, with each instance necessitating both a stable network identity and its persistent disk. Which GKE component aligns best with this requirement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Deployment</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. ReplicaSet</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. ReplicaController</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. StatefulSet</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. StatefulSet</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Stable Network Identities:</strong> Each pod in a StatefulSet is assigned a unique and persistent hostname/DNS entry, allowing reliable communication within and outside the cluster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Persistent Disks:</strong> StatefulSets are designed to work with persistent storage. Pods can be associated with individual PersistentVolumeClaims, ensuring data persists even if pods are rescheduled.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ordered Scaling:</strong> StatefulSets scale pods in a sequential order (0, 1, 2...), which can be important for certain applications that rely on pod identity during initialization or coordination.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Deployment:</strong> Deployments manage stateless pods designed for horizontal scaling. They don't guarantee stable network identities or have built-in mechanisms for persistent storage association.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. ReplicaSet:</strong> Similar to Deployments, ReplicaSets primarily manage stateless replicas and don't directly handle persistent storage or stable identifiers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. ReplicaController:</strong> ReplicaControllers are an older version of ReplicaSets with less functionality and shouldn't be used in new deployments.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/statefulset\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/statefulset</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l2","d3l4"]},"e2q2":{"id":"e2q2","exam":2,"n":2,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>How should you configure the load testing for a set of REST API endpoints deployed to Cloud Run, meeting Google-recommended best practices?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Employ an unmanaged instance group to deploy an image with cURL installed. Configure cURL within the image to execute a test plan, running one instance per VM to initiate load from multiple parallel threads and source IP addresses.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Download a container image of a distributed load testing framework on Cloud Shell. Sequentially launch multiple instances of the container on Cloud Shell to augment the load on the API.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize a managed instance group to deploy an image with cURL installed. Configure cURL within the image to execute a test plan, running one instance per VM to initiate load from multiple parallel threads and source IP addresses.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Implement a distributed load testing framework on a private Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster. Scale by deploying additional Pods as necessary to generate more traffic and support concurrent users.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>Here's a breakdown of the options and why the best choice aligns with Google's recommended practices:</p>\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>III. Implement a distributed load testing framework on a private Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster. Scale by deploying additional Pods as necessary to generate more traffic and support concurrent users.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> GKE is inherently designed for scalability, especially for load testing purposes. Adding pods dynamically allows you to simulate realistic user loads, including spikes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Distributed Testing:</strong> Load testing frameworks are best leveraged in a distributed manner, ensuring that the load generated mimics real-world scenarios with traffic originating from diverse sources. GKE's flexibility facilitates this.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Manageability:</strong> GKE provides managed infrastructure, simplifying cluster configuration and maintenance, allowing you to focus on your test plans.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Best Practices:</strong> Aligns with Google's advice to use tools like JMeter in a distributed setup for thorough Cloud Run load testing.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Not Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Utilizing a managed instance group to deploy an image with cURL installed...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Inefficient Scaling:</strong> Managed instance groups are not as easily scalable as GKE for quick load ramp-up and ramp-down.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Limited Distribution:</strong> Load generated will originate from similar IP addresses, unlike real user scenarios.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Employ an unmanaged instance group to deploy an image with cURL installed...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Even less scalable than option I</strong> and has the same distribution issue.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Management Overhead:</strong> Unmanaged instance groups require more effort for configuration and maintenance.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Download a container image of a distributed load testing framework on Cloud Shell...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Resource Constraints:</strong> Cloud Shell instances have limited resources for extensive load testing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Sequential Launch:</strong> Launching containers sequentially doesn't reflect realistic traffic patterns.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/distributed-load-testing-using-gke#build_the_c\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/distributed-load-testing-using-gke#build_the_</a><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/distributed-load-testing-using-gke#build_the_ccontainer_image\">container_image</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e2q3":{"id":"e2q3","exam":2,"n":3,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In the process of parsing a log file containing timestamp, account number (string), and transaction amount (number) columns, you aim to efficiently compute the sum of transaction amounts for each unique account number. Which data structure is best suited for this task?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. A comma-delimited string</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. A two-dimensional array</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. A linked list</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. A hash table</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. A hash table</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficient Lookups:</strong> Hash tables offer near-constant time lookups based on keys (in this case, the account number). This is critical for quickly finding existing entries or adding new ones as you parse the log file.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Natural Key-Value Fit:</strong> The 'account number' (key) and its corresponding 'total transaction amount' (value) map perfectly to the key-value structure of a hash table.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. A linked list</strong> Searching for the right account number in a linked list would be linear (O(n)), meaning it gets slower as the number of accounts grows.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. A two-dimensional array</strong> While an array could work, it's less efficient than a hash table for lookups if the account numbers are not known beforehand or aren't sequential.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. A comma-delimited string</strong> Storing data this way would make calculations and lookups extremely inefficient. Strings are not ideal for structured data.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference:<br />\n<a href=\"https://open4tech.com/array-vs-linked-list-vs-hash-table/\">https://open4tech.com/array-vs-linked-list-vs-hash-table/</a></p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e2q4":{"id":"e2q4","exam":2,"n":4,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You've containerized a legacy application that relies on configuration stored on an NFS share. How can you ensure the application doesn't serve traffic until its configuration is retrieved when deploying it to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Introduce a startup script to the GKE instance group to mount the NFS share during node startup, transferring the configuration files into the container, and activate the service via an ENTRYPOINT script.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Establish a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) on the GKE cluster, accessing configuration files from the volume, and commence the service via an ENTRYPOINT script.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize the gsutil utility within the Docker container at startup, copying files, and initiate the service via an ENTRYPOINT script.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Incorporate the COPY statement in the Dockerfile to inject the configuration into the container image, validating its availability, and launch the service using an ENTRYPOINT script.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Establish a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) on the GKE cluster, accessing configuration files from the volume, and commence the service via an ENTRYPOINT script.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this stands out as the ideal choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Kubernetes Native:</strong> Using PersistentVolumeClaims (PVCs) integrates seamlessly with Kubernetes, providing a standard way to manage persistent storage for Pods.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Configuration Decoupling:</strong> Separating the configuration from the container image enhances flexibility and maintainability, avoiding re-building images for configuration changes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Availability Check:</strong> Your ENTRYPOINT script can include logic to verify the configuration is accessible from the mounted volume before starting the application, ensuring it doesn't serve traffic prematurely.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. gsutil within the container:</strong> While possible, it adds complexity and potential security risks due to embedding credentials within the container.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Embedding config in the image:</strong> This makes image management less flexible, requiring rebuilds for configuration changes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Instance Group Startup Script:</strong> This approach tightly couples configuration management to the underlying infrastructure, making it less portable and harder to manage within Kubernetes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References</strong> :</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#access-modes  https://cloud.google.com/filestore/docs/accessing-fileshares  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/gcs-fuse\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#access-modes</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#access-modes  https://cloud.google.com/filestore/docs/accessing-fileshares  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/gcs-fuse\">https://cloud.google.com/filestore/docs/accessing-fileshares</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#access-modes  https://cloud.google.com/filestore/docs/accessing-fileshares  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/gcs-fuse\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/gcs-fuse</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3","d3l4"]},"e2q5":{"id":"e2q5","exam":2,"n":5,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Upon installing the Stackdriver Logging Agent to transmit an application's log file to Stackdriver from a Compute Engine virtual machine instance, what is the initial step you should take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Establish a Stackdriver Logs Export Sink with a filter aligning with the application's log entries.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Define the application log file as a custom source.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Provide the instance with complete access to all Cloud APIs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Activate the Error Reporting API for the project.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The initial step you should take upon installing the Stackdriver Logging Agent to transmit an application's log file to Stackdriver from a Compute Engine virtual machine instance is:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Define the application log file as a custom source.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>I. Activating the Error Reporting API for the project:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>While activating the Error Reporting API may be necessary for capturing and reporting errors, it is not the initial step for configuring log forwarding with the Stackdriver Logging Agent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>II. Providing the instance with complete access to all Cloud APIs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Providing the instance with complete access to all Cloud APIs is not necessary for configuring log forwarding with the Stackdriver Logging Agent. It is a security best practice to provide only the necessary permissions to instances.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>III. Defining the application log file as a custom source:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>This is the initial step in configuring log forwarding with the Stackdriver Logging Agent. By defining the application log file as a custom source, you specify which log files should be collected and forwarded to Stackdriver Logging.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>IV. Establishing a Stackdriver Logs Export Sink with a filter aligning with the application's log entries:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Establishing a Stackdriver Logs Export Sink with a filter aligning with the application's log entries is a subsequent step after defining the custom log source. It involves configuring the destination for the logs and specifying any filters to ensure only relevant log entries are exported.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Therefore, the initial step you should take is to define the application log file as a custom source (Option III).</strong></p>\n<p>Ref:<br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/agent/configuration\">https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/agent/configuration</a></p>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e2q6":{"id":"e2q6","exam":2,"n":6,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Your company's development teams wish to incorporate various open-source operating systems into their Docker builds. As images are generated and published in containers within your company's environment, you need to conduct scans for Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs) without hindering software development agility. Additionally, you prefer employing managed services whenever possible. What's the best course of action?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Develop a Cloud Function triggered by code check-ins to perform CVE scans on the code.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Restrict the usage of non-commercially supported base images within your development environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Activate the Vulnerability scanning feature within the Container Registry.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Cloud Monitoring to scrutinize Cloud Build's output and detect if a vulnerable version has been utilized.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Activate the Vulnerability scanning feature within the Container Registry.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this stands out as the most suitable option:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Purpose-Built:</strong> Container Registry's vulnerability scanning feature is specifically designed to scan container images for known CVEs. It aligns directly with your core requirement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Seamless Integration:</strong> It works seamlessly with container images stored in the Container Registry, fitting naturally into your existing workflow.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimal Disruption:</strong> Activating this scanning feature does not require significant changes to the development process, maintaining agility.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Managed Service:</strong> Utilizing this feature within the Container Registry aligns with your stated preference for managed services.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Develop a Cloud Function triggered by code check-ins to perform CVE scans on the code.</strong> Code-level CVE scans are important, but they won't identify vulnerabilities introduced by dependencies in the container image.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Restrict the usage of non-commercially supported base images within your development environment.</strong> This improves security posture but might be overly restrictive and could hinder development flexibility. Not all vulnerabilities stem from lack of commercial support.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Utilize Cloud Monitoring to scrutinize Cloud Build's output and detect if a vulnerable version has been utilized.</strong> Cloud Monitoring is useful for general logging and alerting but lacks the specialized CVE scanning capabilities.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://docs.docker.com/engine/scan/  https://cloud.google.com/container-analysis/docs/os-overview\">https://docs.docker.com/engine/scan/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.docker.com/engine/scan/  https://cloud.google.com/container-analysis/docs/os-overview\">https://cloud.google.com/container-analysis/docs/os-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e2q7":{"id":"e2q7","exam":2,"n":7,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your application comprises a series of loosely interconnected services managed by code executed on Compute Engine. To facilitate the seamless deployment of new Compute Engine instances accessing a specific service version, how should you configure the setup?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Configure your service to utilize a fixed hostname and port for connecting to the desired service, and update the service at the endpoint with the new version.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Specify your service endpoint details as metadata fetched at runtime, enabling connection to the desired service.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Specify your service endpoint details as label data fetched at runtime, facilitating connection to the desired service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Define your service endpoint details to be fetched from an environment variable at runtime, facilitating connection to the desired service.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Specify your service endpoint details as metadata fetched at runtime, enabling connection to the desired service.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Dynamic Configuration:</strong> Metadata is a key strength in Compute Engine. It allows you to provide instance-specific information that code running on the instance can access. This is ideal for discovering the correct endpoint to connect to.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Deployment Flexibility:</strong> With metadata, you don't need to modify code or environment variables with each service version change. Updating the metadata is enough for your new instances to point to the correct location.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Aren't as Suitable</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Labels:</strong> Labels are primarily for organizing resources, not dynamically providing runtime configuration details.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Environment Variables:</strong> Environment variables can work but they introduce another element to manage during your deployment process. Metadata is more streamlined in this case.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Fixed Hostname/Port:</strong> This approach is inflexible, especially if you use load balancing or service discovery, where endpoints may change dynamically.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apis/design/glossary#api_service_endpoint   https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/metadata/overview  https://cloud.google.com/service-infrastructure/docs/service-metadata/reference/rest#service-endpoint\">https://cloud.google.com/apis/design/glossary#api_service_endpoint https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/metadata/overview</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apis/design/glossary#api_service_endpoint   https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/metadata/overview  https://cloud.google.com/service-infrastructure/docs/service-metadata/reference/rest#service-endpoint\">https://cloud.google.com/service-infrastructure/docs/service-metadata/reference/rest#service-endpoint</a></p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e2q8":{"id":"e2q8","exam":2,"n":8,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You're utilizing Cloud Build to create a new Docker image for each source code commit to a Cloud Source Repositories repository. Commits to the master branch trigger builds for your application. How can you automate the release process for specific commits to the master branch?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Configure a build trigger based on a Git branch name pattern, adhering to a Git branch naming convention for releases.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Trigger the build manually for new releases.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Set up a build trigger based on a Git tag pattern, following a Git tag convention for releases.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Store your source code in a secondary Cloud Source Repositories repository with a distinct Cloud Build trigger solely for new releases.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Set up a build trigger based on a Git tag pattern, following a Git tag convention for releases.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Versioning:</strong> Git tags provide a clear and structured way to mark specific commits as releasable versions (e.g., v1.0.0, v1.1.2).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Automation:</strong> A Cloud Build trigger tied to your tag pattern (e.g., <code>v*.**</code>) automatically initiates builds when you create a release tag.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Consistency:</strong> A well-defined tagging convention streamlines the release process and maintains historical tracking of releases.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Trigger the build manually...</strong> This lacks automation and introduces potential for errors due to manual intervention.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Configure a build trigger based on a Git branch name pattern ...</strong> Branch naming conventions might change or be less reliable than an explicit tagging strategy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Store your source code in a secondary Cloud Source Repositories repository ...</strong> This adds unnecessary complexity, fragmenting your codebase and its history.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/source-repositories/docs/integrating-with-cloud-build#create_a_build_trigger\">https://cloud.google.com/source-repositories/docs/integrating-with-cloud-build#create_a_build_trigger</a></p>","lessons":["d3l8"]},"e2q9":{"id":"e2q9","exam":2,"n":9,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>As you develop an event-driven application, you've set up a Pub/Sub topic to receive messages. Your objective is to process these messages in real-time while ensuring the application operates independently, incurring costs only upon message arrival. How should you architect this setup?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Deploy the application on Google Kubernetes Engine, utilizing the Pub/Sub API to establish a pull subscription for reading messages from the Pub/Sub topic.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Deploy the application on Compute Engine, leveraging a Pub/Sub push subscription for processing incoming messages from the topic.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy your code on Cloud Functions, employing a Pub/Sub trigger to manage incoming messages from the topic.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy your code on Cloud Functions, utilizing a Pub/Sub trigger to invoke the function. Use the Pub/Sub API to establish a pull subscription for message retrieval from the topic.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The most suitable architecture for your event-driven application that processes messages in real-time, operates independently, and incurs costs only upon message arrival is:</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Deploy your code on Cloud Functions, employing a Pub/Sub trigger to manage incoming messages from the topic.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Real-time processing:</strong> Cloud Functions are triggered instantly upon message arrival in the Pub/Sub topic, ensuring real-time message handling.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Independent operation:</strong> Cloud Functions are serverless, meaning you don't need to manage any infrastructure. They scale automatically based on the incoming message volume.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cost efficiency:</strong> Cloud Functions are billed based on execution time and the number of invocations. You only pay when messages are processed, making it a cost-effective solution for event-driven applications.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Simplified development:</strong> Cloud Functions allow you to focus on your application logic without worrying about infrastructure setup or scaling.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let's look at why the other options aren't as ideal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Compute Engine:</strong> This requires you to manage a virtual machine instance, which incurs costs even when no messages are being processed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Cloud Functions with pull subscription:</strong> While this uses Cloud Functions, pulling messages manually adds complexity and might introduce delays in message processing compared to a Pub/Sub trigger.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Google Kubernetes Engine:</strong> GKE provides more control but also requires infrastructure management and might not be as cost-efficient as Cloud Functions for this use case.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Therefore, deploying your code on Cloud Functions with a Pub/Sub trigger is the most effective way to meet your requirements for real-time processing, independent operation, and cost efficiency in your event-driven application.</strong></p>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e2q10":{"id":"e2q10","exam":2,"n":10,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Which approach should you take to install a third-party logging solution on each node in your Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster to troubleshoot intermittent performance issues?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Terraform to deploy the third-party solution and deploy the logging Pod as a Kubernetes Deployment</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Manually install the software on GKE nodes via SSH</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Implement the third-party solution as a DaemonSet</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Embed the monitoring software into your container image</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option- I. Implement the third-party solution as a DaemonSet</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Implement the third-party solution as a DaemonSet</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> DaemonSets offer the most suitable approach for this scenario.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong> DaemonSets ensure that a pod (in this case, the logging agent) runs on each node within the Kubernetes cluster. This guarantees consistent logging across all nodes, crucial for investigating intermittent performance problems that could occur anywhere within the cluster.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>II. Embed the monitoring software into your container image</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Option:</strong> While possible, it's not ideal for this use case.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Tight Coupling:</strong> Embedding the logging agent directly in your application containers creates unnecessary dependencies and complicates future updates to either the application or the logging solution.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Node-Level Issues:</strong> This method only logs issues within your application container, missing potential problems at the node or infrastructure level that might be causing the performance problems.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>III. Manually install the software on GKE nodes via SSH</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Option:</strong> This approach should be avoided.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> Manual installation is time-consuming and prone to human error, especially in large clusters.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Updating the logging agent across nodes becomes tedious and harder to track.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Kubernetes Philosophy:</strong> It goes against the declarative nature of Kubernetes, where infrastructure state should be managed through configuration, not manual intervention.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IV. Utilize Terraform to deploy the third-party solution and deploy the logging Pod as a Kubernetes Deployment</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Potentially Correct, but less ideal than DaemonSets:</strong> Terraform can automate the deployment, but...</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Node-Level Granularity:</strong> Regular Deployments don't guarantee a pod on each node. You might get multiple logging pods on the same node or miss some nodes entirely, leading to inconsistent logging coverage.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e2q11":{"id":"e2q11","exam":2,"n":11,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>As part of updating Cloud Build steps to align with best practices, you're tasked with integrating a vulnerability scan for the built container image. The scan results should seamlessly integrate into your Google Cloud deployment pipeline without causing disruptions to other teams' processes. What's the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Enable the Container Scanning API in Artifact Registry to scan the built container images for vulnerabilities.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Integrate Artifact Registry with your Aqua Security instance to perform vulnerability scans on the built container images.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Upload the built container images to your Docker Hub instance and conduct vulnerability scans.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Enable Binary Authorization and configure it to attest that no vulnerabilities exist in the container image.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Enable the Container Scanning API in Artifact Registry to scan the built container images for vulnerabilities.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Native Integration:</strong> The Container Scanning API is a built-in feature of Artifact Registry, providing a seamless and integrated vulnerability scanning experience within Google Cloud.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimal Disruption:</strong> Adding a vulnerability scan in Artifact Registry is unlikely to disrupt other teams' workflows, as it's a natural extension of storing images.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Actionable Results:</strong> Container Scanning can be configured to generate findings viewable in Security Command Center, providing insights and the ability to potentially block deployments based on vulnerability severity.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Binary Authorization:</strong> While critical, Binary Authorization relies on attestations. Vulnerability scanning would be the mechanism to generate such attestations but isn't a vulnerability scanner itself.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Docker Hub Scanning:</strong> This involves pushing images to an external repository and adds another dependency outside of GCP.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Aqua Security Integration:</strong> While Aqua Security is a robust tool, it introduces another external system to manage and adds complexity to your pipeline.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/container-analysis/docs/automated-scanning-howto#view_the_image_vulnerabilities\">https://cloud.google.com/container-analysis/docs/automated-scanning-howto#view_the_image_vulnerabilities</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e2q12":{"id":"e2q12","exam":2,"n":12,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You're tasked with constructing an API for Android and iOS apps, with the following prerequisites:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>HTTPS support is essential</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Bandwidth usage should be minimized</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Seamless integration with mobile apps is a priority</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Which API architecture is the most appropriate choice for meeting these criteria?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. gRPC-based APIs</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. RESTful APIs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. SOAP-based APIs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. MQTT for APIs</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The most appropriate choice for meeting the given criteria is </p>\n<p><strong>III. gRPC-based APIs</strong>. Here's why:</p>\n<p>gRPC-based APIs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>HTTPS support</strong> : gRPC natively supports HTTPS via HTTP/2, ensuring secure data transmission.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Bandwidth usage</strong> : gRPC is optimized for performance by using <strong>HTTP/2</strong> , which allows multiplexing and reduces latency. It also uses <strong>Protobuf</strong> (Protocol Buffers) for data serialization, which is more compact than JSON (used by REST) or XML (used by SOAP), leading to reduced bandwidth consumption.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Seamless integration</strong> : gRPC offers excellent cross-platform compatibility and comes with <strong>code generation tools</strong> for multiple languages (including Android and iOS), simplifying the integration process with mobile apps.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options and why they are less suitable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. RESTful APIs</strong> : While REST is widely used and supports HTTPS, it relies on <strong>JSON</strong> for data exchange, which is bulkier than Protobuf. REST also uses HTTP/1.1, which is not as efficient as HTTP/2 in terms of bandwidth usage and performance, particularly in mobile environments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. MQTT for APIs</strong> : MQTT is a lightweight protocol primarily designed for messaging in IoT systems. It is not ideal for APIs intended for mobile applications as it focuses more on <strong>publish/subscribe messaging</strong> rather than standard request/response patterns that are common in APIs. While it minimizes bandwidth, it's not designed to integrate seamlessly with mobile app APIs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. SOAP-based APIs</strong> : SOAP uses <strong>XML</strong> for message formats, which is much larger and more verbose compared to gRPC’s Protobuf or even REST’s JSON. SOAP APIs are also less flexible and have more overhead in terms of both bandwidth usage and development complexity, especially for mobile platforms.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.imaginarycloud.com/blog/grpc-vs-rest/  https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/api-management/understanding-grpc-openapi-and-rest-and-when-to-use-them\">https://www.imaginarycloud.com/blog/grpc-vs-rest/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.imaginarycloud.com/blog/grpc-vs-rest/  https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/api-management/understanding-grpc-openapi-and-rest-and-when-to-use-them\">https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/api-management/understanding-grpc-openapi-and-rest-and-when-to-use-them</a></p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e2q13":{"id":"e2q13","exam":2,"n":13,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your data resides in Cloud Storage buckets. Colleagues have reported that retrieving data from Cloud Storage is causing sluggish performance in API responses. To investigate the issue thoroughly and provide pertinent details to the GCP support team, which command should you execute?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. <code>**gcloud compute scp example-instance:~/test-data -o output.json gs://my-bucket**</code></p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. <code>**gcloud services test -o output.json gs://my-bucket**</code></p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. <code>**gsutil test -o output.json gs://my-bucket**</code></p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. <code>**gsutil perfdiag -o output.json gs://my-bucket**</code></p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. gsutil perfdiag -o output.json gs://my-bucket</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>gsutil perfdiag:</strong> This command specifically diagnoses Cloud Storage performance issues, capturing detailed metrics and trace information.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>output.json:</strong> The output is stored in a structured JSON file for easy analysis and inclusion in your communication with GCP support.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Targeted Diagnostics:</strong> <code>perfdiag</code> measures factors like network latency, object sizes, throughput, and error rates – all directly influencing data retrieval speed.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. gsutil test ...</strong> This performs basic connectivity tests but doesn't provide the in-depth performance profiling relevant to your scenario.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. gcloud compute scp ...</strong> This command is for copying files between a Compute Engine instance and Cloud Storage. It might be useful in some performance investigations, but not for the initial diagnosis.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. gcloud services test ...</strong> This tests the health status of Google Cloud services in general, not the granular performance of Cloud Storage.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/gsutil/commands/perfdiag#providing-diagnostic-output-to-cloud-storage-team\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/gsutil/commands/perfdiag#providing-diagnostic-output-to-cloud-storage-team</a></p>","lessons":["d3l11"]},"e2q14":{"id":"e2q14","exam":2,"n":14,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>In your Google Cloud-hosted application, you're utilizing a MySQL relational database schema with substantial read and write activity. This necessitates backups and ongoing capacity planning. While your team can handle minor administrative tasks, full database management is challenging. How should you deploy the database?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Configure Bigtable to host the database and import the data.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Implement Cloud SQL for hosting the database, importing the schema accordingly.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Set up Cloud Spanner to host the database, importing the schema.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>V. Utilize Firestore for hosting the database, importing the data.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy MySQL from the Google Cloud Marketplace using a client and import the schema.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I. Implement Cloud SQL for hosting the database, importing the schema accordingly.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Managed MySQL:</strong> Cloud SQL provides a fully managed MySQL service, specifically aligned with the scenario's requirements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reduced Operational Burden:</strong> Cloud SQL handles backups, replication, scaling, and updates, significantly decreasing your team's administrative overhead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Relational Workload Alignment:</strong> Cloud SQL is inherently designed for relational database workloads with high read/write activity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Capacity Planning:</strong> Cloud SQL offers tools and insights for managing and projecting database capacity needs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Deploy MySQL from the Google Cloud Marketplace using a client and import the schema.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Administrative Overhead:</strong> This approach leaves you responsible for managing the entire database lifecycle, including backups, patches, scaling, etc., which your team wants to avoid.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Configure Bigtable to host the database and import the data.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Schema Mismatch:</strong> Bigtable is a NoSQL wide-column database. Its structure and query model are vastly different from a relational MySQL schema.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Set up Cloud Spanner to host the database, importing the schema.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Potential Overkill:</strong> Cloud Spanner is a globally distributed, strongly consistent relational database. While powerful, it might be more than you need for a standard MySQL use-case and could increase costs.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>V. Utilize Firestore for hosting the database, importing the data.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NoSQL Model:</strong> Firestore is a document-oriented NoSQL database, not a suitable replacement for a relational schema.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql\">https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e2q15":{"id":"e2q15","exam":2,"n":15,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>In setting up a build pipeline for an application intended to operate in Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), your team prioritizes security to permit only images generated by the pipeline to be deployed to the GKE cluster. Which combination of Google Cloud services should be employed?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Cloud Build, Cloud Storage, and Binary Authorization</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Google Cloud Deploy, Artifact Registry, and Google Cloud Armor</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Google Cloud Deploy, Cloud Storage, and Google Cloud Armor</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Cloud Build, Artifact Registry, and Binary Authorization</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IV. Cloud Build, Artifact Registry, and Binary Authorization</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Image Pipeline:</strong> Cloud Build is ideal for creating the container images in a secure and controlled build process.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Trusted Repository:</strong> Artifact Registry serves as a secure, managed image repository, providing fine-grained access controls.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Deployment Authorization:</strong> Binary Authorization acts as a gatekeeper, ensuring that only images that have passed specific checks (build provenance, vulnerability scans, etc.) are allowed for deployment to GKE.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Cloud Build, Cloud Storage, and Binary Authorization</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Artifact Registry Advantage:</strong> While Cloud Storage can store images, Artifact Registry offers features tailored for container management, such as versioning and vulnerability scanning integration.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Google Cloud Deploy, Cloud Storage, and Google Cloud Armor</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Focus Mismatch:</strong> Google Cloud Deploy is for managing application deployments, not enforcing image security policy. Google Cloud Armor is a web application firewall (WAF), not directly related to secure build pipelines.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Google Cloud Deploy, Artifact Registry, and Google Cloud Armor</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Missing Authorization:</strong> While Artifact Registry provides a secure repository, it needs Binary Authorization to enforce the \"only approved images\" policy.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/cloud-build  https://cloud.google.com/architecture/app-development-and-delivery-with-cloud-code-gcb-cd-and-gke#objectives\">https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/cloud-build</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/cloud-build  https://cloud.google.com/architecture/app-development-and-delivery-with-cloud-code-gcb-cd-and-gke#objectives\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/app-development-and-delivery-with-cloud-code-gcb-cd-and-gke#objectives</a></p>","lessons":["d3l9"]},"e2q16":{"id":"e2q16","exam":2,"n":16,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>To enhance the performance of your Go application when interacting with a Cloud Spanner database, which approach should you adopt based on Google's recommended best practices?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Utilize Cloud Client Libraries to interface with Cloud Spanner.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize Google API Client Libraries to interact with Cloud Spanner.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Employ a custom gRPC client library for writing to Cloud Spanner.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement a third-party HTTP client library for writing to Cloud Spanner.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>To enhance the performance of your Go application when interacting with a Cloud Spanner database, the best approach based on Google's recommended practices is:</p>\n<p><strong>I. Utilize Cloud Client Libraries to interface with Cloud Spanner.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Optimized for performance:</strong> Cloud Client Libraries are specifically designed and optimized for interacting with Google Cloud services, including Cloud Spanner. They handle low-level details like connection management, retries, and error handling, allowing you to focus on your application logic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Idiomatic Go code:</strong> Cloud Client Libraries provide a natural and intuitive way to interact with Cloud Spanner using Go idioms and conventions, making your code easier to read and maintain.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Built-in best practices:</strong> Cloud Client Libraries incorporate Google's recommended best practices for interacting with Cloud Spanner, ensuring optimal performance and reliability out of the box.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let's look at why the other options aren't as suitable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google API Client Libraries:</strong> While these libraries can be used to interact with various Google APIs, they are not as specialized as Cloud Client Libraries for Cloud Spanner and might not offer the same level of performance optimization.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Custom gRPC client library:</strong> Building a custom gRPC client library requires significant development effort and might not be as efficient or well-maintained as the official Cloud Client Library.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Third-party HTTP client library:</strong> Using a third-party HTTP client library adds another layer of complexity and potential performance overhead compared to the dedicated Cloud Client Library.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Therefore, utilizing Cloud Client Libraries is the recommended approach for maximizing the performance of your Go application when working with Cloud Spanner, aligning with Google's best practices.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apis/docs/cloud-client-libraries\">https://cloud.google.com/apis/docs/cloud-client-libraries</a></p>","lessons":["d4l9","d4l11"]},"e2q17":{"id":"e2q17","exam":2,"n":17,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You are monitoring a web application deployed on Google Kubernetes Engine, developed in Go. An increase in CPU and memory usage has been observed. How can you determine which sections of the source code are consuming the most CPU and memory resources?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Integrate OpenTelemetry and Trace export packages into your application to create the trace provider. Examine the latency data for your application on the Trace overview page to identify bottleneck areas.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Develop a Cloud Logging query to collect the web application's logs. Create a Python script to compute the difference between the timestamps from the beginning and end of the application's longest functions to identify time-intensive functions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Install the Snapshot Debugger agent in your VM. Capture debug snapshots of the functions with the longest execution time. Analyze the call stack frame to identify the local variables at that level in the stack.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Incorporate the Cloud Profiler package into your application and initialize the Profiler agent. Review the generated flame graph in the Google Cloud console to pinpoint time-intensive functions.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>II. Incorporate the Cloud Profiler package into your application and initialize the Profiler agent. Review the generated flame graph in the Google Cloud console to pinpoint time-intensive functions.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Specific to Application Profiling:</strong> Cloud Profiler is a continuous profiling tool designed explicitly to identify performance bottlenecks in applications running on production environments like Google Kubernetes Engine.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Optimized for GKE:</strong> It has seamless integration with GKE, minimizing setup complexity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flame Graphs:</strong> Flame graphs are an effective visual representation for pinpointing resource-heavy code sections, making analysis efficient.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Install the Snapshot Debugger agent in your VM...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Debugging vs. Profiling:</strong> Snapshot Debugger is primarily a debugging tool. While it can provide insights into code execution, it's less tailored for continuous resource usage monitoring.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Overhead:</strong> Introducing a debugger can add overhead to a production environment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Integrate OpenTelemetry and Trace export packages...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Focus on Tracing:</strong> OpenTelemetry is excellent for distributed tracing, which primarily tracks request flows across systems. It's less direct for pinpointing CPU or memory-heavy code sections within the application itself.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Develop a Cloud Logging query to collect the web application's logs...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Manual and Time-Consuming:</strong> Analyzing logs manually can be effective but is often tedious for pinpointing performance issues. Profilers are more efficient for this specific task.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs  https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler#profiling_agent  https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler#environment_and_languages\">https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs  https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler#profiling_agent  https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler#environment_and_languages\">https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler#profiling_agent</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs  https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler#profiling_agent  https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler#environment_and_languages\">https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler#environment_and_languages</a></p>","lessons":["d2l8","d2l9"]},"e2q18":{"id":"e2q18","exam":2,"n":18,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>To deploy a new European version of a website on Google Kubernetes Engine, ensuring both the current and new websites can be accessed via the same HTTP(S) load balancer's external IP address but with different domain names, what action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Define a new Ingress resource with a host rule matching the new domain.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Create a new Service of type LoadBalancer specifying the existing IP address as the loadBalancerIP.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Generate a new Ingress resource and specify the existing IP address as the kubernetes.io/ingress.global-static-ip-name annotation value.c</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Modify the existing Ingress resource with a host rule matching the new domain.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Modify the existing Ingress resource with a host rule matching the new domain.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ingress for Routing:</strong> Ingress is the preferred way to expose Kubernetes services outside the cluster, and it's designed for routing traffic based on domain names.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Shared Load Balancer:</strong> Since you want to reuse the existing HTTP(S) load balancer, modifying the existing Ingress is the most straightforward approach.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Host-based Routing:</strong> Ingress resources use host rules to determine which backend service to route traffic to based on the incoming domain name. By adding a new host rule for the European domain, you can direct traffic to the appropriate service within your GKE cluster.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are not as suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. New Ingress Resource:</strong> While technically possible, creating a separate Ingress would likely result in the provisioning of a new external IP address, which isn't the goal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. New LoadBalancer Service:</strong> Creating a new LoadBalancer service would also provision a new IP address. Moreover, using raw Kubernetes services for this type of routing is less manageable than using an Ingress.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Static IP Annotation:</strong> This annotation is used to associate a specific static IP address with an Ingress resource. While relevant for preserving an IP, it's not the primary mechanism for adding a new domain route.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/ingress/#name-based-virtual-hosting   https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/configuring-domain-name-static-ip\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/ingress/#name-based-virtual-hosting https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/configuring-domain-name-static-ip</a></p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e2q19":{"id":"e2q19","exam":2,"n":19,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your company operates a data warehouse in BigQuery, storing 2 PBs of user data. With the expansion of your user base to include EU users, compliance requirements mandate:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>User account information must be deletable upon user request.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>EU user data must reside within a designated EU region.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Which two actions should you take to meet these requirements? (Choose two.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize a Cloud Dataflow pipeline to re-upload data, filtering out user records based on region.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Implement BigQuery federated queries to access data stored in Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Create a Cloud Storage bucket in the EU region to exclusively store data for EU users.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>V. Execute Data Manipulation Language (DML) statements within BigQuery to update or delete user records as per their requests.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Establish a dataset in the EU region dedicated to storing information for EU users exclusively.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Establish a dataset in the EU region dedicated to storing information for EU users exclusively.</strong> This allows you to comply with data residency requirements by storing EU user data within the specified region.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>V. Execute Data Manipulation Language (DML) statements within BigQuery to update or delete user records as per their requests.</strong> DML statements are necessary to fulfill user requests for data modification or deletion, ensuring compliance with privacy regulations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Not Ideal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Implement BigQuery federated queries to access data stored in Cloud Storage.</strong> While federated queries can be useful, they don't directly address user deletion or data residency requirements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Create a Cloud Storage bucket in the EU region to exclusively store data for EU users.</strong> Using a regional Cloud Storage bucket helps with data residency, but it doesn't intrinsically offer mechanisms for user data deletion. You would still need additional processes in place.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Utilize a Cloud Dataflow pipeline to re-upload data, filtering out user records based on region.</strong> This could achieve compliance, but is likely more complex than necessary. Modifying or deleting data in BigQuery directly is likely to be more efficient.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l2","d1l5"]},"e2q20":{"id":"e2q20","exam":2,"n":20,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>While employing Cloud Build to build and test application source code stored in Cloud Source Repositories, you encounter a requirement for a build tool that isn't available in the Cloud Build environment. What's the recommended course of action?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Embed the binary in your Cloud Source Repositories repository and reference it in your build scripts.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Request the addition of the binary to the Cloud Build environment by submitting a feature request via the Cloud Build public Issue Tracker.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Develop a custom cloud builder image and specify it in your build steps.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Fetch the binary from the internet during the build process.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Develop a custom cloud builder image and specify it in your build steps.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Customization:</strong> Creating a custom cloud builder image gives you complete control over the tools and libraries available in the build environment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reproducibility:</strong> The image ensures that builds are consistent and independent of changes in default Cloud Build environments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Maintainability:</strong> You manage the build tool's installation and version within the image.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Fetch the binary from the internet during the build process.</strong> This introduces potential security risks, network dependency, and could make builds less reproducible if the external source changes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Embed the binary in your Cloud Source Repositories repository and reference it in your build scripts.</strong> Bundling a large binary with your source code can increase repository size and potentially complicate build scripts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Request the addition of the binary...</strong> This is a viable long-term option, but it doesn't give you immediate control and might not be possible if the tool isn't widely used.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/cloud-build/docs/configuring-builds/use-community-and-custom-builders#creating_a_custom_builder\">https://cloud.google.com/cloud-build/docs/configuring-builds/use-community-and-custom-builders#creating_a_custom_builder</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e2q21":{"id":"e2q21","exam":2,"n":21,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You're preparing to deploy a new Go application to Cloud Run, with its source code stored in Cloud Source Repositories. You aim to establish a fully managed, automated, continuous deployment pipeline triggered by each source code commit. Seeking the simplest deployment solution,</strong></p>\n<p><strong>what should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Configure a Jenkins trigger to initiate the container build and deployment process for each source code commit made to Cloud Source Repositories.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Enable continuous deployment of new revisions from the source repository to Cloud Run using buildpacks.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Set up a cron job on your workstations to periodically execute 'gcloud run deploy --source' within the working directory.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Cloud Build with a trigger configured to execute the container build and deploy process for each source code commit to Cloud Source Repositories.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Utilize Cloud Build with a trigger configured to execute the container build and deploy process for each source code commit to Cloud Source Repositories.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Fully Managed:</strong> Cloud Build is a serverless CI/CD platform within Google Cloud, eliminating the need to manage your own build infrastructure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Easy Integration:</strong> Cloud Build integrates seamlessly with Cloud Source Repositories, allowing you to set up triggers that automatically initiate builds on commits.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Run Deployment:</strong> Cloud Build can directly build and deploy container images to Cloud Run, simplifying the entire pipeline.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Simplicity:</strong> This approach offers a streamlined workflow with minimal setup on your side.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Cron Job:</strong> This solution is manual, not automated, and requires you to manage the cron schedule and deployment script.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Jenkins:</strong> While powerful, Jenkins introduces the overhead of maintaining your own Jenkins server and its configuration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Buildpacks:</strong> Buildpacks can be a great option, but they might require additional configuration if you have specific build requirements beyond what buildpacks provide.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/continuous-deployment-with-cloud-build\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/continuous-deployment-with-cloud-build</a></p>","lessons":["d3l8"]},"e2q22":{"id":"e2q22","exam":2,"n":22,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>As your development team refactors an existing monolithic application into a set of composable microservices, which design aspects should you prioritize for the new application? (Select two.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>V. Introduce a versioning scheme to accommodate future changes that might be incompatible with the current interface.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Establish an API contract agreement between the microservice implementation and microservice caller.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Craft the microservice code in the same programming language utilized by the microservice caller.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Ensure adequate instances of the microservice are running to meet performance requirements.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Implement asynchronous communications between all microservice implementations and microservice callers.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Establish an API contract agreement between the microservice implementation and microservice caller.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>V. Introduce a versioning scheme to accommodate future changes that might be incompatible with the current interface.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why these choices are critical:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>API Contracts:</strong> Decoupling is a core principle of microservices. Well-defined API contracts ensure that microservices can change internally without impacting their consumers as long as they honor the agreed-upon contract.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Versioning:</strong> In a distributed system, it's inevitable that you'll need to introduce changes. A robust versioning scheme allows you to evolve microservices while maintaining compatibility or enabling graceful transitions for consumers.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Let's analyze the other options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Same Programming Language:</strong> While using the same language can sometimes be convenient, microservice architectures should prioritize language-agnostic communication protocols (like REST or gRPC) to enable flexibility and choice of technology.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Asynchronous Communication (Always):</strong> While asynchronous communication is often beneficial in microservices, it's not always strictly necessary. Some interactions might be better suited for synchronous communication patterns.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Adequate Instances for Performance:</strong> While scaling is important, it's more of an operational concern rather than a fundamental microservice design aspect.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#api_contracts  https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#versioning  https://cloud.google.com/architecture/microservices-architecture-refactoring-monoliths\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#api_contracts</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#api_contracts  https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#versioning  https://cloud.google.com/architecture/microservices-architecture-refactoring-monoliths\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#versioning</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#api_contracts  https://cloud.google.com/architecture/migrating-a-monolithic-app-to-microservices-gke#versioning  https://cloud.google.com/architecture/microservices-architecture-refactoring-monoliths\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/microservices-architecture-refactoring-monoliths</a></p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e2q23":{"id":"e2q23","exam":2,"n":23,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>For deploying a new API with three distinct URL paths - /students, /teachers, and /classes - each invoking a different function in your code, what action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy three distinct Cloud Functions, each as a separate backend service, exposed via an HTTPS load balancer, and configure them to handle requests for /students, /teachers, and /classes, respectively.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Establish three separate Cloud Functions, each directly exposed, to handle requests for /students, /teachers, and /classes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Deploy a single Cloud Function serving as a backend service, exposed via an HTTPS load balancer, and configure it to handle requests for all three URL paths.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Create a single Cloud Function directly exposed to handle all incoming requests regardless of the URL path.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Deploy three distinct Cloud Functions, each as a separate backend service, exposed via an HTTPS load balancer, and configure them to handle requests for /students, /teachers, and /classes, respectively.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Option IV is the most suitable approach for deploying a new API with three distinct URL paths (/students, /teachers, and /classes), each invoking a different function in your code. Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Three distinct Cloud Functions</strong> : Since each URL path (/students, /teachers, and /classes) invokes a different function in your code, deploying three separate Cloud Functions allows you to encapsulate the logic for each endpoint separately. This ensures better maintainability and scalability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Separate backend services</strong> : By configuring each Cloud Function as a separate backend service, you can route requests to the appropriate function based on the URL path. This allows for clear separation of concerns and efficient handling of incoming requests.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Exposed via an HTTPS load balancer</strong> : Exposing the Cloud Functions via an HTTPS load balancer ensures that incoming requests are distributed evenly and efficiently across the backend services. It also provides HTTPS encryption and scalability features.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options I, II, and III suggest deploying a single Cloud Function or fewer Cloud Functions to handle all incoming requests, which would not align with the requirement of having distinct functions for each URL path.</p>\n<p>Therefore, option IV is the recommended action for deploying a new API with three distinct URL paths on Google Cloud Platform.</p>","lessons":["d1l1","d1l6"]},"e2q24":{"id":"e2q24","exam":2,"n":24,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You are developing a social media application and planning to implement an image upload feature. The images can range from 2 MB to 1 GB in size. You aim to minimize infrastructure operations for this feature. What approach should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Configure a web server on GCP to handle user image uploads. Establish a file store to store uploaded files, and modify the application to fetch images from this file store.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Modify the application to directly accept images and store them in the database along with other user information.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Implement signed URLs for Cloud Storage in the application. Provide these signed URLs to clients for uploading images to Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Create individual buckets for each user in Cloud Storage. Assign separate service accounts to each bucket for write access. Provide client applications with the necessary service account credentials based on user information to upload images to Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Implement signed URLs for Cloud Storage in the application. Provide these signed URLs to clients for uploading images to Cloud Storage.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability and Offloading:</strong> Using Cloud Storage and signed URLs lets you leverage Google's highly scalable object storage. It removes the burden of managing image storage and handling potentially large uploads from your application infrastructure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> Signed URLs can be generated with expiration dates and access restrictions, adding a layer of control.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Operational Simplicity:</strong> This approach avoids the operational overhead of setting up and maintaining web servers and file stores.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Direct Uploads:</strong> Clients upload images directly to Cloud Storage, improving performance and user experience.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Modify the application ... store them in the database ...</strong> Storing large images in a database introduces scalability issues and can lead to database performance bottlenecks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Configure a web server ...</strong> This approach adds complexity by requiring you to manage additional infrastructure (web servers, file storage) compared to using Cloud Storage directly.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Create individual buckets ...</strong> Managing numerous buckets and service accounts per user significantly increases complexity and might have cost implications.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e2q25":{"id":"e2q25","exam":2,"n":25,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You manage an application hosted on a Compute Engine instance and multiple backend services deployed in standalone Docker containers across Compute Engine instances managed by instance groups in different regions. To achieve loose coupling, you aim to call different service implementations based on the value of an HTTP header in the request. Which Google Cloud feature should you utilize to invoke the backend services?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Service Directory</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Internal HTTP(S) Load Balancing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Traffic Director</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Anthos Service Mesh</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Traffic Director</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why Traffic Director is the best fit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Content-based Routing:</strong> Traffic Director allows you to define routing rules based on various attributes of the request, including HTTP headers. This enables you to route requests to different backend services depending on the value of a specific header.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Global Service Management:</strong> Traffic Director is a global service, allowing you to manage traffic across multiple regions and seamlessly steer requests to distributed backend instances.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Integration with Load Balancing:</strong> Traffic Director can integrate with Internal HTTP(S) Load Balancing to distribute traffic among backend instances within each region.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Service Directory:</strong> While Service Directory aids in service discovery, it doesn't directly provide the content-based routing capabilities necessary for your scenario.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Anthos Service Mesh:</strong> Anthos Service Mesh offers advanced traffic management but introduces complexity in this case. Traffic Director is a more streamlined solution for your specific requirement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Internal HTTP(S) Load Balancing:</strong> Internal HTTP(S) Load Balancing lacks the ability to make routing decisions based on HTTP headers.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/traffic-director/docs/set-up-gce-vms  https://cloud.google.com/traffic-director/docs/overview#traffic_management\">https://cloud.google.com/traffic-director/docs/set-up-gce-vms</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/traffic-director/docs/set-up-gce-vms  https://cloud.google.com/traffic-director/docs/overview#traffic_management\">https://cloud.google.com/traffic-director/docs/overview#traffic_management</a></p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e2q26":{"id":"e2q26","exam":2,"n":26,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In response to a surge in errors within an application operating on Cloud Run within your production environment, configured to handle messages from Pub/Sub topic A, process them, and then forward them to topic B, what steps should you take to identify the error's cause, utilizing mock messages for testing?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize the Google Cloud console to generate mock messages for topic A. Adjust the application's logging level to DEBUG or INFO, then analyze the logs for diagnostic information.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Set up the Pub/Sub emulator locally. Redirect the production application to interact with local Pub/Sub topics. Generate mock messages for topic A and analyze the resulting logs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize the gcloud CLI to generate mock messages for topic A. Adjust the application's logging level to DEBUG or INFO, then analyze the logs for insights.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Install the Pub/Sub and Cloud Run emulators on your local system. Deploy the application locally, adjusting the logging level to DEBUG or INFO. Generate mock messages for topic A and analyze the application logs.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>The most effective approach to identify the error's cause in your Cloud Run application using mock messages is:</p>\n<p><strong>I. Install the Pub/Sub and Cloud Run emulators on your local system. Deploy the application locally, adjusting the logging level to DEBUG or INFO. Generate mock messages for topic A and analyze the application logs.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach is the best:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Isolated testing environment:</strong> The emulators allow you to replicate the production environment locally, isolating the issue and eliminating potential interference from other production components.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Controlled testing:</strong> You have complete control over the mock messages generated, enabling you to reproduce specific scenarios that trigger the errors.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Detailed logging:</strong> Adjusting the logging level provides granular insights into the application's behavior during message processing, helping pinpoint the root cause of the errors.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficient debugging:</strong> Debugging locally is generally faster and more convenient than troubleshooting in a production environment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let's analyze why the other options aren't as suitable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Utilize the gcloud CLI to generate mock messages for topic A.</strong> While this allows you to generate mock messages, it doesn't provide an isolated testing environment, potentially making it difficult to isolate the issue.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Set up the Pub/Sub emulator locally. Redirect the production application to interact with local Pub/Sub topics.</strong> This approach involves modifying the production application's configuration, which could introduce risks and disruptions to the live environment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Utilize the Google Cloud console to generate mock messages for topic A.</strong> Similar to option II, this lacks an isolated testing environment and might not be the most efficient way to reproduce and diagnose the errors.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>By installing the emulators and testing locally, you create a safe and controlled environment to thoroughly investigate the errors and identify their root cause without impacting the production system.</strong></p>","lessons":["d2l2","d2l7"]},"e2q27":{"id":"e2q27","exam":2,"n":27,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You are designing a deployment strategy for your new applications on Google Cloud. As part of your deployment planning, you aim to leverage live traffic to collect performance metrics for both new and existing applications. You need to test against the full production load before launch. What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Implement blue/green deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Employ A/B testing with traffic mirroring during deployment.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Opt for canary deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Choose rolling updates deployment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Employ A/B testing with traffic mirroring during deployment.</strong></p>\n<p>Let's break down why this is the most effective choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Live Production Traffic:</strong> Traffic mirroring allows you to test new code with real production traffic without impacting user experience. This gives you the most realistic performance assessment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Full Load Testing:</strong> Mirroring the entire production load ensures you're testing against the true scale of your traffic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A/B Comparison:</strong> Comparing the performance of your new application code against the existing version under identical load provides valuable insights for optimization.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Controlled Rollout:</strong> Based on the results of your A/B tests, you can gradually and safely increase traffic to the new version.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Canary Deployment:</strong> While it offers gradual rollout and risk mitigation, its focus is not on pre-launch load testing. You'd get only partial traffic exposure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Blue/Green Deployment:</strong> This is for rapid cutovers, not gradual testing with full production load. You'd also need to maintain duplicate production environments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Rolling Updates:</strong> This focuses on gradual rollout, but doesn't inherently provide side-by-side comparisons or full production load testing.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/application-deployment-and-testing-strategies#shadow_test_pattern\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/application-deployment-and-testing-strategies#shadow_test_pattern</a></p>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e2q28":{"id":"e2q28","exam":2,"n":28,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>After integrating a new application with OpenTelemetry, you aim to monitor the latency of your application requests within Trace. Your goal is to guarantee that a particular request is consistently traced. What is the recommended course of action?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Employ the Trace API to assign custom attributes to the trace.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Develop a custom script to continuously send requests of this type from your development project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Allow a 10-minute interval to confirm that Trace automatically captures these specific requests.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Append the X-Cloud-Trace-Context header to the request with the correct parameters.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Append the X-Cloud-Trace-Context header to the request with the correct parameters.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Forced Tracing:</strong> The <code>X-Cloud-Trace-Context</code> header directly instructs Cloud Trace to include the specific request in its traces. This ensures the desired request is traced regardless of other factors or sampling configurations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here's why the other options are less ideal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Waiting:</strong> Traces might not automatically capture the request, especially if sampling is in place or the request volume is low.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Scripting:</strong> While a script could help, it's not the most efficient way to force tracing for a specific request type.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Custom Attributes:</strong> Adding attributes helps provide context but doesn't guarantee the request is traced.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>References:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/setup#force-trace\">https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/setup#force-trace</a></p>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e2q29":{"id":"e2q29","exam":2,"n":29,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>As part of your role in an ecommerce company planning global expansion, you are tasked with selecting a SQL database and designing a schema capable of scaling with organizational growth. To store all customer transactions, you need to create a table ensuring uniqueness for both CustomerId and TransactionId. Which approach should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Establish a Cloud SQL table with TransactionId and CustomerId set as primary keys, utilizing random strings (UUID) for TransactionId.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Implement a Cloud Spanner table with TransactionId and CustomerId configured as primary keys, employing random strings (UUID) for TransactionId.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Establish a Cloud SQL table with TransactionId and CustomerId set as primary keys, employing incremental numbers for TransactionId.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement a Cloud Spanner table with TransactionId and CustomerId configured as primary keys, utilizing incremental numbers for TransactionId.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The best option is</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III: Implement a Cloud Spanner table with TransactionId and CustomerId configured as primary keys, employing random strings (UUID) for TransactionId.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Here's why:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> Cloud Spanner is Google Cloud's globally distributed, strongly consistent database designed for massive scale. It's ideal for handling the high volume and geographic spread expected with global expansion.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Uniqueness:</strong> Using UUIDs (Universally Unique Identifiers) for TransactionIds guarantees uniqueness across distributed systems. This is crucial in a global context where transactions might originate from different regions simultaneously.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Strong Consistency:</strong> Cloud Spanner ensures that data is always consistent, even across globally distributed nodes. This is important for financial transactions in e-commerce, where data integrity is paramount.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Cloud SQL with incremental TransactionIds:</strong> Cloud SQL is a regional database and might struggle to scale as your business expands globally. Additionally, sequential transaction IDs can become a bottleneck and increase the risk of collisions in a high-volume, distributed environment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Cloud SQL with UUID TransactionIds:</strong> While UUIDs would address the uniqueness issue, Cloud SQL's regional nature still poses a challenge for a globally distributed application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Cloud Spanner with incremental TransactionIds:</strong> This option combines the scalability of Cloud Spanner with the potential collision risks of sequential transaction IDs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/schema-design#uuid_primary_key\">https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/schema-design#uuid_primary_key</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2","d1l3"]},"e2q30":{"id":"e2q30","exam":2,"n":30,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You're structuring a schema for a Cloud Spanner customer database, aiming to incorporate a phone number array field in the customer table for efficient customer search by phone number. How should you design this schema?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Design a \"Customers\" table featuring an Array field to accommodate multiple phone numbers per customer.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Create a \"Customers\" table along with a separate \"Phones\" table. Include a \"CustomerId\" field in the \"Phones\" table to associate phone numbers with customers.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Set up a \"Customers\" table with an Array field to store phone numbers for each customer. Implement a secondary index on the Array field for efficient querying.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Establish a \"Customers\" parent table and a nested \"Phones\" child table. Interleave the \"Phones\" table into the \"Customers\" table and create an index on the phone number field in the \"Phones\" table for optimized search operations.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Given the requirement to efficiently search customers by phone number in a Cloud Spanner customer database, the most suitable schema design would be:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Establish a \"Customers\" parent table and a nested \"Phones\" child table. Interleave the \"Phones\" table into the \"Customers\" table and create an index on the phone number field in the \"Phones\" table for optimized search operations.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>I. Design a \"Customers\" table featuring an Array field to accommodate multiple phone numbers per customer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>While using an Array field might allow storage of multiple phone numbers per customer, it may not be efficient for searching and querying based on phone numbers, especially in large datasets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>II. Create a \"Customers\" table along with a separate \"Phones\" table. Include a \"CustomerId\" field in the \"Phones\" table to associate phone numbers with customers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>This approach introduces additional complexity with a separate table and a foreign key relationship. While it allows for better organization, it may not be the most efficient for querying customers by phone number.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>III. Set up a \"Customers\" table with an Array field to store phone numbers for each customer. Implement a secondary index on the Array field for efficient querying:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>While using an Array field with a secondary index may improve query performance compared to option I, it may still not provide optimal performance for searching by phone number, especially in large datasets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IV. Establish a \"Customers\" parent table and a nested \"Phones\" child table. Interleave the \"Phones\" table into the \"Customers\" table and create an index on the phone number field in the \"Phones\" table for optimized search operations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Interleaving the \"Phones\" table into the \"Customers\" table allows for co-location of related data, improving query performance. Creating an index on the phone number field in the \"Phones\" table further enhances search efficiency, making it the most suitable option for efficient customer search by phone number.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Therefore, option IV is the most appropriate schema design for efficient customer search by phone number in a Cloud Spanner database.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/schema-design#creating-indexes:\">https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/schema-design#creating-indexes:</a></p>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e2q31":{"id":"e2q31","exam":2,"n":31,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You've recently developed a web application to daily transfer log data to a Cloud Storage bucket. Authenticated users will regularly review logs from the prior two weeks for critical events, while logs will be annually reviewed by an external auditor thereafter. Data retention is required for a minimum of 7 years. To propose a cost-effective storage solution meeting these needs,</strong></p>\n<p><strong>what should you do? (Choose two.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Generate a JSON Web Token (JWT) for users requiring access to the Coldline storage buckets.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Apply the Bucket Lock feature to enforce a retention policy on the data.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>V. Configure a lifecycle management policy to transition objects older than 14 days to the Nearline storage class.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Establish a lifecycle management policy to transition objects older than 14 days to the Coldline storage class.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Schedule a job to transition objects older than 14 days to the Coldline storage class.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answers:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Schedule a job to transition objects older than 14 days to the Coldline storage class.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Establish a lifecycle management policy to transition objects older than 14 days to the Coldline storage class.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Tiered Storage:</strong> Both approaches leverage Cloud Storage's different storage classes. Since recent logs (&lt;= 14 days) are accessed frequently, keeping them on Standard storage makes sense. Older logs can safely transition to the less expensive Coldline storage class.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Automation:</strong> While scheduling a job is possible, lifecycle management policies provide a more declarative and automated way to enforce the storage tiering.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Data Retention:</strong> Neither of these solutions directly address the 7-year retention requirement. That could be handled with Object Lifecycle Management (e.g., deleting after 7 years) or Bucket Lock.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Apply the Bucket Lock feature to enforce a retention policy on the data.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cost:</strong> Bucket Lock is designed for legal holds and compliance, not primarily for cost optimization. It can become expensive for large datasets with long retention periods.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Generate a JSON Web Token (JWT) for users requiring access to the Coldline storage buckets.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Relevance:</strong> JWTs are for authentication, not directly related to cost optimization of storage.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>V. Configure a lifecycle management policy to transition objects older than 14 days to the Nearline storage class.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cost:</strong> Nearline, while less expensive than Standard, is still more costly than Coldline for infrequently accessed data.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References</strong> :<br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/bucket-lock   https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/lifecycle\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/bucket-lock </a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/bucket-lock   https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/lifecycle\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/lifecycle</a></p>","lessons":["d1l5"]},"e2q32":{"id":"e2q32","exam":2,"n":32,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>How should you design a study to determine whether a new color scheme for a global ecommerce web application hosted on Cloud Run will increase sales, utilizing live production traffic?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Utilize an external HTTP(S) load balancer to mirror traffic to the new version of the application with the updated color scheme. Analyze the results to determine whether there is a statistically significant difference in sales.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize an external HTTP(S) load balancer to direct traffic to the original color scheme while creating and testing the new deployment. Upon completion of testing, switch all traffic to the new color scheme. Analyze the results to evaluate any statistically significant difference in sales.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize an external HTTP(S) load balancer to split a predetermined percentage of traffic between two different color schemes of the application. Analyze the results to assess any statistically significant difference in sales.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement a feature flag to display the new color scheme to half of all users. Monitor sales for this group to assess whether there is an increase.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Option I: Utilize an external HTTP(S) load balancer to split a predetermined percentage of traffic between two different color schemes of the application. Analyze the results to assess any statistically significant difference in sales.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Feedback: This option effectively implements A/B testing, allowing for a controlled comparison between the original and new color schemes. By splitting traffic, it enables the assessment of any impact on sales without disrupting the user experience for all users.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Justification for selection: A/B testing is a widely used method for evaluating changes in web applications, as it provides clear insights into the effectiveness of variations. By directing a portion of the traffic to the new color scheme, it allows for a direct comparison with the original scheme, facilitating analysis of any differences in sales.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>II. Utilize an external HTTP(S) load balancer to direct traffic to the original color scheme while creating and testing the new deployment. Upon completion of testing, switch all traffic to the new color scheme. Analyze the results to evaluate any statistically significant difference in sales.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Feedback: This option involves a phased rollout approach, which may not provide immediate insights into the impact on sales. While it minimizes risk by initially testing the new color scheme on a subset of traffic, it delays the assessment of its effectiveness until after full deployment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Justification for not selecting: This approach might not be suitable for immediate evaluation of the new color scheme's impact on sales, as it delays the comparison between the original and new schemes until after full deployment. It's generally preferable to conduct A/B testing to directly compare performance variations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>III. Utilize an external HTTP(S) load balancer to mirror traffic to the new version of the application with the updated color scheme. Analyze the results to determine whether there is a statistically significant difference in sales.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Feedback: While mirroring traffic allows for observation of user behavior without affecting their experience, it doesn't directly compare the performance of the two versions. This approach might not provide clear insights into the impact of the new color scheme on sales.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Justification for not selecting: Without a direct comparison between the original and new color schemes, it may be challenging to determine whether any observed differences in sales are attributable to the color scheme change or other factors.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>IV. Implement a feature flag to display the new color scheme to half of all users. Monitor sales for this group to assess whether there is an increase.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Feedback: Implementing a feature flag allows for random assignment of users to different groups, enabling evaluation of the new color scheme's impact on sales. However, this approach doesn't split traffic evenly and may not provide a clear comparison between the two versions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Justification for not selecting: While feature flags can be useful for experimentation, they may not provide as controlled an environment for comparison as A/B testing. Randomly assigning users to different groups might not result in an even distribution of traffic between the original and new color schemes, potentially biasing the results.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>In summary, while all options present different approaches for evaluating the impact of the new color scheme on sales, Option I (utilizing A/B testing with an external HTTP(S) load balancer) provides the most direct and controlled comparison between the original and new schemes, making it the preferred choice for determining the effectiveness of the color scheme change.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/l7-internal/traffic-management#traffic_actions_weight-based_traffic_splitting  https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/https/setting-up-https-serverless\">https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/l7-internal/traffic-management#traffic_actions_weight-based_traffic_splitting</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/l7-internal/traffic-management#traffic_actions_weight-based_traffic_splitting  https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/https/setting-up-https-serverless\">https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/https/setting-up-https-serverless</a></p>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e2q33":{"id":"e2q33","exam":2,"n":33,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>In your application, users can read and post comments on news articles, and you're configuring Firestore to manage this. How should you structure the schema to handle an unspecified number of comments and articles?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Store each comment as a document and include its key in an array property associated with the article.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Store each comment as a document and include its key in an array property within the user profile.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Place each comment within an array property linked to the article.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Save each comment under a subcollection related to the article.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Save each comment under a subcollection related to the article.</strong></p>\n<p>This is the most efficient and scalable approach for these reasons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Unlimited Comments:</strong> Subcollections provide a way to store an unbounded number of comments associated with each article while maintaining a clear relationship.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficient Retrieval:</strong> Firestore is optimized for retrieving subcollections, allowing you to easily fetch all comments for a specific article.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> This design distributes the load across multiple documents, avoiding potential bottlenecks as the number of comments grows.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Array Property on Article:</strong> Arrays in Firestore have limitations on size and write operations. This could become a scaling issue as the number of comments increases, and could lead to hot-spotting where one document is frequently updated.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Array on Article with Comment IDs</strong> Storing just the comment IDs on the article introduces additional fetch operations and potential consistency concerns if comments are modified or deleted.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Array on User Profile:</strong> This design makes it difficult to efficiently retrieve comments related to a specific article.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://firebase.google.com/docs/firestore/data-model#subcollections  https://firebase.google.com/docs/firestore/best-practices#high_read_write_and_delete_rates_to_a_narrow_document_range\">https://firebase.google.com/docs/firestore/data-model#subcollections</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://firebase.google.com/docs/firestore/data-model#subcollections  https://firebase.google.com/docs/firestore/best-practices#high_read_write_and_delete_rates_to_a_narrow_document_range\">https://firebase.google.com/docs/firestore/best-practices#high_read_write_and_delete_rates_to_a_narrow_document_range</a></p>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e2q34":{"id":"e2q34","exam":2,"n":34,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You're tasked with constructing an API for Android and iOS apps, with the following prerequisites:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>HTTPS support is essential</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Bandwidth usage should be minimized</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Seamless integration with mobile apps is a priority</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Which API architecture is the most appropriate choice for meeting these criteria?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. MQTT for APIs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. SOAP-based APIs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. RESTful APIs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. gRPC-based APIs</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Given the prerequisites of HTTPS support, minimized bandwidth usage, and seamless integration with mobile apps, the most appropriate choice for API architecture would be:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. gRPC-based APIs</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>I. RESTful APIs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>While RESTful APIs are widely used and support HTTPS, they typically use JSON or XML for data serialization, which may not be as efficient in terms of bandwidth usage compared to other options like gRPC.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>II. MQTT for APIs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>MQTT (Message Queuing Telemetry Transport) is a lightweight messaging protocol designed for IoT devices with low bandwidth and high latency. While it's efficient in terms of bandwidth usage, it may not be the best choice for general-purpose APIs used in mobile apps, especially when seamless integration and support for HTTPS are essential.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>III. gRPC-based APIs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>gRPC is a modern, high-performance RPC (Remote Procedure Call) framework developed by Google. It uses HTTP/2 for transport, which supports HTTPS by default. gRPC offers efficient binary serialization using Protocol Buffers, resulting in minimized bandwidth usage compared to textual formats like JSON or XML. Additionally, gRPC provides seamless integration with mobile platforms through generated client libraries, making it a suitable choice for Android and iOS apps.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>IV. SOAP-based APIs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>SOAP (Simple Object Access Protocol) is an older, XML-based protocol for exchanging structured information in the implementation of web services. While SOAP can support HTTPS, it's generally considered more heavyweight and complex compared to other options like RESTful or gRPC-based APIs. SOAP may not be the best fit for mobile app development due to its complexity and overhead.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Therefore, option III, gRPC-based APIs, aligns well with the given prerequisites, providing HTTPS support, minimizing bandwidth usage with efficient binary serialization, and offering seamless integration with mobile apps.</strong></p>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.imaginarycloud.com/blog/grpc-vs-rest/  https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/api-management/understanding-grpc-openapi-and-rest-and-when-to-use-them\">https://www.imaginarycloud.com/blog/grpc-vs-rest/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.imaginarycloud.com/blog/grpc-vs-rest/  https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/api-management/understanding-grpc-openapi-and-rest-and-when-to-use-them\">https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/api-management/understanding-grpc-openapi-and-rest-and-when-to-use-them</a></p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e2q35":{"id":"e2q35","exam":2,"n":35,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You need to transfer the directory \"local-scripts\" and all its contents from your local workstation to a Compute Engine virtual machine instance. Which command should you use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. <code>**gcloud compute mv --project my-gcp-project --recurse ~/local-scripts/ gcp-instance-name:~/server-scripts/ --zone us-east1-b**</code></p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. <code>**gcloud compute scp --project my-gcp-project --recurse ~/local-scripts/ gcp-instance-name:~/server-scripts/ --zone us-east1-b**</code></p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. <code>**gsutil cp --project my-gcp-project -R ~/local-scripts/ gcp-instance-name:~/server-scripts/ --zone us-east1-b**</code></p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. <code>**gsutil cp --project my-gcp-project -r ~/local-scripts/ gcp-instance-name:~/server-scripts/ --zone us-east1-b**</code></p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. gcloud compute scp --project my-gcp-project --recurse ~/local-scripts/ gcp-instance-name:~/server-scripts/ --zone us-east1-b</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>gcloud compute scp:</strong> This is the appropriate tool for secure file transfers between local machines and Compute Engine instances.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>--project my-gcp-project:</strong> Specifies your Google Cloud Project for authentication.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>--recurse:</strong> This flag is essential since you want to copy the entire directory, including its contents.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>~/local-scripts/</strong> : The source path on your local machine.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>gcp-instance-name:~/server-scripts/</strong> : The destination path on the Compute Engine instance (make sure to replace 'gcp-instance-name' with your actual instance's name).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>--zone us-east1-b:</strong> Specifies the zone of your Compute Engine instance.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Incorrect:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I &amp; II. gsutil cp...</strong> The <code>gsutil</code> command is primarily for interacting with Google Cloud Storage buckets, not direct file transfers to Compute Engine instances. While there are ways to make it work, it's less straightforward than using <code>gcloud compute scp</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. gcloud compute mv ...</strong> The <code>mv</code> command is for moving or renaming files, not copying them across machines.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l11"]},"e2q36":{"id":"e2q36","exam":2,"n":36,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>To expand its user base globally for a popular application, your company seeks to ensure 99.999% availability of the database while minimizing read latency for users worldwide. Which two actions should the company prioritize? (Select two.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>V. Create at least two Cloud Spanner instances in separate regions, each with a minimum of one node.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Establish a cluster comprising a minimum of 3 Spanner nodes.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>VI. Develop a Cloud Dataflow pipeline for data replication across diverse databases.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Deploy a multi-regional Cloud Spanner instance configured with \"nam-asia-eur1\".</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Set up a cluster with at least 1 Spanner node.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Implement a multi-regional Cloud Spanner instance with the \"nam3\" configuration.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>To achieve 99.999% availability of the database while minimizing read latency for users worldwide, the company should prioritize options that enhance both availability and latency. The most suitable actions would be:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Deploy a multi-regional Cloud Spanner instance configured with \"nam-asia-eur1.\"</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>This option involves deploying Cloud Spanner in a multi-regional configuration spanning across regions in North America, Asia, and Europe. Multi-regional setups enhance availability by distributing data across multiple geographic regions, reducing the risk of downtime due to regional failures. Additionally, by choosing a configuration that includes regions from multiple continents, the company can minimize read latency for users worldwide by locating data closer to users, thereby improving overall performance.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>III. Establish a cluster comprising a minimum of 3 Spanner nodes.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Having a cluster with multiple nodes improves database availability and fault tolerance. With a minimum of 3 Spanner nodes, the system can maintain availability even if one node fails. This redundancy ensures that the database remains accessible and operational, contributing to the goal of achieving 99.999% availability.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options II, IV, V, and VI are not the optimal choices for achieving the stated objectives:</p>\n<p>II. Implement a multi-regional Cloud Spanner instance with the \"nam3\" configuration.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"nam3\" configuration refers to a regional instance in North America, which may not provide the desired global availability and latency improvements compared to a multi-regional configuration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>IV. Set up a cluster with at least 1 Spanner node.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>While having at least one node is necessary, it doesn't address the need for redundancy and fault tolerance provided by a cluster with multiple nodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>V. Create at least two Cloud Spanner instances in separate regions, each with a minimum of one node.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>While this option provides some redundancy by having multiple instances in separate regions, it doesn't offer the same level of fault tolerance and global availability as a multi-regional instance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>VI. Develop a Cloud Dataflow pipeline for data replication across diverse databases.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>While data replication is essential for disaster recovery and ensuring data availability, it doesn't directly address the goal of minimizing read latency for users worldwide or achieving 99.999% availability of the database.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Therefore, options I and III should be prioritized to achieve the company's objectives.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/instances#configs-multi-region#\">https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/instances#configs-multi-region#</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2","d1l4"]},"e2q37":{"id":"e2q37","exam":2,"n":37,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>As a developer within a large organization, you manage an application written in Go deployed on a production Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster. You're tasked with implementing a new feature that requires access to BigQuery. Your objective is to grant BigQuery access to your GKE cluster, adhering to Google's recommended best practices. How should you proceed?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Generate a Google service account with BigQuery access, store the Google service account JSON key in Secret Manager, and implement an init container to retrieve the secret for the application's usage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Establish both a Google service account and a Kubernetes service account, enable Workload Identity on the GKE cluster, and specify the Kubernetes service account within the application Deployment configuration.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Generate a Google service account with BigQuery access, store the associated JSON key securely in Secret Manager, and utilize the Go client library to access the JSON key.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Generate a Google service account with BigQuery access, incorporate the Google service account JSON key into a Kubernetes secret, and configure the application to utilize this secret.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The best answer is</p>\n<p><strong>IV: Establish both a Google service account and a Kubernetes service account, enable Workload Identity on the GKE cluster, and specify the Kubernetes service account within the application Deployment configuration.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach aligns with Google's best practices:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Workload Identity:</strong> This is the most secure and recommended way to manage service account credentials within GKE. It eliminates the need to handle service account keys directly within your pods, reducing the risk of exposing them.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> Assign the necessary BigQuery permissions only to the Google service account, ensuring that it can't access resources other than those required for the application.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I, II, and III:</strong> All involve direct handling and storage of service account keys either in secrets or an init container. While functional, this increases the potential attack surface if those keys are compromised.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity#what_is\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity#what_is</a></p>","lessons":["d4l2"]},"e2q38":{"id":"e2q38","exam":2,"n":38,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You are deploying your application on a Compute Engine instance, which needs to communicate with Cloud SQL. To ensure minimal access for the role assigned to the service account associated with the instance, following Google's best practices, what action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Assign the Cloud SQL Client role.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Designate the Project Owner role.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Grant the Cloud SQL Editor role.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Allocate the Project Editor role.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Assign the Cloud SQL Client role.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The Cloud SQL Client role is specifically designed to provide the necessary permissions for connecting to a Cloud SQL database and performing basic operations like reading and writing data. It avoids granting excessive permissions that could be misused.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security Best Practice:</strong> Google explicitly recommends using the Cloud SQL Client role for this use case to minimize risks.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are not suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Project Editor ** and </strong>II. Project Owner:** These roles grant broad permissions across the entire project, significantly violating the principle of least privilege and creating unnecessary security risks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Cloud SQL Editor:</strong> This role includes the ability to create, modify, and delete databases, which is far more powerful than what your application likely needs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/roles-and-permissions  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/roles-and-permissions#proxy-roles-permissions\">https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/roles-and-permissions</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/roles-and-permissions  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/roles-and-permissions#proxy-roles-permissions\">https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/roles-and-permissions#proxy-roles-permissions</a></p>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e2q39":{"id":"e2q39","exam":2,"n":39,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You've transitioned an on-premises monolithic application to a microservices setup on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), with backend dependencies such as a CRM system and a MySQL database containing PII residing on-premises for regulatory compliance. Despite establishing a Cloud VPN connection between your on-premises data center and Google Cloud, you're encountering latency-related crashes due to fluctuating bandwidth. How should you tackle these latency issues?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Implement Memorystore to cache frequently accessed PII data from the on-premises MySQL database</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Scale up the number of Cloud VPN tunnels for the connection between Google Cloud and the on-premises services</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Reduce network layer packet size by adjusting the Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU) value from its default setting on Cloud VPN</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize Istio to establish a service mesh encompassing the microservices on GKE and the on-premises services</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Scale up the number of Cloud VPN tunnels for the connection between Google Cloud and the on-premises services.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Scaling Cloud VPN Tunnels: Increasing the number of Cloud VPN tunnels can help distribute the network traffic between your on-premises data center and Google Cloud, thus potentially mitigating the impact of fluctuating bandwidth and reducing latency-related crashes. This approach can provide additional bandwidth and redundancy, improving the stability and reliability of the connection.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Options I, II, and IV are less likely to directly address the latency issues caused by fluctuating bandwidth:</p>\n<p>I. Implementing Memorystore to cache frequently accessed PII data from the on-premises MySQL database might improve performance by reducing the need to access the database frequently, but it may not directly address the latency issues caused by fluctuating bandwidth between the on-premises data center and Google Cloud.</p>\n<p>II. Utilizing Istio to establish a service mesh can help manage and secure communication between microservices, but it may not directly alleviate the latency issues arising from the fluctuating bandwidth between on-premises services and microservices on GKE.</p>\n<p>IV. Adjusting the Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU) value on Cloud VPN could potentially optimize network performance, but it may not be as effective in addressing latency issues caused by fluctuating bandwidth compared to scaling up the number of VPN tunnels. Additionally, adjusting MTU values requires careful consideration and testing to avoid introducing compatibility or performance issues.</p>\n<p><strong>Hint:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/network-connectivity/docs/vpn/concepts/topologies#more-bandwidth  https://cloud.google.com/network-connectivity/docs/vpn/concepts/choosing-networks-routing#route-alignment\">https://cloud.google.com/network-connectivity/docs/vpn/concepts/topologies#more-bandwidth</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/network-connectivity/docs/vpn/concepts/topologies#more-bandwidth  https://cloud.google.com/network-connectivity/docs/vpn/concepts/choosing-networks-routing#route-alignment\">https://cloud.google.com/network-connectivity/docs/vpn/concepts/choosing-networks-routing#route-alignment</a></p>","lessons":["d3l10"]},"e2q40":{"id":"e2q40","exam":2,"n":40,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your existing application manages user state data in a single MySQL database. This data is intensely user-specific and intricately tied to user activity duration. However, maintaining and evolving the schema for diverse user requirements in the MySQL database is proving challenging. Which storage option is the optimal choice in this situation?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Cloud SQL</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Cloud Datastore/Firestore</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Cloud Spanner</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Cloud Storage</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Cloud Datastore/Firestore (Firestore in document mode)</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why it's particularly well-suited to this situation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Schema Flexibility:</strong> Firestore's document model allows for flexibility and variation in your data structures. Each user's document can have its own fields and nested data, accommodating changing requirements without strict schema constraints.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Focus on User Data:</strong> Firestore is designed for storing and retrieving data quickly on a per-user basis, making it a good fit for your intensely user-specific data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> Firestore scales automatically to handle large numbers of users and data without complex management on your part.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Evolving Requirements:</strong> Firestore's flexibility allows you to add or modify fields for individual users as needed, making it easier to adapt to changing needs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Might Be Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud SQL:</strong> While SQL offers robust relational modeling, its schema-on-write approach might hinder your ability to rapidly adapt to evolving user-specific data structures.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Storage:</strong> Cloud Storage is great for storing objects or files, but it's not designed for structured, queryable user data with complex relationships.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Spanner:</strong> Spanner is a powerful distributed database, but its strength lies in its scalability and consistency across large, structured datasets. It might be overkill if the primary challenge is schema flexibility for individual user data.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/datastore/docs/concepts/overview#what_its_good_for\">https://cloud.google.com/datastore/docs/concepts/overview#what_its_good_for</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e2q41":{"id":"e2q41","exam":2,"n":41,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>In the process of auditing all deployed applications in Google Kubernetes Engine, your security team found that some applications transmit data within the cluster in plain text. How can you swiftly encrypt all application traffic, minimizing alterations to applications and ensuring Google support?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Establish Trusted Network ranges within the application, allowing traffic exclusively from these networks.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Employ an automated procedure to procure SSL Certificates for applications from Let's Encrypt and integrate them into the applications.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy Istio, activate proxy injection in the application namespace, and then implement mutual TLS (mTLS).</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Implement Network Policies to restrict traffic between applications.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Deploy Istio, activate proxy injection in the application namespace, and then implement mutual TLS (mTLS).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Istio is a service mesh explicitly engineered for managing, securing, and observing microservices-based applications.</strong> It accomplishes this by adding sidecar proxies next to each application instance (injected automatically). These sidecars regulate the flow of traffic within the cluster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Mutual TLS offers robust encryption and identity verification for service-to-service communication.</strong> This guarantees the confidentiality and integrity of data exchanged between applications.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>The use of Istio and mTLS satisfies the requirement for minimal modifications to the applications themselves.</strong> The proxies manage encryption transparently.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Istio is widely used with Kubernetes and offers robust Google support.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Implement Network Policies to restrict traffic between applications.</strong> Network policies offer traffic control mechanisms. But they neither encrypt data nor address the clear mandate of encrypting application traffic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Establish Trusted Network ranges within the application, allowing traffic exclusively from these networks.</strong> This strategy concentrates on access control and relies on the initial trustworthiness of those \"trusted\" networks. It doesn't inherent encrypt traffic in transit.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Employ an automated procedure to procure SSL Certificates for applications from Let's Encrypt and integrate them into the applications.</strong> While this improves web traffic security, it presents several challenges:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>It requires significant modifications to applications to incorporate those certificates.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>It doesn't address other communication protocols within the cluster beyond standard web traffic.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/istio/docs/istio-on-gke/installing  https://cloud.google.com/istio/docs/istio-on-gke/overview Istio gives you the following benefits\">https://cloud.google.com/istio/docs/istio-on-gke/installing</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/istio/docs/istio-on-gke/installing  https://cloud.google.com/istio/docs/istio-on-gke/overview Istio gives you the following benefits\">https://cloud.google.com/istio/docs/istio-on-gke/overview Istio gives you the following benefits</a></p>","lessons":["d1l8"]},"e2q42":{"id":"e2q42","exam":2,"n":42,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>As a developer working on an internal payroll processing application, you're tasked with implementing a component for timesheet submission, triggering various independent steps. Which approach should you take to ensure effective communication with downstream systems and accommodate future step additions?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Develop a timesheet microservice deployed on Google Kubernetes Engine, orchestrating calls to each downstream step and awaiting successful responses before proceeding.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Set up a Pub/Sub topic dedicated to timesheet submissions, with subscriptions created for each downstream team to receive notifications.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Establish separate Pub/Sub topics for each step, with subscriptions tailored for downstream development teams to subscribe to their specific step.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Implement individual Cloud Functions for each step, enabling direct calls to respective downstream systems.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Set up a Pub/Sub topic dedicated to timesheet submissions, with subscriptions created for each downstream team to receive notifications.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach excels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Loose Coupling:</strong> Pub/Sub promotes a decoupled architecture. The timesheet submission component simply publishes an event; downstream systems subscribe without tight dependencies.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> New steps can be easily added by creating additional subscriptions, without needing to modify the core timesheet submission logic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reliability:</strong> Pub/Sub provides durable messaging, helping handle temporary issues with downstream systems.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Future-Proof:</strong> This architecture aligns with event-driven patterns, accommodating changing requirements gracefully.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l6","d4l7"]},"e2q43":{"id":"e2q43","exam":2,"n":43,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>As you deploy your applications on Compute Engine, you face a failure to launch one of your Compute Engine instances. What steps should you take? (Select two.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Investigate firewall rules or routes on the instance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>V. Review whether network traffic to or from your instance is being dropped.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Check if your instance's boot disk is completely full.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Try accessing Compute Engine using an alternative SSH user.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Evaluate whether your file system is corrupted.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Check if your instance's boot disk is completely full.</strong> A full boot disk can prevent the operating system from loading, hindering the startup process.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Evaluate whether your file system is corrupted.</strong> File system corruption on the boot disk can also cause startup issues.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here's why the other options are less important initially:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Try accessing Compute Engine using an alternative SSH user.</strong> This assumes the instance has at least booted, allowing for SSH access. It's helpful for later-stage troubleshooting, not when the instance won't launch at all.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Investigate firewall rules or routes on the instance.</strong> Similar to the previous point, this assumes the instance has some level of functionality, whereas firewall issues are unlikely to prevent initial startup entirely.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>V. Review whether network traffic to or from your instance is being dropped.</strong> Network connectivity issues, while possible, are less likely to be the sole cause of a complete launch failure.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/troubleshooting/vm-startup#identify_the_reason_why_the_boot_disk_isnt_booting\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/troubleshooting/vm-startup#identify_the_reason_why_the_boot_disk_isnt_booting</a></p>","lessons":["d3l11"]},"e2q44":{"id":"e2q44","exam":2,"n":44,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>As a developer at a large organization deploying a web application to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), you're tasked with configuring a CI/CD pipeline using Cloud Deploy. The pipeline should deploy the application to Dev, Test, and Prod clusters in GKE. Following Google-recommended best practices, how should you configure the process to automatically promote the application from the Dev to the Test cluster after a successful deployment?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Establish a Cloud Build pipeline utilizing the gke-deploy builder. Create a Cloud Build trigger to monitor SUCCEEDED Pub/Sub messages from the cloud-builds topic. Configure this pipeline to execute a deployment step to the Test cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Develop a Cloud Function to call the Google Cloud Deploy API for promoting the application to the Test cluster. Configure this function to be triggered by SUCCEEDED Pub/Sub messages from the cloud-builds topic.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Create a Cloud Function to invoke the Google Cloud Deploy API for promoting the application to the Test cluster. Configure this function to be triggered by SUCCEEDED Pub/Sub messages from the clouddeploy-operations topic.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Set up a Cloud Build trigger to monitor SUCCEEDED Pub/Sub messages from the clouddeploy-operations topic. Configure Cloud Build to include a step for promoting the application to the Test cluster.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>III. Create a Cloud Function to invoke the Google Cloud Deploy API for promoting the application to the Test cluster. Configure this function to be triggered by SUCCEEDED Pub/Sub messages from the clouddeploy-operations topic.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Automation &amp; Integration:</strong> Cloud Deploy emits events to a Pub/Sub topic (<code>clouddeploy-operations</code>) when deployment operations succeed or fail. A Cloud Function directly subscribing to this topic provides a clean, event-driven promotion mechanism.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>API Directness:</strong> Utilizing the Google Cloud Deploy API within the Cloud Function ensures you're directly controlling the promotion step and adhering to Google's prescribed practices.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Cloud Functions provide flexibility if you need additional logic or customization around the promotion process.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Set up a Cloud Build trigger to monitor SUCCEEDED Pub/Sub messages...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scope:</strong> While Cloud Build is excellent for CI/CD pipelines, it's not the primary tool for managing promotions within Cloud Deploy. Using a Cloud Function aligns better with Cloud Deploy's native mechanisms.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. ... Configure this function to be triggered by SUCCEEDED Pub/Sub messages from the cloud-builds topic.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Topic Mismatch:</strong> The <code>cloud-builds</code> topic is related to build events, not Cloud Deploy's deployment lifecycle events.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Establish a Cloud Build pipeline utilizing the gke-deploy builder ...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Separation of Concerns:</strong> While Cloud Build is suitable for the initial deployment steps, it's cleaner to use Cloud Deploy's native eventing and API for managing promotions between environments.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/calling/pubsub   https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/integrating#integrating_with_automated_testing  https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automate-builds-pubsub-events#console_2  https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/integrating#before_you_begin\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/calling/pubsub https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/integrating#integrating_with_automated_testing</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/calling/pubsub   https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/integrating#integrating_with_automated_testing  https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automate-builds-pubsub-events#console_2  https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/integrating#before_you_begin\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automate-builds-pubsub-events#console_2</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/calling/pubsub   https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/integrating#integrating_with_automated_testing  https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automate-builds-pubsub-events#console_2  https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/integrating#before_you_begin\">https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/integrating#before_you_begin</a></p>","lessons":["d3l8"]},"e2q45":{"id":"e2q45","exam":2,"n":45,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>As the manager responsible for your company's ecommerce platform payment system on Google Cloud, you're mandated to retain user logs for specific durations. Logs must be kept for 1 year for internal audits and for 3 years to meet compliance standards. To reduce on-premises storage usage and ensure efficient searchability, what's the optimal method for storing new user logs on Google Cloud with minimal effort while ensuring correct storage?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Store logs in a Cloud Storage bucket with bucket locking enabled.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Store logs in a Cloud Storage bucket with a 3-year retention period.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Employ Cloud Logging to store logs as customized logs with a tailored retention duration.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Initially store logs in a Cloud Storage bucket with a 1-year retention period. After 1 year, transfer logs to another bucket with a 2-year retention period.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The optimal method for storing new user logs on Google Cloud with minimal effort while ensuring correct storage is option III:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Employ Cloud Logging to store logs as customized logs with a tailored retention duration.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Logging (formerly Stackdriver Logging)</strong> : Cloud Logging is a fully managed service by Google Cloud Platform for collecting, viewing, and analyzing logs from applications and services. It offers customizable retention periods, making it an ideal solution for meeting specific compliance requirements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Customized retention duration</strong> : With Cloud Logging, you can set retention periods tailored to your specific needs. In this scenario, you can set a retention period of 1 year for internal audits and 3 years to meet compliance standards.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Options I, II, and IV do not fully address the requirements and may introduce unnecessary complexity:</p>\n<p>I. Storing logs in a Cloud Storage bucket with bucket locking enabled can prevent deletion of objects but doesn't address the need for different retention periods for internal audits and compliance standards.</p>\n<p>II. Storing logs in a Cloud Storage bucket with a fixed 3-year retention period doesn't meet the requirement of retaining logs for 1 year for internal audits.</p>\n<p>IV. Initially storing logs in a Cloud Storage bucket with a 1-year retention period and then transferring logs to another bucket with a 2-year retention period after 1 year adds unnecessary complexity and management overhead. Using Cloud Logging with customized retention durations is a more straightforward solution.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/buckets#custom-retention  https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/central-log-storage\">https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/buckets#custom-retention</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/buckets#custom-retention  https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/central-log-storage\">https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/central-log-storage</a></p>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e2q46":{"id":"e2q46","exam":2,"n":46,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You are utilizing Cloud Run to host a web application and require a secure method to retrieve and display the application's project ID and region to users. Your goal is to implement the most performant approach. What action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Access the Google Cloud console, navigate to the Project Dashboard to collect configuration details, and then proceed to the Cloud Run \"Variables &amp; Secrets\" tab to add the desired environment variables in Key:Value format.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize HTTP requests to query the metadata server available at the <code>**http://metadata.google.internal/**</code> endpoint with the <code>**Metadata-Flavor: Google**</code> header.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Make an API call to the Cloud Asset Inventory API from the application, formatting the request to include instance metadata.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Access the Google Cloud console, navigate to the Project Dashboard to collect configuration details, and write the application configuration information to Cloud Run's in-memory container filesystem.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Utilize HTTP requests to query the metadata server available at the</strong><a href=\"http://metadata.google/\"><strong>http://metadata.google</strong></a><strong>.internal/ endpoint with the Metadata-Flavor: Google header.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficiency:</strong> Accessing this built-in metadata server is the most direct and performant way to retrieve information about the Cloud Run environment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Specificity:</strong> You can query specific values like <code>computeMetadata/v1/project/project-id</code> and <code>computeMetadata/v1/instance/zone</code> for the project ID and region respectively.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> The metadata server is only accessible from within the service instance itself, enhancing security.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Dynamic:</strong> The metadata server provides up-to-date information.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Access the Google Cloud console, navigate to the Project Dashboard to collect configuration details, and then proceed to the Cloud Run \"Variables &amp; Secrets\" tab to add the desired environment variables in Key:Value format.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Manual Intervention:</strong> This requires manual updates every time your deployment changes which is inefficient and prone to errors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Access the Google Cloud console, navigate to the Project Dashboard to collect configuration details, and write the application configuration information to Cloud Run's in-memory container filesystem.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Static:</strong> This method doesn't provide the dynamic aspect of retrieving real-time project and region information. It also creates potential inconsistency if the information changes.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Make an API call to the Cloud Asset Inventory API from the application, formatting the request to include instance metadata.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Overhead:</strong> Calling an external API introduces additional latency and complexity compared to the metadata server.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Here's a simple example (Python) of how to query the metadata server:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Python</strong></p>\n<pre><code>      1.  import requests\n  2.  \n  3. def get_project_id():\n  4.     metadata_url = 'http://metadata.google.internal/computeMetadata/v1/project/project-id'\n  5.     headers = {'Metadata-Flavor': 'Google'}\n  6.     response = requests.get(metadata_url, headers=headers)\n  7.     return response.text\n  8.  \n  9. print(get_project_id())\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/container-contract#metadata-server\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/container-contract#metadata-server</a></p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e2q47":{"id":"e2q47","exam":2,"n":47,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>In developing an application comprising various internal microservices on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), you seek the ability to configure each microservice with a distinct number of replicas. Additionally, you aim for a uniform approach to address any microservice from another, irrespective of the microservice's scaling. How should you implement this solution?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Deploy each microservice as a Pod, exposing it within the cluster via a Service. Employ the Service DNS name for microservice-to-microservice interaction.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Deploy each microservice as a Deployment, exposing it within the cluster via a Service. Utilize the Service DNS name for inter-microservice communication.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy each microservice as a Pod, exposing it within the cluster via an Ingress. Utilize the Ingress IP address for communication between microservices.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy each microservice as a Deployment, exposing it within the cluster via an Ingress. Utilize the Ingress IP address for communication between microservices.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Deploy each microservice as a Deployment, exposing it within the cluster via a Service. Utilize the Service DNS name for inter-microservice communication.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach excels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scaling Flexibility:</strong> Deployments allow you to easily control the number of replicas for each microservice, providing granular scaling to match workload demands.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reliability:</strong> Services provide stable, internal DNS names that remain consistent even if the underlying Pods are rescheduled or replaced.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Abstraction:</strong> Using Service DNS names abstracts away the details of individual Pods, making it simpler to reason about inter-microservice communication within the cluster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Kubernetes-Native:</strong> This approach aligns with standard Kubernetes practices for deploying and exposing microservices.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Deploy each microservice as a Deployment, exposing it within the cluster via an Ingress. Utilize the Ingress IP address for communication between microservices.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ingresses are primarily designed for external traffic entering the cluster. While technically possible, it's less efficient for internal service-to-service communication.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Deploy each microservice as a Pod, exposing it within the cluster via a Service. Employ the Service DNS name for microservice-to-microservice interaction.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pods are ephemeral; they can be created and destroyed frequently. Deployments provide a higher-level abstraction and management capabilities that are better suited for microservices.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Deploy each microservice as a Pod, exposing it within the cluster via an Ingress. Utilize the Ingress IP address for communication between microservices.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This combines the drawbacks of both Pods and using Ingress for internal communication, making it the least desirable option.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e2q48":{"id":"e2q48","exam":2,"n":48,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Your API backend operates across multiple cloud providers, and you need to analyze the network latency of your API. Which two actions should you take? (Select two.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Utilize Zipkin collector to gather data efficiently.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>V. Employ Stackdriver Profiler to identify performance bottlenecks and generate reports.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Leverage Stackdriver Debugger to analyze network latency issues.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Implement Fluentd agent to collect data seamlessly.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Use Stackdriver Trace for generating comprehensive latency reports.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Utilize Zipkin collector to gather data efficiently.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Distributed Tracing:</strong> Zipkin is designed explicitly for tracing requests across distributed systems, including those spanning multiple cloud providers. It's compatible with various frameworks and protocols.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Latency Analysis:</strong> Zipkin captures detailed timing data about each \"span\" within a request, crucial for network latency analysis.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Use Stackdriver Trace for generating comprehensive latency reports.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Trace Analysis:</strong> Stackdriver Trace ingests trace data (including from Zipkin) and provides powerful visualization and analysis tools specifically tailored to investigating latency issues.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cross-Cloud Visibility:</strong> If parts of your backend are on GCP, Stackdriver Trace will integrate with them natively.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Implement Fluentd agent ...</strong> While Fluentd is a versatile log aggregator, it's not focused on distributed tracing. It would require custom processing to extract the latency data you need.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Leverage Stackdriver Debugger ...</strong> Debugger is primarily for debugging running code, not for tracing distributed request paths and measuring network time specifically.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>V. Employ Stackdriver Profiler ...</strong> Profiler focuses on code-level performance issues, not end-to-end network latency.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/zipkin  https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/zipkin#frequently_asked_questions\">https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/zipkin</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/zipkin  https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/zipkin#frequently_asked_questions\">https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/zipkin#frequently_asked_questions</a></p>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e2q49":{"id":"e2q49","exam":2,"n":49,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>Your application relies on service accounts for authentication to GCP services, utilizing credentials stored on its Compute Engine virtual machine instances. You aim to securely distribute these credentials to the host instances. What's the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Generate a P12 file from the GCP Console post-deployment, transferring the credentials to the host instance prior to application launch.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize the instance's service account Application Default Credentials for authentication to the necessary resources.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Embed the credential JSON file within your application's source repository, integrating it into the deployment process via CI/CD for package delivery to the instance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Implement HTTP signed URLs for secure access to the required resources.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Utilize the instance's service account Application Default Credentials for authentication to the necessary resources.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the most secure and streamlined approach:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Automatically Managed by Google:</strong> Compute Engine VMs have metadata servers providing access to the associated service account's credentials. Google handles rotation and management of these credentials.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>No Manual Distribution</strong> : You avoid the risks of storing and transferring sensitive credential files.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> Libraries that support Application Default Credentials (ADCs) make it easy to grant the instance only the permissions it needs by assigning appropriate IAM roles to the service account.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Implement HTTP signed URLs ...</strong> Signed URLs are primarily for granting time-limited access to objects, not for general application authentication to most GCP services.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Generate a P12 file ...</strong> Manually transferring credentials creates security risks (potential leaks) and management overhead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Embed the credential JSON file ...</strong> Embedding credentials in your source code is extremely insecure, risking exposure if the repository is compromised.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/authentication/production#automatically  https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/api/how-tos/authorization:\">https://cloud.google.com/docs/authentication/production#automatically</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/authentication/production#automatically  https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/api/how-tos/authorization:\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/api/how-tos/authorization:</a></p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e2q50":{"id":"e2q50","exam":2,"n":50,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>After mistakenly typing a configuration error in a low-level Linux file, preventing your Compute Engine instance from booting normally, what corrective action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Rectify the mistake by configuring and logging into the Compute Engine instance using a remote desktop client.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Download the file via scp, modify it locally, and then upload the corrected version to the Compute Engine instance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Correct the error by configuring and accessing the Compute Engine instance through the serial port connection.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Access and modify the file directly by configuring and logging into the Compute Engine instance via SSH.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>Here's the most effective way to resolve this issue:</p>\n<p><strong>III. Correct the error by configuring and accessing the Compute Engine instance through the serial port connection.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the best approach:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>System Can't Fully Boot:</strong> The configuration error prevents your instance from booting normally, making traditional remote access options like SSH unavailable.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Serial Port Access:</strong> The serial port provides access even when regular boot processes fail. This lets you directly interact with the early boot stages and file system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Corrects the Root Cause:</strong> You can diagnose and fix the configuration error in the file, addressing the issue preventing a successful boot.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Downloading and Uploading:</strong> This assumes that you can still access the file system somehow, which might not be the case if the system can't boot completely.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. SSH Access:</strong> SSH requires that the instance is booted with a functional network stack – a scenario the configuration error likely prevents.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Remote Desktop:</strong> Remote desktop relies on the instance being fully booted and running a graphical interface, which is unlikely with this error.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/troubleshooting/troubleshooting-using-serial-console\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/troubleshooting/troubleshooting-using-serial-console</a></p>","lessons":["d3l11"]},"e2q51":{"id":"e2q51","exam":2,"n":51,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Your team is responsible for crafting services operating on Google Kubernetes Engine. Storing your team's code in Cloud Source Repositories, you're aiming to swiftly pinpoint bugs in the code prior to deployment to production. Your objective is to streamline developer feedback and optimize the process through automation. What's the recommended course of action?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Leverage Spinnaker to automate the deployment of container images to the production environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Employ Cloud Build to automate the creation of container images from code, initiated by forked versions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Employ Spinnaker to automate the creation of container images from code, triggered by Git tags.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize Cloud Build to automate the creation of container images from code, activated by Git tags.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Utilize Cloud Build to automate the creation of container images from code, activated by Git tags.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Focus on Pre-Deployment Testing:</strong> The goal here is to catch bugs <strong>before</strong> code reaches production. Cloud Build excels at automating image creation, a key part of a pre-deployment testing and validation pipeline.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Git Tag Integration:</strong> Tagging in source control often signifies a release candidate. Using Git tags to trigger Cloud Build ensures the image built reflects a potentially releasable state of your code, ideal for testing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Streamlined Workflow:</strong> Cloud Build offers a well-integrated workflow for building container images directly from Cloud Source Repositories, promoting efficiency for developers.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let's analyze why other options are less optimal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Employ Spinnaker...</strong> Spinnaker is primarily a continuous delivery (CD) platform focused on deployment automation. While it can integrate image building tasks, its strength lies in managing deployments, not pre-deployment testing feedback.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Leverage Spinnaker...</strong> Again, Spinnaker's core competency is deployment, making it less ideal for the bug-identification stage that happens earlier in the pipeline.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Employ Cloud Build...forked versions.</strong> Building images from forked versions could introduce inconsistencies and make it harder to track the connection between code and image for testing purposes. Using Git tags provides a more controlled and predictable workflow.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e2q52":{"id":"e2q52","exam":2,"n":52,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your application is managed by a managed instance group. You aim to distribute a large read-only dataset among all instances in the group. Your goal is to ensure swift instance startup and access to the dataset via the filesystem with minimal latency. Additionally, you seek to minimize the overall solution cost. What's the optimal approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Transfer the data to a Cloud Storage bucket and copy it to the boot disk of each instance using a startup script.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Move the data to a Cloud Storage bucket and mount the bucket on the filesystem using Cloud Storage FUSE.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Migrate the data to a Compute Engine persistent disk and attach the disk in read-only mode to multiple Compute Engine virtual machine instances.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Shift the data to a Compute Engine persistent disk, create a snapshot, produce multiple disks from the snapshot, and attach each disk to its respective instance.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Given the requirements of ensuring swift instance startup, minimal latency for dataset access, and minimizing overall solution cost, the optimal approach would be:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Migrate the data to a Compute Engine persistent disk and attach the disk in read-only mode to multiple Compute Engine virtual machine instances.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>I. Move the data to a Cloud Storage bucket and mount the bucket on the filesystem using Cloud Storage FUSE:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>While Cloud Storage FUSE allows you to mount a Cloud Storage bucket as a filesystem, it may introduce latency, especially for large read-only datasets, as data needs to be fetched over the network. Additionally, there might be cost implications depending on the volume of data accessed and the number of requests made to Cloud Storage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>II. Transfer the data to a Cloud Storage bucket and copy it to the boot disk of each instance using a startup script:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>This approach would copy the data to each instance's boot disk, potentially increasing instance startup time and leading to duplication of data across instances. Additionally, it might not be scalable or cost-effective, especially for large datasets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>III. Migrate the data to a Compute Engine persistent disk and attach the disk in read-only mode to multiple Compute Engine virtual machine instances:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>This approach allows you to store the dataset on a persistent disk, ensuring swift instance startup as the data is readily available on the disk. Attaching the disk in read-only mode ensures that multiple instances can access the dataset without risk of modification. This approach minimizes latency for dataset access and can be cost-effective, especially if the dataset is large and accessed frequently.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>IV. Shift the data to a Compute Engine persistent disk, create a snapshot, produce multiple disks from the snapshot, and attach each disk to its respective instance:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>While this approach allows you to create multiple disks from a snapshot, it might introduce complexity and management overhead. Additionally, attaching multiple disks to instances might not be as efficient as attaching a single read-only disk to multiple instances, especially if the dataset is static and read-only.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Therefore, option III, migrating the data to a Compute Engine persistent disk and attaching the disk in read-only mode to multiple Compute Engine virtual machine instances, is the optimal approach for meeting the requirements outlined.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/disks/sharing-disks-between-vms#use-multi-instances  https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/disks/gcs-buckets#mount_bucket  https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/gcsfuse/\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/disks/sharing-disks-between-vms#use-multi-instances</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/disks/sharing-disks-between-vms#use-multi-instances  https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/disks/gcs-buckets#mount_bucket  https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/gcsfuse/\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/disks/gcs-buckets#mount_bucket</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/disks/sharing-disks-between-vms#use-multi-instances  https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/disks/gcs-buckets#mount_bucket  https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/gcsfuse/\">https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/gcsfuse/</a></p>","lessons":["d1l5"]},"e2q53":{"id":"e2q53","exam":2,"n":53,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You have a variety of packaged and internally developed applications hosted on a Linux Compute Engine instance. These applications log data as text in local files. You want these logs to be stored in Cloud Logging. What's the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Implement a Google-provided version of collectd on the Compute Engine instance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Set up a cron job to transfer the log files to Cloud Storage once a day.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy a Google-provided version of fluentd on the Compute Engine instance.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Configure the files' content to be directed to the Linux Syslog daemon.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Deploy a Google-provided version of Fluentd on the Compute Engine instance.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the best option:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Log Collection &amp; Parsing:</strong> Fluentd is a powerful open-source log collector designed specifically for this purpose. It can parse various log formats, structure data, and send it to Cloud Logging efficiently.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Integration:</strong> Google provides a pre-configured Fluentd agent tailored for integration with Cloud Logging. This simplifies setup and ensures compatibility.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Fluentd can handle not only standard text log files but also diverse log sources, providing you with room for expansion.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Linux Syslog:</strong> While Syslog could be a step, it doesn't directly forward logs to Cloud Logging. You'd need an additional tool to bridge this gap.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Collectd:</strong> Collectd is primarily focused on system and performance metrics, not general-purpose log collection.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Cron job to Cloud Storage:</strong> This approach lacks real-time insights, adds complexity by requiring further processing for Cloud Logging, and isn't ideal for large volumes of logs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/agents/ops-agent\">https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/agents/ops-agent</a></p>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e2q54":{"id":"e2q54","exam":2,"n":54,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In the process of adding unit tests to your application, you aim to assert that published Pub/Sub messages are processed by your subscriber in the correct order. Your priority is to ensure the unit tests are cost-effective and reliable. What approach should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize the Pub/Sub emulator for testing purposes.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Establish a topic and subscription for each tester.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Implement a mocking framework for testing.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Introduce a filter by tester to the subscription.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>Here's the breakdown of why the best option is:</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Utilize the Pub/Sub emulator for testing purposes.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach is the most suitable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cost-Effectiveness:</strong> The Pub/Sub emulator runs locally, eliminating costs associated with using the actual Google Cloud Pub/Sub service during testing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Isolation:</strong> The emulator provides a controlled testing environment, allowing you to focus on message order verification without external interference.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> You can easily manipulate message order and timing in the emulator, enabling thorough testing of your subscriber's logic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reliability:</strong> The emulator offers a consistent and predictable environment for unit testing, enhancing test reliability.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Implement a mocking framework for testing.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mocking can be useful for certain aspects of subscriber testing but may struggle to fully replicate Pub/Sub behavior and message ordering in a complex system.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Establish a topic and subscription for each tester.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This approach introduces provisioning and management overhead within the production Pub/Sub service and can become costly with a large number of testers.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Introduce a filter by tester to the subscription.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Filtering adds complexity and may not be a sca</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e2q55":{"id":"e2q55","exam":2,"n":55,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You are developing an internal application to facilitate organizing community events within your company. The application is deployed on a single Compute Engine instance. Your company utilizes Google Workspace (formerly G Suite), and you need to ensure that company employees can authenticate to the application from anywhere. What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Assign a public IP address to your instance and enable internet traffic. Generate a random hash and create a subdomain containing this hash, pointing it to your instance. Share this DNS address with your company's employees.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Set up an HTTP(S) load balancer in front of the instance and configure Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP). Customize the IAP settings to grant access to the website from your company's domain.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Establish a VPN tunnel between your company network and your instance's VPC location on Google Cloud. Configure necessary firewall rules and routing information for both on-premises and Google Cloud networks.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Provide a public IP address to your instance and restrict access using firewall rules, allowing only your company's proxy as the source IP address.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Set up an HTTP(S) load balancer in front of the instance and configure Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP). Customize the IAP settings to grant access to the website from your company's domain.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the ideal choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Secure and Integrated:</strong> IAP leverages Google Workspace for authentication, ensuring only authorized company employees can access the application. No need to manage separate credentials.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ease of Access:</strong> Users don't need a VPN to access the application from anywhere as long as they can sign in with their Google Workspace account.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> The load balancer can help manage traffic growth if the application becomes more popular.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Best Practice:</strong> IAP is Google's recommended solution for this scenario.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Firewall Restrictions:</strong> While possible, managing firewall rules for individual company proxies can be cumbersome and prone to configuration errors.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. VPN Tunnel:</strong> A VPN adds overhead and complexity, especially for a single application with potentially many remote users.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Obscure URL:</strong> Relying on an obscure URL is not a true security mechanism and offers a poor user experience. It's also not integrated with your company's identity system.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iap/docs/concepts-overview#how_iap_works  https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/developers-practitioners/control-access-your-web-sites-identity-aware-proxy\">https://cloud.google.com/iap/docs/concepts-overview#how_iap_works</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iap/docs/concepts-overview#how_iap_works  https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/developers-practitioners/control-access-your-web-sites-identity-aware-proxy\">https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/developers-practitioners/control-access-your-web-sites-identity-aware-proxy</a></p>","lessons":["d1l8"]},"e2q56":{"id":"e2q56","exam":2,"n":56,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You've created a JavaScript web application requiring access to Google Drive's API to store files in users' Google Drives. What's the recommended authorization approach for your application?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Generate a SAML token.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Generate an OAuth Client ID.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Generate an API key.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Generate a service account.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Generate an OAuth Client ID.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>User-Centric Authorization:</strong> OAuth 2.0 is designed for applications to access user data on behalf of the user, which is exactly what your application needs to do (store files in a user's Drive).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Fine-grained Scopes:</strong> OAuth allows you to request specific access scopes like \"[invalid URL removed]\" to limit your app's access to only the Drive functionality it needs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security and User Control:</strong> OAuth provides a secure authorization flow where users explicitly grant your application permission. They can revoke this access if needed.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Generate an API key.</strong> API keys primarily identify your project to Google but don't associate the request with a specific user. They lack the fine-grained control needed for user-specific data access.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Generate a SAML token.</strong> SAML (Security Assertion Markup Language) is used for Single Sign-On (SSO) with identity providers and not directly related to authorizing data access for web apps.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Generate a service account.</strong> Service accounts are designed for server-to-server communication where there's no direct user interaction involved.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://developers.google.com/drive/api/guides/api-specific-auth\">https://developers.google.com/drive/api/guides/api-specific-auth</a></p>","lessons":["d4l3"]},"e2q57":{"id":"e2q57","exam":2,"n":57,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You are tasked with deploying an internet-facing microservices application to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) and implementing A/B testing for new features. Your deployment must adhere to the following requirements:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Zero downtime during the deployment of new container images.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Testing and verification of new production releases using a subset of production users.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Which approach should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Install Anthos Service Mesh on the GKE cluster. Create two Deployments labeled with different version names. Implement an Istio routing rule to direct a small percentage of traffic to the Deployment with the new application version.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement a rolling update pattern by gradually replacing Pods with the new release version. Validate application performance for the subset of users during the rollout and rollback if necessary.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Create a second namespace on GKE for the new release version. Set up a Deployment configuration in the new namespace with the required number of Pods. Deploy new container versions in this namespace and update the Ingress configuration to direct traffic to it.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Configure your CI/CD pipeline to update the Deployment manifest file with the latest container version. Recreate Pods in the cluster by applying the updated Deployment manifest file. Validate application performance by comparing functionality with the previous release and rollback if necessary.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Install Anthos Service Mesh on the GKE cluster. Create two Deployments labeled with different version names. Implement an Istio routing rule to direct a small percentage of traffic to the Deployment with the new application version.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Zero downtime:</strong> Service meshes like Istio facilitate traffic management without disrupting running Pods. This ensures no downtime during deployments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A/B Testing:</strong> Istio's routing capabilities allow fine-grained control over traffic splitting, ideal for directing a small percentage of users to the new version for testing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Canary Releases:</strong> This approach aligns with the concept of canary releases where you gradually expose new versions to increasing subsets of users.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Replacing Pods:</strong> This will incur downtime as existing pods are terminated before new ones are launched. Not suitable for zero downtime requirements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Separate Namespaces:</strong> While possible, managing traffic redirection through namespaces is less sophisticated than using a service mesh. It can add operational complexity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Rolling Update:</strong> Standard rolling updates achieve zero downtime, but lack the granular traffic control needed for true A/B testing on a subset of production users.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/implementing-deployment-and-testing-strategies-on-gke#perform_an_ab_test\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/implementing-deployment-and-testing-strategies-on-gke#perform_an_ab_test</a></p>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e2q58":{"id":"e2q58","exam":2,"n":58,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You are preparing to test the Pub/Sub integration of your application, which reads credit card data from a Pub/Sub subscription. After completing code and unit testing, what is the recommended approach for testing the Pub/Sub integration before deploying to Google Cloud?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Establish a service for message publication and deploy the Pub/Sub emulator. Publish a predefined set of testing messages from the publishing service to the emulator for testing</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Implement a service for message publication and deploy the Pub/Sub emulator. Capture the messages from Pub/Sub in the production environment and publish them to the emulator for testing.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Set up a service to publish messages to your application. Capture the messages from Pub/Sub in the production environment and replay them through the publishing service for testing.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Develop a service for message publication and deploy the Pub/Sub emulator. Generate random content in the publishing service and publish it to the emulator for testing.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Establish a service for message publication and deploy the Pub/Sub emulator. Publish a predefined set of testing messages from the publishing service to the emulator for testing</strong></p>\n<p>Here's the reasoning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Isolation:</strong> Using the Pub/Sub emulator within a local or isolated testing environment protects your production environment from unintended consequences and experimental data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Predefined test messages allow you to test specific scenarios, edge cases, and potential error conditions relevant to your credit card processing logic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reproducibility:</strong> Having a controlled set of test messages makes the testing process more reproducible, aiding in debugging and ensuring consistent results.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Random Content:</strong> While testing with random data can be useful, it might not cover all the important scenarios for your credit card data processing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II &amp; III. Using Production Messages:</strong> Relying on messages from production introduces these risks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Data Exposure:</strong> Sensitive credit card data may be exposed in test environments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Inconsistency:</strong> Production message patterns might not be suitable for your test cases.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Potential Impact:</strong> Replaying messages could interfere with real production operations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/emulator\">https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/emulator</a></p>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e2q59":{"id":"e2q59","exam":2,"n":59,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You encountered a situation where your application abruptly terminated during a Deployment update in Google Kubernetes Engine due to not closing the database connection properly. Now, you aim to enhance your application to ensure it undergoes a graceful shutdown. What is the recommended action to take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Revise your code to handle a received SIGTERM signal appropriately to gracefully disconnect from the database.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Extend the terminationGracePeriodSeconds for your application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Implement a PodDisruptionBudget to safeguard the Pod from being forcibly terminated.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Set up a PreStop hook to manage the shutdown process of your application.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Update your code to process a received SIGTERM signal to gracefully disconnect from the database.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This directly targets the root cause of the forceful shutdown. Kubernetes sends a SIGTERM signal before terminating a Pod. Your code needs to intercept this signal and initiate a graceful shutdown, including closing database connections.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Configure a PodDisruptionBudget:</strong> While helpful for minimizing disruptions, this won't prevent shutdowns during node failures or manual deletions. It doesn't address the lack of graceful database connection handling.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Increase the terminationGracePeriodSeconds:</strong> This gives your application more time to shut down but doesn't ensure it will actually use that time to properly close connections if the SIGTERM signal isn't handled.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Configure a PreStop hook:</strong> This can be useful for additional shutdown logic but is ineffective if your code still doesn't handle SIGTERM and close the database connection within the hook.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/container-lifecycle-hooks/#container-hooks  https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/#define-a-graceful-shutdown-for-your-application\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/container-lifecycle-hooks/#container-hooks</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/container-lifecycle-hooks/#container-hooks  https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/#define-a-graceful-shutdown-for-your-application\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/#define-a-graceful-shutdown-for-your-application</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3"]},"e2q60":{"id":"e2q60","exam":2,"n":60,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Following a recent Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) API outage caused by a zone failure, you aim to implement a highly available GKE architecture to reduce service interruptions for users during future zone failures. What action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Implement Multi-Zone clusters</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Implement Zonal clusters</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Implement Regional clusters</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement GKE on-premises clusters</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The most appropriate action to take is </p>\n<p><strong>II. Implement Regional clusters</strong>. Here's why:</p>\n<p><strong>Regional clusters</strong> :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>High Availability</strong> : Regional clusters in GKE are designed for <strong>high availability</strong> as they automatically distribute nodes across multiple zones within a region. In the event of a zone failure, workloads are still available in other zones, minimizing service interruptions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Automatic replication</strong> : The GKE control plane and worker nodes are replicated across multiple zones within a region, providing redundancy and failover capabilities.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Managed failover</strong> : Regional clusters automatically handle failover between zones, reducing the risk of service disruption during zone-level outages.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options and why they are less suitable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Implement Zonal clusters</strong> : Zonal clusters are confined to a <strong>single zone</strong>. While they are easier to set up and may have lower costs, they are vulnerable to zone failures. If the zone goes down, all resources in that zone are affected, leading to outages.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Implement Multi-Zone clusters</strong> : Multi-Zone clusters distribute worker nodes across multiple zones but keep the <strong>control plane in a single zone</strong>. While this provides some level of redundancy for the nodes, the <strong>control plane is still a single point of failure</strong> , meaning if the control plane’s zone experiences issues, the entire cluster can be affected.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Implement GKE on-premises clusters</strong> : GKE on-prem is primarily designed for hybrid cloud setups, where Kubernetes clusters are managed both on Google Cloud and in on-premises data centers. While it provides control and flexibility, it is not directly related to improving availability in the event of <strong>zone failures</strong> within Google Cloud.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/types-of-clusters#regional_clusters\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/types-of-clusters#regional_clusters</a></p>","lessons":["d1l4"]},"e3q1":{"id":"e3q1","exam":3,"n":1,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You are porting an existing Apache/MySQL/PHP application stack from a single machine to Google Kubernetes Engine. You need to determine how to containerize the application. Your approach should follow Google-recommended best practices for availability. Which containerization strategy should you adopt?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Deploy each component in an individual container and coordinate their startup using a script.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Containerize each component in a separate container and integrate readiness and liveness probes.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Package the entire application in a single container and utilize a process management tool to handle each component.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Consolidate the entire application into a single container, use a bash script as the entrypoint, and initiate each component as a background process.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Containerize each component in a separate container and integrate readiness and liveness probes.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach excels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Isolation:</strong> Separating Apache, MySQL, and PHP into individual containers provides fault isolation. If one component crashes, it doesn't necessarily bring down the entire application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> Individual components can be scaled independently based on their resource needs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Updates:</strong> Changes to a component (like a PHP update) can be rolled out without redeploying the whole application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Health Checks:</strong> Readiness and liveness probes let Kubernetes monitor the health of each container, restarting or replacing them if they fail, ensuring higher availability.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Package the entire application in a single container ...</strong> This approach violates containerization best practices. It reduces modularity, flexibility, and makes health management difficult.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Deploy each component in an individual container and coordinate their startup using a script.</strong> While better than a single container, managing a complex startup script within the container could introduce fragility. Kubernetes' built-in mechanisms are preferred.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Consolidate the entire application into a single container ... background process.</strong> This has similar drawbacks to option II, limiting benefits of containerizing in the first place.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/best-practices-for-building-containers#package_a_single_app_per_container\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/best-practices-for-building-containers#package_a_single_app_per_container</a></p>","lessons":["d1l4"]},"e3q2":{"id":"e3q2","exam":3,"n":2,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your production application, written in Python, is deployed on Cloud Run. It necessitates reading and writing data stored in a Cloud Storage bucket within the same project. Adhering to the principle of least privilege, how should you grant access to your application?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Establish a user-managed service account with the Project Editor Identity and Access Management (IAM) role, granting extensive permissions across the project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize the default service account linked to the Cloud Run revision in production, potentially providing broader access than necessary.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Establish a user-managed service account with the Storage Admin Identity and Access Management (IAM) role to provide broad access to Cloud Storage functionality.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Establish a user-managed service account with a custom Identity and Access Management (IAM) role tailored to the specific permissions required.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Establish a user-managed service account with a custom Identity and Access Management (IAM) role tailored to the specific permissions required.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> Granting only the minimum necessary permissions is fundamental to good security practices. If your application only needs to read and write to a specific bucket, you should create a custom IAM role that includes only those actions (e.g., <code>storage.objects.get</code>, <code>storage.objects.create</code>, etc.).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reduced Attack Surface:</strong> Limiting the permissions of your service account minimizes the potential impact if those credentials are compromised.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Storage Admin:</strong> This role grants far too many permissions for your scenario. It allows full administrative control over all Cloud Storage resources within the project.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Project Editor:</strong> The Project Editor role is also extremely broad. It allows changes to resources across the entire project, far beyond what's required for your use case.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Default Service Account:</strong> While convenient, the default service account often has broader permissions than strictly necessary, violating the principle of least privilege.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e3q3":{"id":"e3q3","exam":3,"n":3,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Before deploying your new application code to production, you aim to conduct testing across various user profiles. Despite the inherent risks, you intend to assess the new version with production users, controlling access based on their operating systems. If issues arise, you seek a swift rollback process. What's the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Opt for App Engine deployment, employing traffic splitting to direct specific user segments to the new version based on IP addresses.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Consider Compute Engine deployment, leveraging Traffic Director to guide specific user segments to the new version based on predefined weights.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Choose Google Kubernetes Engine with Anthos Service Mesh deployment, employing traffic splitting to route certain user segments to the new version based on the user-agent header.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Opt for Cloud Run deployment, utilizing traffic splitting to direct specific user segments to the new version based on the revision tag.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>II. Choose Google Kubernetes Engine with Anthos Service Mesh deployment, employing traffic splitting to route certain user segments to the new version based on the user-agent header.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Accurate User Targeting:</strong> The user-agent header within HTTP requests reliably conveys the operating system of the user's device. Anthos Service Mesh (and other service meshes) can finely control traffic routing using these headers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Rollbacks and Granular Control:</strong> Service meshes excel at swift rollbacks and granular adjustments to traffic distribution percentages. These attributes are well-suited for mitigating potential risks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Kubernetes as a Foundation:</strong> GKE provides the ideal platform for managing service mesh deployments like Anthos.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Optimal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Opt for Cloud Run deployment, utilizing traffic splitting to direct specific user segments to the new version based on the revision tag.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Limitation:</strong> While Cloud Run's traffic splitting is robust, it usually targets revisions rather than user-agent headers. This makes OS-based segmentation difficult.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Opt for App Engine deployment, employing traffic splitting to direct specific user segments to the new version based on IP addresses.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Imprecise Targeting:</strong> Relying on IP addresses for identifying operating systems is unreliable, as users on different OSes can share IP ranges.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Consider Compute Engine deployment, leveraging Traffic Director to guide specific user segments to the new version based on predefined weights</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Added Complexity:</strong> Though Traffic Director offers fine-grained control, it increases setup and management complexity compared to the Anthos Service Mesh solution built for this use case.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/User-Agent\">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/User-Agent</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l6"]},"e3q4":{"id":"e3q4","exam":3,"n":4,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>As you develop an HTTP API on a Compute Engine virtual machine instance, it's crucial for multiple clients within the same Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) to access it. How can clients retrieve the service's IP address?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Reserve a static external IP address and assign it to an HTTP(S) load balancing service's forwarding rule. Clients should use this IP address for connection.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Ensure clients leverage Compute Engine internal DNS by accessing the instance name through the URL https://[API_NAME]/[API_VERSION]/.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Ensure clients leverage Compute Engine internal DNS by accessing the instance name through the URL https://[INSTANCE_NAME].[ZONE].c.[PROJECT_ID].internal/.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Reserve a static external IP address and assign it to an HTTP(S) load balancing service's forwarding rule. Then, set up an A record in Cloud DNS. Clients should use the A record's name to connect.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Ensure clients leverage Compute Engine internal DNS by accessing the instance name through the URL https://[INSTANCE_NAME].[ZONE].c.[PROJECT_ID].internal/.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the most efficient and reliable approach for clients within the same VPC:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Internal communication:</strong> Compute Engine's internal DNS allows for direct communication between instances within a VPC, bypassing the need for external IP addresses.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Simplicity:</strong> This approach doesn't involve reserving external IP addresses, setting up load balancers, or managing extra DNS records.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cost-effectiveness:</strong> Utilizing internal DNS typically incurs no additional charges, making it efficient and cost-effective.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Dynamic Updates:</strong> Even if the instance's internal IP address changes, the internal DNS record will automatically update, minimizing client-side changes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I &amp; II. Reserve a static external IP address ...</strong> While functional, these options are overly complex for internal VPC communication. They introduce unnecessary components and potential additional costs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. ... using the URL https://[API_NAME]/[API_VERSION]/.</strong> This URL structure doesn't directly provide the IP address. Without internal DNS, client-side configuration would be required to map <code>[API_NAME]</code> to the correct address.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/internal-dns#access_by_internal_DNS  https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/internal-dns\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/internal-dns#access_by_internal_DNS</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/internal-dns#access_by_internal_DNS  https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/internal-dns\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/internal-dns</a></p>","lessons":["d3l10"]},"e3q5":{"id":"e3q5","exam":3,"n":5,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You've deployed a Java application on Cloud Run, requiring access to a Cloud SQL-hosted database. To comply with regulations, the connection must use the Cloud SQL instance's internal IP address. How should you configure connectivity, following Google's best practices?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Adapt your application to use the Cloud SQL Java connector for connectivity.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Set up your Cloud Run service with a Cloud SQL connection.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Configure your Cloud Run service to utilize a Serverless VPC Access connector.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Adjust your application to connect to a Cloud SQL Auth proxy instance.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option: II. Configure your Cloud Run service to utilize a Serverless VPC Access connector.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Serverless VPC Access:</strong> This is the recommended way to connect Cloud Run to Cloud SQL using private IP addresses. It establishes a secure tunnel within your VPC network.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> Private IP connectivity enhances security by preventing database exposure to the public internet.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>No code changes:</strong> This method generally doesn't require any changes to your Java application code.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Cloud SQL connection:</strong> While Cloud Run can connect to Cloud SQL, this usually uses public IP by default, not complying with the internal IP requirement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Cloud SQL Java connector:</strong> The connector is necessary, but on its own, it won't solve the private IP access issue.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Cloud SQL Auth proxy:</strong> The proxy is useful but adds an extra component. Serverless VPC Access is a more streamlined solution for private IP connections from Cloud Run.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/encryption/using-customer-supplied-keys#rest-csek-download-object  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/encryption/customer-supplied-keys#response we\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/encryption/using-customer-supplied-keys#rest-csek-download-object</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/encryption/using-customer-supplied-keys#rest-csek-download-object  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/encryption/customer-supplied-keys#response we\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/encryption/customer-supplied-keys#response we</a></p>","lessons":["d3l10"]},"e3q6":{"id":"e3q6","exam":3,"n":6,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>As you support a business-critical application deployed on Cloud Run in production, the application reports HTTP 500 errors, impacting its usability. You aim to receive alerts when the error rate exceeds 15% of the requests within a specific time frame. What should be your approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Develop a Cloud Function utilizing the Cloud Monitoring API. Schedule this function with Cloud Scheduler to trigger daily, notifying you if errors surpass the defined threshold.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement a Cloud Function consuming the Cloud Monitoring API and schedule it with Cloud Composer to trigger daily, alerting you if errors surpass the set threshold.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Access the Cloud Run page in the Google Cloud console, select the service from the services list, and utilize the Metrics tab to visualize error rates for the revision. Perform this check daily.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Establish an alerting policy within Cloud Monitoring to notify you if errors exceed the specified threshold.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>III. Establish an alerting policy within Cloud Monitoring to notify you if errors exceed the specified threshold.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Purpose-Built:</strong> Cloud Monitoring's alerting functionality is designed specifically for this type of scenario. You can define thresholds, conditions, and notification channels directly within the service.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Real-Time:</strong> Alerting policies continuously monitor your metrics and trigger notifications as soon as the error rate exceeds your defined threshold.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficiency:</strong> This eliminates the need to develop custom Cloud Functions, schedule jobs, or manually check metrics.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Develop a Cloud Function utilizing the Cloud Monitoring API.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Unnecessary Complexity:</strong> While feasible, developing a custom Cloud Function introduces more overhead compared to Cloud Monitoring's native alerting features.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Delayed Alerts:</strong> Utilizing Cloud Scheduler for daily checks means you won't receive timely notifications when issues arise.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Access the Cloud Run page in the Google Cloud console ...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Manual Intervention:</strong> This approach relies on manual checks, which isn't proactive or suitable for a production application where timely error detection is critical.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Implement a Cloud Function ...schedule it with Cloud Composer...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Overkill:</strong> Cloud Composer is designed for complex workflows and data pipelines. Its use for simple error monitoring is over-engineered and adds unnecessary complexity.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/monitoring#custom-metrics   https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/monitoring#add_alerts  https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/alerts\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/monitoring#custom-metrics https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/monitoring#add_alerts</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/monitoring#custom-metrics   https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/monitoring#add_alerts  https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/alerts\">https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/alerts</a></p>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e3q7":{"id":"e3q7","exam":3,"n":7,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>In developing a Java Web Server, requiring interaction with Google Cloud services through the Google Cloud API on behalf of users, what workflow should be implemented for user authentication to the Google Cloud API?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Upon user arrival, request Google username and password. Forward these credentials in an HTTPS request to the Google Cloud authorization server to obtain an access token. Validate user credentials at the Google server, which returns an access token to the application for subsequent Google Cloud API calls.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Redirect users to a Google Cloud consent screen on arrival, presenting requested permissions. Prompt users to sign in via SSO to their Google Account. After sign-in and consent, the application receives an authorization code from a Google server, stored in the browser's cookies. Authenticate to the Google Cloud API using this authorization code.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Upon user arrival, redirect to a Google Cloud consent screen displaying requested permissions. Prompt users to sign in via SSO to their Google Account. Following sign-in and consent, the application receives an authorization code from a Google server. The application exchanges this code for an access token with another request to the Google server, subsequently used for Google Cloud API calls.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Prompt users for their Google username and password upon arrival at the application. Store an SHA password hash in the application's database along with the username. Authenticate to the Google Cloud API using HTTPS requests, including the username and password hash in the Authorization request header.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is </p>\n<p><strong>IV: Upon user arrival, redirect to a Google Cloud consent screen displaying requested permissions. Prompt users to sign in via SSO to their Google Account. Following sign-in and consent, the application receives an authorization code from a Google server. The application exchanges this code for an access token with another request to the Google server, subsequently used for Google Cloud API calls.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Here's why this is the best approach:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> It follows the OAuth 2.0 standard, a secure and widely used framework for delegated authorization. Your application never handles the user's Google credentials directly, reducing security risks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>User Experience:</strong> Leverages Google's Single Sign-On (SSO), providing a seamless login experience for users as they likely already have active Google account sessions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> The consent screen allows users to understand and explicitly grant the specific permissions your application needs to interact with Google Cloud API on their behalf.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Standard Compliance:</strong> Adheres to industry-standard best practices for handling user authentication and authorization.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are not ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Prompt users for their Google username and password...</strong> This is a major security risk. You should never directly ask for or store user passwords. It undermines trust and exposes your application to credential theft.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Upon user arrival, request Google username and password...</strong> While slightly better than option I, it still involves your application handling user credentials and carries the risk of those being compromised.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Redirect users to a Google Cloud consent screen...</strong> This option is partially correct but doesn't go far enough. While it utilizes the consent screen, it doesn't describe the crucial step of exchanging the authorization code for an access token, which is what ultimately allows your application to make API calls.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://developers.google.com/identity/protocols/oauth2\">https://developers.google.com/identity/protocols/oauth2</a></p>","lessons":["d4l3"]},"e3q8":{"id":"e3q8","exam":3,"n":8,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>While leveraging Cloud Build for Docker image creation, you're tasked with integrating unit and integration tests into the build process. In the event of a failure, you aim for clear identification of the failing stage in the build history. What's the optimal strategy to achieve this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Construct a Cloud Build build configuration file featuring distinct cloud builder steps for compiling and executing unit and integration tests.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Develop a Cloud Build build configuration file to trigger a separate cloud build pipeline dedicated to unit and integration tests.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Embed RUN commands within the Dockerfile to execute unit and integration tests.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Craft a Cloud Build build configuration file with a unified build step for compiling unit and integration tests.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Construct a Cloud Build build configuration file featuring distinct cloud builder steps for compiling and executing unit and integration tests.</strong></p>\n<p>Let's break down why it's the best choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Granular Test Stages:</strong> Separate build steps provide a clear demarcation between the unit and integration test stages, making it easier to pinpoint the specific source of a failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Targeted Actions:</strong> Each step can have its own dependencies, configuration, and commands tailored to the type of test being executed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Readable Build History:</strong> The Cloud Build history will clearly display the success or failure status of each individual test stage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flexibility:</strong> You can easily add more test stages or modify the order without affecting the overall build.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Embed RUN commands within the Dockerfile</strong> Cluttering the Dockerfile compromises readability and can create maintenance challenges. Unit and integration tests should ideally be decoupled from the core image build process.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Craft a Cloud Build build configuration file ... unified build step</strong> This approach lumps the tests together, hindering your ability to pinpoint the root cause of a failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Develop a Cloud Build build configuration file to trigger a separate cloud build pipeline ...</strong> This adds unnecessary complexity and potential build time overhead due to managing separate pipelines.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/build-config-file-schema:\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/build-config-file-schema:</a></p>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e3q9":{"id":"e3q9","exam":3,"n":9,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You've recently developed an application and now need to access the Cloud Storage API from a Compute Engine instance without a public IP address. What's the best approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Enable Private Google Access</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Implement Shared VPC networks</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize VPC Network Peering</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Opt for Carrier Peering</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Enable Private Google Access</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the most suitable option:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Internal Networking:</strong> Private Google Access allows instances with private IPs to reach Google APIs and services using internal IP addresses within Google's network.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> This approach eliminates the need to expose your Compute Engine instance to the public internet, improving security posture.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Simplicity:</strong> Private Google Access is often a simple configuration setting on the subnet level, making it easy to implement.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Carrier Peering:</strong> Carrier peering is primarily used for connecting your on-premises network to Google's network and is unrelated to accessing internal Google Cloud services without public IPs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. VPC Network Peering:</strong> While network peering allows communication between different VPC networks, it mainly targets resource sharing and doesn't directly impact access to Google APIs..</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Shared VPC Networks:</strong> Shared VPC enables resource sharing across projects but doesn't inherently grant access to Google APIs from private instances.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/private-google-access\">https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/private-google-access</a></p>","lessons":["d4l11"]},"e3q10":{"id":"e3q10","exam":3,"n":10,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>To streamline developer access to individual Google Cloud projects for each development team in your company while adhering to Google's recommended best practices and optimizing efficiency, what action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Add the users directly to their respective projects, assign them the necessary roles, and provide them with the relevant Project Number.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Establish groups, include the users in their corresponding groups, assign the appropriate roles to these groups, and then furnish the users with the relevant Project ID.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Add the users directly to their respective projects, assign them the necessary roles, and provide them with the relevant Project ID.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Establish groups, include the users in their corresponding groups, assign the appropriate roles to these groups, and then furnish the users with the relevant Project Number.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Establish groups, include the users in their corresponding groups, assign the appropriate roles to these groups, and then furnish the users with the relevant Project ID.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach aligns with Google's best practices and is the most efficient:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Centralized Management:</strong> Using groups simplifies permission management. Adding and removing users from a group automatically updates their permissions across multiple projects.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):</strong> Assigning roles to groups adheres to the principle of least privilege, granting users only the necessary permissions to perform their tasks within specific projects.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> The group-based approach scales well as your organization and the number of projects grow.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Clarity:</strong> Providing users with the Project ID ensures they easily identify the project they need to access.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less optimal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I &amp; II. Individual Permissions:</strong> Adding users and assigning roles at the individual project level becomes cumbersome to manage as the number of users and projects increases. It also makes tracking and auditing permissions difficult.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Project Number:</strong> While helpful, the Project Number alone doesn't provide context about the user's permissions within that project.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e3q11":{"id":"e3q11","exam":3,"n":11,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In the context of developing an online gaming platform on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), where users are experiencing extended loading times for specific URL requests, what is the recommended approach to identify performance bottlenecks and pinpoint HTTP requests with significant latency?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Integrate your microservices with the OpenTelemetry tracing package. Update application code to dispatch traces to Trace for thorough inspection and analysis. Generate an analysis report within Trace to evaluate user requests comprehensively.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Enhance your microservices to log HTTP request methods and URL paths to STDOUT. Utilize the logs router to forward container logs to Cloud Logging. Implement filters within Cloud Logging to assess the latency of user requests across various methods and URL paths.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy tcpdump on your GKE nodes and initiate it to capture network traffic over an extended duration for data collection. Subsequently, analyze the captured data files employing Wireshark to uncover the root cause of high latency.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Configure GKE workload metrics through kubectl, directing all Pods to transmit metrics to Cloud Monitoring. Utilize Cloud Monitoring to craft a custom dashboard showcasing application metrics, facilitating the identification of performance bottlenecks within the GKE cluster.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option: III. Integrate your microservices with the OpenTelemetry tracing package. Update application code to dispatch traces to Trace for thorough inspection and analysis. Generate an analysis report within Trace to evaluate user requests comprehensively.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Distributed Tracing:</strong> OpenTelemetry is specifically designed for distributed tracing in microservices environments. It provides fine-grained visibility into request paths, timings, and potential bottlenecks across services.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Trace Integration:</strong> Trace is natively integrated with Google Cloud, offering a powerful visualization and analysis platform tailored for tracing data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Targeted Analysis:</strong> Traces allow you to pinpoint slow parts of your system specifically related to user-facing HTTP requests, giving you the most relevant data.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. GKE Workload Metrics:</strong> While useful for general cluster health, these metrics are less granular for pinpointing latency at the individual HTTP request level.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Log Analysis:</strong> While logs can help, analyzing raw HTTP logs for latency is less efficient and provides less granular insights than distributed traces.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. tcpdump:</strong> Network packet analysis can be helpful but is very low-level. It is less efficient for quickly understanding bottlenecks within a complex microservices application.#</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/setup#when-to-instrument\">https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/setup#when-to-instrument</a></p>","lessons":["d2l8","d2l9"]},"e3q12":{"id":"e3q12","exam":3,"n":12,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You are deploying a single website on App Engine, which must be accessible via the URL http://www.altostrat.com/. What steps should you take to achieve this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Verify domain ownership through Webmaster Central. Set up a DNS CNAME record directing to the App Engine canonical name ghs.googlehosted.com.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Configure a mapping in dispatch.yaml to link the domain www.altostrat.com to your App Engine service. Set up a DNS CNAME record pointing to the App Engine canonical name ghs.googlehosted.com.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Verify domain ownership via Webmaster Central. Configure an A record pointing to the singular global App Engine IP address.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Set up a mapping in dispatch.yaml to direct the domain www.altostrat.com to your App Engine service. Establish an A record pointing to the singular global App Engine IP address.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Verify domain ownership through Webmaster Central. Set up a DNS CNAME record directing to the App Engine canonical name ghs.googlehosted.com.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Domain Verification:</strong> Webmaster Central allows you to prove ownership of your domain, a prerequisite for configuring custom domains with App Engine.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>CNAME Record:</strong> CNAME records create an alias. In this case, <code>www.altostrat.com</code> becomes an alias for your App Engine's <code>ghs.googlehosted.com</code> address.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>App Engine Configuration:</strong> You also need to configure your App Engine project to recognize and serve <code>www.altostrat.com</code> (this is done within the Google Cloud Console, not <code>dispatch.yaml</code>).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Incorrect:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. ... Configure an A record pointing to the singular global App Engine IP address.</strong> App Engine IP addresses can change, making A records unreliable for the long term. CNAMEs are more flexible.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III &amp; IV. Set up a mapping in dispatch.yaml ...</strong> <code>dispatch.yaml</code> is primarily for routing requests <em>within</em> your App Engine app, not for setting up the initial custom domain mapping. This step is done in the Cloud Console.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://support.google.com/domains/answer/6009957?hl=en app.mydomain.com CNAME 1H ghs.googlehosted.com.\">https://support.google.com/domains/answer/6009957?hl=en app.mydomain.com CNAME 1H ghs.googlehosted.com.</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e3q13":{"id":"e3q13","exam":3,"n":13,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>In an ANSI-SQL compliant database, you have two tables with matching columns that require swift combination into a single table, eliminating duplicate rows from the result. What's the optimal approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Leverage the UNION operator in SQL for table merging.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize the JOIN operator in SQL for table merging.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Employ nested WITH statements for table combination.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy the UNION ALL operator in SQL for table merging.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Leverage the UNION operator in SQL for table merging.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>The <code>UNION</code> operator in SQL specifically addresses the goal of combining data from multiple tables while removing duplicates:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Combination:</strong> The <code>UNION</code> operator appends the results of one query below the results of another.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Duplicate Removal:</strong> <code>UNION</code> automatically eliminates any duplicate rows from the combined result set.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Utilize the JOIN operator in SQL for table merging.</strong> While <code>JOIN</code> is excellent for combining tables based on related columns, it doesn't inherently remove duplicates. You'd need additional clauses (<code>DISTINCT</code>) to achieve the same behavior as <code>UNION</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Employ nested WITH statements for table combination.</strong> <code>WITH</code> statements (Common Table Expressions) are useful for simplifying complex queries but don't directly offer the functionality of <code>UNION</code> in this case.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Deploy the UNION ALL operator in SQL for table merging.</strong> <code>UNION ALL</code> appends results like <code>UNION</code> but <em>retains</em> duplicate rows. This does not satisfy the requirement of duplicate elimination.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l9"]},"e3q14":{"id":"e3q14","exam":3,"n":14,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your team develops stateless services running on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). You need to deploy a new service accessible only by other services within the GKE cluster. This service must scale rapidly to meet fluctuating loads. What's the best approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Utilize a Vertical Pod Autoscaler to adjust container sizes and expose them through a ClusterIP Service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Deploy a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler to scale the containers and expose them through a ClusterIP Service.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler to scale the containers and expose them through a NodePort Service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Implement a Vertical Pod Autoscaler to adjust container sizes and expose them through a NodePort Service.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Deploy a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler to scale the containers and expose them through a ClusterIP Service.</strong></p>\n<p>Let's break down why this is the ideal choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Internal Access:</strong> ClusterIP Services are designed for communication within the Kubernetes cluster, meeting your requirement of internal-only access.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scaling:</strong> The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) automatically adjusts the number of replicas of your pods based on metrics like CPU or memory usage, ensuring your service can handle varying loads.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Statelessness:</strong> HPAs are well-suited for stateless services, allowing you to scale replicas up and down without affecting data consistency or state.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I &amp; II. Vertical Pod Autoscaling:</strong> VPAs adjust resource requests/limits for <em>individual pods</em> , not their count. This is less suitable for rapidly scaling to meet fluctuating demand.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II &amp; IV. NodePort:</strong> NodePort Services expose your service to external traffic, whereas your requirement is internal-only access.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>References:<br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/service\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/service</a></p>","lessons":["d3l2","d3l7"]},"e3q15":{"id":"e3q15","exam":3,"n":15,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your company requires a database solution for storing customer purchase history, with the following demands:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Immediate customer access to their purchases post-submission.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Sorting purchases by multiple fields.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Simultaneous storage of diverse record formats.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Which of the following storage options best fits these requirements?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Firestore in Native mode</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Firestore in Datastore mode utilizing a global query</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Cloud SQL employing a SQL SELECT statement</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Cloud Storage utilizing object reads</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The best storage option that fits the requirements is </p>\n<p><strong>I. Firestore in Native mode</strong>. Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Immediate customer access to their purchases post-submission:</strong> Firestore provides real-time updates, allowing customers to see their purchase history immediately after a purchase is made.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Sorting purchases by multiple fields:</strong> Firestore supports compound queries, allowing you to sort purchases based on multiple fields (e.g., date, price, product category).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Simultaneous storage of diverse record formats:</strong> Firestore's flexible document structure allows you to store various data types within a single document, accommodating different record formats.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let's look at why the other options are less suitable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Cloud Storage utilizing object reads:</strong> Cloud Storage is suitable for storing large objects but lacks the real-time capabilities and querying flexibility needed for this use case.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Cloud SQL employing a SQL SELECT statement:</strong> Cloud SQL is a relational database that provides structured data storage and querying but might not be the most efficient for handling real-time updates and diverse data formats.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Firestore in Datastore mode utilizing a global query:</strong> While Firestore in Datastore mode supports global queries, it is generally less performant than Native mode for common use cases like this one.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>In summary:</strong> Firestore in Native mode provides the real-time updates, flexible querying, and diverse data format support needed to meet the requirements of storing and accessing customer purchase history effectively.</p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/datastore/docs/firestore-or-datastore\">https://cloud.google.com/datastore/docs/firestore-or-datastore</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e3q16":{"id":"e3q16","exam":3,"n":16,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In a distributed microservices architecture, you aim to assess the performance and system resource utilization of a Java-based microservice. What steps should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize OpenCensus to instrument the service for measuring service latency, and subsequently, generate custom metrics in Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Employ Cloud Profiler to instrument the service, enabling measurement of CPU utilization and method-level execution times.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize Debugger to instrument the service for error investigation purposes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Employ Cloud Trace to instrument the service, facilitating the measurement of request latency.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer for assessing the <strong>performance</strong> and <strong>system resource utilization</strong> of a Java-based microservice in a distributed architecture is</p>\n<p><strong>Option I: Employ Cloud Profiler to instrument the service, enabling measurement of CPU utilization and method-level execution times</strong>.</p>\n<p>Why Option I is correct:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud Profiler</strong> is specifically designed to assess <strong>CPU utilization</strong> , memory allocation, and method-level execution times. It continuously samples the application to identify performance bottlenecks and inefficient code paths, making it highly suitable for monitoring system resource utilization in your Java microservice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Why other options are incorrect:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option II: Utilize Debugger for error investigation</strong> :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cloud Debugger is used to track down issues in the code without stopping the service. However, it focuses on debugging and inspecting code rather than performance assessment or system resource utilization. It doesn't provide insights into CPU or memory usage.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option III: Employ Cloud Trace to measure request latency</strong> :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cloud Trace is useful for tracking request latency across services, but it does not give detailed insights into resource utilization (e.g., CPU or memory). Its primary focus is on tracing the flow of requests and measuring how long it takes for services to respond, not on performance profiling.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option IV: Utilize OpenCensus to measure service latency and generate custom metrics</strong> :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>OpenCensus (now part of OpenTelemetry) is used for <strong>distributed tracing</strong> and <strong>latency</strong> monitoring, not directly for CPU or method-level performance analysis. While useful for tracking the flow of requests and generating metrics, it doesn’t provide deep insights into resource utilization like Cloud Profiler does.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In conclusion, <strong>Cloud Profiler (Option I)</strong> is the correct choice for your performance assessment and resource utilization needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs\">https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d2l8","d2l9"]},"e3q17":{"id":"e3q17","exam":3,"n":17,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>In designing a microservices architecture for a cloud and on-premises deployment, prioritizing scalability and managed services, what approach should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Establish a GKE cluster in the cloud and deploy open-source Kubernetes on-premises, utilizing an external load balancer service to distribute traffic between the two environments.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy a GKE cluster in each environment leveraging Anthos, while utilizing Cloud Run for Anthos to deploy the application to each cluster.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Implement a multi-cluster deployment using open source Istio on multiple Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) clusters managed by Anthos.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Set up a GKE cluster in each environment through Anthos, employing Cloud Build to create a Deployment for the application in each cluster.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Deploy a GKE cluster in each environment leveraging Anthos, while utilizing Cloud Run for Anthos to deploy the application to each cluster.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach shines:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Consistency:</strong> Anthos provides unified management for GKE clusters, offering a consistent deployment and operational experience across your hybrid cloud setup.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability &amp; Serverless:</strong> Cloud Run for Anthos seamlessly scales your microservices. Its serverless nature removes the burden of managing underlying infrastructure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Fully Managed:</strong> Both GKE and Cloud Run are fully-managed Google Cloud services, minimizing operational overhead and letting you focus on application development.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Microservices-Friendly:</strong> Cloud Run's container-based model naturally aligns with microservice architectures.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Have Shortcomings:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Istio Complexity:</strong> While Istio offers advanced service mesh features, it adds operational complexity. For a focus on scalability and managed services, this might not be the optimal trade-off.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Lack of Serverless:</strong> Employing Cloud Build and GKE deployments leaves you managing infrastructure capacity, where Cloud Run's serverless aspect would be advantageous.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Heterogeneity &amp; Load Balancing:</strong> This approach introduces management inconsistencies between GKE and on-premises Kubernetes, plus the added overhead of managing a separate load balancing solution.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ref:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/anthos/run/docs/deploy-application\">https://cloud.google.com/anthos/run/docs/deploy-application</a></p>","lessons":["d1l1"]},"e3q18":{"id":"e3q18","exam":3,"n":18,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You're assessing developer tools to facilitate Google Kubernetes Engine adoption and integration within your development ecosystem, which encompasses VS Code and IntelliJ. What's the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Cloud Shell for managing infrastructure and applications via the command line.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Implement a Cloud Notebook instance for data ingestion, processing, and model deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize the Cloud Shell integrated Code Editor for editing code and configuration files.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Employ Cloud Code for application development.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Employ Cloud Code for application development.</strong> Here's why this is the best choice, followed by explanations of why the other options aren't as suitable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Code: The Ideal Fit</strong> Cloud Code is purpose-built to enhance the developer experience within Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) environments. It offers extensions for popular IDEs like VS Code and IntelliJ, providing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Kubernetes-aware coding assistance (e.g., code completion, snippets)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Easy debugging for applications running within the cluster</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Deployment workflows from within the IDE</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Integrated management of cluster resources</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Other Options: Their Limitations</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Cloud Shell Integrated Code Editor:</strong> The Cloud Shell code editor is better suited for quick edits and configuration file changes but lacks the depth and Kubernetes-specific features of Cloud Code for full-fledged application development.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Cloud Notebook Instance:</strong> Cloud Notebooks are designed primarily for data analysis and machine learning tasks. While they can be used in conjunction with GKE, they are not the primary tool for building and deploying applications onto Kubernetes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Cloud Shell:</strong> Cloud Shell is powerful for command-line interaction with GKE (using kubectl, etc.). However, it doesn't provide the rich IDE experience, extensions, and code assistance of Cloud Code.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/code/docs\">https://cloud.google.com/code/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e3q19":{"id":"e3q19","exam":3,"n":19,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You're preparing to transition a MySQL database to Google Cloud's managed Cloud SQL service. Your Compute Engine VM instances must connect to this Cloud SQL instance without requiring IP whitelisting. Which approach should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Define a role within Cloud SQL granting external instance access to the database, then assign this role to the Compute Engine instances.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy a CloudSQL instance in one project, create Compute Engine instances in another project, and establish a VPN connection between them for internal CloudSQL access.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Include Compute Engine instances in a whitelisted project for Cloud SQL access.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Activate private IP for the Cloud SQL instance.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Activate private IP for the Cloud SQL instance.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Efficiency and Security:</strong> Private IP provides direct, internal connectivity between your Compute Engine instances and the Cloud SQL instance. This avoids the need for IP whitelisting, simplifies management, and enhances security.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Include Compute Engine instances in a whitelisted project for Cloud SQL access.</strong> While feasible, it relies on managing IP addresses and potential updates if your Compute Engine configuration changes. It's less streamlined than the private IP approach.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Define a role within Cloud SQL granting external instance access to the database, then assign this role to the Compute Engine instances.</strong> This works but exposes your Cloud SQL instance to the public internet via its public IP, increasing security risks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Deploy a CloudSQL instance in one project, create Compute Engine instances in another project, and establish a VPN connection between them for internal CloudSQL access.</strong> Establishing a VPN adds complexity, overhead, and potential performance bottlenecks compared to direct private IP connectivity.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://shorturl.at/ryMUW  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/roles-and-permissions#proxy-roles-permissions\">https://shorturl.at/ryMUW</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://shorturl.at/ryMUW  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/roles-and-permissions#proxy-roles-permissions\">https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/roles-and-permissions#proxy-roles-permissions</a></p>","lessons":["d3l10"]},"e3q20":{"id":"e3q20","exam":3,"n":20,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>In your company's BigQuery environment, the \"Master\" dataset stores employee travel and expense data, categorized by department. To ensure employees only access relevant departmental information, you seek to implement a streamlined security framework. What's the optimal approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Establish distinct datasets for each department, creating views with appropriate WHERE clauses in the \"Master\" dataset to filter records. Authorize these views to access records from the \"Master\" dataset, granting employees access to their respective departmental datasets.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Create a \"Master\" dataset and develop individual views for each department within it. Grant employees access to the relevant departmental views.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Set up separate datasets for each department and implement data pipelines to copy relevant data from the \"Master\" dataset to department-specific datasets. Grant employees access to these department-specific datasets.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Create a \"Master\" dataset and segregate data into separate tables for each department. Grant employees access to their department's specific table.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Create a \"Master\" dataset and develop individual views for each department within it. Grant employees access to the relevant departmental views.</strong></p>\n<p>Let's break down why this approach offers the best balance of security, efficiency, and maintainability:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Centralized Data:</strong> Maintaining a single \"Master\" dataset ensures data consistency and simplifies updates.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Fine-grained Access Control:</strong> Views provide a secure mechanism to filter data based on department without duplication. Each view acts as a virtual table, exposing only the necessary subset of data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficiency:</strong> Views don't replicate data, saving storage costs and avoiding the overhead of data synchronization.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Maintainability:</strong> Changes to the underlying data in the \"Master\" dataset will automatically propagate to the relevant departmental views without the need to update multiple separate datasets.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Establish distinct datasets for each department ...</strong> This approach introduces data duplication and increases storage costs. It also creates overhead in keeping these datasets synchronized with the \"Master\" dataset.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Set up separate datasets for each department and implement data pipelines ...</strong> Similar to option I, this approach leads to data redundancy and the added complexity of managing data pipelines.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Create a \"Master\" dataset and segregate data into separate tables for each department ...</strong> While this avoids data duplication, it makes queries more complex since you'll likely need to use <code>UNION ALL</code> across multiple tables.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/dataset-access-controls\">https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/dataset-access-controls</a></p>","lessons":["d1l5"]},"e3q21":{"id":"e3q21","exam":3,"n":21,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>In developing a new public-facing application, you're tasked with retrieving specific properties in the metadata of users' objects within their Cloud Storage buckets while adhering to privacy and data residency regulations, ensuring only metadata is retrieved, not object data. The goal is to optimize retrieval performance. How should you approach metadata retrieval?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Employ the patch method for metadata retrieval.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Use the fields request parameter to retrieve only the desired metadata properties, enhancing retrieval performance.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize the compose method for efficient metadata extraction.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Employ the copy method to retrieve metadata while preserving privacy and data residency requirements.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Best Option:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IV. Use the fields request parameter to retrieve only the desired metadata properties, enhancing retrieval performance.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reasons for its superiority:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Targeted Retrieval:</strong> The <code>fields</code> parameter provides the most direct way to extract specific metadata elements without retrieving unnecessary data. This translates to optimized network traffic and reduced processing time.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficiency:</strong> Minimizing data transfer significantly enhances the performance of metadata retrieval operations.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Compliance:</strong> This approach adheres to privacy and data residency regulations by ensuring that you only access the necessary metadata and avoid retrieving any sensitive object data.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Employ the patch method for metadata retrieval.</strong> The <code>patch</code> method is primarily designed to update metadata rather than simply retrieve it. It would often involve fetching the entire metadata object, making it less efficient.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Utilize the compose method for efficient metadata extraction.</strong> The <code>compose</code> method is used for combining multiple objects in Cloud Storage. It's not relevant to the task of retrieving metadata from a single object.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Employ the copy method to retrieve metadata while preserving privacy and data residency requirements.</strong> While copying an object preserves its metadata, this method is primarily focused on creating duplicates. It is less efficient for pure metadata retrieval compared to the <code>fields</code> parameter.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/json_api/v1/objects/get   https://firebase.google.com/docs/storage/web/file-metadata#get_file_metadata\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/json_api/v1/objects/get https://firebase.google.com/docs/storage/web/file-metadata#get_file_metadata</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e3q22":{"id":"e3q22","exam":3,"n":22,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>How should you grant IAM permission to a serverless web application on Cloud Run for accessing images stored in a private Cloud Storage bucket, while adhering to Google's recommended security practices?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Enable public access prevention for the specified bucket. Configure and update the Cloud Run service to utilize a user-managed service account. Assign the Storage Object Viewer IAM role to the service account.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Implement signed URLs for the specified bucket. Configure and update the Cloud Run service to utilize a user-managed service account. Assign the Storage Object Viewer IAM role to the service account.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Implement signed URLs for the specified bucket. Assign the Storage Object Viewer IAM role to the Compute Engine default service account.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Enable public access prevention for the specified bucket. Assign the Storage Object Viewer IAM role to the Compute Engine default service account.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IV. Enable public access prevention for the specified bucket. Configure and update the Cloud Run service to utilize a user-managed service account. Assign the Storage Object Viewer IAM role to the service account.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> User-managed service accounts allow fine-grained access control targeted specifically towards your Cloud Run service. It avoids overly permissive access associated with the Compute Engine default service account.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security Best Practice:</strong> Public access prevention ensures your bucket remains private, aligning with security recommendations. Signed URLs are unsuitable for frequent access, as they require constant regeneration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Isolation:</strong> This method creates a dedicated identity for your Cloud Run service, aiding in auditing and minimizing potential impact should other services with wider permissions be compromised.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Implement signed URLs for the specified bucket...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Management Overhead:</strong> Signed URLs are great for temporary, granular access. However, frequent regeneration and management is cumbersome for a serverless web application expecting regular image access.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Potential Security Concerns:</strong> Signed URL structure leaks some information about bucket and object names.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Enable public access prevention for the bucket...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Overly Broad Permissions:</strong> The Compute Engine default service account often encompasses multiple services, granting more permissions than needed, violating the principle of least privilege.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Implement signed URLs for the specified bucket...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Has the same issues as option I with the overhead of signed URL management.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e3q23":{"id":"e3q23","exam":3,"n":23,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You seek to alert on-call engineers promptly about a service degradation in production, minimizing development time. Which option is the most efficient?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Employ Stackdriver Monitoring to monitor resources and generate alerts.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Implement Cloud Pub/Sub to monitor resources and generate alerts.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize Stackdriver Error Reporting to capture errors and generate alerts.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize Cloud Function to monitor resources and generate alerts.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Employ Stackdriver Monitoring to monitor resources and generate alerts.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Purpose-Built:</strong> Stackdriver Monitoring (now called Cloud Monitoring) is specifically designed for monitoring resources, metrics, and setting up alerting policies based on various conditions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Wide Range of Metrics:</strong> It offers comprehensive coverage of infrastructure and application-level metrics, allowing you to pinpoint relevant indicators of service degradation (e.g., error rates, latency, resource utilization).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Alerting Integration:</strong> Stackdriver Monitoring seamlessly integrates with alerting mechanisms, allowing you to define thresholds and quickly notify on-call engineers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimizes Development Effort:</strong> Using Stackdriver Monitoring requires minimal development time compared to building a custom solution.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Utilize Cloud Function to monitor resources...</strong> While Cloud Functions can be used for this purpose, it involves more development overhead in fetching metrics, defining logic, and handling alerts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Implement Cloud Pub/Sub to monitor resources...</strong> Cloud Pub/Sub is a messaging service. It would need to be integrated with other monitoring and alerting components, adding complexity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Utilize Stackdriver Error Reporting...</strong> Error Reporting is primarily focused on error analysis rather than general resource monitoring and alerting.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/serverless-web-performance-monitoring-using-cloud-functions  https://cloud.google.com/error-reporting/docs/notifications  https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/gcp/drilling-down-into-stackdriver-service-monitoring\">https://cloud.google.com/solutions/serverless-web-performance-monitoring-using-cloud-functions</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/serverless-web-performance-monitoring-using-cloud-functions  https://cloud.google.com/error-reporting/docs/notifications  https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/gcp/drilling-down-into-stackdriver-service-monitoring\">https://cloud.google.com/error-reporting/docs/notifications</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/serverless-web-performance-monitoring-using-cloud-functions  https://cloud.google.com/error-reporting/docs/notifications  https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/gcp/drilling-down-into-stackdriver-service-monitoring\">https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/gcp/drilling-down-into-stackdriver-service-monitoring</a></p>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e3q24":{"id":"e3q24","exam":3,"n":24,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You've recently joined a new team managing a Cloud Spanner database instance in production. The manager tasks you with optimizing the Spanner instance to cut costs while maintaining top-notch reliability and availability. What's the recommended strategy?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Review error logs using Cloud Logging, gradually reducing Spanner processing units until reaching the minimum required capacity.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize Cloud Trace to monitor incoming requests' requests per second to Spanner, gradually decreasing Spanner processing units until identifying the minimum necessary capacity.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Snapshot Debugger to identify application errors, gradually decreasing Spanner processing units until finding the minimum required capacity.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Monitor CPU utilization with Cloud Monitoring, gradually reducing Spanner processing units until reaching the minimum required capacity.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The recommended strategy for optimizing a Cloud Spanner database instance to cut costs while maintaining reliability and availability would be:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Monitor CPU utilization with Cloud Monitoring, gradually reducing Spanner processing units until reaching the minimum required capacity.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Monitoring provides comprehensive metrics for monitoring various aspects of Cloud Spanner, including CPU utilization.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>By monitoring CPU utilization, you can determine the actual resource usage of the Spanner instance and make informed decisions about scaling down processing units.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Gradually reducing processing units based on CPU utilization allows for fine-tuning the capacity while ensuring that the database can handle the workload without compromising performance or availability.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>This approach provides a data-driven method for optimizing costs while maintaining reliability and availability, as it directly correlates resource usage with the actual workload requirements.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options I, II, and IV are less suitable for optimizing Cloud Spanner in terms of cost reduction while maintaining reliability and availability:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Option I focuses on error logs, which may not directly reflect the resource usage and workload demands of the Spanner instance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option II suggests monitoring incoming requests per second, which may not accurately represent the resource utilization or workload patterns of the database.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Option IV involves using Snapshot Debugger to identify application errors, which is not directly related to optimizing the resource allocation of the Spanner instance.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e3q25":{"id":"e3q25","exam":3,"n":25,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your application is deployed as a container within a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster. You need to securely integrate a secret into your application. What should be your approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Safely store the credential in Cloud KMS. Create a Google service account (GSA) to access the credential from Cloud KMS. Export the GSA as a .json file and mount it into the container as a volume, enabling access to the credential from Cloud KMS.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Create a Kubernetes Secret and inject it as an environment variable into the container.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Securely store the credential in Secret Manager. Create a Google service account (GSA) to access the credential from Secret Manager. Establish a Kubernetes service account (KSA) to run the container. Utilize Workload Identity to configure your KSA to act as a GSA.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Implement Application-layer Secret Encryption on the cluster using a Cloud Key Management Service (KMS) key.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IV. Securely store the credential in Secret Manager. Create a Google service account (GSA) to access the credential from Secret Manager. Establish a Kubernetes service account (KSA) to run the container. Utilize Workload Identity to configure your KSA to act as a GSA.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Secret Manager:</strong> Secret Manager is designed specifically to store and manage sensitive data like credentials, offering stronger security than Kubernetes Secrets alone.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Workload Identity:</strong> This method avoids direct handling of service account keys, significantly reducing the risk of key exposure. Kubernetes pods authenticate to Cloud services like Secret Manager using their identity bound to the Kubernetes service account (KSA).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Best Practice:</strong> Google strongly advocates this approach due to its enhanced security posture.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Create a Kubernetes Secret and inject it as an environment variable...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Security Concerns:</strong> While convenient, Kubernetes Secrets are generally base64 encoded, not fully encrypted. They are more vulnerable if the cluster security is compromised.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Implement Application-layer Secret Encryption...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Overhead:</strong> This involves additional complexity, as you'd need to manage encryption/decryption within your application, potentially adding performance impact.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Safely store the credential in Cloud KMS...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Key Handling:</strong> Exporting service account keys as JSON files introduces potential exposure risks. Workload Identity offers a more secure authentication flow.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity   https://cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/overview\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity   https://cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity https://cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e3q26":{"id":"e3q26","exam":3,"n":26,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You aim to create</strong><code>**fully baked**</code><strong>or</strong><code>**golden**</code><strong>Compute Engine images for your application. Your objective is to configure your application to connect to the correct database depending on the environment (test, staging, production). What's the best approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. During Compute Engine instance creation, set up a metadata item with a key named <code>**DATABASE**</code> and specify a value for the appropriate database connection string. In your application, read the <code>**DATABASE**</code> environment variable and use its value to connect to the suitable database.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. During Compute Engine instance creation, assign a tag with the database name to be connected. In your application, retrieve the tags for the current instance via the Compute Engine API, and utilize the tag to construct the appropriate database connection string.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Embed the relevant database connection string in the image and generate a distinct image for each environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. During Compute Engine instance creation, configure a metadata item with a key named <code>**DATABASE**</code> and specify a value for the appropriate database connection string. In your application, query the metadata server for the <code>**DATABASE**</code> value and use it to connect to the suitable database.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. During Compute Engine instance creation, configure a metadata item with a key named DATABASE and specify a value for the appropriate database connection string. In your application, query the metadata server for the DATABASE value and use it to connect to the suitable database.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach excels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Metadata provides a dynamic way to pass environment-specific configuration to your application during instance creation. This avoids building separate images for each environment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Maintainability:</strong> A single image can be used across all environments, simplifying image management and updates.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> Sensitive database connection strings are not embedded directly within the image, improving security posture.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Standard Practice:</strong> Utilizing metadata is a common and recommended way to configure Compute Engine instances.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Embedding Connection Strings:</strong> Hardcoding database connection strings into images creates multiple images, hindering maintainability and security.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Using Instance Tags:</strong> While tags can be useful for classification, they are less optimal for storing sensitive information like database connection strings.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Environment Variables:</strong> Compute Engine doesn't have a built-in mechanism to set environment variables during instance creation like metadata does.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/metadata/querying-metadata  https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/metadata/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/metadata/querying-metadata</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/metadata/querying-metadata  https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/metadata/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/metadata/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3","d3l4"]},"e3q27":{"id":"e3q27","exam":3,"n":27,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>In Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), you aim to update your application for authorized requests to Google Cloud managed services, ensuring a one-time setup while adhering to security best practices for auto-rotating security keys stored in an encrypted manner. Given that you've already configured a service account with the necessary access to Google Cloud services, what should be your next step?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Embed the exported Google Cloud service account directly into the application's source code.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Upload the exported Google Cloud service account to HashiCorp Vault to generate a dynamic service account for your application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Implement Workload Identity to associate the Google Cloud service account with your GKE Pod.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Export the Google Cloud service account and provide it to the Pod as a Kubernetes Secret.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Implement Workload Identity to associate the Google Cloud service account with your GKE Pod.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this choice stands out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> Workload Identity eliminates the need to manage long-lived service account keys directly within your application or cluster. This substantially reduces the risk of keys being leaked or compromised.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Best Practice Alignment:</strong> Google recommends Workload Identity as the preferred method for securely authenticating GKE workloads to Google Cloud services.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Key Rotation:</strong> Workload Identity handles automatic key rotation for you, improving security posture without manual intervention.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Simplicity:</strong> Once configured, Workload Identity provides a seamless and secure way for your GKE pods to obtain the permissions they need.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Not Ideal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Export the Google Cloud service account and provide it to the Pod as a Kubernetes Secret.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security Risk:</strong> Secrets, even when encrypted, are more vulnerable to leakage than Workload Identity's dynamic authorization mechanism.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Management Overhead:</strong> You would be responsible for secure key storage and rotation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Embed the exported Google Cloud service account directly into the application's source code.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Major Security Flaw:</strong> This is extremely insecure! Hardcoding credentials make them highly susceptible to exposure in a source code leak.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Upload the exported Google Cloud service account to HashiCorp Vault to generate a dynamic service account for your application.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unnecessary Complexity:</strong> While HashiCorp Vault offers strong security features, it introduces additional infrastructure to manage and maintain, when Workload Identity provides a simpler, more integrated solution within GKE.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/best-practices-service-accounts#use-workload-identity\">https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/best-practices-service-accounts#use-workload-identity</a></p>","lessons":["d4l2"]},"e3q28":{"id":"e3q28","exam":3,"n":28,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>While developing a new application deployed on Cloud Run and leveraging Cloud Functions, managing ENV variables for interservice communication has become challenging with each feature deployment. Seeking a scalable solution, how should you implement dynamic discovery?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Adopt a well-documented naming convention to rename Cloud Functions and Cloud Run services endpoints.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Establish a Service Directory namespace, utilizing API calls to register services during deployment and query them at runtime.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Configure your microservices to utilize the Cloud Run Admin and Cloud Functions APIs for querying deployed services within the Google Cloud project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy HashiCorp Consul on a single Compute Engine instance, registering services with Consul during deployment and querying them at runtime.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>II. Establish a Service Directory namespace, utilizing API calls to register services during deployment and query them at runtime.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Designed for Dynamic Discovery:</strong> Service Directory is Google Cloud's native solution specifically designed for service registration and discovery. It handles dynamic changes as your application evolves.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> Service Directory is a managed service, providing scalability and reliability without needing to manage your own infrastructure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ease of Integration:</strong> It integrates well with other GCP services (Cloud Run, Cloud Functions), offering APIs for seamless registration and discovery.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Cloud Run/Functions APIs:</strong> While these APIs provide some service information, they are not primarily designed for service discovery and might become complex to manage at scale.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Naming Conventions:</strong> Relying solely on naming conventions is not robust, prone to errors, and hard to maintain as the application grows.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Consul on Compute Engine:</strong> Consul is a powerful service discovery tool, but deploying and managing it on a Compute Engine instance introduces overhead and potential scaling limitations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://medium.com/google-cloud/fine-grained-cloud-dns-iam-via-service-directory-446058b4362e   https://cloud.google.com/service-directory/docs/overview\">https://medium.com/google-cloud/fine-grained-cloud-dns-iam-via-service-directory-446058b4362e https://cloud.google.com/service-directory/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d4l11"]},"e3q29":{"id":"e3q29","exam":3,"n":29,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You're managing an application employing an HTTP Cloud Function to process user activity from both desktop browser and mobile application clients. This function serves as the endpoint for all metric submissions using HTTP POST. However, due to legacy constraints, the function must be linked to a domain separate from the one requested by users on web or mobile sessions. The Cloud Function's domain is https://fn.example.com, while desktop and mobile clients use https://www.example.com. To restrict metric submissions solely to browser and mobile sessions, which response header should you include?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Add Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Add Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://www.example.com</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Add Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://*.example.com</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Add Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://fn.example.com</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Add Access-Control-Allow-Origin:</strong><a href=\"https://www.example.com/\"><strong>https://www.example.com</strong></a>.</p>\n<p>Here's the reasoning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing):</strong> CORS is a browser mechanism that controls whether JavaScript code running on one domain can send requests and receive responses from another domain.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>The 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header:</strong> This header, sent by your Cloud Function, tells the browser which domains are explicitly allowed to make requests.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Why</strong><a href=\"https://www.example.com/\"><strong>https://www.example.com</strong></a><strong>:</strong> Setting this header allows requests only from the 'www' subdomain of your main website, not arbitrary domains or the Cloud Function domain.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Why the other options are less suitable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *** :</strong> This is too permissive, allowing any website to send requests to your Cloud Function, defeating the purpose of the restriction.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://*.example.com</strong> :** This allows requests from any subdomain of 'example.com', which might be broader than you desire.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Access-Control-Allow-Origin:</strong><a href=\"https://fn.example.com/\"><strong>https://fn.example.com</strong></a>:** This allows only requests from the function domain itself, preventing the intended user activity.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.example.com and back-end service domain where cloud function runs is https://fn.example.com  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/samples/functions-http-cors\">https://www.example.com and back-end service domain where cloud function runs is https://fn.example.comhttps://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/samples/functions-http-cors</a></p>","lessons":["d4l4"]},"e3q30":{"id":"e3q30","exam":3,"n":30,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>While running a containerized application on Google Kubernetes Engine with container images stored in Container Registry and employing CI/CD practices, how can you prevent the deployment of containers containing known critical vulnerabilities?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Enable the Container Scanning API for vulnerability scanning, review vulnerability reporting in Container Registry within the Cloud Console, and provide an attestation of the container's freedom from critical vulnerabilities, enforced by Binary Authorization policy before deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize Web Security Scanner to automatically crawl the application, review scan results in application logs, and provide an attestation of the container's freedom from critical vulnerabilities, enforced by Binary Authorization policy before deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Enable the Container Scanning API for vulnerability scanning, programmatically review vulnerability reporting through the Container Scanning API, and provide an attestation of the container's freedom from critical vulnerabilities, enforced by Binary Authorization policy before deployment.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize Web Security Scanner to automatically crawl the application, review scan results in the Cloud Console's scan details page, and provide an attestation of the container's freedom from critical vulnerabilities, enforced by Binary Authorization policy before deployment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Enable the Container Scanning API for vulnerability scanning, programmatically review vulnerability reporting through the Container Scanning API, and provide an attestation of the container's freedom from critical vulnerabilities, enforced by Binary Authorization policy before deployment.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this stands out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Automation &amp; Integration:</strong> Programmatic interaction with the Container Scanning API enables you to integrate vulnerability checks directly into your CI/CD pipeline for a seamless workflow.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Customizable Severity Assessment:</strong> Programmatically parsing the vulnerability scan results gives you fine-grained control to define what constitutes a \"critical\" vulnerability based on your organization's risk tolerance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Enforcement:</strong> Binary Authorization provides a robust mechanism to enforce policies based on the scan results, preventing vulnerable images from being deployed into your GKE clusters.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> This solution scales well, enabling you to automate vulnerability checks across a large number of container images.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I &amp; II. Web Security Scanner:</strong> While useful for web application security, Web Security Scanner doesn't focus on container image vulnerability scanning.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Manual Review in Console:</strong> Relying on manual review in the Cloud Console slows down your CI/CD pipelines and is less scalable for larger deployments.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l9"]},"e3q31":{"id":"e3q31","exam":3,"n":31,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>As you develop a microservice-based application to operate on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), certain services require access to various Google Cloud APIs. How should you configure authentication for these services within the cluster, adhering to Google's recommended best practices? (Select two.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Activate Workload Identity within the cluster via the gcloud command-line tool.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>V. Bind the Kubernetes service account and the Google service account using roles/iam.workloadIdentity with gcloud.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize the service account associated with the GKE node.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Centralize the storage of Google service account keys within a secret management service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Retrieve the Google service account keys from a secret management service.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The two most suitable approaches are:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Activate Workload Identity within the cluster via the gcloud command-line tool.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>V. Bind the Kubernetes service account and the Google service account using roles/iam.workloadIdentity with gcloud.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Workload Identity:</strong> This is the preferred method for authentication in GKE. It eliminates the need to manage service account keys directly within your pods. Instead, Kubernetes service accounts are securely mapped to Google service accounts, simplifying permissions management.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IAM Binding:</strong> The <code>roles/iam.workloadIdentity</code> binding is the mechanism that associates the Kubernetes and Google service accounts, enabling your pods to use the correct Google Cloud permissions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Aren't as Suitable</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. GKE Node Service Account:</strong> While technically possible, this is strongly discouraged. It grants broad permissions to all pods on a node, violating the principle of least privilege.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III &amp; IV. Service Account Key Management:</strong> Managing keys directly creates overhead. Keys can be revoked, rotated, and potentially leaked. Workload Identity eliminates these concerns.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/authenticating-to-cloud-platform#use_workload_identity  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/kubernetes-service-accounts\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/authenticating-to-cloud-platform#use_workload_identity  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/kubernetes-service-accounts\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/authenticating-to-cloud-platform#use_workload_identity</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/authenticating-to-cloud-platform#use_workload_identity  https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/kubernetes-service-accounts\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/kubernetes-service-accounts</a></p>","lessons":["d4l2"]},"e3q32":{"id":"e3q32","exam":3,"n":32,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You manage an application utilizing the Cloud Storage API. Upon reviewing the logs, you notice several HTTP 503 Service Unavailable errors from the API. Your application logs these errors but does not take any immediate action. To enhance success rates, you want to implement Google-recommended retry logic. Which approach should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Retry each failed operation at increasing time intervals, up to a maximum number of tries.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Retry each failed operation at consistent time intervals, up to a maximum number of attempts.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Retry failed operations in batches after a defined number of failures have been logged.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Retry each failed operation at decreasing time intervals, up to a maximum number of tries.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Retry each failed operation at increasing time intervals, up to a maximum number of attempts.</strong></p>\n<p>This is generally known as exponential backoff. Here's why it's the recommended approach:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Resolve Transient Errors:</strong> HTTP 503 errors often signal temporary issues like resource contention or throttling. Exponential backoff gives the system time to recover, increasing the likelihood of success on subsequent retries.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Avoid Overloading:</strong> Increasing intervals between retries prevent your application from hammering the service further when it's already experiencing issues.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Best Practices:</strong> Exponential backoff is a standard retry mechanism recommended by Google, particularly for transient errors like 503.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Retrying in Batches:</strong> While batching retries could be useful in certain scenarios, it doesn't guarantee immediate retries for individual failures and might delay error resolution.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Consistent Intervals:</strong> This could still overload the system if it's under strain. Increasing intervals are generally more considerate.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Decreasing Intervals:</strong> This goes against the principle of backing off and risks worsening the situation for the overwhelmed service.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/retry-strategy#exponential-backoff  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/retry-strategy\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/retry-strategy#exponential-backoff</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/retry-strategy#exponential-backoff  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/retry-strategy\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/retry-strategy</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e3q33":{"id":"e3q33","exam":3,"n":33,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>In crafting a new API, your objective is to minimize storage costs and optimize image serving speed. Which architectural strategy is most appropriate?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Implement Compute Engine with Persistent Disk as the backend.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy Cloud Content Delivery Network (CDN) with Cloud Storage as the backend.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Employ Transfer Appliance with Cloud Filestore as the backend.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize App Engine with Cloud Storage as the backend.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Deploy Cloud Content Delivery Network (CDN) with Cloud Storage as the backend.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cost-Effectiveness:</strong> Cloud Storage offers an economical solution for storing image files.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Performance:</strong> Cloud CDN leverages Google's global edge cache network, delivering images to users from locations closest to them, thereby significantly enhancing serving speed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> Both Cloud Storage and Cloud CDN automatically scale to handle traffic surges, avoiding performance bottlenecks.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Utilize App Engine with Cloud Storage as the backend.</strong> App Engine is designed for serving application code. While it can technically serve images, using Cloud CDN is more optimized for image delivery.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Implement Compute Engine with Persistent Disk as the backend.</strong> This setup introduces management overhead. Compute Engines require provisioning and scaling, while Cloud Storage and CDN offer serverless scalability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Employ Transfer Appliance with Cloud Filestore as the backend.</strong> These are generally used for large-scale data migrations or high-performance file workloads. The complexity and cost don't align well with a straightforward image-serving API.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l5","d1l9"]},"e3q34":{"id":"e3q34","exam":3,"n":34,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You're planning to publish a straightforward HTML application online, which serves as an FAQ resource for your application. This static site comprises images, HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Your aim is to make it accessible on the internet with minimal effort. What's the recommended action?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Deploy your application to an App Engine environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Set up a Compute Engine instance with Apache web server installed. Configure Apache to host the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Containerize your application first. Then, deploy this container to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) and assign an external IP address to the GKE pod hosting the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Upload your application to Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Upload your application to Cloud Storage.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>For a simple, static website, Cloud Storage is the easiest way to make it publicly accessible:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Simplicity:</strong> Directly upload your files (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Images) to a Cloud Storage bucket and configure the bucket for website hosting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cost-effective:</strong> Cloud Storage is a cost-effective option for simple website hosting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>No Server Management:</strong> You don't have to manage virtual machines or webserver software.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Global Distribution:</strong> Cloud Storage leverages Google's global infrastructure for low-latency content delivery.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Deploy your application to an App Engine environment.</strong> App Engine is overkill for a static site, adding unnecessary complexity and potential overhead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Set up a Compute Engine instance ...</strong> This involves managing a virtual machine, installing, and configuring a web server – much more work than necessary for a static site.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Containerize your application ...</strong> Containerizing and using Kubernetes adds complexity (and cost) without offering significant benefits for a purely static site.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://examlab.co/google/google-cloud-professional-cloud-developer\">https://examlab.co/google/google-cloud-professional-cloud-developer</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e3q35":{"id":"e3q35","exam":3,"n":35,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Upon migrating certain applications to Google Cloud, you observe that your on-premises legacy monitoring platform, used for both on-premises and cloud applications, is responding sluggishly to urgent issues in the cloud deployments. What's the recommended course of action?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Revert some traffic to your previous platform and conduct A/B testing between the two platforms simultaneously.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Install the Cloud Monitoring agent on your Compute Engine instances.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring for log capture, monitoring, and alerting, directing alerts to your existing platform.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Transition to Cloud Monitoring as a replacement for your current monitoring platform.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Utilize Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring for log capture, monitoring, and alerting, directing alerts to your existing platform.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Integration Focus:</strong> This approach focuses on integrating the strengths of Google Cloud's native monitoring tools with your existing platform. This minimizes disruption and leverages both tools effectively.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud-Native Advantages:</strong> Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring are optimized for Google Cloud environments. They provide in-depth visibility into your cloud resources and offer features tailored to GCP.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimize Disruption:</strong> Integrating alerts with your existing platform avoids a complete overhaul of your monitoring setup and associated processes. This ensures a smoother transition and lessens the risk of overlooking issues.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flexibility:</strong> This approach offers flexibility as you gradually evaluate the capabilities of Cloud Monitoring. If needed, you can further expand your use of Cloud Monitoring over time.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Transition to Cloud Monitoring as a replacement for your current monitoring platform.</strong> A complete replacement introduces a significant change which could be disruptive to existing workflows. Integration may be a better starting point.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Install the Cloud Monitoring agent on your Compute Engine instances.</strong> This improves resource monitoring on Compute Engine, but it may not fully address the broader cloud application monitoring requirements and the integration needs with your existing system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Revert some traffic to your previous platform and conduct A/B testing between the two platforms simultaneously.</strong> While helpful for evaluation, splitting traffic and A/B testing setups can be complex, and might not be the most efficient approach to improve monitoring responsiveness for urgent issues.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e3q36":{"id":"e3q36","exam":3,"n":36,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your application is deployed using a custom machine image, with multiple unique deployments organized into separate managed instance groups, each with its own template. Each deployment requires distinct configuration values. How can you provide these unique values to each deployment while using the same custom machine image across all deployments, leveraging Compute Engine's built-in functionalities?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Store the unique configuration values in a Cloud Bigtable table, accessible by each deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Include the unique configuration values in the instance template's startup script.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Store the unique configuration values in a persistent disk accessible to each deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Embed the unique configuration values in the instance template's instance metadata.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Embed the unique configuration values in the instance template's instance metadata.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this stands out as the most suitable solution:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Built-in Mechanism:</strong> Instance metadata is designed specifically for providing configuration data to Compute Engine instances.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Metadata can be defined at the instance template level, allowing customization for each deployment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> Metadata is accessible only from within the VM instance, enhancing security compared to external storage solutions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ease of Management:</strong> You can manage metadata through the Cloud Console, gcloud commands, or API calls, making updates straightforward.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Have Drawbacks:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Store the unique configuration values in a persistent disk ...</strong> This introduces dependencies on additional infrastructure (the disk) and potential complications around disk mounting and access permissions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Store the unique configuration values in a Cloud Bigtable table ...</strong> Using Bigtable adds complexity. It's more suitable for large-scale, structured data rather than simple deployment-specific configuration values.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Include the unique configuration values in the instance template's startup script.</strong> Hardcoding values in the startup script makes it inflexible. Updates would require modifying the script and potentially rebuilding the machine image.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/startup-scripts  https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/storing-retrieving-metadata#custom\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/startup-scripts</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/startup-scripts  https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/storing-retrieving-metadata#custom\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/storing-retrieving-metadata#custom</a></p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e3q37":{"id":"e3q37","exam":3,"n":37,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>As a developer managing three Google Kubernetes Engine clusters on Google Cloud, you need to enable your team to switch between clusters seamlessly while adhering to Google-recommended best practices. What is the best approach to configure access to multiple clusters?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Advise the developers to open three terminals on their workstation and utilize <code>**kubectl config**</code> to set up access to each cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Request the developers to utilize Cloud Shell and execute <code>**gcloud container clusters get-credential**</code> to switch to another cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Define the clusters, users, and contexts in a configuration file, and distribute the file to developers, instructing them to employ <code>**kubectl config**</code> to incorporate cluster, user, and context details.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Instruct the developers to install the gcloud CLI on their workstations and execute <code>**gcloud container clusters get-credentials**</code> to switch to another cluster.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>II. Define the clusters, users, and contexts in a configuration file, and distribute the file to developers, instructing them to employ kubectl config to incorporate cluster, user, and context details.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Centralized Management:</strong> Maintaining a single configuration file (often named <code>kubeconfig</code>) streamlines cluster access for your entire team and keeps configurations consistent.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>kubectl Standard:</strong> This approach leverages the standard <code>kubectl</code> tool designed for managing Kubernetes resources across clusters.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Best Practice:</strong> Aligns with Google's recommendations and Kubernetes documentation for configuring access to multiple clusters.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Request the developers to utilize Cloud Shell...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Impractical:</strong> Cloud Shell sessions are transient, requiring repeated configuration and are less suitable for ongoing development work.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Local Tooling:</strong> Developers often need to interact with clusters from their local development environments.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Instruct the developers to install the gcloud CLI...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Additional Overhead:</strong> While the <code>gcloud</code> CLI is useful, it introduces an extra tool dependency. <code>kubectl</code> is the primary interface for Kubernetes interaction.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Advise the developers to open three terminals...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inefficient:</strong> Manually switching context in separate terminals is cumbersome and less manageable than a centralized configuration file.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/access-application-cluster/configure-access-multiple-clusters/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/access-application-cluster/configure-access-multiple-clusters/</a></p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e3q38":{"id":"e3q38","exam":3,"n":38,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In the development process of a Cloud Function triggered by Cloud Storage events, your team seeks to streamline testing and development while adhering to Google-recommended best practices. Which method should you employ?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Set up the Functions Framework library and configure the Cloud Function on a local environment. Duplicate the function and modify the new version. Use curl to test the updated version locally.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Duplicate the Cloud Function and refactor the code to trigger on HTTP requests. Test the updated version by invoking the HTTP endpoint and assess functionality using mock requests.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Duplicate the Cloud Function in the Google Cloud console and edit the source code of the new function using the in-line editor. Adapt your web application to call the new function and test the updated version in production.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Develop a new Cloud Function triggered by Cloud Audit Logs detecting the cloudfunctions.functions.sourceCodeSet operation in the original Cloud Function. Evaluate functionality using mock requests.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>III. Set up the Functions Framework library and configure the Cloud Function on a local environment. Duplicate the function and modify the new version. Use curl to test the updated version locally.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Local Development:</strong> The Functions Framework allows you to emulate Cloud Function behavior directly on your local machine, accelerating development and testing cycles.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Isolation:</strong> Testing changes locally before deploying them to the cloud environment minimizes the risk of introducing errors in production.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ease of Testing:</strong> Using <code>curl</code> or other HTTP tools lets you easily send mock requests and verify your function's behavior.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Develop a new Cloud Function triggered by Cloud Audit Logs...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complexity:</strong> While feasible, setting up another Cloud Function just for testing introduces unnecessary complexity and overhead.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Duplicate the Cloud Function and refactor the code to trigger on HTTP requests...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Limited Testing:</strong> This approach can be useful for basic HTTP testing, but it doesn't fully simulate the Cloud Storage event trigger behavior.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Duplicate the Cloud Function in the Google Cloud console ...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Production Risk:</strong> Testing directly in a production environment is risky and strongly discouraged. It could lead to unexpected behavior for users.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/function-frameworks  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/calling#cloudevent_functions  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/overview#choosing_an_abstraction_layer  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/writing/write-event-driven-functions   https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/calling/storage\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/function-frameworks</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/function-frameworks  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/calling#cloudevent_functions  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/overview#choosing_an_abstraction_layer  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/writing/write-event-driven-functions   https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/calling/storage\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/calling#cloudevent_functions</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/function-frameworks  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/calling#cloudevent_functions  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/overview#choosing_an_abstraction_layer  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/writing/write-event-driven-functions   https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/calling/storage\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/overview#choosing_an_abstraction_layer</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/function-frameworks  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/calling#cloudevent_functions  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/overview#choosing_an_abstraction_layer  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/writing/write-event-driven-functions   https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/calling/storage\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/writing/write-event-driven-functions </a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/function-frameworks  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/calling#cloudevent_functions  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/overview#choosing_an_abstraction_layer  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/writing/write-event-driven-functions   https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/calling/storage\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/calling/storage</a></p>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e3q39":{"id":"e3q39","exam":3,"n":39,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>In ensuring private traffic on Google Cloud for an application utilizing PostgreSQL and Cloud Run, which approach, aligning with Google's best practices and leveraging managed services, should be pursued?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Utilize Cloud SQL and Cloud Run in separate projects. Configure a private IP address for Cloud SQL, enable private services access, and establish a Serverless VPC Access connector. Set up a VPN connection between the projects and configure Cloud Run to use the connector for connecting to Cloud SQL.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy PostgreSQL on a Compute Engine virtual machine (VM) and enable Cloud Run in the same project. Allocate a private IP address to the VM, enable private services access, and create a Serverless VPC Access connector. Configure Cloud Run to utilize this connector for connecting to the VM hosting PostgreSQL.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Enable Cloud SQL and Cloud Run within the same project. Configure a private IP address for Cloud SQL and activate private services access. Set up a Serverless VPC Access connector, then configure Cloud Run to utilize it for connecting to Cloud SQL.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy PostgreSQL on a Compute Engine VM and enable Cloud Run in different projects. Assign a private IP address to the VM, enable private services access, and create a Serverless VPC Access connector. Set up a VPN connection between the projects and configure Cloud Run to utilize the connector for accessing the VM hosting PostgreSQL.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Enable Cloud SQL and Cloud Run within the same project. Configure a private IP address for Cloud SQL and activate private services access. Set up a Serverless VPC Access connector, then configure Cloud Run to utilize it for connecting to Cloud SQL.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the most streamlined, secure, and Google-recommended approach:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Simplified Networking:</strong> Keeping Cloud SQL and Cloud Run in the same project and using a Serverless VPC Access connector eliminates complex VPN configuration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Managed Services:</strong> This leverages fully managed Google Cloud services, minimizing operational overhead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Secure by Default:</strong> Private services access and private IPs enhance security by isolating traffic within Google's internal network.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Best Practice:</strong> This is the approach explicitly recommended by Google for this scenario.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Self-managed PostgreSQL:</strong> Deploying PostgreSQL on a Compute Engine VM adds the burden of managing the database instance and its security.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III &amp; IV. Cross-project VPN:</strong> Setting up VPN tunnels introduces complexity, potential performance overhead, and the need to manage additional networking infrastructure.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/connect-run#configure   https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/connect-run#private-ip\">https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/connect-run#configure https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/connect-run#private-ip</a></p>","lessons":["d3l10"]},"e3q40":{"id":"e3q40","exam":3,"n":40,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You are designing an application to receive messages from a single Pub/Sub topic and insert corresponding rows into a database. The application runs on Linux and utilizes preemptible virtual machines to reduce costs. To ensure a graceful shutdown, what should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Implement a shutdown script that writes a file in a specified location, which the application polls every five minutes. Upon reading the file, the application disconnects from the database.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Develop a shutdown script utilizing inter-process signals to notify the application process to disconnect from the database.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Create a shutdown script that broadcasts a message to all logged-in users, informing them of the Compute Engine instance shutdown, and advises them to save their work and log out.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Write a shutdown script that publishes a message to the Pub/Sub topic indicating a shutdown is in progress. After the application reads the message, it disconnects from the database.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Develop a shutdown script utilizing inter-process signals to notify the application process to disconnect from the database.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the most suitable approach:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reliability:</strong> Signals are a standard and reliable mechanism for inter-process communication in Linux, ensuring the message is likely to be received by the application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficiency:</strong> Signals are efficient, ensuring a prompt response to the shutdown event.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Preemptible VM Specific:</strong> Shutdown scripts are the recommended way to handle graceful termination of preemptible VMs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Focus:</strong> This solution specifically addresses graceful shutdown of the application and database connection, without unnecessary steps.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are not as good:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. User Notification:</strong> While user notification <em>might</em> be helpful in a different context, it's not relevant to graceful shutdown of the automated process.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. File Polling:</strong> Polling adds overhead, introduces delays (especially with a 5-minute interval), and is generally less reliable than signals.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Pub/Sub Overkill:</strong> While sending a shutdown message to Pub/Sub could be used as an additional notification, it overcomplicates graceful shutdown for a single subscriber application.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/preemptible#preemption\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/preemptible#preemption</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3"]},"e3q41":{"id":"e3q41","exam":3,"n":41,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>Your analytics system executes queries against a BigQuery dataset. The SQL query is run in batch mode, where the contents of a SQL file are passed to the BigQuery CLI. Subsequently, the output of the BigQuery CLI is redirected to another process. However, a permission error occurs when executing the queries.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>You aim to resolve this issue. What action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Create a new dataset in BigQuery, copy the source table to the new dataset, and then query the new dataset and table from the CLI.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Grant the service account the BigQuery Data Viewer and BigQuery Job User roles.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Grant the service account the BigQuery Data Editor and BigQuery Data Viewer roles.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Create a view in BigQuery based on the SQL query, then query the view using the CLI.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>To resolve the permission error when executing queries using the BigQuery CLI, the most appropriate action would be:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Grant the service account the BigQuery Data Viewer and BigQuery Job User roles.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Grant the service account the BigQuery Data Viewer and BigQuery Job User roles:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>The BigQuery Data Viewer role grants read access to datasets and tables within BigQuery, allowing the service account to view the data needed for the queries.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>The BigQuery Job User role allows the service account to create and manage jobs (such as query jobs) within BigQuery. This role is necessary for executing queries using the BigQuery CLI.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>II. Grant the service account the BigQuery Data Editor and BigQuery Data Viewer roles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The BigQuery Data Editor role grants read and write access to datasets and tables within BigQuery, which might be more permissions than necessary and could potentially lead to unintended modifications to the data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>While the BigQuery Data Viewer role is appropriate for granting read access to datasets and tables, the Data Editor role is not necessary and could introduce unnecessary risks.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>III. Create a view in BigQuery based on the SQL query, then query the view using the CLI:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>While creating a view could be a workaround, it might not address the underlying permission issue. Additionally, it adds complexity to the solution without directly addressing the problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>IV. Create a new dataset in BigQuery, copy the source table to the new dataset, and then query the new dataset and table from the CLI:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>This approach involves unnecessary duplication of data and resources, which is not optimal for resolving a permission error.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Therefore, option I, granting the service account the BigQuery Data Viewer and BigQuery Job User roles, is the most appropriate action to resolve the permission error when executing queries using the BigQuery CLI.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/access-control#bigquery\">https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/access-control#bigquery</a></p>","lessons":["d4l1","d4l10"]},"e3q42":{"id":"e3q42","exam":3,"n":42,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>As you deploy your application to a Compute Engine virtual machine instance, it logs its files to disk. You want to view these logs in Stackdriver Logging without modifying the application code. What's the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Install the Stackdriver Logging Agent and set it up to transmit the application logs.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Modify the application to log to /var/log so that its logs are automatically sent to Stackdriver Logging.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Specify the log file folder path in the instance's metadata to configure it for sending the application logs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize a Stackdriver Logging Library to directly log from the application to Stackdriver Logging.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Install the Stackdriver Logging Agent and set it up to transmit the application logs.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>The Stackdriver Logging Agent (now called the Cloud Logging Agent) is the most flexible and least invasive way to achieve this:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>No Code Changes:</strong> You don't have to modify your existing logging mechanisms, preserving your application logic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Customization:</strong> The agent can monitor custom log files and even parse them to extract structured data, making it adaptable to your application's log format.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Centralization:</strong> The agent reliably sends logs to Stackdriver Logging, giving you a unified view in the Google Cloud Console.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Utilize a Stackdriver Logging Library...</strong> This requires changes to your code, adding the library and potentially modifying your logging statements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Specify the log file folder path...</strong> This method relies on specific agent configurations and log formats, making it less flexible than actively configuring the agent.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Modify the application to log to /var/log...</strong> This assumes your application can easily be reconfigured to write to <code>/var/log</code>. It also assumes standard log paths, which might not always be true.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/agent/logging/installation\">https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/agent/logging/installation</a></p>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e3q43":{"id":"e3q43","exam":3,"n":43,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You're tasked with transferring an internal file upload API, which enforces a 500-MB file size limit, to App Engine. What's the optimal approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Implement signed URLs for file uploads.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Use CPanel for file uploads.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize FTP for uploading files.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Modify the API to function as a multipart file upload API.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The optimal approach for transferring an internal file upload API to App Engine with a 500-MB file size limit would be:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Modify the API to function as a multipart file upload API.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>I. Utilize FTP for uploading files.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Using FTP (File Transfer Protocol) for uploading files might not be the best option for App Engine as it is a platform-as-a-service (PaaS) offering by Google Cloud Platform, and direct FTP access is not typically supported. Additionally, managing file uploads through FTP might introduce complexities in terms of security, scalability, and integration with the App Engine environment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>II. Use CPanel for file uploads.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CPanel is a web hosting control panel that allows users to manage their website and server settings. While it provides file management capabilities, it's not directly applicable to App Engine, which is a managed platform for building and deploying applications. CPanel is more suitable for traditional web hosting environments rather than cloud platforms like App Engine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>III. Implement signed URLs for file uploads.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Signed URLs can be useful for providing temporary, secure access to resources stored in cloud storage services like Google Cloud Storage. However, App Engine itself is capable of handling file uploads, and implementing signed URLs might add unnecessary complexity to the solution. Additionally, signed URLs don't inherently address the multipart file upload requirement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IV. Modify the API to function as a multipart file upload API.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>This option is the most appropriate as it involves adapting the existing API to support multipart file uploads, which is a common approach for handling large file uploads in web applications. By modifying the API to support multipart file uploads, you can ensure compatibility with the App Engine environment and adhere to the specified file size limit. This approach allows you to leverage the capabilities of App Engine for handling file uploads while ensuring efficient and scalable processing of large files.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Therefore, option IV is the optimal approach for transferring the internal file upload API to App Engine with a 500-MB file size limit.</strong></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e3q44":{"id":"e3q44","exam":3,"n":44,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your team is developing a backend application to handle the business logic for an interactive voice response (IVR) system supporting a payroll application. The IVR system exhibits the following technical attributes:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Each customer phone call is associated with a unique IVR session.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A separate persistent gRPC connection is established to the backend for each session.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Interruption of connections prompts the IVR system to establish new connections, causing slight latency for the call.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Analysis of current call data reveals:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Call duration spans from 1 to 30 minutes.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Calls are predominantly made during business hours.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Significant call surges occur around specific dates (e.g., paydays) or during major payroll changes.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>To minimize cost, effort, and operational overhead, where should you deploy the backend appliction?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Cloud Functions</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Cloud Run</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Google Kubernetes Engine cluster in Standard mode</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Given the technical attributes of the IVR system and the analysis of current call data, deploying the backend application on Cloud Run would be the most suitable option. Here's why:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Cloud Run:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud Run is a fully managed serverless platform that allows you to run containerized applications without worrying about managing the underlying infrastructure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Since each customer phone call is associated with a unique IVR session and a separate persistent gRPC connection is established for each session, Cloud Run's scalability and ability to handle concurrent requests make it an ideal choice.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud Run automatically scales up or down based on incoming request traffic, allowing it to handle surges in call volume around specific dates or during major payroll changes without manual intervention.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud Run is cost-effective because you only pay for the resources consumed during request processing, making it suitable for a system where calls may vary in duration and occur predominantly during business hours. It automatically scales to zero when there's no traffic, minimizing costs during periods of inactivity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud Run's ease of deployment and management reduces operational overhead, as it abstracts away infrastructure management tasks, allowing the team to focus on developing the business logic for the IVR system.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hence, deploying the backend application on Cloud Run would minimize cost, effort, and operational overhead while effectively handling the technical requirements and call patterns of the IVR system.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/tutorials/secure-services#:~:text=The%20backend%20service%20is%20private,Google%20Cloud%20except%20where%20necessary.\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/tutorials/secure-services#:~:text=The%20backend%20service%20is%20private,Google%20Cloud%20except%20where%20necessary.</a></p>","lessons":["d1l1"]},"e3q45":{"id":"e3q45","exam":3,"n":45,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>In the development of your web application, which access configuration should you implement for controlling access to private images and videos stored in a Cloud Storage bucket, considering that your users are anonymous and don't have Google Accounts?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Set up Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP) for user authentication within the web application. Grant users access to the bucket post-authentication via IAP.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Generate a signed URL providing read access to the bucket. Users can access the URL following authentication through your web application.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Cache each web application user's IP address and establish a named IP table using Google Cloud Armor. Then, create a Google Cloud Armor security policy permitting user access to the backend bucket.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Assign the Storage Object Viewer IAM role to allUsers. Users can access the bucket upon authentication through your web application.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>Here's why the best option is still </p>\n<p><strong>IV. Generate a signed URL providing read access to the bucket. Users can access the URL following authentication through your web application.</strong></p>\n<p>Let's reiterate the reasoning and address some potential concerns :</p>\n<p><strong>Why Signed URLs are Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Granular Control:</strong> You maintain complete control over access duration and expiration of the signed URLs, ensuring a short-lived authorization mechanism.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Authentication Tie-in</strong> You integrate the signed URL generation with your application's authentication logic, ensuring that only authenticated users receive access.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Anonymity Compliant:</strong> Signed URLs don't require Google Accounts, making them perfect for anonymous users.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Limitations and Mitigations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Overly Broad Permissions:</strong> If your bucket contains other sensitive objects, you'll need additional logic to prevent signed URLs from granting access to them. Carefully structure your bucket and possibly use separate buckets to isolate private resources.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>URL Exposure Risk:</strong> If a signed URL leaks, temporary unauthorized access is possible. Minimize this risk by using short expiration periods for the signed URLs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Others are Less Suitable (Recap):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. (IP-Based Access):</strong> IP addresses are unreliable for individual user authorization as they are often shared or dynamic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. (allUsers Permission):</strong> Exposes your bucket to the entire internet, a serious security vulnerability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. (IAP):</strong> IAP primarily handles authentication, not fine-grained authorization for specific objects in a bucket.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference</strong> :</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/signed-urls#should-you-use\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/signed-urls#should-you-use</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e3q46":{"id":"e3q46","exam":3,"n":46,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>During the development of an application with multiple microservices on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), one microservice requires connectivity to a third-party on-premises database. It's crucial to securely store database credentials and ensure regular rotation, following security best practices. What's the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Securely store credentials as a Kubernetes Secret, employing the Cloud Key Management Service plugin for encryption and decryption.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Store credentials in an encrypted volume mount, linking a Persistent Volume Claim to the client Pod.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Save credentials in a sidecar container proxy, using it for database connections.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Set up a service mesh to control traffic between microservice Pods and the database.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The recommended approach for securely storing and managing database credentials for a microservice running on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) would be option IV:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Securely store credentials as a Kubernetes Secret, employing the Cloud Key Management Service plugin for encryption and decryption.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Kubernetes Secrets</strong> : Kubernetes provides a built-in resource called Secrets, designed specifically for storing sensitive information such as passwords, OAuth tokens, and SSH keys. Kubernetes Secrets are a natural fit for storing database credentials securely within the cluster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Key Management Service (KMS)</strong> : Google Cloud Platform offers a Key Management Service (KMS) that allows you to manage cryptographic keys for your cloud services. By using KMS, you can encrypt the Secrets stored in Kubernetes, adding an extra layer of security to the sensitive data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Plugin Integration</strong> : Kubernetes allows for plugins to integrate with external key management services like Google Cloud KMS. By using the Cloud KMS plugin, you can ensure that the Secrets stored in Kubernetes are encrypted using keys managed by Cloud KMS, adding another layer of security.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Options I, II, and III are not the best fit for securely managing database credentials in this scenario:</p>\n<p>I. Saving credentials in a sidecar container proxy might introduce unnecessary complexity and potential security vulnerabilities. Additionally, sidecar containers are typically used for tasks such as logging, monitoring, or additional functionality, rather than securely storing sensitive data.</p>\n<p>II. Setting up a service mesh could help control traffic between microservices within the cluster, but it's not directly related to securely storing database credentials.</p>\n<p>III. Storing credentials in an encrypted volume mount is better than plain text storage, but it lacks the fine-grained control and integration with key management services provided by Kubernetes Secrets with Cloud KMS integration. Additionally, managing encryption keys and rotation might be more complex compared to using Kubernetes Secrets with a KMS plugin.</p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/encrypting-secrets#reencrypt-secrets\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/encrypting-secrets#reencrypt-secrets</a></p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e3q47":{"id":"e3q47","exam":3,"n":47,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>Your company is developing an application that uploads reports to a Cloud Storage bucket. Upon uploading a report, you aim to trigger the publication of a message to a Cloud Pub/Sub topic. You want a solution that requires minimal implementation effort. What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy an application on a Google Kubernetes Engine cluster to handle the uploaded files and publish messages to the Cloud Pub/Sub topic upon receipt.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Configure the Cloud Storage bucket to activate Cloud Pub/Sub notifications when objects are modified.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Develop an App Engine application to receive the uploaded files and then publish messages to the Cloud Pub/Sub topic.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Create a Cloud Function triggered by changes in the Cloud Storage bucket, responsible for publishing messages to the Cloud Pub/Sub topic.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Configure the Cloud Storage bucket to activate Cloud Pub/Sub notifications when objects are modified.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this stands out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Built-in Integration:</strong> Cloud Storage has native integration with Cloud Pub/Sub. You can configure notifications directly, without writing custom code.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Serverless and Scalable:</strong> The notification mechanism is managed by Google, handling scaling and reliability concerns for you.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Focus on Core Logic:</strong> This approach eliminates the need to develop and deploy code solely for reacting to Cloud Storage events.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Require More Effort:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Develop an App Engine application ...</strong> This involves developing application code, handling deployments, and potentially scaling logic for the App Engine app.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Create a Cloud Function ...</strong> While less overhead than a full App Engine app, you still need to write the Cloud Function and manage its deployment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Deploy an application on a Google Kubernetes Engine cluster ...</strong> Kubernetes offers flexibility, but introduces the highest complexity for this simple use case</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications#overview  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications#other_notification_options  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications#overview</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications#overview  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications#other_notification_options  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications#other_notification_options</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications#overview  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications#other_notification_options  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications</a></p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e3q48":{"id":"e3q48","exam":3,"n":48,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Your company stores its source code in a Cloud Source Repositories repository. The company aims to build and test the code with each source code commit to the repository, seeking a managed solution with minimal operational overhead. Which method should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Implement a source code commit trigger to send a message to a Cloud Pub/Sub topic, subsequently activating an App Engine service to build the source code.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy Jenkins via the Google Cloud Platform Marketplace, set up to monitor source code commits.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize Cloud Build with a trigger configured for each source code commit.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Employ a Compute Engine virtual machine instance with an open-source continuous integration tool, configured to monitor source code commits.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Utilize Cloud Build with a trigger configured for each source code commit.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the best choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Managed Service:</strong> Cloud Build is a serverless CI/CD solution from Google Cloud, minimizing operational burden compared to self-managing CI tools.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Integration:</strong> Cloud Build has direct integration with Cloud Source Repositories, simplifying setup.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Cloud Build can be customized for various build and test scenarios.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Deploy Jenkins via the Google Cloud Platform Marketplace ...</strong> Jenkins is a versatile CI tool, but deploying it through the Marketplace still involves managing a VM, updates, etc., increasing operational overhead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Employ a Compute Engine virtual machine instance ...</strong> This approach has the most operational overhead, requiring provisioning, OS maintenance, and CI tool configuration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Implement a source code commit trigger to send a message ...</strong> While feasible, this introduces more moving parts (Pub/Sub, App Engine), adding complexity compared to the direct Cloud Build integration.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e3q49":{"id":"e3q49","exam":3,"n":49,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You're preparing to deploy a new application revision to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) in a production environment. Considering potential container issues, you aim to minimize risks while following Google's recommended practices. Which approach should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Convert the Deployment to a StatefulSet and conduct a rolling update with a PodDisruptionBudget set at 80%.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Convert the Deployment to a StatefulSet and execute a rolling update with a HorizontalPodAutoscaler scale-down policy value of 0.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Execute a rolling update with a HorizontalPodAutoscaler scale-down policy value of 0.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Conduct a rolling update with a PodDisruptionBudget set at 80%.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I. Conduct a rolling update with a PodDisruptionBudget set to 80%.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimizing Downtime:</strong> Rolling updates replace pods gradually, ensuring service availability during the update process.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Safety Net:</strong> A PodDisruptionBudget (PDB) limits the number of pods that can be unavailable simultaneously. Setting it to 80% guarantees that at least 80% of your pods remain active, mitigating the risk of full service disruption.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google-Recommended:</strong> This strategy aligns with Google's best practices for production deployments on GKE.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Execute a rolling update with a HorizontalPodAutoscaler scale-down policy value of 0.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HPA's Purpose:</strong> A HorizontalPodAutoscaler (HPA) is meant to adjust the number of replicas based on load, not primarily for deployment strategies. Disabling scaling down during the upgrade might help but doesn't provide the same level of control as a PDB.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Convert the Deployment to a StatefulSet and conduct a rolling update with a PodDisruptionBudget set at 80%.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Convert the Deployment to a StatefulSet and execute a rolling update with a HorizontalPodAutoscaler scale-down policy value of 0.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unnecessary for Stateless Apps:</strong> StatefulSets are designed for stateful applications that require stable identifiers and persistent storage. If your application is stateless, converting to a StatefulSet adds unnecessary complexity.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/configure-pdb/#identify-an-application-to-protect  https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/kubernetes-basics/update/update-intro/   https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/ensuring-reliability-and-uptime-for-your-gke-cluster  https://blog.knoldus.com/how-to-avoid-outages-in-your-kubernetes-cluster-using-pdb/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/configure-pdb/#identify-an-application-to-protect</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/configure-pdb/#identify-an-application-to-protect  https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/kubernetes-basics/update/update-intro/   https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/ensuring-reliability-and-uptime-for-your-gke-cluster  https://blog.knoldus.com/how-to-avoid-outages-in-your-kubernetes-cluster-using-pdb/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/kubernetes-basics/update/update-intro/ https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/ensuring-reliability-and-uptime-for-your-gke-cluster</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/configure-pdb/#identify-an-application-to-protect  https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/kubernetes-basics/update/update-intro/   https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/ensuring-reliability-and-uptime-for-your-gke-cluster  https://blog.knoldus.com/how-to-avoid-outages-in-your-kubernetes-cluster-using-pdb/\">https://blog.knoldus.com/how-to-avoid-outages-in-your-kubernetes-cluster-using-pdb/</a></p>","lessons":["d3l2","d3l6"]},"e3q50":{"id":"e3q50","exam":3,"n":50,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You are setting up a continuous integration pipeline with Cloud Build to automate the deployment of new container images to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). The pipeline includes building the application from its source code, running unit and integration tests separately, and pushing the container to Container Registry. The application operates on a Python web server.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>The Dockerfile provided is as follows:</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Select a virtual machine (VM) size with higher CPU for Cloud Build runs.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>V. Store application source code on Cloud Storage, and configure the pipeline to use gsutil to download the source code.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy a Container Registry on a Compute Engine VM in a VPC, and utilize it to store the final images.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Change the base image in the Dockerfile to ubuntu:latest, and install Python 3.7 using a package manager utility.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Cache the Docker image for subsequent builds using the --cache-from argument in your build config file.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>I. <strong>Select a virtual machine (VM) size with higher CPU for Cloud Build runs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Build allows you to specify machine type and size for your builds.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Choosing a VM size with higher CPU can speed up build times, especially for CPU-intensive tasks like building Docker images.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>This option can improve build performance and reduce overall pipeline execution time.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>II. <strong>Deploy a Container Registry on a Compute Engine VM in a VPC, and utilize it to store the final images:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Google Container Registry (GCR) is a managed Docker container registry that integrates well with Google Cloud Platform services like Cloud Build and GKE.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>GCR provides secure, reliable, and scalable storage for Docker images without the need to manage infrastructure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Deploying a separate Container Registry on Compute Engine adds unnecessary complexity and maintenance overhead compared to using GCR directly.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>III. <strong>Cache the Docker image for subsequent builds using the --cache-from argument in your build config file:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Build supports caching Docker images to improve build performance.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>By caching Docker images, subsequent builds can reuse layers from previous builds, reducing build times.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>This option helps optimize build times and improve pipeline efficiency.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>IV. <strong>Change the base image in the Dockerfile to ubuntu:latest, and install Python 3.7 using a package manager utility:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Changing the base image to Ubuntu and installing Python 3.7 using a package manager utility like apt can introduce compatibility issues.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The Docker image might not match the runtime environment of Google Kubernetes Engine, which typically uses container-optimized OS images.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>It's generally recommended to use base images specifically designed for container environments to ensure compatibility and security.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>V. <strong>Store application source code on Cloud Storage, and configure the pipeline to use gsutil to download the source code:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Storing application source code on Cloud Storage and using gsutil to download it adds unnecessary complexity to the pipeline.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud Build provides native integration with source code repositories like Cloud Source Repositories or GitHub.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Leveraging native integrations simplifies pipeline configuration and management.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Considering the requirements and best practices, the most suitable options would be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Select a virtual machine (VM) size with higher CPU for Cloud Build runs.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Cache the Docker image for subsequent builds using the --cache-from argument in your build config file.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These options optimize build performance and efficiency without introducing unnecessary complexity or potential compatibility issues.</p>\n<p>References:<br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/optimize-builds/increase-vcpu-for-builds   https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/optimize-builds/building-leaner-containers#building_leaner_containers  https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/optimize-builds/speeding-up-builds#using_a_cached_docker_image\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/optimize-builds/increase-vcpu-for-builds https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/optimize-builds/building-leaner-containers#building_leaner_containers</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/optimize-builds/increase-vcpu-for-builds   https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/optimize-builds/building-leaner-containers#building_leaner_containers  https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/optimize-builds/speeding-up-builds#using_a_cached_docker_image\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/optimize-builds/speeding-up-builds#using_a_cached_docker_image</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e3q51":{"id":"e3q51","exam":3,"n":51,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>To migrate an independent Java application operating on an on-premises Linux virtual machine (VM) to Google Cloud cost-effectively without employing the lift-and-shift method, the plan is to modernize the application by converting it to a container. How should this task be accomplished?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Employ Migrate for Anthos to transition the VM to your Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster as a container.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Jib to construct a Docker image from your source code, subsequently uploading it to Artifact Registry. Then, deploy the application within a GKE cluster and perform testing.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Use Migrate for Compute Engine to transfer the VM to a Compute Engine instance, and leverage Cloud Build for container conversion.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Export the VM as a raw disk and import it as an image. Then, instantiate a Compute Engine instance from the imported image.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The most appropriate approach for modernizing the Java application by converting it into a container on Google Cloud would be option IV:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Utilize Jib to construct a Docker image from your source code, subsequently uploading it to Artifact Registry. Then, deploy the application within a GKE cluster and perform testing.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>Jib: Jib is a tool provided by Google for building Docker and OCI images for Java applications without needing to write a Dockerfile or manage Docker daemon. Jib integrates seamlessly with Maven or Gradle, making it easy to build container images directly from your Java source code.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Artifact Registry</strong> : Artifact Registry is a managed service provided by Google Cloud for storing and managing container images. Once the Docker image is built using Jib, you can upload it to Artifact Registry for secure storage and distribution.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Deploy to GKE</strong> : Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) is a managed Kubernetes service provided by Google Cloud. Once the Docker image is stored in Artifact Registry, you can deploy it to GKE for container orchestration. GKE offers scalability, reliability, and ease of management for running containerized applications.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Options I, II, and III are not the best fit for modernizing the application as described:</p>\n<p>I. Migrate for Anthos is a tool for modernizing and migrating workloads to containers and Kubernetes, but it's typically used for more complex applications that may require additional refactoring or integration with Kubernetes features. For a simple Java application, using Jib and deploying to GKE directly would be more straightforward and cost-effective.</p>\n<p>II. Exporting the VM as a raw disk and importing it as an image to instantiate a Compute Engine instance doesn't involve containerization. While it can migrate the application to Google Cloud, it doesn't achieve the modernization goal of running the application in a containerized environment.</p>\n<p>III. Migrate for Compute Engine is a tool for migrating VMs to Compute Engine instances, but it doesn't inherently involve containerization. Leveraging Cloud Build for container conversion might add unnecessary complexity compared to using Jib directly for building Docker images.</p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/application-development/introducing-jib-build-java-docker-images-better\">https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/application-development/introducing-jib-build-java-docker-images-better</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e3q52":{"id":"e3q52","exam":3,"n":52,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You aim to monitor the memory usage of your application deployed on Compute Engine. What's the appropriate action?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Implement the Stackdriver Client Library.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize the Stackdriver Metrics Explorer.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy the Stackdriver Monitoring Agent.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Access the Google Cloud Platform Console.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Deploy the Stackdriver Monitoring Agent.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the best option:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Comprehensive Metrics:</strong> The Stackdriver Monitoring Agent automatically collects a wide range of system-level metrics, including memory usage, and sends them to Stackdriver Monitoring.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimal Configuration:</strong> Agent installation and basic configuration are relatively straightforward.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Visualization &amp; Alerting:</strong> Stackdriver Monitoring provides tools for visualizing memory usage data over time and creating alerts based on thresholds you define.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Implement the Stackdriver Client Library.</strong> While this library <em>can</em> be used for custom metrics, it's overkill for basic memory usage. The agent handles this automatically.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Utilize the Stackdriver Metrics Explorer.</strong> Metrics Explorer is for viewing and querying data collected by Stackdriver Monitoring – this relies on the agent collecting those metrics first.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Access the Google Cloud Platform Console.</strong> The GCP console offers a high-level overview of resources, but lacks the granular historical views and alerting capabilities you'd need for detailed memory monitoring.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>References:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/api/metrics_agent#agent-memory  https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/agent\">https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/api/metrics_agent#agent-memory</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/api/metrics_agent#agent-memory  https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/agent\">https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/agent</a></p>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e3q53":{"id":"e3q53","exam":3,"n":53,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You are developing a web application accessible over both HTTP and HTTPS, hosted on Compute Engine instances. Periodically, you'll require SSH access from your remote laptop to conduct maintenance on these instances. How should you configure the instances while ensuring compliance with Google-recommended best practices?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Establish a backend with Compute Engine web server instances utilizing private IP addresses behind an HTTP(S) load balancer. Additionally, set up a bastion host with a public IP address and open firewall ports. Access the web instances via the bastion host.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Deploy a backend with Compute Engine web server instances utilizing private IP addresses behind a TCP proxy load balancer.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Implement the Cloud Identity-Aware Proxy API for SSH access. Then, configure the Compute Engine servers with private IP addresses behind an HTTP(s) load balancer for web traffic.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Configure firewall rules to permit all incoming traffic to connect to the Compute Engine web servers, assigning each server a unique external IP address.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Implement the Cloud Identity-Aware Proxy API for SSH access. Then, configure the Compute Engine servers with private IP addresses behind an HTTP(S) load balancer for web traffic.</strong></p>\n<p>Let's break down why this is the most secure and streamlined option:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>IAP provides strong authentication and access control for SSH, integrated with Google accounts, enabling fine-grained authorization and auditing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Private IP addresses for Compute Engine instances minimize the attack surface by preventing direct access from the internet.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Manageability:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>A single HTTP(S) load balancer handles both web traffic and SSH access (tunneled through IAP), simplifying your networking setup.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>You avoid the overhead of managing bastion hosts or additional firewall rules.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Best Practices:</strong> This approach aligns with Google's strong recommendations around network security and access management.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. TCP Proxy Load Balancer:</strong> This solution would work but is less secure than using IAP for managing SSH access specifically.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Firewall Rules with Public IPs:</strong> Exposing Compute Engine instances directly to the internet with wide firewall rules significantly increases the risk profile.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Bastion Host:</strong> A bastion host adds an additional point of management and potential vulnerability and is less streamlined than the IAP-based approach.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/connect/ssh-using-iap  https://medium.com/@larry_nguyen/use-identity-aware-proxy-iap-instead-of-bastion-host-to-connect-to-private-virtual-machines-in-9885bc7c12d  https://cloud.google.com/solutions/connecting-securely#storing_host_keys_by_enabling_guest_attributes\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/connect/ssh-using-iap</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/connect/ssh-using-iap  https://medium.com/@larry_nguyen/use-identity-aware-proxy-iap-instead-of-bastion-host-to-connect-to-private-virtual-machines-in-9885bc7c12d  https://cloud.google.com/solutions/connecting-securely#storing_host_keys_by_enabling_guest_attributes\">https://medium.com/@larry_nguyen/use-identity-aware-proxy-iap-instead-of-bastion-host-to-connect-to-private-virtual-machines-in-9885bc7c12dhttps://cloud.google.com/solutions/connecting-securely#storing_host_keys_by_enabling_guest_attributes</a></p>","lessons":["d1l8"]},"e3q54":{"id":"e3q54","exam":3,"n":54,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>In designing an application comprising multiple microservices, each with its RESTful API deployed as separate Kubernetes Services, ensuring minimal impact on API consumers during changes is paramount. Additionally, it's crucial to prevent disruptions to third-party systems upon new API releases. How should you configure the application's connection, adhering to Google's recommended best practices?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Consolidate multiple versions within the same service and specify the API version in the POST request.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize a Service Discovery system to connect to the backend specified in the request.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Implement multiple clusters and route requests to distinct versioned backends via DNS entries.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Employ an Ingress that directs requests to the appropriate backend based on the API's URL.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I. Employ an Ingress that directs requests to the appropriate backend based on the API's URL.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Using an Ingress aligns best with Google's recommendations and provides several advantages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Centralized Traffic Management:</strong> An Ingress acts as a single entry point for your APIs, routing traffic based on URL paths or other rules. This simplifies management and allows you to control which backend services handle specific API versions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Versioning:</strong> Ingress rules enable precise URL-based versioning (e.g., /api/v1/, /api/v2/). Users can continue accessing older versions while you introduce new ones.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Ingress controllers are highly configurable. You can implement load balancing, SSL/TLS termination, and other features at the Ingress level.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Cloud Integration:</strong> Ingress works seamlessly with Google Cloud's load balancing and networking services.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Utilize a Service Discovery system to connect to the backend specified in the request.</strong> While service discovery is essential in microservices, it's usually not directly exposed to clients to manage versioning.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Implement multiple clusters and route requests to distinct versioned backends via DNS entries.</strong> This is overly complex and expensive for API versioning alone. Multiple clusters are better suited for scenarios like isolating environments or scaling specific API versions independently.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Consolidate multiple versions within the same service and specify the API version in the POST request.</strong> This approach violates RESTful principles and complicates API client development and maintainability.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/ingress#deprecated_annotation   https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/ingress#features_of_https_load_balancing\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/ingress#deprecated_annotation https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/ingress#features_of_https_load_balancing</a></p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e3q55":{"id":"e3q55","exam":3,"n":55,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You've deployed a new application to Google Kubernetes Engine and are facing performance degradation. Logs are directed to Cloud Logging, and Prometheus metrics are captured using a sidecar model. How do you effectively correlate metrics and log data to troubleshoot and set up real-time alerts, all while minimizing costs?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Export both Cloud Logging logs and Prometheus metrics to Cloud Bigtable. Conduct a query to combine the data for analysis within Google Data Studio.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Export Prometheus metrics and utilize Cloud Monitoring to visualize them as external metrics. Configure Cloud Monitoring to establish log-based metrics from the logs and correlate them with the Prometheus data.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Export Cloud Logging logs and stream Prometheus metrics to BigQuery. Set up periodic queries to merge the data and trigger notifications using Cloud Tasks.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Derive custom metrics from Cloud Logging logs, then leverage Prometheus to import them via the Cloud Monitoring REST API.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Export Prometheus metrics and utilize Cloud Monitoring to visualize them as external metrics. Configure Cloud Monitoring to establish log-based metrics from the logs and correlate them with the Prometheus data.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this stands out as the best choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Unified Insights:</strong> Cloud Monitoring provides a centralized platform for both Prometheus metrics and log-based metrics, enabling easier correlation and visualization.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Real-time Alerting:</strong> Cloud Monitoring's built-in alerting mechanisms allow you to create alerts directly based on both metrics and log-based metrics.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cost Optimization:</strong> Cloud Monitoring is generally a more cost-effective solution compared to exporting all data to BigQuery or Bigtable.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimized Complexity:</strong> This approach avoids the overhead of setting up streaming pipelines and additional data transformations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Deriving Custom Metrics:</strong> While possible, it adds complexity and potential latency to your troubleshooting workflow.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Cloud Bigtable &amp; Data Studio:</strong> Bigtable is geared towards large-scale data management. Data Studio adds cost and complexity when Cloud Monitoring can cover visualization and alerting needs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. BigQuery &amp; Cloud Tasks:</strong> While queries in BigQuery are powerful, setting up this pipeline increases operational overhead and might introduce latency for real-time troubleshooting.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/gke/prometheus#viewing_metrics\">https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/gke/prometheus#viewing_metrics</a></p>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e3q56":{"id":"e3q56","exam":3,"n":56,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>While developing an application requiring user-specific subdirectories in Cloud Storage to store files, what action should be taken to ensure the creation of an empty subdirectory when a new user is added?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Establish an object with the subdirectory name and promptly delete it to create an empty subdirectory structure.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Produce an object with the subdirectory name, ensuring it has a zero-byte size, and set the Content-Type metadata to CLOUDSTORAGE_FOLDER.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Generate an object with the subdirectory name, appending a trailing slash ('/') to denote an empty directory.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Create an object with the subdirectory name, ensuring it has a zero-byte size and WRITER access control list permission.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Generate an object with the subdirectory name, appending a trailing slash ('/') to denote an empty directory.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach excels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Storage Conventions:</strong> Cloud Storage recognizes objects ending in a trailing slash as representing folders (subdirectories). This behavior is widely understood and aligns with common file system concepts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Simplicity and Efficiency:</strong> Creating a zero-byte object with a trailing slash is straightforward and efficient. There's no need for additional metadata management.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Tool Compatibility:</strong> Most tools and libraries that work with Cloud Storage will recognize and handle these objects as folders, ensuring seamless integration.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Create and Delete:</strong> This method is unnecessarily convoluted; it introduces an additional step (deletion) without any benefits.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. WRITER ACL:</strong> While setting WRITER permission is important for security, it doesn't directly address folder creation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Content-Type Metadata:</strong> Cloud Storage infers folders from the trailing slash, so setting specific Content-Type metadata is redundant.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/folders\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/folders</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e3q57":{"id":"e3q57","exam":3,"n":57,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>As the lead developer overseeing a new retail system operating on Cloud Run and Firestore in Datastore mode, you've successfully implemented the functionality to display a list of available products on the web UI. However, you've encountered HTTP 500 errors during peak usage times, indicating Cloud Run instances exceeding memory limits, coinciding with spikes in Datastore entity reads. To optimize system performance and prevent crashes, what action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Refine the query retrieving the product list by applying limits.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Adjust the query retrieving the product list by employing integer offsets.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Refine the query retrieving the product list by implementing cursors.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Adjust the Cloud Run configuration to raise memory limits.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>IV. Refine the query retrieving the product list by implementing cursors.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cursors for Pagination:</strong> Cursors are essential for efficiently managing large result sets in Firestore. They allow you to break down the retrieval of the product list into smaller, more manageable chunks, preventing memory overload in Cloud Run instances.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Integer Offsets:</strong> While offsets can be used for pagination, they become less efficient as the dataset grows. Firestore needs to skip over the specified number of results each time, leading to increased latency and read costs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Limits:</strong> Simply limiting the results per query helps, but if you have many products, users may need multiple requests to see everything. This leads to a less seamless user experience.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Increasing Memory Limits:</strong> This is a temporary fix that masks the underlying problem. It doesn't address inefficient data retrieval and will likely lead to higher costs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/datastore/docs/concepts/queries#cursors_limits_and_offsets\">https://cloud.google.com/datastore/docs/concepts/queries#cursors_limits_and_offsets</a></p>","lessons":["d4l9"]},"e3q58":{"id":"e3q58","exam":3,"n":58,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>As you develop a new web application using Cloud Run and commit code to Cloud Source Repositories, you aim to deploy new code efficiently. You've already crafted a Cloud Build YAML file that builds a container and executes the command: gcloud run deploy. What's the next step?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Schedule a Cron job to run the command gcloud builds submit every 24 hours.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Establish a Pub/Sub topic to receive notifications when code is pushed to the repository. Set up a Pub/Sub trigger to execute the build file upon receiving an event on the topic.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Configure a build trigger to activate the build file when code is pushed to the development branch of the repository.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Implement a webhook build trigger to execute the build file upon receiving HTTP POST calls at the webhook URL.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>II. Configure a build trigger to activate the build file when code is pushed to the development branch of the repository.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Direct Integration:</strong> Cloud Build triggers offer the most seamless integration with Cloud Source Repositories. They directly monitor your repo for changes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Branch Specificity:</strong> You can configure the trigger to activate only on pushes to the designated branch (development in this case), ensuring builds happen when you expect them.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficiency:</strong> This automated process eliminates the need for manual intervention or external systems for triggering builds.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Establish a Pub/Sub topic ...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Added Complexity:</strong> While Pub/Sub could be used to trigger Cloud Build, it introduces an additional layer to manage compared to a direct build trigger.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Implement a webhook build trigger...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>External Dependency:</strong> Webhooks require an external system to send the HTTP POST request, adding a dependency outside of Cloud Build.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Schedule a Cron job...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inflexible:</strong> Cron jobs are good for scheduled tasks but less responsive to dynamic events like code pushes. Also, daily builds could be too infrequent for active development.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-manage-triggers  https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/triggers  https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-manage-triggers#connect_repo\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-manage-triggers</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-manage-triggers  https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/triggers  https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-manage-triggers#connect_repo\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/triggers</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-manage-triggers  https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/triggers  https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-manage-triggers#connect_repo\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-manage-triggers#connect_repo</a></p>","lessons":["d3l8"]},"e3q59":{"id":"e3q59","exam":3,"n":59,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your team is developing an ecommerce platform where users log in, add items to their shopping cart, and are automatically logged out after 30 minutes of inactivity. Upon logging back in, their shopping cart should be preserved. How should you store users' session and shopping cart information while adhering to Google-recommended best practices?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Save session data in Pub/Sub and shopping cart information in Cloud SQL.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Memorystore for Redis or Memorystore for Memcached for session data and Firestore for shopping cart information.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Maintain both session and shopping cart information in a MySQL database deployed across multiple Compute Engine instances.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Store shopping cart details in Cloud Storage files, with filenames as the SESSION ID.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Utilize Memorystore for Redis or Memorystore for Memcached for session data and Firestore for shopping cart information.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this combination is ideal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Session Management:</strong> Memorystore (Redis or Memcached) is designed for fast, in-memory session storage. It offers automatic expiration (essential for your 30-minute inactivity logout).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Shopping Cart Persistence:</strong> Firestore's scalability and flexible data model make it suitable for storing shopping cart data that needs persistence beyond session expiry.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Separation of Concerns:</strong> Using separate stores for session data and shopping cart data aligns with the principle of separation of concerns, improving scalability and maintainability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Best Practices:</strong> This approach leverages Google-recommended services specifically suited for these tasks.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Pub/Sub &amp; Cloud SQL:</strong> Pub/Sub is primarily for event-driven messaging and Cloud SQL for relational data. They are not optimal for session or shopping cart data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Cloud Storage:</strong> Storing session data in files adds overhead and lacks the in-memory performance of dedicated session stores. It's also not ideal for frequent updates to shopping cart data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. MySQL on Compute Engine:</strong> While MySQL can manage both, self-managing a database adds operational complexity compared to managed services like Memorystore and Firestore.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/memorystore/docs/redis/redis-overview\">https://cloud.google.com/memorystore/docs/redis/redis-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d1l9"]},"e3q60":{"id":"e3q60","exam":3,"n":60,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You manage an application hosted on a production Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster, deploying updates through Cloud Deploy. As you anticipate frequent code modifications, you need tools to test these changes locally. These tools should:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Automate testing of local changes.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ensure local deployment mirrors production deployment.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Which tools should you select to test container building and execution on your local machine with minimal resource usage?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Docker Compose and dockerd</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Minikube and Skaffold</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Terraform and kubeadm</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. kaniko and Tekton</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>III. Minikube and Skaffold</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minikube:</strong> This provides a lightweight, single-node Kubernetes cluster on your local machine, replicating a production-like environment for testing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Skaffold:</strong> Skaffold handles local container builds, pushes them to a registry, and deploys them to the Minikube cluster. It also automates rebuilds/redeploys whenever you make local code changes. This combination is optimized for developer experience and resource efficiency during local testing.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Docker Compose and dockerd:</strong> Docker Compose helps with container orchestration but doesn't fully emulate a Kubernetes environment, making it less ideal for ensuring your local deployment mirrors production. <code>dockerd</code> focuses on image management rather than the full deployment process.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Terraform and kubeadm:</strong> This combination focuses on infrastructure provisioning (GKE cluster creation) rather than streamlined local development and testing workflows.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. kaniko and Tekton:</strong> Kaniko is useful for building container images within a Kubernetes cluster, and Tekton provides a CI/CD framework. While powerful, they are heavier solutions than necessary for optimized local development and testing.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e4q1":{"id":"e4q1","exam":4,"n":1,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You’re developing an integration for an ecommerce website to manage all steps in order fulfillment. You’ve created several Cloud Functions for each step in the order process and need to orchestrate their execution so that each function’s output guides the workflow. To minimize latency, what should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>C. Use Cloud Composer to call the functions and apply an Apache Airflow HTTP operator to manage execution logic.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use Workflows to call the functions and apply conditional jumps to manage execution logic.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>A. Use Workflows to call the functions and implement callbacks to manage execution logic.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use Cloud Composer to call the functions and apply an Apache Airflow operator to manage execution logic.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The correct answer is</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Use Workflows to call the functions and apply conditional jumps to manage execution logic.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p><strong>Google Cloud Workflows</strong> is designed to orchestrate the execution of Cloud Functions and other Google Cloud services in a <strong>low-latency, serverless environment</strong>. Workflows allows you to create sequences and apply <strong>conditional jumps</strong> to control the logic based on the output of each Cloud Function. This minimizes latency, as Workflows is optimized for coordinating serverless functions with minimal overhead and is more lightweight than Cloud Composer for simple, step-by-step orchestration needs.</p>\n<p>Key advantages of using Workflows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Conditional jumps</strong> allow dynamic control of execution flow based on the response of each function, ensuring that the next function is only called if certain conditions are met.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Low latency</strong> orchestration designed for serverless applications makes it ideal for real-time order processing and fulfillment tasks.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Use Workflows with callbacks</strong><br />\nWhile Workflows can use callbacks, <strong>conditional jumps</strong> are more straightforward and directly suited for decision-based execution flows, avoiding the complexity of managing callbacks in a sequential workflow.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Use Cloud Composer with an Apache Airflow HTTP operator</strong><br />\nCloud Composer (Apache Airflow) is a powerful tool for complex, long-running ETL workflows, but it introduces more latency and complexity than Workflows. It’s not as suitable for low-latency orchestration in a real-time serverless environment like order fulfillment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Use Cloud Composer with an Apache Airflow operator</strong><br />\nSimilar to Option C, using Cloud Composer adds unnecessary latency for a serverless, low-latency workflow. Workflows is a better choice for orchestrating serverless functions efficiently.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more on Google Cloud Workflows, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/workflows/docs\">Google Cloud Workflows Documentation</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/workflows/docs/samples/workflows-step-conditional-jump\">Workflows Conditional Jumps</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e4q2":{"id":"e4q2","exam":4,"n":2,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You manage a popular mobile game deployed on Google Cloud using Firebase, Firestore, and Cloud Functions. Recently, the game experienced a spike in usage, resulting in HTTP 429 RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED errors when accessing the Firestore API. With an upcoming marketing campaign, another usage surge is expected. To prevent this issue from recurring, what should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>D. Optimize database queries to reduce read/write operations and update the code to retry Firestore API calls using exponential backoff.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>A. Request a quota increase and update the code to retry Firestore API calls using fixed backoff.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Request a quota increase and update the code to retry Firestore API calls using exponential backoff.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Optimize database queries to reduce read/write operations and update the code to retry Firestore API calls using fixed backoff.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is </p>\n<p><strong>D. Optimize database queries to reduce read/write operations and update the code to retry Firestore API calls using exponential backoff.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>The <strong>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED (HTTP 429)</strong> error indicates that Firestore's quota or rate limits have been exceeded. To prevent this from happening again, it's essential to both <strong>optimize database queries</strong> and implement a <strong>robust retry strategy</strong> with <strong>exponential backoff</strong> :</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Optimize Database Queries</strong> : Reducing unnecessary read/write operations will help keep usage within limits, making the application more efficient and reducing the chance of hitting Firestore's quota limits.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Retry with Exponential Backoff</strong> : Exponential backoff is the recommended retry strategy for handling temporary quota limits, as it prevents overwhelming the system by gradually increasing the delay between retries. This approach improves resilience by reducing the chances of immediate repeated requests that could exacerbate quota issues.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Request a quota increase and use fixed backoff</strong><br />\nWhile a quota increase may help, fixed backoff is not an optimal retry strategy for handling quota limits, as it can lead to repeated requests without adjusting to the system's capacity, increasing the likelihood of continued 429 errors.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Request a quota increase and use exponential backoff</strong><br />\nWhile exponential backoff is beneficial, simply increasing the quota without optimizing queries may not be enough to avoid quota issues during future spikes. Optimization is essential to efficiently manage resource use and reduce load on Firestore.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Optimize database queries and use fixed backoff</strong><br />\nAlthough optimizing queries is important, fixed backoff is not ideal for managing high-load scenarios, as it does not adapt as well as exponential backoff to fluctuating system loads.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more on Firestore rate limits and best practices for managing resource usage, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/firestore/quotas\">Firestore Quotas and Limits</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l9"]},"e4q3":{"id":"e4q3","exam":4,"n":3,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You've developed an application intended for Cloud Run, where log records are currently being stored as text in local files. To optimize maintenance and ensure logs are directed to Cloud Logging, what approach is recommended?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Integrate the Cloud Logging library into your codebase and utilize it for logging purposes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Direct logs to Standard Output (stdout) and Standard Error (stderr) streams using your programming language's logger.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Set up a cron job to transfer the log files to Cloud Storage once per day for archival purposes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Make the log files accessible via a public URL like www.mycompany.com/logs, manually download them, and then upload to Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>Correct answer</p>\n<p><strong>II. Use your programming language logger to write logs to Standard output (stdout) and Standard error (stderr) streams.</strong></p>\n<p>Justifications:</p>\n<p>I. This is incorrect. While it's technically possible to use the library, that would result in a lot of code to maintain.<br />\nII. This is correct. Cloud Run has direct integration to Cloud Logging via stdout and stderr.<br />\nIII. This is incorrect. This task would have to be performed manually. Also this approach isn’t the most secure.<br />\nIV. This is incorrect. While data can be copied to Cloud Storage buckets, this doesn't help us with our goal of storing log data within Cloud Logging.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"%0d https:/cloud.google.com/run/docs/logging#container-logs\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/logging#container-logs</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"%0d https:/cloud.google.com/logging/docs/reference/libraries%0d\">https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/reference/libraries</a></p>","lessons":["d2l7"]},"e4q4":{"id":"e4q4","exam":4,"n":4,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>When migrating an on-premises container from Knative to Google Cloud without changing the application's deployment strategy and preferring a fully managed service, which Google Cloud service is the most suitable option for deploying your container?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. App Engine flexible environment</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Cloud Run</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Google Kubernetes Engine</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>Here's why <strong>Cloud Run</strong> (option I) is the most suitable choice in this scenario:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Fully Managed:</strong> Cloud Run eliminates the need to manage infrastructure, allowing you to focus on your application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Knative Compatibility:</strong> Cloud Run is built upon Knative, ensuring a smooth migration experience without changes to your deployment strategy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Stateless Containers:</strong> Cloud Run is optimized for stateless containerized applications, making it a good fit for many existing Knative use cases.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scaling:</strong> Cloud Run automatically scales your service up or down based on traffic, including the ability to scale down to zero when not in use (ideal for cost optimization).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are less suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Compute Engine (II):</strong> Compute Engine provides VMs, so you would still need to manage the underlying infrastructure, including Kubernetes setup and maintenance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Kubernetes Engine (III):</strong> While GKE is a powerful option, it involves managing a Kubernetes cluster. If your goal is a fully managed experience, Cloud Run is a better fit.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>App Engine flexible environment (IV):</strong> App Engine flexible environment is designed for more traditional application architectures and can involve more significant changes to your application for migration.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/serverless-options/  https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/the-appengine-environments  https://cloud.google.com/knative/  https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/when-to-use-google-kubernetes-engine-vs-cloud-run-for-containers\">https://cloud.google.com/serverless-options/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/serverless-options/  https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/the-appengine-environments  https://cloud.google.com/knative/  https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/when-to-use-google-kubernetes-engine-vs-cloud-run-for-containers\">https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/the-appengine-environments</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/serverless-options/  https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/the-appengine-environments  https://cloud.google.com/knative/  https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/when-to-use-google-kubernetes-engine-vs-cloud-run-for-containers\">https://cloud.google.com/knative/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/serverless-options/  https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/the-appengine-environments  https://cloud.google.com/knative/  https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/when-to-use-google-kubernetes-engine-vs-cloud-run-for-containers\">https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/when-to-use-google-kubernetes-engine-vs-cloud-run-for-containers</a></p>","lessons":["d1l1"]},"e4q5":{"id":"e4q5","exam":4,"n":5,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>How can you test conversion rates between three different website designs without altering the application code deployed on Compute Engine?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Deploy the website on Cloud Functions as three separate functions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Deploy the website on Cloud Run and utilize traffic splitting to test different designs.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy the website on Cloud Run as three separate revisions to test different designs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy the website on Cloud Functions and implement custom code to display different designs.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>To test conversion rates between three different website designs without altering the application code deployed on Compute Engine, the most appropriate approach is:</p>\n<p><strong>I. Deploy the website on Cloud Run and utilize traffic splitting to test different designs.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Option I involves deploying the website on Cloud Run and utilizing traffic splitting to direct users to different versions of the website with different designs. Cloud Run allows for easy deployment of containerized applications, and traffic splitting enables directing a portion of the traffic to different revisions of the same service. This approach allows for testing different designs in a real-world scenario without modifying the application code.</p>\n<p>Option II suggests deploying the website on Cloud Run as three separate revisions to test different designs. While this approach is feasible, it may require more manual management and monitoring of the different revisions compared to traffic splitting.</p>\n<p>Option III proposes deploying the website on Cloud Functions as three separate functions. However, Cloud Functions are designed for event-driven serverless execution, and deploying a website as separate functions may not be the most suitable approach for testing different designs.</p>\n<p>Option IV suggests deploying the website on Cloud Functions and implementing custom code to display different designs. While technically possible, this approach would require significant custom development and may introduce complexity and maintenance overhead.</p>\n<p>Therefore, option I is the most appropriate approach for testing conversion rates between three different website designs without altering the application code deployed on Compute Engine.</p>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Traffic splitting in Cloud Run: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-deployments#traffic_splitting\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-deployments#traffic_splitting</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l6"]},"e4q6":{"id":"e4q6","exam":4,"n":6,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>What action should you take to ensure that the Cloud Function is deleted even if the test fails during the automated testing process?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Change the order of the steps to delete the Cloud Function before performing the test.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Have the Cloud Build test step set its outcome in an environment variable called result and return 0. Add a final step after the Cloud Function deletion that checks whether the environment variable contains the expected results.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Include a waitFor option in the Cloud Build step that deletes the Cloud Function test step as a required preceding step.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Have the Cloud Build step write the Cloud Function results to a file and return 0. Add a step after the Cloud Function deletion that checks whether the file contains the expected results and fails if it doesn't.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>The most suitable action to ensure that the Cloud Function is deleted even if the test fails during the automated testing process is:</p>\n<p><strong>III. Have the Cloud Build step write the Cloud Function results to a file and return 0. Add a step after the Cloud Function deletion that checks whether the file contains the expected results and fails if it doesn't.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Option I suggests changing the order of the steps to delete the Cloud Function before performing the test. While this might ensure that the Cloud Function is deleted regardless of the test outcome, it doesn't address the issue of handling a failed test effectively.</p>\n<p>Option II proposes including a waitFor option in the Cloud Build step that deletes the Cloud Function test step as a required preceding step. This approach might ensure that the Cloud Function is deleted only if the test step succeeds, but it doesn't provide a mechanism to handle a failed test and still delete the Cloud Function.</p>\n<p>Option IV suggests having the Cloud Build test step set its outcome in an environment variable and adding a final step to check whether the variable contains the expected results. While this approach allows for capturing the test outcome, it doesn't ensure that the Cloud Function is deleted if the test fails.</p>\n<p>Option III is the recommended approach because it involves writing the Cloud Function results to a file during the Cloud Build step and then adding a subsequent step to check whether the file contains the expected results. If the test fails, the subsequent step can still execute and verify whether the Cloud Function was deleted as expected, ensuring cleanup regardless of the test outcome.</p>\n<p>Therefore, option III is the most suitable action to ensure that the Cloud Function is deleted even if the test fails during the automated testing process.</p>\n<p><strong>Documentation</strong> :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cloud Build: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-manage-triggers\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-manage-triggers</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e4q7":{"id":"e4q7","exam":4,"n":7,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>As the overseer of a microservice-driven ecommerce platform on Google Cloud encountering HTTP 500 errors when triggering a Cloud Function responsible for sending confirmation emails, what adjustment should be made to minimize email loss?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Extend the Cloud Function's timeout to nine minutes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Modify the sender application to store emails in Memorystore and trigger the Cloud Function. Upon invocation, the function retrieves email details from Memorystore and dispatches them to the email service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Revise the sender application to dispatch outgoing emails as messages to a Pub/Sub topic. Update the Cloud Function configuration to consume the Pub/Sub queue.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Configure the sender application to implement Cloud Function execution retries every one second in case of request failures.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The most suitable solution:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Revise the sender application to dispatch outgoing emails as messages to a Pub/Sub topic. Update the Cloud Function configuration to consume the Pub/Sub queue.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reliability:</strong> Pub/Sub introduces a durable message queue, ensuring emails aren't lost even if the Cloud Function temporarily fails.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Decoupling:</strong> Separates email generation from email sending, making the components more resilient to individual failures.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> Pub/Sub can handle bursts of emails without overwhelming the Cloud Function.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Extend the Cloud Function's timeout to nine minutes.</strong> While increasing the timeout might help in some cases, it doesn't fundamentally address potential issues with the email sending process itself. Long timeouts can also lead to higher costs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Modify the sender application to store emails in Memorystore and trigger the Cloud Function. Upon invocation, the function retrieves email details from Memorystore and dispatches them to the email service.</strong> Memorystore is primarily for transient caching. It introduces an additional dependency and potential point of failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Configure the sender application to implement Cloud Function execution retries every one second in case of request failures.</strong> Aggressive retries might overwhelm the system. They don't solve the root cause of the errors and don't provide the durability of Pub/Sub.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e4q8":{"id":"e4q8","exam":4,"n":8,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You are developing an application with a microservices architecture that includes Cloud Run, Bigtable, and Pub/Sub. To create a minimally viable product, you want to complete the testing and debugging process quickly and at minimal cost. What is the best approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>D. Use Cloud Code for developing, deploying, and testing microservices, and review logs with Cloud Logging.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use Cloud Build to set up a pipeline, adding stages for unit testing and manual approval, then deploy the code to your Google Cloud project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use emulators to locally test cloud resources, then deploy the code to your Google Cloud project.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>A. Use Cloud Shell Editor and Cloud Shell for deployment, and test the application using the Google Cloud Console in the project.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is</p>\n<p><strong>B. Use emulators to locally test cloud resources, then deploy the code to your Google Cloud project.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Using <strong>emulators</strong> is the best approach for quick and cost-effective testing of cloud services in a development environment. Google Cloud provides emulators for services like <strong>Bigtable</strong> and <strong>Pub/Sub</strong> that allow you to simulate the behavior of these resources on your local machine without incurring cloud costs. By testing your application locally with emulators, you can debug and validate its functionality before deploying to Google Cloud, saving both time and expenses in the early stages of development.</p>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Use Cloud Shell Editor and Cloud Shell for deployment, and test the application using the Google Cloud Console in the project</strong><br />\nWhile Cloud Shell provides a development environment, testing entirely in the cloud could incur higher costs due to resource usage. This option is less suitable for minimizing costs during the MVP phase compared to using local emulators.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Use Cloud Build to set up a pipeline, adding stages for unit testing and manual approval, then deploy the code to your Google Cloud project</strong><br />\nWhile CI/CD pipelines are valuable, setting up a full Cloud Build pipeline for an MVP may be unnecessary overhead. Emulators offer a simpler and more cost-effective solution for early-stage testing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Use Cloud Code for developing, deploying, and testing microservices, and review logs with Cloud Logging</strong><br />\nCloud Code is helpful for microservices development but involves deploying to Google Cloud for testing, which can incur additional costs. Emulators provide a more cost-efficient local testing option.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more information on using emulators in Google Cloud, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigtable/docs/emulator\">Google Cloud Bigtable Emulator</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/ruby/docs/reference/google-cloud-pubsub/latest/EMULATOR\">Google Cloud Pub/Sub Emulator</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e4q9":{"id":"e4q9","exam":4,"n":9,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Your company recently deployed a new API to the App Engine Standard environment. However, during testing, the API isn't functioning as anticipated. You aim to continuously monitor the application to diagnose potential issues within the application code without necessitating redeployment. Which tool is most suitable for this purpose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Implement Stackdriver Debug Logpoints to dynamically insert log statements into the running application code for real-time debugging.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Make use of Stackdriver Debug Snapshots to capture and analyze application state and variables at specific points in time.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Employ Stackdriver Monitoring to track and monitor the health and performance metrics of the deployed API.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize Stackdriver Trace to trace and analyze request latency and performance across the application.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IV. Implement Stackdriver Debug Logpoints to dynamically insert log statements into the running application code for real-time debugging.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reasoning:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dynamic Logging:</strong> Stackdriver Debug Logpoints provide the most appropriate approach in this scenario because they allow you to add detailed log statements directly into your running code without the need for code changes or redeployment. This enables you to gather real-time insights into the application's behavior and pinpoint the root cause of the unexpected issues.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options and Why:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Utilize Stackdriver Trace to trace and analyze request latency and performance across the application.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While Stackdriver Trace is valuable, it focuses on tracing requests and uncovering latency bottlenecks within a distributed system. It's less suited for diagnosing issues within the code itself without changes.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Employ Stackdriver Monitoring to track and monitor the health and performance metrics of the deployed API.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stackdriver Monitoring provides insights into the API's overall health and resource usage. It's helpful for general health checks but doesn't offer the granular code-level debugging needed in this case.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Make use of Stackdriver Debug Snapshots to capture and analyze application state and variables at specific points in time.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Although Stackdriver Debug Snapshots are useful for debugging, they provide a static view of the application's state at a given moment. Logpoints offer a more dynamic and real-time perspective, which is better suited to finding issues in a continuously running application.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Supporting Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Stackdriver Debugger (Logpoints):</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/gcp/add-log-statements-to-your-application-on-the-fly-with-stackdriver-debugger-logpoints/\">https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/gcp/add-log-statements-to-your-application-on-the-fly-with-stackdriver-debugger-logpoints/</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Stackdriver Trace:</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/trace\">https://cloud.google.com/trace</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Stackdriver Monitoring:</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring\">https://cloud.google.com/monitoring</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l7","d2l9"]},"e4q10":{"id":"e4q10","exam":4,"n":10,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>In your production environment, your application runs on Compute Engine virtual machine instances managed by a managed instance group and accessed through an HTTP(s) load balancer. Users are reporting inability to access the application. How can you set up monitoring to receive alerts when the application becomes unavailable?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Enable managed instance group health checks to monitor the health of your instances.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Implement smoke tests to detect application unavailability.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Set up Stackdriver uptime checks to monitor the availability of your application.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Configure Cloud Load Balancing health checks to monitor the health of your instances.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Option II, \"Set up Stackdriver uptime checks to monitor the availability of your application,\" is the most suitable choice for setting up monitoring to receive alerts when the application becomes unavailable.</strong></p>\n<p>Justification:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Stackdriver uptime checks provide a straightforward method to monitor the availability of your application by periodically pinging its endpoints. This directly addresses the need to monitor application unavailability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Stackdriver integrates seamlessly with Google Cloud Platform's monitoring and alerting capabilities, allowing you to configure alerts based on the results of uptime checks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>By setting up Stackdriver uptime checks, you can quickly detect when your application becomes unavailable and receive timely alerts to take necessary actions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This option focuses specifically on monitoring application availability, which is crucial for ensuring a good user experience.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>While the other options (I, III, and IV) also contribute to monitoring and maintaining the health of your infrastructure and application, they do not directly address the requirement of setting up monitoring to receive alerts when the application becomes unavailable.</p>\n<p>For further details, you can refer to the documentation on <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/uptime-checks\">Monitoring uptime</a>.</p>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e4q11":{"id":"e4q11","exam":4,"n":11,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You are building an application component to capture and stream user behavior data into BigQuery using the BigQuery Storage Write API. To prevent duplicate data in BigQuery, you need a straightforward solution. What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use the default type to create a write stream.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use the committed type to create a write stream.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Set up a Pub/Sub topic and use Cloud Functions to subscribe and remove duplicates.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Set up a Kafka cluster and use a unique identifier (UUID) to filter out duplicate messages.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is <strong>B. Use the committed type to create a write stream</strong>.</p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Using the <strong>committed</strong> type to create a write stream with the <strong>BigQuery Storage Write API</strong> is the recommended approach to prevent duplicate data. The committed stream type ensures that data written to BigQuery is immediately visible to queries and that each write operation is atomic. The BigQuery Storage Write API handles duplicate checks internally, making it a straightforward solution to avoid duplicate entries without additional infrastructure or deduplication logic.</p>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Use the default type to create a write stream</strong><br />\nThe default stream type is <strong>buffered</strong> , which means data is not immediately visible and requires a flush operation to make it available in BigQuery. Buffered streams are more prone to duplicates because they don’t enforce atomic writes as the committed type does.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Set up a Kafka cluster and use a unique identifier (UUID) to filter out duplicate messages</strong><br />\nThis would require additional infrastructure (Kafka) and a more complex deduplication setup, which is unnecessary given BigQuery’s built-in capabilities with the committed stream type.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Set up a Pub/Sub topic and use Cloud Functions to subscribe and remove duplicates</strong><br />\nAlthough this approach could manage deduplication, it would introduce additional latency and complexity. Using the committed type write stream is a simpler and more efficient solution for directly streaming data into BigQuery without duplicates.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more details on the <strong>BigQuery Storage Write API</strong> and stream types, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/write-api-streaming\">BigQuery Storage Write API Documentation</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/write-api-best-practices\">BigQuery Streaming Best Practices</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l10"]},"e4q12":{"id":"e4q12","exam":4,"n":12,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>To create a Cloud Function in Project A that stores its output in a Cloud Storage bucket in Project B, while following the principle of least privilege, what is the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Create a Google service account in Project A. Assign this service account the roles/storage.objectCreator role on the storage bucket in Project B. Deploy the Cloud Function using the service account from Project A.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Determine the default App Engine service account (PROJECT_ID@appspot.gserviceaccount.com) in Project A. Assign the default App Engine service account the roles/storage.objectCreator role on the storage bucket in Project B. Deploy the Cloud Function using the default App Engine service account in Project A.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Create a Google service account in Project B. Assign this service account the roles/storage.objectCreator role on the storage bucket in Project B. Deploy the Cloud Function in Project A using the service account from Project B.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Determine the default App Engine service account (PROJECT_ID@appspot.gserviceaccount.com) in Project B. Assign the default App Engine service account the roles/storage.objectCreator role on the storage bucket in Project B. Deploy the Cloud Function using the default App Engine service account in Project A.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The recommended approach for creating a Cloud Function in Project A that stores its output in a Cloud Storage bucket in Project B, while following the principle of least privilege, is:</p>\n<p><strong>II. Create a Google service account in Project A. Assign this service account the roles/storage.objectCreator role on the storage bucket in Project B. Deploy the Cloud Function using the service account from Project A.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach is recommended:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Service Account Isolation</strong> : It's generally a good practice to use a service account from the same project where the Cloud Function is deployed. This helps in maintaining clear ownership and isolation of permissions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Least Privilege</strong> : By creating a specific service account in Project A and assigning it only the necessary role (<code>roles/storage.objectCreator</code>) on the storage bucket in Project B, you adhere to the principle of least privilege. This ensures that the service account has only the permissions required to perform its intended function and nothing more.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Access Control</strong> : Assigning the <code>roles/storage.objectCreator</code> role specifically grants the Cloud Function permission to create objects in the Cloud Storage bucket in Project B, without providing unnecessary access to other resources or operations.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Options I, III, and IV are not recommended for the following reasons:</p>\n<p>I. Deploying the Cloud Function in Project A using a service account from Project B introduces unnecessary complexity and potential security risks. It's not a recommended practice to use service accounts from different projects unless there's a specific requirement for cross-project access, which doesn't seem to be the case here.</p>\n<p>III. and IV. Utilizing the default App Engine service account in either Project A or Project B is not ideal because it's a shared service account that may have broader permissions than necessary. Assigning it the <code>roles/storage.objectCreator</code> role on the storage bucket in Project B grants unnecessary permissions to all services running in Project A or Project B, which goes against the principle of least privilege.</p>\n<p>Therefore, option II is the recommended approach for creating a Cloud Function in Project A that stores its output in a Cloud Storage bucket in Project B while following the principle of least privilege.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#per-function_identity  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/iam-roles  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/using-iam-securely#least_privilege  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/understanding-service-accounts#granting_minimum_permissions_to_service_accounts  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#runtime_service_account\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#per-function_identity</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#per-function_identity  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/iam-roles  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/using-iam-securely#least_privilege  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/understanding-service-accounts#granting_minimum_permissions_to_service_accounts  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#runtime_service_account\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/iam-roles</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#per-function_identity  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/iam-roles  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/using-iam-securely#least_privilege  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/understanding-service-accounts#granting_minimum_permissions_to_service_accounts  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#runtime_service_account\">https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/using-iam-securely#least_privilege</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#per-function_identity  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/iam-roles  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/using-iam-securely#least_privilege  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/understanding-service-accounts#granting_minimum_permissions_to_service_accounts  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#runtime_service_account\">https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/understanding-service-accounts#granting_minimum_permissions_to_service_accounts</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#per-function_identity  https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/iam-roles  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/using-iam-securely#least_privilege  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/understanding-service-accounts#granting_minimum_permissions_to_service_accounts  https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#runtime_service_account\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/securing/function-identity#runtime_service_account</a></p>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e4q13":{"id":"e4q13","exam":4,"n":13,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>To facilitate updating the current stock price in an in-memory database for an application monitoring stock prices, how should you configure the Pub/Sub subscription, given each message includes the stock symbol, price, and timestamp?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Establish a push subscription with both ordering and exactly-once delivery disabled.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Configure a pull subscription with ordering enabled, utilizing the stock symbol as the ordering key.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Set up a push subscription with exactly-once delivery enabled.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Create a pull subscription with both ordering and exactly-once delivery disabled.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Configure a pull subscription with ordering enabled, utilizing the stock symbol as the ordering key.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Pull Subscriptions:</strong> Provide better control over message consumption, beneficial when updating an in-memory database.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ordering:</strong> Ensures stock price updates for the same symbol are processed sequentially, maintaining accuracy in your in-memory database.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Stock Symbol as Ordering Key:</strong> Guarantees that updates for a specific stock are applied in the correct order, preventing outdated values from overwriting newer ones.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Set up a push subscription with exactly-once delivery enabled.</strong> While push subscriptions can be useful, they relinquish control over message processing order, which might lead to inconsistencies in your in-memory database.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Create a pull subscription with both ordering and exactly-once delivery disabled.</strong> No ordering means you could update your in-memory database with older stock prices over newer ones.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Establish a push subscription with both ordering and exactly-once delivery disabled.</strong> Combines the drawbacks of push subscriptions (lack of ordering control) with the potential for duplicate messages.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e4q14":{"id":"e4q14","exam":4,"n":14,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>As a manager of a system utilizing stateless Compute Engine VMs and Cloud Run instances connected to a VPC with internal ingress settings, you encounter a 403 Permission Denied error when attempting to schedule tasks on Cloud Run. How should you resolve this issue?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Set up a cron job on the Compute Engine VMs to trigger Cloud Run based on the schedule.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Assign the roles/run.developer IAM role to the service account.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Cloud Scheduler in conjunction with Pub/Sub to invoke Cloud Run.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Modify the Cloud Run ingress setting to 'Internal and Cloud Load Balancing.'</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The most likely solution is:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Utilize Cloud Scheduler in conjunction with Pub/Sub to invoke Cloud Run.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Scheduler:</strong> Designed specifically for scheduling tasks on Google Cloud. It has the necessary permissions to work with Cloud Run.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Pub/Sub:</strong> A decoupling mechanism between Cloud Scheduler and Cloud Run, ensuring the security of your internal ingress settings. Cloud Scheduler can publish a message to a Pub/Sub topic, which your Cloud Run service can subscribe to.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Might Not Be the Best Fit:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Assign the roles/run.developer IAM role to the service account.</strong> Giving the <code>roles/run.developer</code> role to an external service account might loosen security too much for an internally facing Cloud Run instance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Set up a cron job on the Compute Engine VMs to trigger Cloud Run based on the schedule.</strong> While possible, it introduces more management overhead and potential points of failure compared to dedicated services like Cloud Scheduler.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Modify the Cloud Run ingress setting to 'Internal and Cloud Load Balancing.'</strong> This would expose your Cloud Run instance, breaking your internal ingress requirement.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l4","d4l7"]},"e4q15":{"id":"e4q15","exam":4,"n":15,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your team manages an application that aggregates news articles from various sources, with a real-time monitoring dashboard accessible to external stakeholders and analysts via a Compute Engine instance running a web application. These stakeholders require secure access to reports without authentication. How should you configure this secure channel?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Employ Cloud Scheduler to trigger Cloud Build hourly to produce an export from the reports, storing them in a publicly accessible Cloud Storage bucket.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Introduce an HTTP(S) load balancer in front of the monitoring dashboard and deploy a Google-managed SSL certificate on the load balancer for traffic encryption.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Implement an HTTP(S) load balancer preceding the monitoring dashboard and configure Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP) to secure the communication channel.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize the service account key of the instance to encrypt the traffic.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The recommended approach to configure a secure channel for external stakeholders to access reports without authentication for the web application running on a Compute Engine instance is:</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Introduce an HTTP(S) load balancer in front of the monitoring dashboard and deploy a Google-managed SSL certificate on the load balancer for traffic encryption.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Option I, utilizing the service account key of the instance to encrypt the traffic, is not suitable for securing access to the web application for external stakeholders. Service account keys are typically used for server-to-server communication or accessing Google Cloud resources, not for securing web application traffic.</p>\n<p>Option II, employing Cloud Scheduler to trigger Cloud Build hourly to produce an export from the reports and storing them in a publicly accessible Cloud Storage bucket, does not provide real-time access to the reports. It also introduces unnecessary complexity and potential security risks by making the reports publicly accessible without authentication.</p>\n<p>Option III, implementing an HTTP(S) load balancer preceding the monitoring dashboard and configuring Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP) to secure the communication channel, is a valid option. However, it adds complexity by requiring users to authenticate with Google credentials via IAP. Since the requirement specifies that external stakeholders require access without authentication, this option may not be suitable.</p>\n<p>Option IV is the recommended approach because it provides encryption for the traffic between external stakeholders and the monitoring dashboard without requiring authentication. Deploying a Google-managed SSL certificate on the HTTP(S) load balancer ensures that the communication channel is encrypted, providing a secure channel for stakeholders to access reports without authentication.</p>\n<p>Therefore, option IV is the most appropriate configuration for securing the communication channel for external stakeholders to access reports without authentication.</p>\n<p>Documentation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Securing user traffic to Compute Engine instances with SSL/TLS: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/ssl-certificates#compute-ssl-proxy\">https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/ssl-certificates#compute-ssl-proxy</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>HTTP(S) Load Balancing: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/https\">https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/https</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l8"]},"e4q16":{"id":"e4q16","exam":4,"n":16,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You're developing a new application and require it to trigger only upon updates to a specific file in your Cloud Storage bucket. Your trigger specifications may vary, necessitating a process that accommodates different trigger types. Additionally, you aim for a straightforward configuration to enable multiple team members to update triggers in the future. What is the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Establish an Eventarc trigger to monitor your Cloud Storage bucket for a designated filename, with the target set as Cloud Run.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Configure a Cloud Function designed to execute your application, triggered by object updates in Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Configure a Firebase function to execute your application, triggered by object updates in Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Configure Cloud Storage events to dispatch to Pub/Sub, leveraging Pub/Sub events to initiate a Cloud Build job executing your application.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The <strong>recommended approach is:</strong></p>\n<p>✅ <strong>II. Establish an Eventarc trigger to monitor your Cloud Storage bucket for a designated filename, with the target set as Cloud Run.</strong></p>\n<p>✅ <strong>Explanation for Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Eventarc + Cloud Run (✅ Recommended)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Eventarc</strong> allows you to configure triggers for <strong>specific Cloud Storage events</strong> , such as <code>google.cloud.storage.object.v1.finalized</code> or <code>metadataUpdated</code>, and you can <strong>filter by object name</strong> (i.e., specific file).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Eventarc supports <strong>advanced filtering</strong> , <strong>multiple trigger types</strong> , and <strong>targeting services like Cloud Run</strong> , which is suitable for modern, scalable apps.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>It enables <strong>simple YAML-based configuration</strong> , so multiple team members can manage/update triggers easily.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This is the <strong>most flexible</strong> , <strong>scalable</strong> , and <strong>modern</strong> approach recommended by Google Cloud.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Official Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/eventarc/docs\">https://cloud.google.com/eventarc/docs</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/tutorials/eventarc\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/tutorials/eventarc</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>❌ <strong>Explanation for Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Cloud Storage → Pub/Sub → Cloud Build</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Pub/Sub only supports event types like</strong><code>**OBJECT_FINALIZE**</code><strong>, not specific filename filtering</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Involving <strong>Cloud Build</strong> to execute your app is <strong>not ideal</strong> , as Cloud Build is primarily for <strong>CI/CD</strong> , not app runtime execution.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>More complex</strong> , and managing filters in Pub/Sub is harder and less maintainable.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Docs:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/pubsub-notifications</a><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-pubsub-triggers\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-pubsub-triggers</a></p>\n<p><strong>III. Cloud Function triggered by Cloud Storage</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud Functions can be triggered by Cloud Storage events, but they <strong>don’t support advanced filtering</strong> , such as matching <strong>specific filenames</strong> directly.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Also, if your app grows in complexity, Cloud Run offers better scalability and control compared to Cloud Functions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Not as configurable or scalable as Eventarc + Cloud Run.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Docs:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/calling/storage\">https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/calling/storage</a></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Firebase Functions with Cloud Storage</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Firebase Functions</strong> are typically used in conjunction with <strong>Firebase Storage</strong> , not general-purpose <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> buckets.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This option is <strong>not recommended</strong> for enterprise or team workflows unless the application is Firebase-based.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Docs:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://firebase.google.com/docs/functions/gcp-storage-events\">https://firebase.google.com/docs/functions/gcp-storage-events</a></p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e4q17":{"id":"e4q17","exam":4,"n":17,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>What approach should your company adopt to enable data analysts to analyze events using SQL on a near real-time basis, while also accommodating rapid growth and leveraging managed services?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Create a Pub/Sub topic and a subscription. Stream events from the source into the Pub/Sub topic. Utilize Dataflow to ingest these events into BigQuery.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy a Kafka instance on a large Compute Engine instance. Stream events from the source into a Kafka pipeline. Leverage Dataflow to ingest these events into Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Create a Pub/Sub topic and a subscription. Stream events from the source into the Pub/Sub topic. Utilize Dataflow to ingest these events into Firestore in Datastore mode.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Create a Pub/Sub topic and a subscription. Stream events from the source into the Pub/Sub topic. Utilize Dataflow to ingest these events into Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The best approach here is </p>\n<p><strong>I. Create a Pub/Sub topic and a subscription. Stream events from the source into the Pub/Sub topic. Utilize Dataflow to ingest these events into BigQuery.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this combination is optimal for your requirements:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Near Real-Time Processing:</strong> Pub/Sub's asynchronous messaging enables low-latency event streaming, ensuring near-real-time data availability for analysis.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability with Managed Services:</strong> Pub/Sub and Dataflow are fully managed services on GCP, meaning they automatically handle the scaling and infrastructure needs to accommodate rapid growth.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>SQL Analysis and Big Data:</strong> BigQuery excels at managing vast datasets and provides a familiar SQL interface for analysts to work with.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Dataflow's Power:</strong> Dataflow handles complex data transformations and the reliable streaming of data into BigQuery.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Create a Pub/Sub topic and a subscription. Stream events from the source into the Pub/Sub topic. Utilize Dataflow to ingest these events into Cloud Storage.</strong> Cloud Storage is for object storage, not structured analysis. Additional processing would be required to query data from Cloud Storage effectively.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Create a Pub/Sub topic and a subscription. Stream events from the source into the Pub/Sub topic. Utilize Dataflow to ingest these events into Firestore in Datastore mode.</strong> Firestore in Datastore mode is optimized for real-time applications, not large-scale analytics suitable for SQL querying.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Deploy a Kafka instance on a large Compute Engine instance. Stream events from the source into a Kafka pipeline. Leverage Dataflow to ingest these events into Cloud Storage.</strong> This introduces overhead and complexity of manually managing a Kafka instance compared to the fully managed Pub/Sub.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Pub/Sub:</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub\">https://cloud.google.com/pubsub</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Dataflow:</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow\">https://cloud.google.com/dataflow</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>BigQuery:</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery\">https://cloud.google.com/bigquery</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l10"]},"e4q18":{"id":"e4q18","exam":4,"n":18,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You manage an application deployed on <strong>GKE clusters</strong> across multiple environments, using <strong>Cloud Build</strong> for user acceptance testing (UAT). <strong>Artifact Analysis</strong> is integrated with <strong>Cloud Build</strong> , and the <strong>Binary Authorization API</strong> is enabled in all Google Cloud projects for your environments. To ensure that only container images passing specific UAT tests are deployed to production, and with an attestor already created, what should you do next?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>D. After the UAT phase, sign the attestation with a key stored in Cloud Key Management Service (KMS) and add a GKE cluster-specific rule in Binary Authorization for the production Google Cloud project policy.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>A. After the UAT phase, sign the attestation with a key stored as a Kubernetes secret and add a GKE cluster-specific rule in Binary Authorization for the UAT Google Cloud project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. After the UAT phase, sign the attestation with a key stored in Cloud Key Management Service (KMS) and add a default rule in Binary Authorization for the UAT Google Cloud project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. After the UAT phase, sign the attestation with a key stored as a Kubernetes secret and add a GKE cluster-specific rule in Binary Authorization for the production Google Cloud project policy.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is </p>\n<p><strong>D. After the UAT phase, sign the attestation with a key stored in Cloud Key Management Service (KMS) and add a GKE cluster-specific rule in Binary Authorization for the production Google Cloud project policy.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>To ensure that only container images passing UAT are deployed to production, you need to follow these steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Sign the attestation</strong> after the UAT phase, indicating that the image has passed the necessary UAT tests. Using <strong>Cloud Key Management Service (KMS)</strong> to store the signing key is a best practice for security and management, as it allows centralized control over cryptographic keys.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Add a GKE cluster-specific rule in Binary Authorization for the production Google Cloud project</strong>. This rule ensures that only container images with the required attestation can be deployed to production. By applying this rule to the production project, you restrict deployments to images that have passed UAT.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Sign with a key stored as a Kubernetes secret and add a rule in Binary Authorization for the UAT Google Cloud project</strong><br />\nStoring the signing key as a Kubernetes secret is less secure than using Cloud KMS. Additionally, the Binary Authorization rule should be set in the <strong>production</strong> project, as UAT is not the target deployment environment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Sign with a key stored as a Kubernetes secret and add a rule in Binary Authorization for the production project</strong><br />\nAlthough this option uses the correct environment for Binary Authorization (production), storing the signing key as a Kubernetes secret is not recommended for security. Using Cloud KMS for key management is more secure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Sign with a key stored in Cloud KMS and add a default rule in Binary Authorization for the UAT project</strong><br />\nAdding a Binary Authorization rule in the UAT project does not control deployments to the production environment. You need the rule in the production project to ensure only tested images are deployed there.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more information on setting up Binary Authorization and best practices with Cloud KMS, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/overview\">Binary Authorization Overview</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs\">Using Binary Authorization with GKE</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l9"]},"e4q19":{"id":"e4q19","exam":4,"n":19,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>To ensure a more even distribution of demand within the Cloud Bigtable cluster and generate human-readable row keys, what approach should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Utilize serially generated integer values for row keys.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Employ a subset of the MD5 hash of the row contents for row key generation.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Concatenate multiple human-readable attributes for row key generation.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Adopt UNIX epoch-styled timestamps represented in milliseconds for row key generation.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The best approach for ensuring even distribution and human-readable row keys in Cloud Bigtable is </p>\n<p><strong>C. Concatenate multiple human-readable attributes for row key generation.</strong></p>\n<p>Let's understand why and examine the limitations of other choices:</p>\n<p><strong>C. Concatenate multiple human-readable attributes for row key generation.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Hotspot Prevention:</strong> Distributes data across the table by including multiple attributes, avoiding concentrated access on a single node or range.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Readability:</strong> Allows for easier understanding of data and debugging using meaningful components in the row key.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flexibility:</strong> You can control the granularity and data organization based on your use case.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cons:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Length:</strong> Row keys can get lengthy if many attributes are combined.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ordering:</strong> Carefully consider the order of attributes to optimize query patterns (put most selective attributes first).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A. Utilize serially generated integer values for row keys.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Causes hotspots as data is sequentially inserted, overloading specific nodes.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B. Employ a subset of the MD5 hash of the row contents for row key generation.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While hashing helps with distribution, it sacrifices human readability.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>D. Adopt Unix epoch-styled timestamps represented in milliseconds for row key generation.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Leads to hotspots if all writes happen within a similar time frame, and also sacrifices readability.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example of good rowkey design:</strong></p>\n<p>If you're storing product orders, a row key might look like:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. &lt;region&gt;_&lt;customerID&gt;_&lt;orderID&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Bigtable Schema Design:</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigtable/docs/schema-design\">https://cloud.google.com/bigtable/docs/schema-design</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Key Visualizer (for hotspot analysis):</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigtable/docs/keyvis-overview\">https://cloud.google.com/bigtable/docs/keyvis-overview</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e4q20":{"id":"e4q20","exam":4,"n":20,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>As you're developing a web application to function on a Google Cloud runtime, with the aim of allowing users to save files to their Drive without domain restrictions, how should you configure authentication for the Google Drive API?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Employ an OAuth Client ID along with delegated domain-wide authority.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize a service account empowered with delegated domain-wide authority.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Leverage a service account and request the https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file scope to generate a signed JSON Web Token (JWT).</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Opt for an OAuth Client ID and request the https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file scope to acquire an access token for each user.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>Here's why <strong>III is the most suitable approach with explanations on why the others fall short:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Opt for an OAuth Client ID and request the</strong><a href=\"https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file\"><strong>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file</strong></a><strong>scope to acquire an access token for each user.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>User-Centric Access:</strong> OAuth 2.0 is designed for granting applications access to a user's data on their behalf.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Fine-Grained Permissions:</strong> The <code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file</code> scope allows you to control permissions to the level of individual files, which seems appropriate for your web application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> Access tokens enable user-specific access and can be revoked as needed, enhancing security.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options aren't as ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Employ an OAuth Client ID along with delegated domain-wide authority.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Excessive Permissions:</strong> Domain-wide authority gives the app access to <em>all</em> user data within the Google Workspace domain. This is rarely needed and poses a significant security risk if the application is compromised.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Utilize a service account empowered with delegated domain-wide authority.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Identity Mismatch:</strong> Service accounts are best for server-to-server interactions. With user-driven file uploads to Drive, OAuth 2.0 is more aligned.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Leverage a service account and request the</strong><a href=\"https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file\"><strong>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file</strong></a><strong>scope to generate a signed JSON Web Token (JWT).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complexity:</strong> JWTs with service accounts are generally used for when a separate application needs to act on a user's behalf, adding complexity without offering clear advantage in this scenario.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>OAuth 2.0 for Web Applications:</strong> <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/identity/protocols/oauth2/web-server\">https://developers.google.com/identity/protocols/oauth2/web-server</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Drive API Scopes:</strong> <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/drive/api/v3/about-auth\">https://developers.google.com/drive/api/v3/about-auth</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l3"]},"e4q21":{"id":"e4q21","exam":4,"n":21,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>To swiftly assess Pub/Sub integration for each new build of a web application that publishes messages to Pub/Sub, how should local testing be configured?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Use gcloud to install the Pub/Sub emulator, initiating it with a valid Google Project ID. Configure the local application to use the emulator by exporting the PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST variable.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Enable the Pub/Sub API in the Google Cloud console and configure the local application to call pubsub.googleapis.com.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Install Cloud Code in the integrated development environment (IDE), enable Pub/Sub in the Cloud APIs against a valid Google Project ID, and configure the local application to call pubsub.googleapis.com.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Set the Pub/Sub endpoint to the emulator by executing 'gcloud config set api_endpoint_overrides/pubsub https://pubsubemulator.googleapis.com.com/' before starting the application.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Use gcloud to install the Pub/Sub emulator, initiating it with a valid Google Project ID. Configure the local application to use the emulator by exporting the PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST variable.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Emulator for Local Testing:</strong> The Pub/Sub emulator provides a local environment that mimics the production Pub/Sub service, allowing you to test your integration without interacting with the actual cloud service.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>gcloud for Setup:</strong> gcloud offers a convenient way to manage the installation and initiation of the emulator.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST:</strong> This environment variable ensures your local application correctly targets the emulator instead of the production service during development.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Enable the Pub/Sub API in the Google Cloud console and configure the local application to call pubsub.googleapis.com.</strong> This would connect your local application to the production Pub/Sub service, not ideal for isolated development and testing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Set the Pub/Sub endpoint to the emulator by executing 'gcloud config set api_endpoint_overrides/pubsub</strong><a href=\"https://pubsubemulator.googleapis.com.com/\"><strong>https://pubsubemulator.googleapis.com.com/</strong></a><strong>' before starting the application.</strong> This endpoint is not valid. The emulator runs locally, and you'd use the <code>PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST</code> to point to it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Install Cloud Code in the integrated development environment (IDE), enable Pub/Sub in the Cloud APIs against a valid Google Project ID, and configure the local application to call pubsub.googleapis.com.</strong> Cloud Code might provide some tools, but it unnecessarily adds IDE dependencies and, by default, would still connect to the production service.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/emulator\">https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/emulator</a></p>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e4q22":{"id":"e4q22","exam":4,"n":22,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>While testing your ecommerce web application, which employs Cloud Run and Memorystore for Redis, you encounter a 502 Bad Gateway error in the browser. Upon investigation, you discover that the application is failing to connect to Memorystore. What is the probable cause of this error?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. The firewall rule permitting a connection between Cloud Run and Memorystore was inadvertently removed during an infrastructure update by the DevOps team.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. You configured your application to utilize a Serverless VPC Access connector on a different subnet in a distinct region than your Cloud Run service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Your Memorystore for Redis instance was deployed without a public IP address.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. You configured your Serverless VPC Access connector in a different region than your Cloud Run service.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>Correct answer</p>\n<p><strong>II. You configured your Serverless VPC Access connector in a different region than your Cloud Run service.</strong></p>\n<p>I is not correct because Cloud Run connects to Memorystore via the Serverless VPC Connector. Connections are over private networks. Public addresses are not required.<br />\nII is correct. All of the components must be in the same region.<br />\nIII is not correct because for connectivity between Cloud Run and Memorystore all that is required is a Serverless VPN Connector.<br />\nIV is not correct. The Serverless VPC Connector is configured with a non-overlapping subnet that is not associated with the VPC.  </p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"%0d https:/cloud.google.com/memorystore/docs/redis/redis-overview#connecting\">https://cloud.google.com/memorystore/docs/redis/redis-overview#connecting</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"%0d https:/cloud.google.com/memorystore/docs/redis/connect-redis-instance-cloud-run%0d\">https://cloud.google.com/memorystore/docs/redis/connect-redis-instance-cloud-run</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"%0d https:/cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/configure-serverless-vpc-access#creating_a_connector\">https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/configure-serverless-vpc-access#creating_a_connector</a></p>","lessons":["d3l10"]},"e4q23":{"id":"e4q23","exam":4,"n":23,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You're deploying an application to a Compute Engine virtual machine instance equipped with the Stackdriver Monitoring Agent. The application, a Unix process, needs monitoring to alert if it hasn't run for at least 5 minutes. However, you can't modify the application to generate metrics or logs. Which alert condition is most appropriate for this scenario?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Configure process health monitoring to detect any issues with the Unix process.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize metric absence detection to alert when the Unix process hasn't been active for the specified duration.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Set up an uptime check to monitor the availability of the virtual machine instance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Establish a metric threshold to trigger an alert when the Unix process activity falls below the defined threshold.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>III. Utilize metric absence detection to alert when the Unix process hasn't been active for the specified duration.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<p>Metric absence detection perfectly aligns with the scenario where the application doesn't generate custom metrics or logs. Since most Unix processes utilize system resources, their activity often reflects in system-level metrics like CPU or memory usage. Metric absence allows you to configure an alert that triggers if these metrics haven't been reported for a certain timeframe (5 minutes in this case). This signifies that the process is likely inactive.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Supporting Documentation: <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/53095774/stackdriver-monitoring-metric-absent\">https://stackoverflow.com/questions/53095774/stackdriver-monitoring-metric-absent</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Set up an uptime check to monitor the availability of the virtual machine instance.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Justification: Uptime checks are effective for ensuring the entire VM is operational, but they don't provide insight into the status of individual processes running within the VM. A VM can be online while a specific process within it might be inactive.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Configure process health monitoring to detect any issues with the Unix process.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Justification: While this might seem like a suitable solution, Stackdriver Monitoring Agent has limitations when monitoring arbitrary Unix processes. It requires the application to send custom metrics for effective process health monitoring. In this scenario, the application doesn't emit such metrics.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Supporting Documentation: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/custom-metrics\">https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/custom-metrics</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Establish a metric threshold to trigger an alert when the Unix process activity falls below the defined threshold.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Justification: This approach could be viable if the Unix process has a metric explicitly indicating its activity level. However, if the only available metrics are system-level ones (CPU, memory), a complete drop to zero is a more reliable indicator of process inactivity compared to a specific low threshold.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Setting a threshold on these system metrics might trigger alerts due to normal process behavior (e.g., the process might sleep occasionally, causing a temporary dip in CPU usage).</p>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e4q24":{"id":"e4q24","exam":4,"n":24,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>In order to conduct near real-time analysis of audit activity logs captured in Cloud Logging, with multiple processes performing different types of analysis on the logging data, what approach should you adopt?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Develop an application to directly read the logs from the Cloud Logging API.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure a Cloud Logging sink to Pub/Sub, and retrieve the logs from a Pub/Sub topic.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Implement a Cloud Function endpoint to directly read from the Cloud Logging API and transfer the logs to Dataproc.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Establish a Cloud Logging sink to Cloud Storage and retrieve the logs from a Cloud Storage bucket.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The most appropriate approach to conduct near real-time analysis of audit activity logs captured in Cloud Logging, with multiple processes performing different types of analysis on the logging data, is:</p>\n<p><strong>B. Configure a Cloud Logging sink to Pub/Sub, and retrieve the logs from a Pub/Sub topic.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Option B involves configuring a Cloud Logging sink to export the logs to a Pub/Sub topic. This approach decouples the processing of logs from the logging infrastructure and allows multiple processes to subscribe to the Pub/Sub topic and perform different types of analysis on the logs in near real-time. Pub/Sub provides reliable message delivery and scales automatically to accommodate varying message throughput, making it suitable for handling large volumes of logs.</p>\n<p>Option A suggests developing an application to directly read the logs from the Cloud Logging API. While this approach is feasible, it requires managing the infrastructure for log retrieval and may not be as scalable or fault-tolerant as using Pub/Sub.</p>\n<p>Option C proposes implementing a Cloud Function endpoint to directly read from the Cloud Logging API and transfer the logs to Dataproc. While Cloud Functions can be used for log processing, transferring logs directly to Dataproc may introduce additional complexity and may not be the most efficient approach for real-time analysis.</p>\n<p>Option D suggests establishing a Cloud Logging sink to Cloud Storage and retrieving the logs from a Cloud Storage bucket. While Cloud Storage can be used to store logs for archival purposes, retrieving logs from Cloud Storage may not provide the near real-time processing capabilities required for real-time analysis.</p>\n<p>Therefore, option B is the most suitable approach for conducting near real-time analysis of audit activity logs captured in Cloud Logging with multiple processes performing different types of analysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Exporting Logs with Sinks: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/export/configure_export#configuring-log-sinks\">https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/export/configure_export#configuring-log-sinks</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l6","d4l10"]},"e4q25":{"id":"e4q25","exam":4,"n":25,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>How can you streamline the management effort required to transition from decomposing a monolithic application into microservices, especially when dealing with multiple instances that require read and write access to a configuration file stored on a shared file system, without necessitating the rewriting of the application code?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Opt for a new persistent disk, configuring it as a shared PersistentVolume to fulfill the shared file system requirement.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Introduce a new ConfigMap along with volumeMount to efficiently store and access the contents of the configuration file.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Deploy a new Filestore instance and link the volume as an NFS PersistentVolume for seamless access to the configuration file.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize a Cloud Storage bucket, establishing it as a mounted resource within the container using FUSE.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The recommended approach is:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Deploy a new Filestore instance and link the volume as an NFS PersistentVolume for seamless access to the configuration file.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Shared File System:</strong> Filestore offers a managed NFS service, creating a shared file system accessible by multiple container instances, perfectly suited for your scenario. (<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/filestore/docs/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/filestore/docs/overview</a>)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Performance and Scalability:</strong> NFS offers better performance and can scale efficiently for multiple concurrent file accesses compared to FUSE-based workarounds on Cloud Storage. (<a href=\"https://www.linuxtechi.com/configure-nfs-persistent-volume-kubernetes/\">https://www.linuxtechi.com/configure-nfs-persistent-volume-kubernetes/</a>)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>No Code Changes:</strong> This approach avoids the need to rewrite application code, minimizing the transition effort.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Considerations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cost:</strong> While convenient, Filestore can be more expensive than object storage solutions. Evaluate your budget and expected usage patterns.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Management Overhead:</strong> Introducing a new service like Filestore adds a small layer of additional management complexity.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Utilize a Cloud Storage bucket, establishing it as a mounted resource within the container using FUSE.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Performance Limitations:</strong> FUSE-based mounts on Cloud Storage can introduce performance overhead and latency, especially with frequent reads and writes compared to native file systems like NFS. (<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/gcs-fuse\">https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/gcs-fuse</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Opt for a new persistent disk, configuring it as a shared PersistentVolume to fulfill the shared file system requirement.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complexity and Single Point of Failure:</strong> Shared PersistentVolumes often involve complex setups like ReadWriteMany volumes in Kubernetes. Additionally, a single disk failure would impact all instances accessing the configuration file. (<a href=\"https://www.shebanglabs.io/how-to-set-up-read-write-many-rwx-persistent-volumes-with-nfs-on-kubernetes/\">https://www.shebanglabs.io/how-to-set-up-read-write-many-rwx-persistent-volumes-with-nfs-on-kubernetes/</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Introduce a new ConfigMap along with volumeMount to efficiently store and access the contents of the configuration file.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Limited Use Case:</strong> ConfigMaps are well-suited for storing configuration data within a Kubernetes cluster, but they are not designed for concurrent modification by multiple containers, which is a key requirement for your shared configuration file. (<a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/configmap/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/configmap/</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e4q26":{"id":"e4q26","exam":4,"n":26,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>In the context of an application monitoring numerous stock prices, how should the Pub/Sub subscription be configured to manage a high volume of messages and update the current stock price in a single large in-memory database, considering each message includes the stock symbol, price, and timestamp?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Establish a pull subscription with both ordering and exactly-once delivery disabled.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Create a push subscription with both ordering and exactly-once delivery disabled.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Set up a pull subscription with exactly-once delivery enabled.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Configure a push subscription with exactly-once delivery enabled.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Given the context of an application monitoring numerous stock prices and updating the current stock price in a single large in-memory database, considering each message includes the stock symbol, price, and timestamp, the most suitable configuration for the Pub/Sub subscription would be:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Configure a push subscription with exactly-once delivery enabled.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Push Subscription</strong> : Push subscriptions are generally preferred for high-volume message processing because they can handle a large number of incoming messages more efficiently than pull subscriptions. Push subscriptions allow Google Cloud Pub/Sub to push messages to the subscriber, reducing the need for the subscriber to continuously poll the Pub/Sub topic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Exactly-once Delivery Enabled</strong> : Ensuring exactly-once delivery helps prevent duplicate processing of messages, which is crucial for maintaining data consistency, especially in scenarios where updates to the stock prices need to be accurate and not duplicated. This ensures that each message is processed exactly once, avoiding potential issues with data integrity.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Option I is less suitable because pull subscriptions might not be as efficient for managing a high volume of messages, as they require the subscriber to actively pull messages from the Pub/Sub topic.</p>\n<p>Option II is not ideal because push subscriptions are generally preferred for high-volume message processing, and disabling exactly-once delivery could lead to potential issues with data consistency.</p>\n<p>Option IV might not be the most efficient choice for managing a high volume of messages, and disabling exactly-once delivery could lead to potential issues with data consistency.</p>\n<p><strong>Therefore, option III, configuring a push subscription with exactly-once delivery enabled, is the most appropriate choice for efficiently managing a high volume of messages and ensuring data consistency in updating the current stock prices.</strong></p>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e4q27":{"id":"e4q27","exam":4,"n":27,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>As part of an e-commerce company, you are designing a new Orders API to be hosted via Apigee. You created two environments in Apigee, orders-test and orders-prod, and plan to use unique URLs, test.lnk-42.com/api/v1/orders and lnk-42.com/api/v1/orders, for each environment. How can you ensure that each environment is only accessible via its designated URL?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>D. 1. Attach orders-test to the test environment group and orders-prod to the production environment group.<br />\n2. Add each hostname to the relevant environment group.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>A. 1. Attach orders-test and orders-prod to the orders environment group.<br />\n2. Add each hostname to the respective environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. 1. Attach orders-test to the test environment group and orders-prod to the production environment group.<br />\n2. Add each hostname to the appropriate environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. 1. Attach orders-test and orders-prod to the orders environment group.<br />\n2. Add each hostname to the orders environment group.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is <strong>D.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Attach</strong><code>**orders-test**</code><strong>to the test environment group and</strong><code>**orders-prod**</code><strong>to the production environment group.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Add each hostname to the relevant environment group.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>In <strong>Apigee</strong> , environment groups allow you to manage hostnames and route traffic based on environments. To ensure that each environment (<code>orders-test</code> and <code>orders-prod</code>) is only accessible via its designated URL, you need to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Attach</strong><code>**orders-test**</code><strong>to the test environment group</strong> and <code>orders-prod</code> to the production environment group.** This setup isolates the environments by their groups, so test environments and production environments don’t overlap in URL accessibility.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Add each hostname to the relevant environment group</strong> (e.g., <code>test.lnk-42.com</code> to the test environment group and <code>lnk-42.com</code> to the production environment group). By adding each hostname to its specific environment group, you ensure that each URL points only to the corresponding environment.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Attach both</strong><code>**orders-test**</code><strong>and</strong><code>**orders-prod**</code><strong>to the orders environment group</strong><br />\nAttaching both environments to a single environment group would make both URLs accessible within the same group, which would not isolate them as intended.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Add each hostname to the</strong><code>**orders**</code><strong>environment group</strong><br />\nLike Option A, using a single environment group does not separate access to test and production URLs, allowing possible cross-access between environments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Attach</strong><code>**orders-test**</code><strong>to the test environment group and</strong><code>**orders-prod**</code><strong>to the production environment group, then add each hostname to the appropriate environment</strong><br />\nAdding hostnames directly to environments, instead of environment groups, will not configure host-based access control correctly, as hostname bindings must be set at the environment group level.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more on Apigee environment groups and hostnames, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/fundamentals/environmentgroups-working-with\">Apigee Environment Groups</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l5"]},"e4q28":{"id":"e4q28","exam":4,"n":28,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>To align with your company's IAM tracking procedures and support rapid release cycles with minimal merge conflicts, how should you design a version control system?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Create a GitHub repository, mirror it to a Cloud Source Repositories repository, and implement trunk-based development.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Create a GitHub repository, mirror it to a Cloud Source Repositories repository, and employ feature-based development.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Establish a Cloud Source Repositories repository and implement trunk-based development.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Set up a Cloud Source Repositories repository and employ feature-based development.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Likely Best Solution:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Establish a Cloud Source Repositories repository and implement trunk-based development.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IAM Tracking:</strong> Cloud Source Repositories integrates well with Google Cloud's IAM auditing and logging, providing a clear audit trail for changes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reduced Merge Conflicts:</strong> Trunk-based development encourages small, frequent commits to the main branch, minimizing the likelihood of large, complex merge conflicts.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Consideration if GitHub is Mandated:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Create a GitHub repository, mirror it to a Cloud Source Repositories repository, and implement trunk-based development.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Workflow Familiarity:</strong> Allows developers to leverage GitHub if it's their preferred tool.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IAM Tracking:</strong> Mirroring to Cloud Source Repositories ensures IAM auditability within Google Cloud.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Potential Complexity:</strong> Maintaining synchronization between repositories introduces a slight overhead.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why the Feature Branch Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Set up a Cloud Source Repositories repository and employ feature-based development.</strong> Feature branches, while common, increase the potential for larger merge conflicts, especially in rapid release environments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Create a GitHub repository, mirror it to a Cloud Source Repositories repository, and employ feature-based development.</strong> Adds the complexity of mirroring on top of the merge conflict concerns with feature branches.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e4q29":{"id":"e4q29","exam":4,"n":29,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>How can you ensure that your Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster optimizes performance and minimizes cost by automatically adjusting the number of pods and nodes based on CPU load fluctuations?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Modify the MIG to enable Autoscaling to configure max and min amount of nodes based on CPU load, and configure the Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA) to scale workloads based on CPU load.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Modify the managed instance group (MIG) to enable Autoscaling to configure max and min amount of nodes based on CPU load.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Enable Cluster Autoscaler on the GKE cluster, and configure the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) to autoscale the workload based on CPU load.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Enable Cluster Autoscaler on the GKE cluster, and configure the HPA to autoscale the workloads based on a custom metric.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The best solution is</p>\n<p><strong>II. Enable Cluster Autoscaler on the GKE cluster, and configure the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) to autoscale the workload based on CPU load.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cluster Autoscaler:</strong> This is the essential component for scaling the number of nodes in a GKE cluster based on resource demands (<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/cluster-autoscaler\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/cluster-autoscaler</a>).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA):</strong> The HPA works in tandem with Cluster Autoscaler. It scales the number of pods in your deployments based on CPU utilization metrics (<a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale/</a>).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Modify the managed instance group (MIG) to enable Autoscaling to configure max and min amount of nodes based on CPU load.</strong> While MIG autoscaling helps, it does not directly manage the scaling of pods within your Kubernetes deployments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Enable Cluster Autoscaler on the GKE cluster, and configure the HPA to autoscale the workloads based on a custom metric.</strong> In most cases, CPU utilization is the ideal metric for horizontal pod scaling to match demand. Custom metrics might be needed in specific scenarios, but they introduce potential additional complexity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Modify the MIG to enable Autoscaling to configure max and min amount of nodes based on CPU load, and configure the Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA) to scale workloads based on CPU load.</strong> The VPA adjusts resource requests and limits for individual pods but doesn't directly increase or decrease the number of pods like the HPA.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e4q30":{"id":"e4q30","exam":4,"n":30,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>In order to deploy new features quickly while minimizing the risk of negatively impacting all users in a Cloud Run application with significant traffic volume, which approach should you choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Schedule weekly load tests against the production application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement traffic splitting to have a smaller portion of users test out new features, gradually adjusting traffic splitting until all users receive the new features.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize the local development environment to perform load testing outside Google Cloud.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Deploy new features as a new version, conduct smoke tests before allowing all users access, and then enable all users to access the new features.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The best approach for your scenario is</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Implement traffic splitting to have a smaller portion of users test out new features, gradually adjusting traffic splitting until all users receive the new features.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's a breakdown of why this is superior and the limitations of the other choices:</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Traffic Splitting:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Controlled Rollout:</strong> Allows you to expose new features to a subset of users, minimizing the blast radius of potential issues.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Monitoring and Feedback:</strong> You can monitor real-world performance and gather feedback from the initial rollout before exposing all users.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Gradual Increase:</strong> Traffic splitting allows you to carefully increase the exposure of new features, making it easier to identify and address problems before they become widespread.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Limitations of Other Approaches:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Schedule weekly load tests against the production application.</strong> Load testing is important but doesn't fully simulate real-world user behavior and edge cases that might arise with new features.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Utilize the local development environment to perform load testing outside of Google Cloud.</strong> Local environments rarely match the production environment's scale and complexity, making testing less reliable.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Deploy new features as a new version, conduct smoke tests before allowing all users access, and then enable all users to access the new features.</strong> While smoke testing is valuable, it lacks the phased rollout and user feedback that traffic splitting provides.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Traffic Splitting on Cloud Run</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run natively supports traffic splitting, allowing you to precisely control the percentage of traffic directed to different revisions of your service (<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-rollbacks-traffic-migration\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-rollbacks-traffic-migration</a>).</p>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e4q31":{"id":"e4q31","exam":4,"n":31,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You are developing a mobile app for creating and managing <strong>to-do lists</strong> with the following requirements:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Data storage and synchronization</strong> across multiple mobile devices.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Offline access</strong> capability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Real-time updates</strong> on each user’s device.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You need to select a database solution that minimizes operational effort. Which approach should you choose?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>D. Implement a SQLite database on each device and use a scheduled job to synchronize each device’s database with a copy stored in Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>A. Set up a Cloud SQL for MySQL instance, design a data model for storing to-do information, and create indexes for frequently used queries.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Set up a Bigtable instance, design the schema to avoid write hotspots, and use Bigtable change streams to capture data changes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use Firestore as the database, enable offline persistence to cache data locally, and listen for document changes to update the app in real-time.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is </p>\n<p><strong>C. Use Firestore as the database, enable offline persistence to cache data locally, and listen for document changes to update the app in real-time.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p><strong>Firestore</strong> is ideal for mobile apps with requirements for <strong>data storage, synchronization, offline access, and real-time updates</strong>. Firestore provides built-in features that address these needs with minimal operational effort:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Offline Persistence</strong> : Firestore supports offline data persistence, enabling users to access and update their data even when disconnected. Data is automatically synchronized with the cloud when the device reconnects.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Real-Time Updates</strong> : Firestore’s real-time listeners allow the app to receive updates whenever data changes, keeping multiple devices in sync without additional setup.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Automatic Scaling and Managed Service</strong> : Firestore is fully managed, eliminating the need to handle operational tasks like scaling, backups, and server management.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Set up a Cloud SQL for MySQL instance</strong><br />\nWhile Cloud SQL supports relational data, it lacks built-in offline persistence and real-time updates, both of which would require additional development work. Also, managing Cloud SQL would introduce operational overhead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Set up a Bigtable instance</strong><br />\n<strong>Bigtable</strong> is a NoSQL database designed for high-throughput analytics rather than real-time mobile app use cases. It doesn’t support offline access or real-time updates, making it less suited to this requirement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Implement a SQLite database on each device with synchronization via Cloud Storage</strong><br />\nUsing SQLite would require custom synchronization logic, and Cloud Storage is not designed for database-like synchronization or real-time updates. This approach would involve significant operational and development overhead to meet the requirements.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more on Firestore’s offline persistence and real-time capabilities, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://firebase.google.com/docs/firestore/manage-data/enable-offline\">Firestore Offline Persistence</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://firebase.google.com/docs/firestore/query-data/listen\">Firestore Real-Time Updates</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e4q32":{"id":"e4q32","exam":4,"n":32,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>In the process of developing an application on Cloud Run that relies on REST API calls to interact with Google Cloud services, and considering the absence of support for Cloud Client Libraries in the selected programming language, the goal is to configure the associated service account to serve as the authorization identity for these calls. What is the recommended approach to achieve this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Embed an API key within the application and transmit its value through the Authorization header.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize an API key associated with the service account by setting its value as the GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS environment variable.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Obtain the access token from the metadata server and transmit its value through the Authorization header.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. During startup, fetch and store the access token using gcloud auth application-default print-access-token, saving it in a file whose path is specified in the GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS environment variable.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>A breakdown of why </p>\n<p><strong>II (obtain the access token from the metadata server) is the best approach</strong> and why the others aren't as suitable:</p>\n<p><strong>II. Obtain the access token from the metadata server and transmit its value through the Authorization header.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> The metadata server provides a secure way to get short-lived access tokens, minimizing the risk of exposing long-term credentials.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Run Integration:</strong> Cloud Run automatically injects a service account into your container. Using the metadata server leverages this seamlessly.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ease of Use:</strong> This method is the standard way to authorize Google Cloud API calls from within Cloud Run.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options aren't as ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Embed an API key within the application and transmit its value through the Authorization header.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security Risk:</strong> Embedding keys directly in code is strongly discouraged. If code is compromised, the key is exposed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Management:</strong> API keys typically have more limited scope compared to service accounts.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Utilize an API key associated with the service account by setting its value as the GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS environment variable.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Same Risks as I:</strong> Embedding a key, even if it's an API Key associated with a service account, poses similar security risks.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. During startup, fetch and store the access token using gcloud auth application-default print-access-token...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Unnecessary Complexity:</strong> Requires external scripts running at startup and file management for storing the token.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Token Expiration:</strong> Access tokens expire; your application would need a mechanism to refresh the token. The metadata server handles all of this automatically.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Run service account and the Metadata Server:</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/metadata/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/metadata/overview</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Obtaining Access Tokens on Cloud Run:</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/authenticating/service-to-service\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/authenticating/service-to-service</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e4q33":{"id":"e4q33","exam":4,"n":33,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>To allow a Compute Engine instance without a public IP address to access the Cloud Storage API, what should you implement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Implement VPC Network Peering.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize Carrier Peering.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Deploy Shared VPC networks.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Enable Private Google Access.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>Here's the breakdown of why the correct answer is </p>\n<p><strong>IV – Enable Private Google Access</strong> and why the other options aren't the best fit.</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Enable Private Google Access</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Private Google Access allows VMs in a subnet to access Google APIs and services using internal IP addresses, even if they don't have external IPs. This is the exact functionality needed in this scenario.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>How it Works:</strong> It extends the internal IP address space of your VPC network to include Google's network. (<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/configure-private-google-access\">https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/configure-private-google-access</a>)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options aren't suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Utilize Carrier Peering</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carrier Peering facilitates direct network connections between your on-premises infrastructure and Google Cloud. It's unrelated to internal traffic within a VPC. (<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/network-connectivity/docs/carrier-peering\">https://cloud.google.com/network-connectivity/docs/carrier-peering</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Implement VPC Network Peering</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>VPC Network Peering connects two VPC networks privately for resource sharing. It doesn't directly solve the issue of a VM without an external IP accessing Google APIs. (<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/vpc-peering\">https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/vpc-peering</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Deploy Shared VPC Networks</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shared VPC enables centralized management of network resources across multiple projects. It doesn't inherently solve the issue of internal VMs reaching Google APIs without external IPs. (<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/shared-vpc\">https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/shared-vpc</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l11"]},"e4q34":{"id":"e4q34","exam":4,"n":34,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You are designing a Node.js-based mobile news feed application that will store data on Google Cloud. The database should provide zonal resiliency by default, low-latency responses, ACID compliance, optional middle-tier functionality, semi-structured data storage, and support for network-partition-tolerant and offline-mode client libraries. Which database should you choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>D. Set up BigQuery and use the BigQuery REST API in the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>A. Set up Firestore and use the Firestore client library in the application.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Set up Cloud SQL and use the Google Client Library for Cloud SQL in the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Set up Bigtable and use the Bigtable client in the application.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is</p>\n<p><strong>A. Set up Firestore and use the Firestore client library in the application</strong>.</p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p><strong>Firestore</strong> is a managed NoSQL document database on Google Cloud that meets the requirements specified for the mobile news feed application:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Zonal resiliency by default</strong> : Firestore offers high availability and resilience, automatically replicating data across multiple zones within a region.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Low-latency responses</strong> : Firestore provides low-latency access suitable for mobile applications with real-time data needs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>ACID compliance</strong> : Firestore transactions are ACID-compliant, ensuring data consistency.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Optional middle-tier functionality</strong> : Firestore can be accessed directly from client-side applications, allowing for direct client-server interactions without needing a middle-tier.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Semi-structured data storage</strong> : Firestore supports semi-structured data storage, allowing for flexible data models that can adapt to evolving data structures.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Network-partition-tolerant and offline-mode client libraries</strong> : Firestore’s client libraries offer offline mode, allowing apps to function when disconnected and synchronizing data when connectivity is restored.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Set up Bigtable and use the Bigtable client in the application</strong><br />\n<strong>Bigtable</strong> is a NoSQL wide-column database suitable for analytics and high-throughput use cases but does not support ACID transactions or offline-mode client libraries, making it unsuitable for this mobile application’s needs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Set up Cloud SQL and use the Google Client Library for Cloud SQL in the application</strong><br />\n<strong>Cloud SQL</strong> is a relational database service that provides ACID compliance and zonal resiliency, but it does not support offline mode for client libraries. It is also not optimized for semi-structured data or mobile application latency needs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Set up BigQuery and use the BigQuery REST API in the application</strong><br />\n<strong>BigQuery</strong> is designed for large-scale data analysis and is not suitable for transactional, low-latency, or semi-structured data storage needs for a mobile application.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more details, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/firestore/docs\">Firestore Documentation</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/storage?hl=en\">Google Cloud Storage Options</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e4q35":{"id":"e4q35","exam":4,"n":35,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>In deploying an application on a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster, it needs to connect to a legacy REST service spread across two GKE clusters in different regions. The objective is to establish a resilient connection with minimal steps and enable probe-based health checks on the legacy service via a separate port. How should the connection be configured? (Select two options.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>V. Configure the legacy service's firewall to allow health checks initiated from the Traffic Director control plane.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Configure the legacy service's firewall to allow health checks initiated from the sidecar proxy.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Implement a proxyless Traffic Director configuration for the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Utilize Traffic Director alongside a sidecar proxy to link the application to the service.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Configure the legacy service's firewall to allow health checks initiated from the application.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>To configure the connection between the application deployed on a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster and the legacy REST service spread across two GKE clusters in different regions, while enabling probe-based health checks on the legacy service via a separate port, the following options would be suitable:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Utilize Traffic Director alongside a sidecar proxy to link the application to the service.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Traffic Director can help manage traffic between different services and clusters. Using a sidecar proxy alongside Traffic Director can facilitate connectivity and resilience between the application and the legacy service.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>III. Configure the legacy service's firewall to allow health checks initiated from the sidecar proxy.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Since the health checks are initiated from the sidecar proxy, the firewall rules of the legacy service should allow these health checks to pass through.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Explanation for excluding other options:</p>\n<p>II. Implement a proxyless Traffic Director configuration for the application.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proxyless Traffic Director configuration might not provide the necessary flexibility and control required for connecting to the legacy service, especially when dealing with health checks on a separate port.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>IV. Configure the legacy service's firewall to allow health checks initiated from the application.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It's not typically recommended to directly initiate health checks from the application, as it may introduce complexities and potential issues with managing health checks separately from the networking infrastructure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>V. Configure the legacy service's firewall to allow health checks initiated from the Traffic Director control plane.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Health checks initiated from the Traffic Director control plane might not directly interact with the legacy service's firewall. The health checks are typically initiated from proxies or other agents deployed alongside the application, which then communicate with the legacy service. Therefore, configuring the firewall to allow health checks from the sidecar proxy (option III) is more appropriate.</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l11"]},"e4q36":{"id":"e4q36","exam":4,"n":36,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>In accordance with the principle of least privilege, which role should you assign to the service account linked to your application's instance, which communicates with Cloud SQL via Cloud SQL Auth Proxy on Google Kubernetes Engine?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Assign the Project Owner role.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Assign the Project Editor role.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Assign the Cloud SQL Client role.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Assign the Cloud SQL Editor role.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The correct answer is</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Assign the Cloud SQL Client role.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> This principle dictates that you should only grant the minimum necessary permissions for a task. The Cloud SQL Client role specifically grants the ability to connect to Cloud SQL instances.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> Assigning broader roles (Project Editor or Owner) opens up unnecessary permissions to a wide range of resources in the project, increasing potential attack vectors.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are not suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Project Editor:</strong> This role grants far-reaching permissions to edit most resources within a project, far exceeding what's required for simply connecting to Cloud SQL.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Project Owner:</strong> This role has full administrative control over the entire project, posing a high security risk if the service account is compromised.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Cloud SQL Editor:</strong> This role allows modification of Cloud SQL instances themselves. Unless your application specifically requires database modification capabilities, this role also grants excessive permissions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/understanding-roles  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/connect-instance-kubernetes#set_up_a_service_account  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/connect-instance-kubernetes#set_up_a_service_account\">https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/understanding-roles</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/understanding-roles  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/connect-instance-kubernetes#set_up_a_service_account  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/connect-instance-kubernetes#set_up_a_service_account\">https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/understanding-roles  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/connect-instance-kubernetes#set_up_a_service_account  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/connect-instance-kubernetes#set_up_a_service_account\">https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/connect-instance-kubernetes#set_up_a_service_account</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/understanding-roles  https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/connect-instance-kubernetes#set_up_a_service_account  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/connect-instance-kubernetes#set_up_a_service_account\">https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/connect-instance-kubernetes#set_up_a_service_account</a></p>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e4q37":{"id":"e4q37","exam":4,"n":37,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>As a cluster administrator overseeing Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), your organization's clusters are enrolled in a release channel. You require timely updates on pertinent events impacting your GKE clusters, including available upgrades and security bulletins. What is the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Establish an RSS subscription to receive a daily summary of the GKE release notes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Schedule a query against the google_cloud_release_notes BigQuery dataset to retrieve relevant information.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Set up cluster notifications to be dispatched to a Pub/Sub topic.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Query the GKE API to ascertain available versions.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Set up cluster notifications to be dispatched to a Pub/Sub topic.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Proactive Updates:</strong> GKE cluster notifications pushed to a Pub/Sub topic provide real-time alerts for important events, including upgrade availability and security notices.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flexibility:</strong> You can integrate Pub/Sub with various tools (e.g., email, Slack, webhooks) to tailor how you receive the critical notifications.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimizes Manual Effort:</strong> Avoids the need for manual queries or polling.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Schedule a query against the google_cloud_release_notes BigQuery dataset...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reactive:</strong> Regularly querying BigQuery is still a manual approach. While you'll get release information, it's less timely than Pub/Sub notifications.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Query the GKE API to ascertain available versions.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Focused Scope:</strong> Only provides information on available versions, missing out on other important security notices.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Establish an RSS subscription to receive a daily summary...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Delayed:</strong> Daily summaries may not be timely enough for critical security bulletins or essential upgrades.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/cluster-notifications\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/cluster-notifications</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/release-channels#channels\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/release-channels#channels</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/cluster-notifications\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/cluster-notifications</a></p>","lessons":["d3l11"]},"e4q38":{"id":"e4q38","exam":4,"n":38,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>As a lead developer, you need to recommend a development environment for an organization that recently integrated multiple Google Cloud services within VPC environments secured by VPC Service Controls and Private Service Connect endpoints. Developers use various operating systems, frameworks, and IDEs. Your recommendation should:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Enforce consistent security controls.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Access Google Cloud resources and applications within the VPC.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Allow installation of custom tools and utilities in the development environments.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What solution should you recommend?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>D. Use the Cloud Code extension with the organization’s IDEs and enable access to VPC services using Identity-Aware Proxy.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use Cloud Workstations with preconfigured base images, and for custom tools and utilities, use custom images that are rebuilt weekly.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Use the Cloud Code extension with the organization’s IDEs and configure Cloud VPN for VPC access.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>A. Use Cloud Workstations and allow developers to create custom images as needed.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>B. Use Cloud Workstations with preconfigured base images, and for custom tools and utilities, use custom images that are rebuilt weekly.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why B is the best choice:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Enforces Consistent Security Controls:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Preconfigured base images ensure that all development environments start with a standardized security baseline.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Weekly rebuilds guarantee that the latest security patches and configurations are applied.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Access to Google Cloud Resources and Applications within the VPC:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud Workstations provide a secure and isolated development environment that can access VPC resources and applications.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>VPC Service Controls and Private Service Connect ensure that only authorized resources can access the VPC.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Allows Installation of Custom Tools and Utilities:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Custom images enable developers to install specific tools and utilities tailored to their needs, while maintaining security standards.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Weekly rebuilds of custom images keep them up-to-date and secure.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are not as suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A. Cloud Workstations with Custom Images:</strong> While this option provides flexibility, it can lead to inconsistencies in security configurations if not managed carefully.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C. Cloud Code Extension with Cloud VPN:</strong> This option can be complex to set up and manage, and it may not provide the same level of security and isolation as Cloud Workstations.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>D. Cloud Code Extension with Identity-Aware Proxy:</strong> This option can be more complex to configure and manage than Cloud Workstations, and it may not provide the same level of security and isolation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By choosing Option B, the organization can balance security, flexibility, and developer productivity.</p>\n<p><strong>For more information, refer to the following documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud Workstations: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/workstations/docs\">https://cloud.google.com/workstations/docs</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>VPC Service Controls: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vpc-service-controls/docs\">https://cloud.google.com/vpc-service-controls/docs</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Private Service Connect: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/private-service-connect\">https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/private-service-connect</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e4q39":{"id":"e4q39","exam":4,"n":39,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>In your mobile web application where global users vote on popular topics, with a very high volume of votes expected during each 30-minute voting window for each topic, you need to capture and count votes within these intervals while also storing them for future analysis and reporting. How should you approach this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Save the votes to Memorystore, then utilize Cloud Functions to insert the counts into BigQuery, and visualize the results in Google Data Studio.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Publish the votes to Pub/Sub and use Cloud Functions to insert both the counts and votes into Cloud Storage, thereafter displaying the results in Google Data Studio.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement Firebase for user authentication, enabling the direct publication of votes to Firestore. Export the votes to a CSV file and import them into Sheets for reporting purposes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Publish the votes to Pub/Sub, and employ a Dataflow pipeline to insert both the counts and votes into BigQuery, subsequently visualizing the results in Google Data Studio.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>Given the high volume of votes expected during each voting window for each topic in your mobile web application, along with the need to capture, count, and store votes for future analysis and reporting, the most suitable approach would be:</p>\n<p><strong>II. Publish the votes to Pub/Sub, and employ a Dataflow pipeline to insert both the counts and votes into BigQuery, subsequently visualizing the results in Google Data Studio.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Option I suggests saving the votes to Memorystore and using Cloud Functions to insert counts into BigQuery. While this approach can work, using Memorystore might not be the most efficient solution for capturing and storing high volumes of votes, especially if real-time processing and analysis are required. Dataflow is better suited for processing large streams of data, making it a more appropriate choice.</p>\n<p>Option III proposes publishing votes to Pub/Sub and using Cloud Functions to insert both counts and votes into Cloud Storage. While this approach could work, Cloud Storage is primarily used for object storage rather than structured data storage like BigQuery. Additionally, Cloud Functions might not be the best choice for handling high-volume data processing and insertion.</p>\n<p>Option IV suggests using Firebase for user authentication and Firestore for storing votes. While Firebase and Firestore are suitable for real-time data synchronization and user authentication, exporting votes to a CSV file and importing them into Sheets for reporting purposes might not be the most efficient or scalable solution for large volumes of data.</p>\n<p>Option II is the recommended approach because it leverages Pub/Sub for handling high-volume data streams, Dataflow for processing and transforming data in real-time, and BigQuery for storing and analyzing structured data. Visualizing the results in Google Data Studio allows for easy and customizable reporting and visualization.</p>\n<p>Therefore, option II is the most appropriate approach for capturing, counting, and storing votes in your mobile web application while also facilitating future analysis and reporting.</p>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Pub/Sub: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Dataflow: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs\">https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>BigQuery: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs\">https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Google Data Studio: <a href=\"https://datastudio.google.com/\">https://datastudio.google.com/</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l10"]},"e4q40":{"id":"e4q40","exam":4,"n":40,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your application logs to Stackdriver, and you need to obtain the count of all requests made to endpoints starting with /api/alpha/. How can you achieve this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Export the logs to Cloud Pub/Sub and count the lines matching the pattern /api/alpha.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Create a Stackdriver counter metric for the path /api/alpha/.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Create a Stackdriver counter metric for the endpoint /api/alpha/*.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Export the logs to Cloud Storage and count the lines matching the pattern /api/alpha.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The most suitable approaches:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Create a Stackdriver counter metric for the endpoint /api/alpha/*.</strong> This is the most direct and efficient way. Steps involved:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>Go to Stackdriver Monitoring.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Create a custom counter metric.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Filter this metric with the HTTP request path <code>/api/alpha/*</code></p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why the other options aren't the best fit:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Create a Stackdriver counter metric for the path /api/alpha/.</strong> This is too specific. It won't count requests to sub-endpoints like <code>/api/alpha/resource1</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Export the logs to Cloud Storage and count the lines matching the pattern /api/alpha.</strong> This method works, but it's less efficient for real-time monitoring:</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Set up a logs export to Cloud Storage.</p>\n<p>Filter logs for lines containing <code>/api/alpha/*</code>.</p>\n<p>Use tools (like command-line tools or a script) to count the matching lines.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IV. Export the logs to Cloud Pub/Sub and count the lines matching the pattern /api/alpha.</strong> This adds unnecessary complexity. Pub/Sub is good for real-time message distribution, but not optimized for log analysis and counting.</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e4q41":{"id":"e4q41","exam":4,"n":41,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>What action should you take to ensure that consumers of your API endpoint are notified when a write request to Firestore in Datastore mode fails due to encountering an HTTP 5xx server error?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>B. Return an HTTP 406 Not Acceptable response.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>A. Return an HTTP 204 No Content response.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Return an HTTP 500 Internal Server Error response.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Retry the Datastore API with exponential backoff until Firestore returns an HTTP 2xx response.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>Here's why the best approach is a combination of </p>\n<p><strong>C. Return an HTTP 500 Internal Server Error response</strong> and <strong>D. Retry the Datastore API with exponential backoff until Firestore returns an HTTP 2xx response.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C. Return an HTTP 500 Internal Server Error response.</strong> This clearly signals to API consumers that a server-side error has occurred, indicating the client request was likely valid, but the server failed to process it correctly.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>D. Retry the Datastore API with exponential backoff until Firestore returns an HTTP 2xx response.</strong> Retries with exponential backoff (increasing wait times between attempts) help handle potential transient errors and improve the likelihood of successful writes. However, make sure to limit retries to prevent infinite loops.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A. Return an HTTP 204 No Content response.</strong> A 204 response implies success, which would be misleading in this case, preventing API consumers from taking corrective actions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B. Return an HTTP 406 Not Acceptable response.</strong> A 406 response typically indicates issues with content negotiation. It's not the most accurate representation of a server-side failure during a write request.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Important Considerations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Logging and Monitoring:</strong> Implement robust logging and monitoring to help pinpoint the root cause of the 5xx errors, allowing you to address them proactively.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Idempotency:</strong> If possible, design API operations to be idempotent (producing the same result with multiple executions) to make retries safer.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>User Feedback:</strong> If retry strategies fail after a reasonable number of attempts, provide informative error messages to the API user.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>HTTP Status Codes:</strong> <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Status\">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Status</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Exponential Backoff:</strong> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponential_backoff\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponential_backoff</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l9"]},"e4q42":{"id":"e4q42","exam":4,"n":42,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Which approach should you take to quickly gather data points and suggest improvements to your Tech Lead for optimizing the performance and cost-effectiveness of the web application after migrating it to Google Cloud?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Migrate the database to Cloud SQL. Then import the data into Firestore in Native mode.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Migrate the database to BigQuery. Then import the data into Firestore in Native mode.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Create a Compute Engine virtual machine instance in Google Cloud, and install PostgreSQL and MongoDB Server software. Migrate the database to the new PostgreSQL, and then migrate the data to MongoDB.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Migrate the database to Cloud SQL. Then import the data into Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>To quickly gather data points and suggest improvements to your Tech Lead for optimizing the performance and cost-effectiveness of the web application after migrating it to Google Cloud, the most appropriate approach would be:</p>\n<p><strong>III. Migrate the database to Cloud SQL. Then import the data into Firestore in Native mode.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Option I suggests migrating the database to Cloud SQL and then importing the data into Cloud Storage. While Cloud SQL can be a suitable choice for relational databases, importing data into Cloud Storage might not directly provide insights into optimizing performance and cost-effectiveness. Additionally, Cloud Storage is an object storage solution and may not be the best fit for database storage.</p>\n<p>Option II suggests migrating the database to BigQuery and then importing the data into Firestore in Native mode. BigQuery is a data warehouse solution and is typically used for analytical purposes rather than transactional databases. Firestore in Native mode is a NoSQL document database, and while it might be suitable for some use cases, it may not provide the necessary insights for optimizing performance and cost-effectiveness of a web application.</p>\n<p>Option IV suggests creating a Compute Engine virtual machine instance, installing PostgreSQL and MongoDB Server software, and migrating the database. This approach introduces unnecessary complexity and may not align with best practices for managing databases on Google Cloud. Managing databases on Compute Engine instances requires manual provisioning, scaling, and maintenance, which can increase operational overhead and costs.</p>\n<p>Option III is the most appropriate approach because it involves migrating the database to Cloud SQL, which is a fully managed relational database service on Google Cloud. This simplifies database management and allows you to quickly gather performance and cost-related metrics. Additionally, importing data into Firestore in Native mode can provide insights into optimizing performance and cost-effectiveness, especially if the application can benefit from Firestore's scalability and flexibility.</p>\n<p>Therefore, option III is the most suitable approach for quickly gathering data points and suggesting improvements for optimizing the performance and cost-effectiveness of the web application after migrating it to Google Cloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud SQL: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql\">https://cloud.google.com/sql</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Firestore: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/firestore\">https://cloud.google.com/firestore</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e4q43":{"id":"e4q43","exam":4,"n":43,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>Your company relies on a BigQuery data mart to furnish analytics insights to numerous employees. One user requires the ability to run jobs without impeding important workloads, showing no concern for job duration. Your goal is to fulfill this request while minimizing both company expenses and your own effort. What action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Permit the user to execute jobs during periods when critical workloads are not active, ensuring minimal disruption while utilizing existing resources efficiently.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Establish a separate project dedicated to the user, providing autonomy for job execution without impacting existing workloads.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Grant the user the job.user role within the current project, enabling them to submit jobs without unnecessary administrative overhead.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Instruct the user to execute the jobs as batch jobs, allowing for flexible scheduling and resource allocation.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I. Instruct the user to execute the jobs as batch jobs, allowing for flexible scheduling and resource allocation.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reasoning:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cost-effective and Efficient:</strong> Batch jobs in BigQuery are the ideal solution for this scenario. They are executed when resources are available, ensuring other workloads are not impacted. Batch jobs are also generally less expensive than on-demand queries.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimal Overhead:</strong> This option minimizes administrative effort, as batch jobs require no extra project setup or advanced permissions. It's a straightforward way to accommodate the user's needs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options and Why:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Establish a separate project dedicated to the user, providing autonomy for job execution without impacting existing workloads.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While isolating the user's jobs would work, creating and managing a separate project introduces unnecessary complexity and overhead.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Grant the user the job.user role within the current project, enabling them to submit jobs without unnecessary administrative overhead.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <code>job.user</code> role doesn't address the core problem of prioritizing workloads. Providing it won't guarantee that the user's jobs won't interfere with important tasks.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Permit the user to execute jobs during periods when critical workloads are not active, ensuring minimal disruption while utilizing existing resources efficiently.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This requires continuous monitoring and manual timing, making it inconvenient and prone to error. It doesn't leverage BigQuery's built-in resource management capabilities.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Supporting Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>BigQuery Batch Queries:</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/batch/docs/get-started\">https://cloud.google.com/batch/docs/get-started</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>BigQuery Pricing</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/pricing\">https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/pricing</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l10"]},"e4q44":{"id":"e4q44","exam":4,"n":44,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>In order to minimize operational overhead while using Cloud Run to write every Pub/Sub message to both a Cloud Storage object and a BigQuery table, which architecture should you implement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>D. Two topics, 2 subscriptions, 2 Cloud Run services: Two independent Pub/Sub topics are used. Each topic has its own subscription, and a Cloud Run service listens to each subscription. This configuration allows for handling completely separate message streams with independent processing.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>A. One topic, 1 subscription, 2 Cloud Run services: A single Pub/Sub topic publishes messages to one subscription. Two separate Cloud Run services can be configured to listen on that subscription and process the messages.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. One topic, 1 Cloud Run service, 1 push subscription: One Pub/Sub topic with a single subscription. A Cloud Run service receives messages via a push subscription, meaning Pub/Sub actively delivers messages to the service. Data can be written to BigQuery and then Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. One topic, 2 subscriptions, 2 Cloud Run services: Similar to A, but with two subscriptions. Each Cloud Run service listens to a different subscription, allowing for further message filtering or processing specialization.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>To minimize operational overhead while using Cloud Run to write every Pub/Sub message to both a Cloud Storage object and a BigQuery table, the most suitable architecture to implement is:</p>\n<p><strong>B. One topic, 2 subscriptions, 2 Cloud Run services: Similar to A, but with two subscriptions. Each Cloud Run service listens to a different subscription, allowing for further message filtering or processing specialization.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Option B involves setting up one Pub/Sub topic with two subscriptions. Each subscription can be configured to deliver messages to a separate Cloud Run service. This architecture allows for decoupling of message processing logic, enabling each Cloud Run service to handle a specific aspect of message processing (e.g., writing to Cloud Storage or BigQuery). It also provides flexibility for future enhancements or modifications to each processing pipeline independently.</p>\n<p>Option A, while simpler, may result in a single Cloud Run service handling all message processing tasks, leading to potential scalability and maintenance challenges as the system grows.</p>\n<p>Option C suggests using a single Cloud Run service with a push subscription, which may limit scalability and introduce complexity if the processing logic for writing to Cloud Storage and BigQuery needs to be separated or modified independently in the future.</p>\n<p>Option D involves using two independent Pub/Sub topics, each with its own subscription and Cloud Run service. This option may introduce unnecessary complexity unless there are distinct message streams that require separate processing pipelines.</p>\n<p>Therefore, option B is the most suitable architecture to minimize operational overhead while using Cloud Run to write every Pub/Sub message to both a Cloud Storage object and a BigQuery table.</p>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud Run: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run\">https://cloud.google.com/run</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Pub/Sub: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub\">https://cloud.google.com/pubsub</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e4q45":{"id":"e4q45","exam":4,"n":45,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>As a developer at a social media firm transitioning from MySQL to Firestore on Google Cloud to manage user profiles and posts, how should you structure the Firestore collections?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Opt for a single root collection for user profiles and another for user posts.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Create a root collection for user posts, with subcollections dedicated to each user's profile.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Choose a single root collection for user profiles, and establish subcollections for each user's posts.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Implement a single root collection for user profiles, storing each user's posts as a nested list within their profile document.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Choose a single root collection for user profiles, and establish subcollections for each user's posts.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> Root-level collections scale better than large, nested documents. This accommodates potential growth in the number of users and their posts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Optimized Queries:</strong> Separating user profiles and posts facilitates efficient queries. You can easily fetch a user's profile without loading all their associated posts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Data Relationships:</strong> Subcollections establish a clear one-to-many relationship between users and their posts.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Opt for a single root collection for user profiles and another for user posts.</strong> While workable, completely separate collections might make it harder to fetch a user's profile and posts together when needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Implement a single root collection for user profiles, storing each user's posts as a nested list within their profile document.</strong> This risks hitting the 1 MB document size limit in Firestore if users have many posts. It also hinders efficient querying for posts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Create a root collection for user posts, with subcollections dedicated to each user's profile.</strong> This structure prioritizes posts over users, which might not be optimal for a social media application where the user is the central entity.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e4q46":{"id":"e4q46","exam":4,"n":46,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>To troubleshoot the performance difference between your locally tested application and its deployed version on a Compute Engine instance with minimal adjustments, what approach is recommended?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Utilize Cloud Debugger snapshots to examine a specific moment in the application's execution.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Integrate logging commands into the application and use Cloud Logging to identify areas of latency.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Leverage Cloud Profiler to identify the functions within the application that consume the most time.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. File a ticket with Cloud Support to report the performance gap between local and deployed environments.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The recommended approach for troubleshooting the performance difference between a locally tested application and its deployed version on a Compute Engine instance with minimal adjustments is:</p>\n<p><strong>III. Leverage Cloud Profiler to identify the functions within the application that consume the most time.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Identifying Performance Bottlenecks</strong> : Cloud Profiler is a powerful tool designed specifically for analyzing the performance of applications running on Google Cloud Platform. It provides detailed insights into which functions or methods within your application are consuming the most time, helping you identify performance bottlenecks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimal Adjustments</strong> : Cloud Profiler requires minimal adjustments to your application code or deployment configuration. Once enabled, it automatically profiles your application's performance without requiring significant modifications.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Options I, II, and IV are not the most suitable for this scenario:</p>\n<p>I. Filing a ticket with Cloud Support may not be the most efficient or effective approach for troubleshooting performance differences, especially when there are dedicated tools available for performance analysis like Cloud Profiler. Support tickets are typically reserved for addressing technical issues or inquiries that cannot be resolved through self-service methods.</p>\n<p>II. While Cloud Debugger can be useful for examining specific moments in the application's execution, it may not provide comprehensive insights into overall performance differences between the local and deployed environments. Cloud Debugger is more focused on debugging specific issues or errors rather than performance analysis.</p>\n<p>IV. Integrating logging commands into the application and using Cloud Logging can help identify areas of latency, but it may not provide the detailed insights into performance bottlenecks that Cloud Profiler offers. Logging can be useful for capturing information about application behavior, but it may not be as effective for pinpointing specific performance issues within the codebase.</p>\n<p>Therefore, leveraging Cloud Profiler to identify the functions within the application that consume the most time is the recommended approach for troubleshooting performance differences between a locally tested application and its deployed version on a Compute Engine instance with minimal </p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/\">https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/</a></p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e4q47":{"id":"e4q47","exam":4,"n":47,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>As a developer within a large organization utilizing Git for source code management (SCM), you aim to align your team with Google-recommended best practices to enhance software delivery efficiency. Which SCM process should your team adopt?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Each developer creates a branch for their individual work, commits their changes to their branch, and merges their code into the main branch on a daily basis.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Groups of developers create a feature branch from the main branch for their work, commit their changes to their branch, and merge their code into the main branch following approval from the change advisory board.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Groups of developers copy the repository, commit their changes to their respective repositories, and merge their code into the main repository before each product release.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Developers commit their code to the main branch before each product release, conduct testing, and initiate rollbacks if integration issues arise.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IV. Groups of developers create a feature branch from the main branch for their work, commit their changes to their branch, and merge their code into the main branch following approval from the change advisory board.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Feature Branch Workflow:</strong> This approach is the core of GitFlow (and similar branching strategies), a widely-adopted model that aligns with Google's best SCM practices. Feature branches promote collaboration while isolating changes, reducing the risk of conflicts and errors in the main branch.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Change Approval:</strong> Having a change advisory board ensures code quality and adherence to standards before integrating features into the main branch.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Collaboration and Isolation:</strong> This process fosters teamwork while keeping unreviewed code safely out of the main branch, aiding quality control.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Not Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Developers commit their code to the main branch...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High Risk:</strong> Directly committing to the main branch increases the likelihood of introducing errors and breaking the build.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Groups of developers copy the repository...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Inefficient Merging:</strong> Merging changes from separate repositories adds complexity and increases the risk of conflicts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Coordination Overhead:</strong> Maintaining multiple repositories for a single project demands extra coordination.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Each developer creates a branch...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Code Review:</strong> Lacks a formal code review process, which could lead to quality issues slipping into the main branch.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e4q48":{"id":"e4q48","exam":4,"n":48,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You’re preparing to load test a Cloud Run service using JMeter and need to orchestrate the process and tools for effective testing and analysis. You aim to follow Google’s recommended practices. What steps should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>D.</strong> Set up a <strong>Compute Engine</strong> instance, install <strong>JMeter</strong> on the instance, create a log sink to <strong>BigQuery</strong> , and use <strong>Looker Studio</strong> for analysis.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p><strong>A.</strong> Install <strong>JMeter</strong> on your local machine, set up a log sink to <strong>BigQuery</strong> , and use <strong>Looker</strong> to analyze the test results.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C.</strong> Set up a <strong>Compute Engine</strong> instance, install <strong>JMeter</strong> on the instance, create a log sink to a <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> bucket, and use <strong>Looker</strong> for analysis.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>B.</strong> Set up a <strong>Compute Engine</strong> instance, install <strong>JMeter</strong> on the instance, create a log sink to a <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> bucket, and use <strong>Looker Studio</strong> for analysis.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is </p>\n<p><strong>D. Set up a Compute Engine instance, install JMeter on the instance, create a log sink to BigQuery, and use Looker Studio for analysis.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Following Google’s recommended practices for load testing a <strong>Cloud Run</strong> service involves the following key steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Use a dedicated Compute Engine instance for JMeter</strong> : Running JMeter on a Compute Engine instance allows you to scale your load tests independently of your local resources, ensuring consistent and high-performance load testing without impacting your local network or machine.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Create a log sink to BigQuery</strong> : BigQuery is designed to handle large volumes of log data efficiently, making it ideal for storing and querying log data generated during load testing. Using BigQuery allows for detailed analysis, flexible querying, and seamless integration with other Google Cloud tools.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Use Looker Studio for analysis</strong> : Looker Studio (formerly known as Data Studio) provides a powerful and user-friendly platform for visualizing BigQuery data. By connecting Looker Studio to BigQuery, you can create interactive reports and dashboards to analyze test results and gain insights into performance trends and bottlenecks.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Install JMeter on your local machine, set up a log sink to BigQuery, and use Looker to analyze the test results</strong><br />\nRunning JMeter on a local machine is not recommended for load testing at scale. Using a Compute Engine instance provides more consistent resources and scalability. Additionally, Google recommends using <strong>Looker Studio</strong> rather than Looker for direct integration with BigQuery for reporting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Set up a Compute Engine instance, install JMeter, create a log sink to a Cloud Storage bucket, and use Looker Studio for analysis</strong><br />\nStoring logs in <strong>BigQuery</strong> is preferred over Cloud Storage because BigQuery offers efficient querying and analysis capabilities, which are essential for analyzing large volumes of load test data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Set up a Compute Engine instance, install JMeter, create a log sink to Cloud Storage, and use Looker for analysis</strong><br />\nThis approach is incorrect due to both the use of <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> (instead of BigQuery) for logs and <strong>Looker</strong> (instead of Looker Studio) for analysis. BigQuery and Looker Studio are better suited for handling and visualizing large-scale load testing data.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e4q49":{"id":"e4q49","exam":4,"n":49,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>In your Google Cloud project, you're defining system tests for an application hosted in Cloud Run. You need to establish a testing environment that's separate from the production environment. Your goal is to fully automate the creation of this testing environment with minimal effort and execute automated tests. What is the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Utilize Cloud Build to execute Terraform scripts, thereby creating a new Google Cloud project and deploying a Cloud Run instance of your application within it.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize Cloud Build to execute gcloud commands, facilitating the creation of a new Google Cloud project and deploying a Cloud Run instance of your application within it.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Employ Cloud Build to execute a Terraform script, deploying a new Cloud Run revision within the existing Google Cloud project. Implement traffic splitting to direct traffic to your test environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Employ Cloud Build to execute gcloud commands, deploying a new Cloud Run revision within the existing Google Cloud project. Implement traffic splitting to direct traffic to your test environment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I. Utilize Cloud Build to execute Terraform scripts, thereby creating a new Google Cloud project and deploying a Cloud Run instance of your application within it.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Isolation:</strong> Creating a separate Google Cloud project establishes the strongest level of isolation, guaranteeing that production resources remain unaffected by your system tests.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Automation:</strong> Terraform enables infrastructure-as-code, allowing you to fully define your test environment's resources in a declarative way, ensuring consistency and easy replication.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Build Integration:</strong> Cloud Build seamlessly integrates with Terraform for executing your scripts, automating environment setup.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimizing Production Impact:</strong> Deploying your application in a separate project ensures your system tests won't affect your production environment's stability.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Employ Cloud Build to execute a Terraform script, deploying a new Cloud Run revision...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lack of Isolation:</strong> Deploying a new revision within your production project still carries the risk of affecting the production environment if tests have unintended side effects.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Utilize Cloud Build to execute gcloud commands...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Less Structured:</strong> <code>gcloud</code> commands offer less declarative resource definition than Terraform, potentially making the test environment more difficult to replicate and prone to manual configuration errors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Employ Cloud Build to execute gcloud commands, deploying a new Cloud Run revision...</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Shares the challenges of options II and III, with less structured configuration and lesser isolation.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/best-practices-for-terraform\">https://cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/best-practices-for-terraform</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/resource-management/managing-infrastructure-as-code\">https://cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/resource-management/managing-infrastructure-as-code</a></p>","lessons":["d3l8"]},"e4q50":{"id":"e4q50","exam":4,"n":50,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>To effectively manage a significant surge in traffic and ensure efficient scalability during the redesign of the audit event ingestion from the authentication service, currently sharing a Compute Engine virtual machine with the audit service, you plan to separate them into their own pools of Compute Engine VM instances and utilize Pub/Sub for event transmission. How should you configure the Pub/Sub topics and subscriptions?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>C. Implement a single Pub/Sub topic and create a push subscription.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>A. Establish a single Pub/Sub topic and set up a single pull subscription.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Set up a single Pub/Sub topic and create a pull subscription for each instance of the audit service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. Set up a Pub/Sub topic for each instance of the authentication service and create a push subscription for each topic.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a Pub/Sub topic for each instance of the authentication service and set up a pull subscription for each topic.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong><em>A</em> is correct. This is the most flexible way to scale, allowing the authentication and audit services to be sized independently according to load.</strong></p>\n<p>B is incorrect. This will cause messages to be duplicated, one copy per subscription.<br />\nC is incorrect. This will allow the system to scale, but push subscriptions are less suited to handle large volumes of messages.<br />\nD is incorrect. This will allow the system to scale, however each audit service will listen to all subscriptions.<br />\nE. is incorrect. This will allow the system to scale, however it will require each audit service to listen to all subscriptions. Also push subscriptions are less suited to handle large volumes of messages.</p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"%0d https:/cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/subscriber#push-subscription\">https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/subscriber#push-subscription</a></p>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e4q51":{"id":"e4q51","exam":4,"n":51,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>What is the recommended method for deploying a microservice developed in C++ with custom software libraries for a Google Cloud-hosted application, ensuring minimal infrastructure management?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Utilize Cloud Functions for deploying the microservice.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Employ Cloud Build for container creation and deployment on Cloud Run.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize Cloud Shell for containerizing the microservice and deploy it on a Container-Optimized OS Compute Engine instance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Leverage Cloud Shell for containerization and deployment on standard Google Kubernetes Engine.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Employ Cloud Build for container creation and deployment on Cloud Run.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Run</strong> is a fully managed serverless platform perfect for deploying stateless containerized applications. It handles scaling and infrastructure management for you.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Build</strong> is a service to streamline building and deploying containers. This aligns well for creating the container image of your C++ microservice.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Utilize Cloud Functions for deploying the microservice.</strong> Cloud Functions are best for short-lived, event-driven tasks rather than continuously running microservices.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Utilize Cloud Shell for containerizing the microservice and deploy it on a Container-Optimized OS Compute Engine instance.</strong> While viable, it introduces more manual infrastructure management than Cloud Run, which defeats the purpose of minimizing overhead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Leverage Cloud Shell for containerization and deployment on standard Google Kubernetes Engine.</strong> Kubernetes is powerful but brings complexity. For a single microservice with minimal management preference, Cloud Run is simpler.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e4q52":{"id":"e4q52","exam":4,"n":52,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In an application utilizing Cloud Spanner as its primary datastore, how can you prevent performance regressions by running automated performance tests for each commit made via Cloud Build, ensuring concurrent test execution for multiple commits?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>IV. Initiate the Cloud Spanner emulator locally, load necessary data, and shut down the emulator after test execution.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Generate a new project with a random name for every build, load necessary data, and delete the project post-test execution.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Establish a new Cloud Spanner instance for each build, load the requisite data, and delete the Cloud Spanner instance after testing.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Set up a project with a Cloud Spanner instance containing the required data, and modify the Cloud Build build file to restore data to its initial state automatically post-test.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The <strong>correct answer is:</strong></p>\n<p>✅ <strong>II. Establish a new Cloud Spanner instance for each build, load the requisite data, and delete the Cloud Spanner instance after testing.</strong></p>\n<p>✅ <strong>Explanation for Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Create a new Cloud Spanner instance per build</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>This approach ensures <strong>isolation</strong> between test runs from different commits.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>It <strong>avoids shared-state conflicts</strong> , enables <strong>parallel execution</strong> , and provides a <strong>realistic performance environment</strong> , since you’re testing against <strong>actual Cloud Spanner infrastructure</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>You can use <strong>Cloud Build</strong> to script instance creation, schema/data loading, testing, and teardown.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Although it incurs cost, it’s optimal for <strong>realistic, scalable, and concurrent performance testing</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Official Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud Spanner performance testing guide: https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/performance</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud Build integration: https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/configuring-builds/run-bash-scripts</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Instance creation via gcloud: https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/instances</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>❌ <strong>Explanation for Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Generate a new project per build</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Creating a new GCP project for each build</strong> is <strong>time-consuming</strong> , involves <strong>org-level permissions</strong> , and <strong>slow resource provisioning</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>It is <strong>not recommended</strong> for fast, automated CI pipelines.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Project creation can also hit <strong>quota limits</strong> and requires IAM &amp; billing setup.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>III. Reuse a single Spanner instance</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Sharing a single Spanner instance</strong> across builds may lead to <strong>race conditions</strong> and <strong>non-deterministic test results</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Resetting data between tests introduces complexity and increases chances of <strong>state leakage or contention</strong> , particularly with concurrent builds.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IV. Use the Cloud Spanner emulator locally</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The <strong>Spanner emulator does not simulate performance characteristics of the real service</strong> (e.g., latency, transactions).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Good for functional unit tests, <strong>but not suitable for performance benchmarking</strong> or scalability tests.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\nhttps://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/emulator</p>\n<p>📌 <strong>Best Practice:</strong><br />\nUse <strong>Cloud Build</strong> to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Create a new Cloud Spanner instance.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Load schema and test data.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Run performance tests.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Delete the instance post-test.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This ensures <strong>concurrency, test isolation, and reliable performance metrics</strong>.</p>","lessons":["d2l2","d2l4","d2l6"]},"e4q53":{"id":"e4q53","exam":4,"n":53,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>As you monitor a Go-based web application deployed on Google Kubernetes Engine, you notice an uptick in CPU and memory usage. How can you pinpoint the functions consuming the most CPU and memory resources?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Implement OpenTelemetry and Trace export packages within the application and set up the trace provider. Analyze latency data for the application on the Trace overview page to identify functions causing the most latency.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Incorporate the Cloud Profiler package into the application and initialize the Profiler agent. Examine the generated flame graph in the Google Cloud console to identify functions with significant time consumption.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>I. Integrate print commands into the application's source code to log function calls, then redeploy the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Develop a Cloud Logging query to collect the application's logs. Utilize a Python script to compute the duration between timestamps of the longest functions, aiding in identifying time-intensive functions.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>To pinpoint the functions consuming the most CPU and memory resources in a Go-based web application deployed on Google Kubernetes Engine, the most effective approach would be:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Incorporate the Cloud Profiler package into the application and initialize the Profiler agent. Examine the generated flame graph in the Google Cloud console to identify functions with significant time consumption.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Here's why:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Profiler</strong> : Google Cloud Profiler is specifically designed for profiling applications running on Google Cloud Platform, including applications deployed on Google Kubernetes Engine. It collects CPU and memory allocation data with minimal overhead and provides detailed insights into the performance of the application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flame Graphs</strong> : Cloud Profiler generates flame graphs, which are visual representations of stack traces showing where CPU time is being spent. This allows you to easily identify the functions and code paths that are consuming the most CPU and memory resources.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Option I is less suitable because manually adding print commands to the source code and redeploying the application can be time-consuming and may not provide detailed insights into CPU and memory usage.</p>\n<p>Option II involves developing custom scripts to analyze application logs, which may not provide accurate or comprehensive data about CPU and memory usage compared to specialized profiling tools like Cloud Profiler.</p>\n<p>Option III suggests using OpenTelemetry and Trace export packages, which are primarily focused on distributed tracing for monitoring application latency and performance. While tracing can provide valuable insights into the performance of distributed systems, it may not provide the detailed CPU and memory profiling needed to identify specific functions causing resource consumption.</p>\n<p><strong>Therefore, option IV, incorporating the Cloud Profiler package into the application and examining the generated flame graph, is the most effective approach for pinpointing the functions consuming the most CPU and memory resources in the Go-based web application deployed on Google Kubernetes Engine.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://dev.to/billcchung/profiling-go-grpc-service-with-google-cloud-profiler-1k8l and   https://www.cncf.io/blog/2020/03/27/continuous-profiling-go-application-running-in-kubernetes/\">https://dev.to/billcchung/profiling-go-grpc-service-with-google-cloud-profiler-1k8l and https://www.cncf.io/blog/2020/03/27/continuous-profiling-go-application-running-in-kubernetes/</a></p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e4q54":{"id":"e4q54","exam":4,"n":54,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>As you're finalizing the migration of an on-premises data center to Google Cloud, you encounter a web API on a server set for decommissioning. The goal is to modernize this API efficiently while transitioning to Google Cloud, ensuring it's written in Python 3.x, capable of autoscaling during peak traffic, facilitating rapid deployment of new versions due to frequent code changes, all while minimizing costs and operational overhead. Which solution is recommended?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Modernize and deploy the code on App Engine flexible environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Modernize and deploy the code on Cloud Run.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Advise the development team to rewrite the application to operate as a container on Google Kubernetes Engine.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Deploy the modernized application to an n1-standard-1 Compute Engine instance.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Modernize and deploy the code on Cloud Run.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Serverless:</strong> Cloud Run is a fully managed serverless platform, eliminating the need to manage infrastructure and simplifying operations.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scaling:</strong> Cloud Run scales automatically based on traffic, including scaling down to zero when not in use, optimizing costs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Container Support:</strong> Cloud Run is ideal for containerized applications, which is likely how you'd package your modernized Python API.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Rapid Deployment:</strong> Cloud Run streamlines deployments, supporting frequent code updates.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options aren't as ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Modernize and deploy the code on App Engine flexible environment.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Operational Overhead:</strong> App Engine flexible, while supporting Python, still involves some management of virtual machine instances.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scaling:</strong> App Engine flexible's scaling model might be less dynamic compared to Cloud Run's true \"scale-to-zero\" feature.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Deploy the modernized application to an n1-standard-1 Compute Engine instance.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Management Burden:</strong> This is the most traditional approach, requiring you to manage the VM, OS, and application updates.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scaling Limitations:</strong> Manually scaling a single VM is less efficient than Cloud Run's automatic scaling.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Advise the development team to rewrite the application to operate as a container on Google Kubernetes Engine.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unnecessary Complexity:</strong> While GKE is powerful, it introduces a significant learning curve and operational complexity. Overkill for a simple modernized API unless you plan on having many services that would benefit from Kubernetes orchestration.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Run:</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run\">https://cloud.google.com/run</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>App Engine Flexible:</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/flexible\">https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/flexible</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE):</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l1"]},"e4q55":{"id":"e4q55","exam":4,"n":55,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>How can you ensure that error messages from your Java application appear in the Error Reporting console on Cloud Run?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Confirm that the Java application includes the necessary Cloud Monitoring client libraries.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Implement logging of exceptions using appropriate logging mechanisms rather than System.out.println.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Validate that application errors are being written to the standard error (stderr) stream.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Ensure that the application logs are correctly directed to the appropriate regional storage bucket.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>To ensure that error messages from your Java application appear in the Error Reporting console on Cloud Run, you should:</p>\n<p><strong>C. Validate that application errors are being written to the standard error (stderr) stream.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Cloud Run automatically captures logs from the standard output (stdout) and standard error (stderr) streams of your containerized application. When an application encounters an error, writing the error message to the stderr stream ensures that Cloud Run captures it and sends it to Cloud Logging. From there, errors can be analyzed and viewed in the Error Reporting console.</p>\n<p>Option A, confirming that the Java application includes the necessary Cloud Monitoring client libraries, is not directly related to ensuring error messages appear in the Error Reporting console. While Cloud Monitoring can provide additional monitoring and alerting capabilities, Error Reporting primarily relies on logs captured in Cloud Logging.</p>\n<p>Option B, ensuring that the application logs are correctly directed to the appropriate regional storage bucket, is not the recommended approach for Cloud Run. Cloud Run automatically manages logs and directs them to Cloud Logging, where they can be viewed and analyzed, including error messages.</p>\n<p>Option D, implementing logging of exceptions using appropriate logging mechanisms rather than System.out.println, is good practice for improving logging clarity and maintainability. However, it does not guarantee that errors will appear in the Error Reporting console unless they are written to stderr.</p>\n<p>Therefore, option C is the correct approach to ensure that error messages from your Java application appear in the Error Reporting console on Cloud Run.</p>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Error Reporting for Cloud Run</strong> : <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/error-reporting\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/error-reporting</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/error-reporting/docs/troubleshooting\">https://cloud.google.com/error-reporting/docs/troubleshooting</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l7"]},"e4q56":{"id":"e4q56","exam":4,"n":56,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You're developing a web application on a Compute Engine instance that writes files to users' Google Drive accounts. To enable authentication with the Google Drive API, what should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Implement an OAuth Client ID with the https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file scope to acquire an access token for each user.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Configure an OAuth Client ID with delegated domain-wide authority.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Employ the App Engine service account with delegated domain-wide authority.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize the App Engine service account along with the https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file scope to generate a signed JSON Web Token (JWT).</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I. Implement an OAuth Client ID with the</strong><a href=\"https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file\"><strong>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file</strong></a><strong>scope to acquire an access token for each user.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reasoning:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>User-Centric Authorization:</strong> OAuth 2.0 is the standard for allowing users to grant applications access to their data on other services. In this case, you need users to directly authorize your web application to access their Google Drive.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Appropriate Scope:</strong> The <code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file</code> scope grants the necessary permission to write files to the user's Drive while limiting access to only the required actions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options and Why:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Configure an OAuth Client ID with delegated domain-wide authority.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Domain-wide delegation is powerful and usually reserved for admin-like actions across an entire Google Workspace domain. It's not necessary for typical user-driven file writing.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Utilize the App Engine service account along with the</strong><a href=\"https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file\"><strong>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file</strong></a><strong>scope to generate a signed JSON Web Token (JWT).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While service accounts are useful for server-to-server communication, they don't offer the user-level authorization needed in this scenario.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Employ the App Engine service account with delegated domain-wide authority.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Like option II, this grants excessive administrative-level access, which is unnecessary and poses security risks.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Supporting Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>OAuth 2.0 for Web Server Applications:</strong> <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/identity/protocols/oauth2/web-server\">https://developers.google.com/identity/protocols/oauth2/web-server</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Drive API - Scopes:</strong> <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/drive/api/v3/about-auth\">https://developers.google.com/drive/api/v3/about-auth</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l3"]},"e4q57":{"id":"e4q57","exam":4,"n":57,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>For an application deployed on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), how should you set up alerts to be notified when the average memory consumption of containers dips below 20% or exceeds 80%?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>III. Create a scheduled Cloud Function to execute 'kubectl top' on all cluster workloads and send an email alert if the average memory consumption falls outside the specified bounds.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Design a script to gather memory consumption at the OS level and send an email alert if the average memory usage crosses the defined threshold.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Develop a Cloud Function leveraging the Monitoring API. Schedule it to run hourly and alert you if the average memory consumption strays from the specified range.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Within Cloud Monitoring, establish an alerting policy to notify you of any deviations in average memory consumption from the designated range.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The most suitable option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Within Cloud Monitoring, establish an alerting policy to notify you of any deviations in average memory consumption from the designated range.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Native Integration:</strong> Cloud Monitoring is tightly integrated with GKE, and designed specifically for metric-based alerting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficiency:</strong> You avoid the overhead of custom code and don't need to reinvent the wheel. Cloud Monitoring does the tracking and alerting for you.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Customization:</strong> Alerting policies offer flexibility in notification channels and escalation configurations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Develop a Cloud Function leveraging the Monitoring API. Schedule it to run hourly and alert you if the average memory consumption strays from the specified range.</strong> This is workable, but more complex than necessary. It introduces custom code and scheduling that Cloud Monitoring already handles.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Create a scheduled Cloud Function to execute 'kubectl top' on all cluster workloads and send an email alert if the average memory consumption falls outside the specified bounds.</strong> <code>kubectl top</code> is useful for spot checks, but not ideal for continuous monitoring. It's also less scalable than Cloud Monitoring's metrics collection.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Design a script to gather memory consumption at the OS level and send an email alert if the average memory usage crosses the defined threshold.</strong> This approach focuses on OS-level metrics rather than container-level metrics, which are more relevant for GKE. It also adds the overhead of script development and management.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring\">https://cloud.google.com/monitoring</a></p>","lessons":["d3l12"]},"e4q58":{"id":"e4q58","exam":4,"n":58,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You’ve deployed an application to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) where the Pods write data to a Compute Engine persistent disk. You’ve already created the PersistentVolume (PV) and PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) objects and referenced the PVC in your Deployment manifest.</p>\n<p>After expanding the persistent disk due to nearing full capacity, you log into one of the running Pods and notice that the increased disk space isn’t reflected inside the container’s file system.<br />\n<strong>What should you do to make the additional space available to the Pods?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>C. Modify the <code>spec.resources.requests.storage</code> field in the PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) to match the new disk size and apply the change using <code>kubectl</code>.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Use a tool like <code>fdisk</code> or <code>parted</code> inside the Pod to manually resize the partition to utilize the full disk.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Restart the application by deleting and reapplying the Deployment using the commands:<br />\n<code>kubectl delete deployment DEPLOYMENT_NAME &amp;&amp; kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml</code>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>A. Update the <code>spec.capacity.storage</code> field in the PersistentVolume (PV) configuration to reflect the new disk size, then apply the changes using <code>kubectl</code>.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is:<br />\n<strong>C. Modify the spec.resources.requests.storage field in the PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) to match the new disk size and apply the change using kubectl.</strong></p>\n<p>✅ <strong>Explanation of the Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p>✅ Option C:</p>\n<p><strong>\"Modify the spec.resources.requests.storage field in the PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) to match the new disk size and apply the change using kubectl.\"</strong></p>\n<p>When you <strong>resize a Compute Engine persistent disk</strong> , GKE does <strong>not automatically expand the file system inside the Pods</strong>. To propagate the change, you must also update the associated <strong>PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)</strong> with the new desired size.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Kubernetes supports <strong>expanding PVCs</strong> if the underlying StorageClass allows volume expansion (<code>allowVolumeExpansion: true</code>).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>After expanding the PVC, <strong>GKE will automatically resize the file system</strong> when the Pod is restarted or rescheduled.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Official Documentation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/storage-overview\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/storage-overview</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#expanding-persistent-volumes\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#expanding-persistent-volumes</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>❌ <strong>Why Other Options Are Incorrect:</strong></p>\n<p>❌ Option A:</p>\n<p><strong>\"Update the spec.capacity.storage field in the PersistentVolume (PV) configuration to reflect the new disk size.\"</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>You <strong>should never manually edit the PV object</strong> to reflect storage changes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>PVs are usually <strong>bound to PVCs</strong> , and their capacity is <strong>automatically managed</strong> by Kubernetes based on the associated PVC.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Editing the PV directly can cause inconsistencies and is <strong>not the supported method</strong> for resizing volumes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#persistentvolume\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#persistentvolume</a></p>\n<p>❌ Option B:</p>\n<p><strong>\"Restart the application by deleting and reapplying the Deployment.\"</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Restarting the Deployment <strong>does not trigger PVC resizing</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If the PVC’s <code>requests.storage</code> field hasn't been updated, <strong>the file system inside the Pod remains at the original size</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>❌ Option D:</p>\n<p><strong>\"Use a tool like fdisk or parted inside the Pod to manually resize the partition.\"</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>This is <strong>not recommended or supported</strong> in GKE.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Kubernetes abstracts storage management, and manual partition changes <strong>can lead to data corruption or unsupported states</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>✅ Summary:</p>\n<p>To reflect the increased disk size in the Pod’s file system, update the <strong>PVC’s storage request</strong>.</p>\n<p>📌 <strong>Correct Action:</strong><br />\nEdit the PVC and run: kubectl edit pvc YOUR_PVC_NAME</p>\n<p>Then update:</p>\n<p>spec:</p>\n<p>resources:</p>\n<p>requests:</p>\n<p>storage: 100Gi # Update to new size</p>\n<p>Kubernetes will handle the rest, especially if the StorageClass has <code>allowVolumeExpansion: true</code>.</p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e4q59":{"id":"e4q59","exam":4,"n":59,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>In order to ensure global consistency and high availability for the point trading feature in your multi-player game, which database option should your company select for migrating from the current MySQL backend?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>II. Cloud SQL</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Google Cloud Spanner</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Cloud Bigtable</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>I. Google BigQuery</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The best choice for your scenario is</p>\n<p><strong>III. Google Cloud Spanner.</strong> Here's why:</p>\n<p><strong>Cloud Spanner's Strengths:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Global Consistency:</strong> Spanner is specifically designed for globally distributed databases with strong consistency guarantees. This ensures that point balances for players are accurate and synchronized worldwide ([invalid URL removed]).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>High Availability:</strong> Spanner maintains exceptional uptime (99.999%) even for multi-region deployments, crucial for minimizing disruptions in a multi-player game ([invalid URL removed]).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> Spanner seamlessly scales to handle fluctuations in game traffic and an increasing number of players.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Relational Structure:</strong> Since you're migrating from MySQL, Spanner's familiar relational structure and SQL-like interface ease migration.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Google BigQuery:</strong> While excellent for data analytics, it's not suited for highly transactional, consistent, global applications like your game. (<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery\">https://cloud.google.com/bigquery</a>)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Cloud SQL:</strong> Provides managed MySQL instances but lacks Spanner's global consistency and extreme uptime guarantees. (<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql\">https://cloud.google.com/sql</a>)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Cloud Bigtable:</strong> Bigtable is a NoSQL wide-column store; its eventual consistency model might introduce discrepancies in point balances, and the different data model from MySQL complicates the migration. (<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigtable\">https://cloud.google.com/bigtable</a>)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l2","d1l4"]},"e6q1":{"id":"e6q1","exam":6,"n":1,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your ecommerce application is rapidly gaining new users, resulting in performance issues caused by a high volume of requests to your backend API. This API, which your team maintains, connects to a Cloud SQL database that is currently overwhelmed, leading to high latency and frequent timeouts. You need to find a solution to optimize API performance and enhance the user experience.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What approach should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use Apigee to expose your API, integrate Memorystore for Redis to cache frequently requested data, and implement exponential backoff in the application to retry failed requests.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Use Apigee to manage your API, apply rate limiting and access control policies in Apigee to regulate API traffic, and utilize Pub/Sub to queue requests and reduce database load.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Expose your API through Cloud Load Balancing, place Cloud CDN in front of the load balancer to cache responses, and incorporate exponential backoff for retrying failed requests.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use Cloud Load Balancing to expose your API, increase the database instance memory to support more concurrent requests, and build a custom rate-limiting mechanism within your application code to control API traffic.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The correct answer is</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Use Apigee to expose your API, integrate Memorystore for Redis to cache frequently requested data, and implement exponential backoff in the application to retry failed requests.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>This approach combines multiple techniques to optimize API performance and reduce load on your backend <strong>Cloud SQL database</strong> :</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Use Apigee to expose your API</strong> : Apigee provides API management capabilities such as traffic control, monitoring, and analytics, which can help manage high traffic more effectively. It also offers caching and rate limiting features, which can further protect your backend from being overwhelmed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Integrate Memorystore for Redis to cache frequently requested data</strong> : Adding Redis caching for commonly requested data offloads traffic from Cloud SQL by returning cached data for repeat requests, reducing latency and decreasing the number of direct calls to the database.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Implement exponential backoff for retrying failed requests</strong> : Exponential backoff allows the application to retry failed requests more gradually, helping to reduce repeated bursts of traffic to Cloud SQL and improving resiliency during peak loads.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Apigee with rate limiting and Pub/Sub for request queuing</strong><br />\nAlthough rate limiting helps control traffic, Pub/Sub is more suited for asynchronous message processing rather than synchronous API request handling. This setup would not solve the immediate need to reduce latency and improve response times for synchronous API calls.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Cloud Load Balancing with Cloud CDN and exponential backoff</strong><br />\nCloud CDN is suitable for caching static content but not ideal for caching dynamic API responses connected to a database. Additionally, without an in-memory cache like Redis, this setup lacks efficient backend caching for frequently accessed data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Cloud Load Balancing, increasing database memory, and custom rate limiting</strong><br />\nSimply increasing database memory may provide temporary relief but does not address the core issue of high database load. A custom rate-limiting mechanism would also add development overhead, whereas Apigee offers built-in rate-limiting capabilities.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l9"]},"e6q2":{"id":"e6q2","exam":6,"n":2,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You are tasked with deploying a new feature to production on Cloud Run. Your company’s SRE team requires that all deployments be gradual to minimize the risk of downtime due to potential code issues. You want to set up this deployment with minimal configuration effort.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up the application’s frontend load balancer to alternate traffic between the new and previous revisions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Modify the application code to route a small portion of users to the new revision.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy the feature with the “Serve this revision immediately” option turned off, configure the new revision to handle a small percentage of traffic, monitor for issues, and gradually increase traffic if no errors are found.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy the feature with “Serve this revision immediately” enabled. Check for any issues, roll back to the prior revision if errors occur, and repeat until the deployment is confirmed to be error-free.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is </p>\n<p><strong>C. Deploy the feature with the “Serve this revision immediately” option turned off, configure the new revision to handle a small percentage of traffic, monitor for issues, and gradually increase traffic if no errors are found.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Cloud Run supports <strong>traffic splitting</strong> between revisions, allowing you to roll out a new version gradually. By deploying the new feature with the <strong>“Serve this revision immediately”</strong> option turned off, you can initially direct a small percentage of traffic to the new revision. This strategy minimizes the risk of downtime because you can monitor for issues with a limited user base. If no issues are detected, you can gradually increase the traffic until 100% is routed to the new revision. This approach aligns with the SRE team’s requirement for a gradual rollout and minimizes configuration effort.</p>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Set up the load balancer to alternate traffic between revisions</strong><br />\nConfiguring a load balancer to alternate traffic is complex and unnecessary. Cloud Run’s built-in traffic splitting feature provides a simpler, more effective solution for gradual rollouts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Modify application code to route users to the new revision</strong><br />\nCode-based traffic routing adds unnecessary complexity and maintenance overhead. Cloud Run’s traffic splitting can handle this without code changes, providing a more efficient deployment strategy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Enable “Serve this revision immediately” and roll back if issues arise</strong><br />\nServing the revision immediately directs 100% of traffic to the new version, which doesn’t allow for a gradual rollout. This approach increases the risk of downtime if issues are detected only after deployment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more information, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/configuring/traffic-splitting\">Traffic Splitting in Cloud Run</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e6q3":{"id":"e6q3","exam":6,"n":3,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You are developing an external-facing application on GKE that provides a streaming API with two subscription tiers, “basic” and “premium.” Each tier limits the number of API requests allowed per client application per day. You want to design the application architecture to support these subscription tiers following Google-recommended practices. What approach should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A.</strong></p>\n<p>Set up the service on <strong>GKE</strong> as a backend to an <strong>Apigee proxy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Provide <strong>API keys</strong> to users to identify client applications.</p>\n<p>Configure a <strong>Quota policy</strong> in <strong>Apigee</strong> based on the subscription tier for each API key.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p><strong>B.</strong></p>\n<p>Set up the service on <strong>GKE</strong> as a backend to an <strong>Apigee proxy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Provide <strong>API keys</strong> to users to identify client applications.</p>\n<p>Configure a <strong>SpikeArrest policy</strong> in <strong>Apigee</strong> for API keys based on the subscription tier.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C.</strong></p>\n<p>Configure the service on <strong>GKE</strong> as a backend to two separate projects, each with its own <strong>Application Load Balancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>Set up quotas for <strong>“Queries per second (QPS) per region per network”</strong> for each project individually.</p>\n<p>Assign users API endpoints based on their subscription tier.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>D.</strong></p>\n<p>Deploy the application to two <strong>GKE clusters</strong> , one for each subscription tier, with separate <strong>Ingress</strong> configurations.</p>\n<p>Set up each cluster as a backend to an <strong>Apigee proxy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Provide <strong>API keys</strong> to users to identify client applications.</p>\n<p>Configure different rate limits based on the subscription tier.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The correct answer is A.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Using <strong>Apigee</strong> as a proxy for your GKE backend service provides a robust API management layer that can handle <strong>subscription-based quota policies</strong> effectively. The steps outlined in Option A align with best practices by allowing:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>API Keys for Client Identification</strong> : Apigee can issue and manage API keys to identify different client applications, making it easy to enforce access control based on user subscription tiers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Quota Policy</strong> : Apigee's <strong>Quota policy</strong> allows you to enforce daily limits on API requests based on subscription tier (e.g., “basic” and “premium”). This policy is ideal for managing the number of API requests per day, aligning with the requirement to limit daily requests based on subscription levels.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This approach is scalable, follows Google-recommended practices, and does not require duplicating infrastructure, which minimizes operational overhead.</p>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: SpikeArrest policy</strong><br />\nWhile <strong>SpikeArrest</strong> controls the rate of requests to prevent traffic spikes, it is not intended for managing daily quotas. SpikeArrest limits burst requests but does not provide daily quota enforcement, which is necessary for subscription tiers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Separate projects and Application Load Balancers</strong><br />\nConfiguring separate projects and load balancers adds unnecessary complexity. Using Apigee with a Quota policy is a more efficient way to manage subscription-based quotas without duplicating infrastructure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Separate GKE clusters for each subscription tier</strong><br />\nDeploying separate GKE clusters for each subscription tier is costly and complex. Apigee's Quota policy allows quota management per subscription tier without needing multiple clusters, making it a more scalable solution.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Relevant Documentation:</p>\n<p>For more information, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/reference/policies/quota-policy\">Apigee Quota Policy</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l5"]},"e6q4":{"id":"e6q4","exam":6,"n":4,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your organization has users and groups set up in an external identity provider (IdP). You want to allow all employees to access the Google Cloud console through this IdP and personalize the sign-in experience by showing each user’s name and photo. What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up workforce identity federation with the external IdP, and configure attribute mapping to display user information.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Create a service account for each employee using their name and photo, and grant each user permissions to impersonate their respective service accounts.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Set up workload identity federation to obtain tokens from the external IdP, then use these tokens to enable access to the Google Cloud console.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a Google group that includes all employees’ organization email addresses. Instruct users to use the same name, email, and password when registering and signing in.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is</p>\n<p><strong>A. Set up workforce identity federation with the external IdP, and configure attribute mapping to display user information.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p><strong>Workforce identity federation</strong> allows you to integrate an external identity provider (IdP) with Google Cloud, enabling users to authenticate through the external IdP to access the Google Cloud console. Workforce identity federation supports <strong>attribute mapping</strong> , which can be used to personalize the sign-in experience by displaying user details such as name and photo. This approach ensures secure, centralized identity management while providing a customized experience.</p>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Create a service account for each employee</strong><br />\nService accounts are intended for applications and workloads, not individual user access. Granting impersonation permissions for individual access is not secure and does not support user personalization.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Set up workload identity federation</strong><br />\n<strong>Workload identity federation</strong> is designed for applications and services running on external platforms to access Google Cloud resources, not for users accessing the Google Cloud console.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Create a Google group and instruct users to register with specific details</strong><br />\nCreating a Google group without proper identity federation does not integrate with the external IdP. This approach lacks centralized identity management and does not allow for attribute mapping or secure authentication through the IdP.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more information, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/workforce-identity-federation\">Workforce Identity Federation Overview</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e6q5":{"id":"e6q5","exam":6,"n":5,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You are developing a new API to create requests on an asynchronous message service, which will be consumed by different services. You want to use a gRPC interface to expose the API and minimize infrastructure management overhead. How should you deploy the API?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy the API to App Engine, create a Pub/Sub topic, and configure the API to publish messages to this topic.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy the API as a Cloud Run service, create a Pub/Sub topic, and configure the API to publish messages to this topic.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy the API to a GKE cluster, set up a Kafka cluster, and configure the API to send messages to this Kafka cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy the API on a Compute Engine instance, set up a Kafka cluster, and configure the API to send messages to this Kafka cluster.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is</p>\n<p><strong>B. Deploy the API as a Cloud Run service, create a Pub/Sub topic, and configure the API to publish messages to this topic.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p><strong>Cloud Run</strong> is a fully managed, serverless platform that supports <strong>gRPC</strong> and minimizes infrastructure management overhead, making it an ideal choice for deploying the API. By using <strong>Cloud Pub/Sub</strong> as the asynchronous message service, you can publish messages that will be consumed by different services, allowing for scalable, decoupled communication between the producer and consumer services.</p>\n<p>Key benefits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Serverless</strong> : Cloud Run handles scaling, load balancing, and infrastructure management automatically.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>gRPC Support</strong> : Cloud Run natively supports gRPC, enabling efficient API communication.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Pub/Sub Integration</strong> : Using Pub/Sub for message distribution provides high scalability, reliability, and minimal configuration.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: App Engine with Pub/Sub</strong><br />\nWhile App Engine is managed and supports Pub/Sub integration, it does not support gRPC natively. Cloud Run is a better choice for gRPC-based APIs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: GKE with Kafka</strong><br />\nGKE requires managing Kubernetes clusters, which increases infrastructure management overhead. Additionally, Kafka requires additional setup and maintenance compared to the serverless and fully managed Pub/Sub service.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Compute Engine with Kafka</strong><br />\nCompute Engine requires managing virtual machines, which adds infrastructure management overhead. Kafka also involves additional configuration and maintenance, making this option more complex than using Cloud Run with Pub/Sub.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more details, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/triggering/grpc\">Cloud Run and gRPC</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l1","d1l6"]},"e6q6":{"id":"e6q6","exam":6,"n":6,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You are preparing to deploy an application running on a Windows-based Compute Engine instance that connects to a Cloud SQL database. To establish this connection, you plan to use the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy while adhering to Google’s best practices and the principle of least privilege. You have already set up a custom service account.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What is your next step?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a custom role for the service account, granting only the cloudsql.instances.connect permission. Then, update the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy startup command with your instance connection name.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Assign the roles/cloudsql.client role to the custom service account. Modify the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy startup command to include the --unix-socket option.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Grant the custom service account the roles/cloudsql.editor role for broad permissions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Assign the roles/cloudsql.viewer role to the custom service account and adjust the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy command to specify your instance connection name.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is </p>\n<p><strong>A. Create a custom role for the service account, granting only the cloudsql.instances.connect permission. Then, update the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy startup command with your instance connection name.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>According to Google’s best practices and the <strong>principle of least privilege</strong> , it is recommended to create a custom role that grants only the permissions necessary for the task. For connecting to Cloud SQL using the <strong>Cloud SQL Auth Proxy</strong> , the <code>**cloudsql.instances.connect**</code> permission is sufficient to establish a secure connection to the Cloud SQL instance. By limiting the permissions to only what’s necessary, you enhance security by reducing the potential for unintended access.</p>\n<p>Additionally, updating the <strong>Cloud SQL Auth Proxy startup command</strong> with the instance connection name is essential to correctly establish the connection to your Cloud SQL instance.</p>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Assign the roles/cloudsql.client role with the --unix-socket option</strong><br />\nWhile the <code>roles/cloudsql.client</code> role includes the <code>cloudsql.instances.connect</code> permission, it also includes other permissions that may not be necessary, which doesn’t align with the principle of least privilege. The <code>--unix-socket</code> option is specific to UNIX-based systems and is not applicable to Windows.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Grant the roles/cloudsql.editor role</strong><br />\nThe <code>roles/cloudsql.editor</code> role provides broad permissions, including modifying database instances, which violates the principle of least privilege and poses a security risk.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Assign the roles/cloudsql.viewer role</strong><br />\nThe <code>roles/cloudsql.viewer</code> role allows read-only access to Cloud SQL resources but does not grant the <code>cloudsql.instances.connect</code> permission needed for the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy to establish a connection.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more information, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/sql-proxy\">Using the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e6q7":{"id":"e6q7","exam":6,"n":7,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You are building a secure platform for sharing documents, where users can share files with others, including individuals outside their organization. To maintain security, access to shared documents must expire after a customizable period. These documents are stored in Cloud Storage.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>How should you set up Cloud Storage to support this access expiration?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use signed policy documents on the Cloud Storage bucket to control access.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure access control lists (ACLs) on the Cloud Storage bucket to manage permissions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Generate a signed URL for each document that users want to share temporarily.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Assign the Storage Object Viewer IAM role to all authenticated users to allow document access.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is </p>\n<p><strong>C. Generate a signed URL for each document that users want to share temporarily.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p><strong>Signed URLs</strong> are the recommended method for providing <strong>temporary access</strong> to files in Cloud Storage. They allow you to generate a URL that grants time-limited access to a specific object, and the expiration time can be customized as needed. This approach provides a secure and controlled way to share documents with external users without altering bucket-level permissions.</p>\n<p>Key benefits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Customizable expiration</strong> : Signed URLs allow you to set an expiration period, ensuring access automatically expires after the specified time.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Granular access</strong> : Each signed URL is unique to a specific document, and access is limited to the document associated with the URL, maintaining security for other stored objects.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Use signed policy documents</strong><br />\nSigned policy documents provide temporary access but are primarily intended for uploading objects to Cloud Storage, not for controlling access to already stored objects.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Configure access control lists (ACLs)</strong><br />\nACLs allow fine-grained access control but do not provide time-bound access. Managing expiration through ACLs is complex and requires manual updates to remove permissions after the expiration period.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Assign the Storage Object Viewer IAM role to all authenticated users</strong><br />\nThis option would grant broad access to all authenticated users, which does not meet the security requirement of time-limited, controlled access. IAM roles are not suitable for temporary, user-specific access control.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more information, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/signed-urls\">Signed URLs for Cloud Storage</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e6q8":{"id":"e6q8","exam":6,"n":8,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You work for an environmental agency in a large metropolitan area, developing a new platform to monitor air quality. Thousands of sensors will capture air quality data across the city, and you want each device to send and receive data payloads to a RESTful backend every minute using a curl command. The backend is hosted in a single cloud region with Premium Tier networking. Your goal is to connect the devices to the backend in a way that minimizes the average daily latency, specifically focusing on reducing Time to First Byte (TTFB).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What setup should you use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. 1. Host the air quality backend on Compute Engine VMs.<br />\n2. Set up a weighted round-robin routing policy on Cloud DNS.<br />\n3. Configure the devices to connect using this DNS setup.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. 1. Host the air quality backend on Compute Engine VMs.<br />\n2. Create a round-robin routing policy on Cloud DNS for these VMs.<br />\n3. Configure the devices to connect using this DNS configuration.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. 1. Run the air quality backend in a managed instance group.<br />\n2. Set up an external passthrough Network Load Balancer linked to the managed instance group.<br />\n3. Configure the devices to connect through the Network Load Balancer.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. 1. Run the air quality backend in a managed instance group.<br />\n2. Set up an external Application Load Balancer and connect it to the managed instance group.<br />\n3. Configure the devices to connect through the Application Load Balancer.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is <strong>D.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>To achieve minimal latency and optimize <strong>Time to First Byte (TTFB)</strong> for a RESTful backend with thousands of devices, the best approach is to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Use an external Application Load Balancer</strong> : Google Cloud's external <strong>Application Load Balancer</strong> is built on the <strong>Premium Tier network</strong> , offering global load balancing with proximity-based routing. This setup reduces latency by routing each request to the backend instance closest to the device, minimizing TTFB.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Managed Instance Group</strong> : Using a managed instance group for the backend ensures scalability and resilience, as it can automatically scale based on demand and distribute traffic efficiently.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The Application Load Balancer also supports caching, SSL termination, and session affinity, which can improve performance and security for high-frequency, low-latency data exchanges.</p>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Compute Engine VMs with weighted round-robin routing in Cloud DNS</strong><br />\nCloud DNS round-robin routing lacks proximity-based routing and does not optimize for the closest regional endpoint, which is essential for reducing TTFB. This setup would likely result in higher latency.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Compute Engine VMs with round-robin Cloud DNS routing</strong><br />\nBasic round-robin DNS routing does not provide latency optimization or failover, and Cloud DNS does not route based on geographic proximity, making it unsuitable for minimizing TTFB across a large number of devices.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Network Load Balancer with a managed instance group</strong><br />\nThe Network Load Balancer operates at Layer 4 (TCP/UDP) and does not support HTTP(S) protocols natively. It also lacks advanced features like proximity-based routing, which is crucial for reducing TTFB for a geographically dispersed user base.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more information, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/https\">Google Cloud External Application Load Balancer</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l8"]},"e6q9":{"id":"e6q9","exam":6,"n":9,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>Your infrastructure team manages Compute Engine VMs and uses the Google Cloud console and gcloud CLI to provision resources for the development environment. To meet compliance requirements, all Compute Engine VMs must have the correct labels. You need a solution that automatically corrects any missing labels without altering the current provisioning process. You want this solution to be highly scalable.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up a trigger in Cloud Audit Logs to activate a Cloud Function whenever a Compute Engine VM is created. The function should check for missing labels and add them if needed.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Use Terraform to deploy resources and run the gcloud terraform vet command with a policy that ensures each Compute Engine VM provisioned by Terraform has the required labels.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a script that periodically checks all Compute Engine VMs for missing labels using Cloud Scheduler. Have the script apply labels where they are missing.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Manually review all Compute Engine VMs for missing labels on a regular basis and use the console to assign any necessary lab</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The correct answer is</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Set up a trigger in Cloud Audit Logs to activate a Cloud Function whenever a Compute Engine VM is created. The function should check for missing labels and add them if needed.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>This solution uses <strong>Cloud Audit Logs</strong> to trigger a <strong>Cloud Function</strong> automatically whenever a new Compute Engine VM is created. The function can be programmed to check for the required labels and add any that are missing, ensuring compliance without manual intervention. This approach is scalable and does not alter the current provisioning process, as it operates independently and responds in real-time to new VM creations.</p>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Use Terraform with</strong><code>**gcloud terraform vet**</code><br />\nWhile Terraform can manage resources with labels, this solution would require a complete shift to Terraform for provisioning. The <code>gcloud terraform vet</code> command is used to validate Terraform configurations but does not directly apply to existing resources provisioned via the Google Cloud Console or gcloud CLI. This approach would not work well with the existing provisioning process.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Use Cloud Scheduler to run a script periodically</strong><br />\nAlthough a scheduled script could check and apply labels, this solution is less efficient and scalable than a trigger-based approach. Periodic checks would introduce latency in correcting missing labels and increase resource usage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Manually review and assign labels</strong><br />\nManual reviews are inefficient, prone to human error, and do not scale effectively with large environments. Automated enforcement is preferred for compliance.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e6q10":{"id":"e6q10","exam":6,"n":10,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You are developing a discussion portal hosted on Cloud Run, where incoming requests pass through multiple microservices before a response is returned. Some of these microservices connect to databases. To optimize performance, you want to conduct a load test to identify any potential bottlenecks when the application is under heavy traffic. You plan to follow Google’s recommended practices.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Modify responses to include a time series showing the elapsed time for each service. Then, use Log Analytics in Cloud Logging to create a heatmap highlighting any bottleneck services.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B<strong>.</strong> Enable Cloud Trace to track requests from load testing clients and review request timings in Cloud Trace.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Expose latency metrics for each service with every request. Set up Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus to collect and analyze these metrics.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Add log statements that capture elapsed time for each service. Use BigQuery to analyze the logs and metrics.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The correct answer is</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Enable Cloud Trace to track requests from load testing clients and review request timings in Cloud Trace.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p><strong>Cloud Trace</strong> is a distributed tracing service designed for tracking request latencies across microservices, which is ideal for identifying bottlenecks in a complex application architecture like the discussion portal. By enabling Cloud Trace, you can capture latency data for each service in the request path, allowing you to pinpoint performance issues and evaluate how each microservice impacts overall response times. Cloud Trace is part of Google’s recommended practices for identifying latency bottlenecks in distributed systems.</p>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Modify responses to include a time series</strong><br />\nAdding time series data to responses would require substantial changes to response formatting and is not a scalable or efficient way to monitor service latency. Log Analytics is useful but would not offer as comprehensive a view as Cloud Trace for end-to-end request tracking.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Use Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus with latency metrics</strong><br />\nExposing latency metrics for each request can be helpful for ongoing monitoring, but Prometheus is less suited for detailed, end-to-end request tracing compared to Cloud Trace, especially in scenarios involving multiple microservices.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Capture elapsed time with log statements and analyze in BigQuery</strong><br />\nLogging elapsed times for each service can provide useful data, but it’s less efficient for real-time performance analysis across multiple services. Cloud Trace is specifically optimized for distributed tracing and offers more granular insights into request latency.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more information, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview\">Google Cloud Trace Overview</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l6","d2l9"]},"e6q11":{"id":"e6q11","exam":6,"n":11,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your team uses Bigtable as the backend database for your application. In the app profile, you observe that the Bigtable connection is set to single-cluster routing, with manual failover configured if the cluster becomes unavailable. You want to improve the application’s Bigtable connectivity to be more efficient and highly available.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Implement a Memcached layer so that queries are directed to the cache first, retrieving data from Bigtable only on a cache miss.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Increase the connection pool size in the Bigtable client to allow more simultaneous connections.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Set up a Dataflow template and use a Beam connector to stream data changes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Change the app profile to use multi-cluster routing for improved availability.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The correct answer is</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Change the app profile to use multi-cluster routing for improved availability.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>By changing the <strong>Bigtable app profile to use multi-cluster routing</strong> , you enable <strong>automatic failover</strong> and <strong>load balancing</strong> across multiple clusters, which enhances both the <strong>availability</strong> and <strong>resilience</strong> of the application. With multi-cluster routing, Bigtable can direct requests to any available cluster in the instance, automatically managing failover if a cluster becomes unavailable. This setup ensures seamless, highly available connections without requiring manual intervention.</p>\n<p><strong>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Implement a Memcached layer</strong><br />\nWhile caching with Memcached can improve read latency, it does not address high availability or failover needs for Bigtable connectivity. A cache does not eliminate the need for a more resilient connection strategy in the event of a cluster failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Increase the connection pool size in the Bigtable client</strong><br />\nIncreasing the connection pool size may help with throughput but does not impact availability or failover capability. It also does not improve resilience if a cluster becomes unavailable.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Set up a Dataflow template with a Beam connector</strong><br />\nUsing Dataflow with a Beam connector is suited for data processing and transformation but does not impact the availability or connectivity resilience of the Bigtable app profile. It is unrelated to Bigtable client connectivity and failover.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l4"]},"e6q12":{"id":"e6q12","exam":6,"n":12,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You work for an ecommerce company migrating several applications to Google Cloud, and you're involved in migrating one of these applications. The application currently runs on a VM without specific OS dependencies. You’ve created a Dockerfile and uploaded the resulting image to Artifact Registry. To reduce infrastructure management and operational complexity, you need an appropriate deployment option.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy the image to Cloud Run.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy the image to a GKE Autopilot cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy the image to a GKE Standard cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy the image on a Compute Engine instance.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is</p>\n<p><strong>A. Deploy the image to Cloud Run</strong>.</p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p><strong>Cloud Run</strong> is a fully managed, serverless platform designed for deploying containerized applications. It automatically scales based on traffic, significantly reduces infrastructure management, and requires minimal operational overhead. Since your application has no specific OS dependencies and you’re aiming to reduce infrastructure management and complexity, Cloud Run is the best choice.</p>\n<p>Key benefits of Cloud Run:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Serverless</strong> : Cloud Run handles all infrastructure management, including scaling, load balancing, and updates.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cost-effective</strong> : You only pay when the application is serving requests, making it ideal for unpredictable or bursty traffic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Low complexity</strong> : Cloud Run requires minimal configuration compared to setting up and maintaining a Kubernetes cluster or virtual machine.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Deploy the image to a GKE Autopilot cluster</strong><br />\nAlthough GKE Autopilot reduces some Kubernetes management overhead, it still requires more configuration and ongoing Kubernetes management than Cloud Run. Cloud Run is a simpler and more cost-effective choice for an application without OS dependencies.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Deploy the image to a GKE Standard cluster</strong><br />\nA GKE Standard cluster provides more control over configuration but requires significant Kubernetes management, which increases complexity and operational overhead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Deploy the image on a Compute Engine instance</strong><br />\nUsing Compute Engine would involve managing VM instances, including scaling, health checks, and OS updates, adding unnecessary complexity compared to a serverless option like Cloud Run.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more information on Cloud Run, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/quickstarts\">Getting started with Cloud Run</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e6q13":{"id":"e6q13","exam":6,"n":13,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You’re a developer for a local concert venue, and customers use your company’s website to purchase event tickets. You need to ensure customers receive immediate confirmation when they reserve a specific seat.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>How should you structure the ticket reservation process?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Place the seat reservation request in a Cloud Tasks queue, which initiates a Workflow to handle the reservation processing.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Publish the seat reservation to a Pub/Sub topic and configure the backend service to use Eventarc for processing the reservation on GKE.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Store the seat reservation in a Cloud Storage bucket, triggering an event to a Cloud Run service that processes the order.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Send the seat reservation as an HTTP POST request to an Application Load Balancer, which distributes it to a Compute Engine managed instance group that processes the reservation.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The most suitable approach to ensure immediate confirmation and efficient processing of seat reservations is:</p>\n<p><strong>B. Publish the seat reservation to a Pub/Sub topic and configure the backend service to use Eventarc for processing the reservation on GKE.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Immediate confirmation:</strong> Pub/Sub ensures a reliable and scalable messaging system. The reservation request is immediately published to the topic, allowing the client to receive confirmation as soon as the request is acknowledged.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficient processing:</strong> Eventarc triggers the backend service on GKE automatically when a new message arrives on the Pub/Sub topic. This eliminates the need for polling or constant monitoring of the queue, leading to efficient processing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> Pub/Sub and Eventarc are highly scalable, allowing the system to handle increased load during peak booking times.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reliability:</strong> Pub/Sub ensures reliable message delivery, reducing the risk of lost reservations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are not suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A. Cloud Tasks and Workflow:</strong> While this approach can be used, it might introduce latency as the reservation request is first queued and then processed by Workflow. This could delay the confirmation process.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C. Cloud Storage:</strong> Storing the reservation in Cloud Storage and triggering a Cloud Run service is not the most efficient way to handle real-time processing. It might introduce unnecessary delays.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>D. HTTP POST and Load Balancer:</strong> While this approach can work, it relies on polling the load balancer for new requests, which can be less efficient than event-driven architectures. Additionally, it might introduce complexities in scaling the backend service.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By using Pub/Sub and Eventarc, you can achieve a reliable, scalable, and efficient seat reservation process, ensuring immediate confirmation for your customers.</p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e6q14":{"id":"e6q14","exam":6,"n":14,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You work at a financial services company that prioritizes containerized applications, and your team develops microservices. A Cloud Build pipeline is set up to create container images, run regression tests, and publish the images to Artifact Registry. To ensure only containers that pass regression tests are deployed to GKE clusters, you have already enabled Binary Authorization on the clusters.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What should you do next?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy both Voucher Server and Voucher Client components. After a container image passes the regression tests, run Voucher Client as a step in the Cloud Build pipeline.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Set up an attestor and a policy, then perform a vulnerability scan and create an attestation for the container image as part of the Cloud Build pipeline.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Set up an attestor and a policy. Create an attestation for container images that pass regression tests as a step in the Cloud Build pipeline.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Apply the Restricted level of Pod Security Standard to the relevant namespaces. Digitally sign the container images that pass regression tests as a step in the Cloud Build pipeline.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is </p>\n<p><strong>C. Set up an attestor and a policy. Create an attestation for container images that pass regression tests as a step in the Cloud Build pipeline.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>With <strong>Binary Authorization</strong> enabled, deploying only trusted container images requires creating <strong>attestations</strong> that certify an image has met specific criteria. By setting up an <strong>attestor</strong> and defining a <strong>policy</strong> in Binary Authorization, you can restrict deployments to images that have a valid attestation. Adding an attestation step in the <strong>Cloud Build pipeline</strong> ensures that only images passing regression tests are signed, thus allowing only those images to be deployed to the GKE clusters.</p>\n<p>This approach leverages Binary Authorization to enforce security policies, ensuring that only verified and tested images are deployed to production environments.</p>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A: Deploy Voucher Server and Client</strong><br />\nVoucher is a third-party tool for creating attestations, but Google Cloud provides native support for attestations with Binary Authorization. Using Voucher introduces additional complexity and is unnecessary if Binary Authorization and Cloud Build are already integrated.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Perform a vulnerability scan and create an attestation</strong><br />\nA vulnerability scan is useful, but the question specifies ensuring that images passing regression tests are deployed. While scanning for vulnerabilities is beneficial, the attestation step should focus on confirming that regression tests have passed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Apply the Restricted Pod Security Standard and sign images</strong><br />\nThe Restricted Pod Security Standard governs security settings for pod configurations but does not verify or enforce container image provenance. Binary Authorization attestations are necessary to enforce that only approved images (those that passed tests) are deployed.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more information, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/overview\">Binary Authorization Overview</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/making-attestations\">Creating Attestations in Binary Authorization</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l9"]},"e6q15":{"id":"e6q15","exam":6,"n":15,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your team is preparing to use AlloyDB as the database backend for a new application release. The application is hosted in a separate project and network from the AlloyDB instances. You need a secure way to connect the application to the AlloyDB instance while keeping both projects isolated. You also want to minimize additional setup steps and follow Google’s best practices.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>How should you set up network connectivity for the database?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a Shared VPC and add both the application and AlloyDB projects as service projects to enable connectivity.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Use the AlloyDB Auth Proxy and configure the application project’s firewall to allow traffic on port 5433.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Generate a service account from the AlloyDB project, then use the JSON key file of this service account as the --credentials-file for connecting to AlloyDB.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Request that the database team deploy AlloyDB databases within the same project and network as the application.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The correct answer is </p>\n<p><strong>A. Create a Shared VPC and add both the application and AlloyDB projects as service projects to enable connectivity.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>Using a <strong>Shared VPC</strong> allows secure and efficient communication between resources in separate projects by enabling them to share the same VPC network while maintaining isolation at the project level. By adding both the application and AlloyDB projects as <strong>service projects</strong> to the Shared VPC, the application can securely connect to AlloyDB without requiring additional complex networking setups or firewall adjustments. This approach follows Google’s best practices for inter-project connectivity and ensures a secure, centralized network management setup.</p>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Use the AlloyDB Auth Proxy and adjust firewall settings</strong><br />\nThe AlloyDB Auth Proxy helps secure database connections but does not address the need for inter-project network connectivity directly. Additionally, manually adjusting firewall rules in the application project could introduce security risks and increase operational complexity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: Use a service account key file for connecting to AlloyDB</strong><br />\nWhile service accounts can authenticate access, this option does not address network connectivity between separate projects. It also involves managing and distributing sensitive credentials, which can introduce security risks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: Deploy AlloyDB within the same project and network as the application</strong><br />\nConsolidating resources in a single project reduces isolation, making it harder to manage project-specific permissions and security policies independently. It’s also not a scalable or flexible setup for larger organizations with separate ownership of resources.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relevant Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p>For more information, refer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/shared-vpc\">Shared VPC Overview</a></li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l8"]},"e6q16":{"id":"e6q16","exam":6,"n":16,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You have a containerized service running on-premises, written in the current stable version of Python 3, and accessible only to users in the United States. This service experiences high traffic during the day and no traffic at night. You want to migrate this application to Google Cloud, track error logs using Error Reporting after migration, and keep costs and setup effort low.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy the service on Cloud Run, and configure it to log errors to standard error.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy the service on Cloud Run, and set it up to stream errors to a Cloud Storage bucket.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy the service on a GKE Autopilot cluster, and configure it to log errors to standard error.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy the service on a GKE Autopilot cluster, and set it up to log errors to a Cloud Storage bucket.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The correct answer is</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Deploy the service on Cloud Run, and configure it to log errors to standard error.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cloud Run</strong> is a fully managed, serverless platform that automatically scales up and down based on traffic, which is ideal for an application with high traffic during the day and no traffic at night. Cloud Run charges only for active usage, keeping costs low during off-peak hours.</p>\n<p>Configuring the service to log errors to <strong>standard error</strong> enables <strong>Google Cloud Error Reporting</strong> to automatically capture and track errors, providing insights without additional configuration or infrastructure setup. This setup is both cost-effective and requires minimal setup effort.</p>\n<p>Justifications for Incorrect Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B: Cloud Run with errors logged to Cloud Storage</strong><br />\nLogging to Cloud Storage does not provide direct integration with Error Reporting, so additional setup would be required to track errors. Logging to standard error is simpler and allows direct integration with Google Cloud’s monitoring tools.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C: GKE Autopilot with errors logged to standard error</strong><br />\nGKE Autopilot is managed but still requires more setup and operational management than Cloud Run. For a stateless, containerized service with intermittent traffic, Cloud Run is more cost-effective.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D: GKE Autopilot with errors logged to Cloud Storage</strong><br />\nUsing GKE Autopilot and Cloud Storage for logging increases both costs and setup complexity, as it doesn’t integrate directly with Error Reporting.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l1"]}}};
